window.BBC_6MIN_EPISODES = [{"epNum":1,"id":"ep-261119","date":"2026-11-19","title":"Are microplastics harming us?","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-261119","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/261119_6_minute_english_are_microplastics_harming_us_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/261119_6_minute_english_are_microplastics_harming_us_transcript.pdf","summary":"How much plastic do you use on a daily basis? And how much of it is used to package or prepare food? Recently, scientists are becoming increasingly concerned that microplastics, tiny bits of plastic invisible to the naked eye, are entering our bodies through these food sources... but it is unclear exactly what the long-term health effects will be. Becca and Phil discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"How long would it take to ingest a credit-card-sized amount of microplastics?","options":["a) 13,000 years","b) 23,000 years","c) 33,000 years"],"topic":"健康医学","titleZh":"微塑料正在危害我们的健康吗？","summaryZh":"你每天要用到多少塑料？其中又有多少用于包装或制作食物？近年来，科学家越来越担忧肉眼看不见的微塑料正通过食物进入人体，但它们对健康的长期影响究竟如何，目前尚无定论。Becca 和 Phil 将探讨这一话题并讲解新词汇。","questionZh":"人体摄入相当于一张信用卡大小的微塑料，需要多长时间？","optionsZh":["a) 13,000 年","b) 23,000 年","c) 33,000 年"],"answer":{"letter":"b","explanationZh":"Phil 在节目结尾揭晓了答案：网传“每周吃下一张信用卡大小的塑料”并不属实，实际摄入这么多微塑料需要 23,000 年。"},"vocab":[{"word":"marine","defEn":"related to, found in or produced by the sea","pos":"adj.","zh":"海洋的；海生的","exampleEn":"And he showed me all these marine animals that looked like they had plastics in their livers and hind guts.","ipa":"/məˈriːn/"},{"word":"emerging","defEn":"coming into view after being hidden, allowing a better understanding or development","pos":"adj.","zh":"新兴的；逐渐显现的","exampleEn":"I looked at the data, and there was emerging evidence that there was plastics in the liver...","ipa":"/ɪˈmɜːdʒɪŋ/"},{"word":"correlation","defEn":"relationship between two or more things","pos":"n.","zh":"相关性；关联","exampleEn":"And the scientists think that there may be a correlation between this and diseases that they developed later on.","ipa":"/ˌkɒrəˈleɪʃən/"},{"word":"spike","defEn":"sudden, sharp increase in something","pos":"n.","zh":"激增；急剧上升","exampleEn":"Well, you know, we have been exposed for decades and surely if it was that bad, you know, we'd have seen a spike or something by now.","ipa":"/spaɪk/"},{"word":"quantified","defEn":"measured and given a number or a value","pos":"adj.","zh":"量化的；用数字衡量的","exampleEn":"And this is not a quantified measure, but our exposure seems to be increasing, and that's the worry for me.","ipa":"/ˈkwɒntɪfaɪd/"},{"word":"exposure","defEn":"come into contact with something that may have an impact on you","pos":"n.","zh":"接触；暴露（于某物中）","exampleEn":"So, Mark concludes that he is worried about our exposure to plastics.","ipa":"/ɪkˈspəʊʒə/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"dive deeper into","zh":"深入研究、深挖（某项数据或话题）；职场汇报和学术讨论高频短语","contextEn":"Yes, these pictures of marine animals with plastics in their bodies inspired Shilpa to dive deeper into the data."},{"en":"have mixed feelings about","zh":"对……喜忧参半 / 看法复杂；雅思口语表达中立或辩证态度非常地道","contextEn":"Science professor Mark Miodownik, from University College London, has mixed feelings about microplastics and explained these to BBC World Service programme The Evidence."},{"en":"be exposed to","zh":"接触到、暴露在……之下（常用于健康、环境风险或新事物语境）","contextEn":"For example, scientists have been looking at people who worked in nylon factories where they were exposed to high levels of microplastic."},{"en":"thank goodness","zh":"谢天谢地、好险！日常口语表达松了一口气时的自然感叹","contextEn":"Oh, thank goodness!"}],"sentences":[{"en":"For example, scientists have been looking at people who worked in nylon factories where they were exposed to high levels of microplastic.","zh":"例如，科学家一直在研究曾在尼龙工厂工作的工人，他们在那里接触到了高浓度的微塑料。","coreEn":"Scientists have been looking at people.","coreZh":"科学家一直在研究（一群）人。","cars":[{"text":"For example,","hook":"过渡短语","noteZh":"引出具体例证，说明前文观点"},{"text":"who worked in nylon factories","hook":"关系词 who","noteZh":"限定是哪一群人：在尼龙工厂工作过的人"},{"text":"where they were exposed to high levels of microplastic","hook":"关系词 where","noteZh":"接着修饰前面的 nylon factories，补充说明在这些工厂里发生了什么"}],"tipZh":"雅思阅读和听力中常出现“套娃式”后置修饰（people → who... → where...），顺着英文语序像剥洋葱一样逐层往后理解，不要倒着翻译。"},{"en":"Well, scientists have found that seafood is one of the main food groups where microplastics can be found, and BBC World Service programme The Evidence has been investigating this.","zh":"嗯，科学家发现海鲜是能检测到微塑料的主要食物类别之一，BBC 国际台的《证据》节目也一直在调查此事。","coreEn":"Scientists have found that seafood is one of the main food groups, and The Evidence has been investigating this.","coreZh":"科学家发现海鲜是主要食物类别之一，而且《证据》节目一直在调查这件事。","cars":[{"text":"where microplastics can be found","hook":"关系词 where","noteZh":"修饰前面的 food groups，说明是“能找到微塑料的”食物类别"},{"text":"BBC World Service programme","hook":"同位修饰","noteZh":"在节目名 The Evidence 之前亮明身份，交代媒体来源"}],"tipZh":"听到 and 连接两个并列分句时，大脑要及时“清空前半句、准备接新主语”，同时留意 where 不仅指地点，也能指代“在某类食物/情况中”。"},{"en":"She found emerging evidence, and the adjective emerging describes something coming into view after being hidden, allowing it to develop or be better understood.","zh":"她发现了逐渐显现的证据，而形容词 emerging 用来形容某事物在隐藏之后浮出水面，从而得以发展或被更好地理解。","coreEn":"She found emerging evidence, and the adjective emerging describes something.","coreZh":"她发现了新显现的证据，形容词 emerging 描述某事物。","cars":[{"text":"coming into view","hook":"-ing 修饰","noteZh":"紧跟 something 后面，说明是“正在进入视野的”某事物"},{"text":"after being hidden","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"交代时间背景：在被隐藏之后"},{"text":"allowing it to develop or be better understood","hook":"逗号 + -ing 结果补充","noteZh":"补充说明浮出水面后带来的结果：使其得以发展或更好被理解"}],"tipZh":"逗号后面紧跟 -ing（如 , allowing...）是雅思阅读解释“顺带产生的结果”的高频结构，直接顺译成“从而使……”。"},{"en":"And another tip from BBC World Service's The Evidence is to replace tea bags, which often contain microplastics, with loose-leaf tea.","zh":"BBC 国际台《证据》节目给出的另一条建议是，用散装茶叶替代通常含有微塑料的茶包。","coreEn":"Another tip is to replace tea bags with loose-leaf tea.","coreZh":"另一条建议是用散茶替代茶包。","cars":[{"text":"from BBC World Service's The Evidence","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"贴在 tip 后面，交代建议的出处"},{"text":"which often contain microplastics","hook":"逗号插入语 + 关系词 which","noteZh":"插在 replace A with B 中间，补充说明为什么茶包不好（通常含微塑料）"}],"tipZh":"遇到 replace A, which..., with B 这种被插入语隔开的固定搭配时，先把逗号里的“车厢”跳过去，一眼锁死 replace... with... 的骨架。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Hi Becca.","zh":"嗨，Becca。","s":0.18,"e":0.82,"wt":[0,6]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Hi Phil.","zh":"嗨，Phil。","s":1,"e":1.81,"wt":[0,20]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Becca, I've got a question for you. What's the last plastic thing you had in your hands?","zh":"Becca，我有个问题想问你。你刚才手里拿过的最后一样塑料制品是什么？","s":1.81,"e":7.08,"wt":[0,47,65,83,91,139,171,241,264,267,321,363,412,419,445,451,467]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Well, I just did my makeup, so probably a makeup brush.","zh":"嗯，我刚化完妆，所以大概是化妆刷吧。","s":7.28,"e":11.62,"wt":[0,39,40,77,105,156,205,215,290,299,354]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Many of us own lots of things made of plastic. It's cheap, easy to shape and long lasting, but that's not always a good thing. In this episode of 6 Minute English, we'll be hearing from experts as they teach us about microplastics – those are tiny plastic pieces. And we'll be asking the question, 'Are microplastics affecting our health?'","zh":"我们很多人都有一大堆塑料制品。塑料便宜、易塑形又耐用，但这并不总是好事。在本期《六分钟英语》中，我们将听专家科普微塑料——也就是极其微小的塑料颗粒，并探讨一个问题：“微塑料正在影响我们的健康吗？”","s":11.68,"e":37.64,"wt":[0,52,72,109,153,183,200,256,296,328,431,454,517,538,552,604,620,701,782,820,878,896,910,922,944,1038,1072,1139,1163,1184,1195,1253,1304,1316,1338,1350,1416,1430,1442,1470,1504,1522,1605,1705,1744,1766,1808,1835,1902,1992,2028,2040,2068,2086,2140,2144,2249,2360,2448,2528]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"OK. Well, let's start with our quiz, Becca. There was a social media post that says that every week we ingest the same amount of plastic as there is in a credit card, but that's not true.","zh":"好，那我们先从问答开始吧，Becca。之前社交媒体上有个帖子说，我们每周吃进去的塑料量相当于一张信用卡，不过那其实不是真的。","s":39.22,"e":52.64,"wt":[0,24,84,104,104,128,172,221,310,342,352,367,404,462,478,518,558,630,655,750,810,900,924,948,964,1006,1054,1054,1055,1074,1102,1108,1161,1232,1244,1271,1286]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Oh, thank goodness!","zh":"哦，谢天谢地！","s":52.7,"e":53.76,"wt":[0,34,51]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"So, my question for you is how long would it really take to ingest that amount of microplastics? Would it be: a) 13,000 years, b) 23,000 years, or c) 33,000 years?","zh":"所以我的问题是，实际摄入那么多微塑料究竟需要多长时间？是：a) 13,000 年，b) 23,000 年，还是 c) 33,000 年？","s":53.88,"e":70.88,"wt":[0,12,26,56,78,94,162,194,234,246,292,345,374,394,452,485,533,548,692,692,708,760,856,1000,1094,1200,1322,1455,1508,1548,1643]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Well, I want to be hopeful, so I'll go with the longest period of time. That would be 33,000 years.","zh":"嗯，我想乐观一点，所以我选时间最长的那个，也就是 33,000 年。","s":71.24,"e":77.98,"wt":[0,26,33,72,79,156,212,228,228,228,255,292,333,395,402,474,482,492,524,628]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"OK. Well, we'll find out the answer at the end of the programme.","zh":"好的，节目最后我们来揭晓答案。","s":78.24,"e":81.48,"wt":[0,26,34,68,82,92,122,174,182,196,208,216,244]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"So, the World Health Organisation says that microplastics have been found in many different food groups. But Phil, what is the food group that is more affected by microplastics?","zh":"那么，世界卫生组织表示，在许多不同类别的食物中都检测到了微塑料。不过 Phil，受微塑料影响更大的是哪类食物呢？","s":81.78,"e":92.48,"wt":[0,36,56,88,142,244,249,285,390,402,418,451,465,492,556,581,646,667,734,736,749,773,803,826,842,868,893,946,968]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Well, scientists have found that seafood is one of the main food groups where microplastics can be found, and BBC World Service programme The Evidence has been investigating this. Professor Shilpa Chokshi told host Claudia Hammond about some pictures that she was shown by a microplastics expert.","zh":"嗯，科学家发现海鲜是能检测到微塑料的主要食物类别之一，BBC 国际台的《证据》节目一直在调查这件事。Shilpa Chokshi 教授向主持人 Claudia Hammond 讲述了某位微塑料专家给她看的一些照片。","s":92.48,"e":114.36,"wt":[0,62,105,142,160,216,255,284,293,310,322,372,410,454,504,592,592,648,719,744,785,848,912,962,1002,1068,1068,1133,1176,1290,1296,1340,1505,1540,1603,1632,1744,1755,1783,1839,1862,1862,1906,1949,1958,1965,2057]},{"speaker":"Professor Shilpa Chokshi","en":"And he showed me all these marine animals that looked like they had plastics in their livers and hind guts. And from that initial discussion, I went away and I looked at the literature and I looked at the data, and there was emerging evidence that there was plastics in the liver, and there was this correlation increase.","zh":"他给我看了所有这些海洋动物的照片，它们的肝脏和后肠里看起来都有塑料。在那次初步讨论之后，我回去查阅了文献和数据，发现越来越多新证据表明肝脏里确实存在塑料，而且这种相关性还在上升。","s":114.7,"e":133.16,"wt":[0,14,36,59,91,115,142,156,214,219,248,248,278,300,312,326,336,414,438,474,540,546,578,642,714,756,772,792,821,826,870,886,896,920,968,976,988,988,998,1044,1112,1123,1135,1146,1226,1270,1304,1354,1382,1439,1458,1474,1523,1548,1590,1610,1649,1804]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Shilpa says that plastics have been found in marine animals. Marine describes anything related to, found in or produced by the sea.","zh":"Shilpa 说在海洋动物（marine animals）体内发现了塑料。marine 用来形容任何与海洋有关、存在于海洋中或产自海洋的事物。","s":133.19,"e":142.48,"wt":[0,38,85,107,173,191,223,253,275,330,415,457,529,586,633,713,734,752,781,859,869,888]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Yes, these pictures of marine animals with plastics in their bodies inspired Shilpa to dive deeper into the data. She found emerging evidence, and the adjective emerging describes something coming into view after being hidden, allowing it to develop or be better understood.","zh":"没错，这些体内含有塑料的海洋动物照片促使 Shilpa 深入研究数据。她发现了“逐渐显现的”（emerging）证据，形容词 emerging 用来描述某事物在隐藏之后浮现出来，从而得以发展或被更好地认识。","s":142.66,"e":161.9,"wt":[0,33,79,118,158,187,244,254,330,335,359,454,500,544,568,610,660,690,716,782,804,859,940,1029,1047,1068,1158,1274,1314,1364,1401,1446,1471,1546,1556,1636,1684,1693,1707,1760,1766,1814,1835]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"And from this emerging evidence, Shilpa noticed a correlation . You'll hear this word a lot when talking about data. A correlation is a link or connection between two or more things. So, Phil, we know how toxic microplastics get into our body, but are they definitely harmful?","zh":"从这些逐渐显现的证据中，Shilpa 注意到了一种“相关性”（correlation）。谈论数据时你会经常听到这个词。correlation 指两个或多个事物之间的联系或关联。那么 Phil，我们知道有毒微塑料是怎么进入人体的，但它们肯定有害吗？","s":161.91,"e":179.92,"wt":[0,31,51,81,138,203,279,301,310,374,417,458,465,505,512,519,544,574,595,621,720,727,811,823,834,855,903,975,1013,1031,1045,1073,1146,1161,1206,1230,1258,1317,1333,1438,1474,1500,1547,1593,1635,1639,1665,1733]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Well, plastics have been a big part of daily life since the 1950s, but it's only recently that we've started thinking about microplastics as a health issue. For example, scientists have been looking at people who worked in nylon factories where they were exposed to high levels of microplastic. And the scientists think that there may be a correlation between this and diseases that they developed later on. Science professor Mark Miodownik, from University College London, has mixed feelings about microplastics and explained these to BBC World Service programme The Evidence.","zh":"嗯，自 20 世纪 50 年代以来，塑料就成了日常生活的重要组成部分，但直到最近我们才开始把微塑料当作健康问题来审视。例如，科学家一直在研究曾在尼龙工厂工作的工人，他们在那里接触了高浓度的微塑料，科学家认为这与他们后来患上的疾病之间可能存在关联。伦敦大学学院的科学教授 Mark Miodownik 对微塑料看法复杂，他在 BBC 国际台《证据》节目中阐述了自己的观点。","s":179.92,"e":219.78,"wt":[0,72,111,140,146,168,206,229,244,278,309,352,368,496,568,599,641,696,712,760,789,850,916,983,998,1005,1050,1131,1158,1260,1274,1329,1336,1379,1400,1429,1469,1490,1502,1556,1605,1634,1662,1688,1726,1739,1798,1809,1824,1974,1977,2002,2062,2068,2082,2132,2174,2204,2264,2310,2390,2454,2477,2552,2569,2608,2660,2692,2764,2814,2870,2896,3000,3006,3086,3137,3201,3272,3289,3362,3424,3506,3534,3598,3622,3670,3681,3720,3777,3865,3879]},{"speaker":"Professor Mark Miodownik","en":"Well, you know, we have been exposed for decades and surely if it was that bad, you know, we'd have seen a spike or something by now. But on the other hand, you know, I think, you know, if you look at how much plastic packaging is in supermarkets, it's gone up and up and up and up. And this is not a quantified measure, but our exposure seems to be increasing, and that's the worry for me.","zh":"嗯，你知道，我们接触塑料已经几十年了，如果它真的那么糟糕，到现在肯定早就出现某种病例激增之类的现象了。但另一方面，你看超市里的塑料包装数量，一直在节节攀升。虽然这不是一项量化指标，但我们的接触量似乎在不断增加，这正是让我担心的地方。","s":220.16,"e":242.96,"wt":[0,0,0,56,56,56,93,120,180,196,230,258,267,286,301,337,426,432,477,507,538,544,586,614,638,700,717,768,786,786,830,850,872,880,898,920,929,933,941,959,988,1010,1015,1064,1076,1126,1168,1178,1197,1274,1314,1325,1341,1360,1384,1396,1406,1416,1497,1584,1594,1610,1624,1638,1668,1715,1717,1734,1800,1825,1833,1846,1990,2152,2152,2176,2190,2222]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Mark mentions that some people say if microplastics were harmful, we surely would have seen a spike in issues related to them by now. A spike is literally a pointy shape, but here it means a sudden, sharp increase in something.","zh":"Mark 提到，有人认为如果微塑料真有害，我们到现在肯定早就看到相关健康问题出现“激增”（spike）了。spike 字面意思是尖刺状的东西，但在这里指某事物的突然、急剧上升。","s":242.96,"e":257.78,"wt":[0,52,102,108,135,184,216,262,320,374,418,440,466,508,530,548,553,596,598,646,680,702,728,729,850,850,861,950,984,1014,1038,1106,1123,1169,1172,1215,1222,1262,1304,1378,1414]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Right. And though the amount of plastic packaging we use has increased, this isn't a quantified measure. If something is quantified , then it's measured and given a number or a value.","zh":"对。而且尽管我们使用的塑料包装量增加了，但这并不是一个“量化”（quantified）指标。如果某事物是 quantified，意思是它经过了测量，并被赋予了具体的数字或数值。","s":257.9,"e":271.22,"wt":[0,34,58,98,124,203,218,287,326,356,380,422,506,538,568,616,696,762,787,820,906,959,983,1017,1062,1119,1144,1174,1194,1240,1252,1298]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"So, Mark concludes that he is worried about our exposure to plastics. Exposure means coming into contact with something that may have an impact on you. OK. Phil, so, if people are worried about their exposure to microplastics, what can they do to reduce this?","zh":"所以 Mark 总结说，他担心的是我们对塑料的“接触”（exposure）。exposure 指接触到某种可能对你产生影响的物质。好的 Phil，那如果人们担心自己接触太多微塑料，能做些什么来减少接触呢？","s":271.36,"e":289.01,"wt":[0,38,112,153,162,184,208,288,301,327,383,398,546,550,626,626,686,709,734,808,832,864,897,912,952,980,1102,1124,1190,1202,1228,1256,1296,1310,1333,1360,1424,1440,1592,1596,1617,1644,1660,1682,1720]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Well, one thing they can do is to replace things like plastic chopping boards, utensils and food storage containers. And another tip from BBC World Service's The Evidence is to replace tea bags, which often contain microplastics, with loose-leaf tea. OK. Becca, now it's time to get the answer to our quiz question. And I asked you how long it would take to ingest a credit-card-sized amount of microplastics.","zh":"嗯，他们可以做的一件事是替换掉塑料砧板、塑料厨具和塑料保鲜盒这类物品。BBC 国际台《证据》节目给出的另一条建议，是用散装茶叶替代通常含有微塑料的茶包。好了 Becca，现在该揭晓问答的答案了。我问你摄入一张信用卡大小的微塑料需要多长时间。","s":289.01,"e":321.16,"wt":[0,42,63,104,127,148,171,177,225,251,299,339,378,430,496,599,629,675,707,823,823,885,932,963,1018,1030,1099,1108,1176,1197,1255,1265,1313,1397,1447,1501,1505,1593,1622,1700,1899,1954,2011,2025,2045,2045,2072,2079,2161,2167,2239,2281,2345,2401,2410,2418,2459,2499,2547,2620,2635,2675,2714,2733,2849,2879,2951,2985,3033]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Yes, and I said 33,000 years.","zh":"是的，我选的是 33,000 年。","s":321.4,"e":325.66,"wt":[0,70,92,111,172,324]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"OK. Well, I've got two bits of bad news for you, Becca. One, you were wrong. And two, I'm afraid it's only 23,000 years that it would take to ingest that plastic. OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we learned in this programme, starting with marine , which describes anything related to, found in or produced by the sea.","zh":"好吧，Becca，我有两个坏消息要告诉你。第一，你答错了。第二，恐怕只需要 23,000 年就会摄入那么多塑料。好了，让我们回顾一下本期节目学到的词汇，先从 marine 开始，它指任何与海洋有关、存在于海中或产自海洋的事物。","s":325.84,"e":349.86,"wt":[0,0,7,35,63,93,114,122,173,180,196,236,303,344,366,422,467,504,536,552,603,628,663,870,890,900,910,910,923,937,976,1022,1102,1182,1199,1272,1315,1369,1384,1406,1440,1480,1563,1612,1672,1729,1766,1782,1862,1919,1986,2072,2074,2096,2172,2190,2243,2252]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"The adjective emerging describes something coming into view after being hidden, allowing it to develop or be better understood.","zh":"形容词 emerging 指某事物在隐藏后逐渐显现出来，从而能够发展或被更好地理解。","s":349.86,"e":359.21,"wt":[0,36,92,172,263,310,349,385,452,475,520,610,650,663,688,739,753,769,852]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"A correlation is a relationship between two or more things.","zh":"correlation 指两个或多个事物之间的关联、相关性。","s":359.21,"e":363.92,"wt":[0,9,127,143,159,263,304,332,353,409]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"A spike is a sudden, sharp increase in something.","zh":"spike 指某事物突然、急剧地上升。","s":363.92,"e":367.92,"wt":[0,60,108,108,156,186,266,270,342]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"If something is quantified , it's measured and given a number or a value.","zh":"如果某事物是 quantified，表示它经过了量化测量并被赋予了数值。","s":367.92,"e":373.5,"wt":[0,14,83,160,207,228,257,318,341,379,394,447,448,454]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"And exposure means to come into contact with something that may have an impact on you.","zh":"而 exposure 指接触到某种可能对你造成影响的事物。","s":373.5,"e":380.06,"wt":[0,106,113,178,204,226,250,338,356,436,444,498,512,552,584,602]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Once again, our six minutes are up. Test what you've learned with the worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"六分钟又到了。欢迎前往我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 做练习题，检测你的学习成果。","s":380.1,"e":388.94,"wt":[0,66,107,118,164,202,227,279,310,350,380,390,406,436,498,520,584,608]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0pnsz8p.mp3"},{"epNum":2,"id":"ep-261122","date":"2026-11-12","title":"Learning from different generations","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-261122","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/261112_6_minute_english_learning_from_different_generations_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/261112_6_minute_english_learning_from_different_generations_transcript.pdf","summary":"When you were younger, did you have a special bond with your grandparents? Many of us develop particularly close attachments with older family members, but did you know that this ability for the oldest and youngest of our society to get along in a friendly way is not limited to families? In fact, there are some projects which pair together nurseries and retirement homes, to help reduce feelings of loneliness and improve everybody's general health and happiness. Georgie and Phil discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"What generation would someone born in 1982 be in?","options":["a) Generation X","b) Millennial","c) Gen Z"],"topic":"心理社交","titleZh":"跨越代际的双向学习","summaryZh":"你小时候和祖父母有特别的羁绊吗？很多人都会与家中长辈建立格外亲密的感情，但你知道吗？社会中最年长和最年幼的人友好相处的能力并不局限于家庭内部。事实上，一些项目正把幼儿园和养老院结合在一起，帮助减少孤独感，提升大家的整体身心健康和幸福感。Georgie 和 Phil 将讨论这一话题并讲解新词汇。","questionZh":"1982 年出生的人属于哪一代？","optionsZh":["a) X 世代（Generation X）","b) 千禧一代（Millennial）","c) Z 世代（Gen Z）"],"answer":{"letter":"b","explanationZh":"Phil 在节目尾声揭晓答案：1982 年出生的人属于千禧一代（Millennial），而不是 Georgie 猜的 X 世代。"},"vocab":[{"word":"generation","defEn":"group of people who are of a similar age","pos":"n.","zh":"一代人；同龄人群体","exampleEn":"And you might feel that it's difficult to understand or agree with people from a different generation .","ipa":"/ˌdʒenəˈreɪʃən/"},{"word":"fill a void","defEn":"help to feel better after experiencing loss","pos":"短语","zh":"填补空虚；抚平失落","exampleEn":"Ian was once a fighter pilot, and getting involved in the nursery has helped him fill a void .","ipa":"/fɪl ə vɔɪd/"},{"word":"such and such","defEn":"reference to something without giving a specific example","pos":"短语","zh":"某某；这个那个（泛指不具体举例的事物）","exampleEn":"And the people down in the nursery, they phoned me up and said, \"We were doing such and such a thing. Why don't you come and join us?\"","ipa":"/sʌtʃ ənd sʌtʃ/"},{"word":"hear on the grapevine","defEn":"hear a rumour","pos":"短语","zh":"道听途说；听小道消息","exampleEn":"If you hear something on the grapevine , you hear a rumour.","ipa":"/hɪər ɒn ðə ˈɡreɪpvaɪn/"},{"word":"break down stereotypes","defEn":"find evidence that common beliefs about a group of people are untrue","pos":"短语","zh":"打破刻板印象","exampleEn":"And as I realised, these interactions also seem to be breaking down stereotypes .","ipa":"/breɪk daʊn ˈsteriətaɪps/"},{"word":"lively","defEn":"loud and with lots of energy","pos":"adj.","zh":"充满活力的；热闹活泼的","exampleEn":"Your first thought would be how lively and energetic young children can be, and what we found is so can older people.","ipa":"/ˈlaɪvli/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"a wide range of","zh":"各种各样的、范围很广的……（雅思口语和写作替换 many/lots of 的万金油表达）","contextEn":"I do, actually, because I'm in a choir, so there's quite a wide range of ages – lots of older people, lots of younger people."},{"en":"get involved in","zh":"参与、投入到某项活动或项目中（比 join 更地道且偏主动投入）","contextEn":"Ian was once a fighter pilot, and getting involved in the nursery has helped him fill a void ."},{"en":"calm down","zh":"冷静下来、安静点儿（口语中既可安抚情绪，也可让吵闹的人静下来）","contextEn":"And so sometimes, you know, it's the older people that we're saying, \"Can we just calm this down a little bit?\""},{"en":"big characters","zh":"个性鲜明、极具魅力或很活跃的人（英式口语夸人“很有个性、很会活跃气氛”）","contextEn":"The older people in my choir are big characters. It's often them who are dragging us all to the pub...!"}],"sentences":[{"en":"Today on 6 Minute English, we're going to be finding out about a project that is gathering together the oldest people – that's those that are retired – with the youngest, and that's children who attend nursery.","zh":"在今天的《六分钟英语》中，我们将了解一个把社会上最年长的人——也就是退休老人——和最年幼的人，也就是上幼儿园的孩子们聚集在一起的项目。","coreEn":"We're going to be finding out about a project.","coreZh":"我们将了解一个项目。","cars":[{"text":"Today on 6 Minute English,","hook":"时间与场景状语","noteZh":"交代节目标志性开场背景"},{"text":"that is gathering together the oldest people – that's those that are retired – with the youngest","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"说明这个项目是做什么的：把最年长的人和最年幼的人聚到一起"},{"text":"– that's those that are retired –","hook":"破折号插入语","noteZh":"顺口解释前面的 the oldest people 具体指退休人员"},{"text":"and that's children who attend nursery","hook":"补充说明分句","noteZh":"顺口解释前面的 the youngest 具体指上幼儿园的小朋友"}],"tipZh":"英语播客和演讲喜欢用破折号或 that's... 随时插播定义，听/读的时候把解释性插入语当成“括号里的备注”，就能稳住主线不跑偏。"},{"en":"Well, the charity Ready Generations, which runs the nursery, says it works with universities to study the effects of mixing generations on health and wellbeing, and they say they've seen positive results.","zh":"嗯，运营这家幼儿园的慈善机构 Ready Generations 表示，它正与多所大学合作，研究跨代共处对健康和福祉的影响，而且他们表示已经看到了积极的成果。","coreEn":"The charity Ready Generations says it works with universities, and they say they've seen positive results.","coreZh":"慈善机构 Ready Generations 说它与大学合作，并且看到了积极结果。","cars":[{"text":"which runs the nursery","hook":"逗号插入语 + 关系词 which","noteZh":"插在主语和谓语动词 says 之间，交代这家机构的身份：负责运营该幼儿园"},{"text":"to study the effects of mixing generations on health and wellbeing","hook":"不定式 to do（表目的）","noteZh":"说明与大学合作的目的：研究跨代共处对身心健康的影响"}],"tipZh":"主语和动词之间如果被逗号插入语（, which runs...,）隔开，阅读时先把主语和后面的动词连起来看（The charity ... says），句子立刻通透。"},{"en":"And there have been studies around the world on projects that encourage different generations to mix, and they found that they can help reduce loneliness, improve how healthy and well people feel, and even boost learning and creativity.","zh":"世界各地已有不少针对鼓励跨代共处项目的研究，他们发现这些项目有助于减少孤独感、提升人们的身心健康感受，甚至还能促进学习力和创造力。","coreEn":"There have been studies, and they found that they can help reduce loneliness, improve how people feel, and boost learning and creativity.","coreZh":"已经有一些研究，而且它们发现这些项目能减少孤独、改善健康感受并提升学习与创造力。","cars":[{"text":"around the world","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"限定研究的范围：全球各地"},{"text":"on projects that encourage different generations to mix","hook":"介词短语 + 关系词 that","noteZh":"说明这些研究针对的主题：鼓励不同世代共处的项目"},{"text":"reduce loneliness, improve how healthy and well people feel, and even boost learning and creativity","hook":"三个并列动词短语","noteZh":"一口气列举三个积极好处，共用前面的情态动词 can help / can"}],"tipZh":"雅思听力里经常出现连续三个并列好处（A, B, and even C），最后一个 and even 后面往往藏着填空题或选择题的得分点。"},{"en":"Ian explains that after his wife died, staff at the nursery would phone him to tell him about the daily activities, which he refers to as such and such .","zh":"Ian 解释说，在他妻子去世后，幼儿园的工作人员会打电话告诉他日常安排了哪些活动，他把这些活动泛称为“这样那样的事”（such and such）。","coreEn":"Ian explains that staff would phone him.","coreZh":"Ian 解释说工作人员会给他打电话。","cars":[{"text":"after his wife died","hook":"状语从句（表时间）","noteZh":"插在 that 从句开头，交代事情发生的时间背景"},{"text":"at the nursery","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"限定是哪里的工作人员：幼儿园的"},{"text":"to tell him about the daily activities","hook":"不定式 to do（表目的）","noteZh":"说明打电话的目的：告诉他每天的活动安排"},{"text":"which he refers to as such and such","hook":"逗号 + 关系词 which","noteZh":"补充说明他对“日常活动”的称呼方式（refer to A as B：把 A 称为 B）"}],"tipZh":"看到 would 在过去语境里出现，不一定是“将会”，而是表示“过去常常会”（would phone him = 过去常给他打电话），这在英语故事叙述中极常见。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Hi Georgie.","zh":"嗨，Georgie。","s":5.43,"e":6.44,"wt":[0,14]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Hi Phil.","zh":"嗨，Phil。","s":10.73,"e":16.24,"wt":[0,138]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"So, Georgie, do you often spend time with people who are much older than you or much younger than you?","zh":"Georgie，你平时经常跟比你年长很多或者年轻很多的人相处吗？","s":16.24,"e":23.32,"wt":[0,62,143,173,179,214,256,286,305,344,371,400,479,498,521,560,576,616,656,672]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"I do, actually, because I'm in a choir, so there's quite a wide range of ages – lots of older people, lots of younger people.","zh":"其实经常，因为我参加了一个合唱团，里面的年龄跨度很大——有很多年长者，也有很多年轻人。","s":23.39,"e":31.12,"wt":[0,6,24,98,170,190,199,217,283,293,323,323,365,399,449,453,491,524,541,551,581,632,659,674,727]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"We can call an age group of people a generation . And you might feel that it's difficult to understand or agree with people from a different generation . Today on 6 Minute English, we're going to be finding out about a project that is gathering together the oldest people – that's those that are retired – with the youngest, and that's children who attend nursery. And we'll be asking the question, 'What can we learn from different generations ?'","zh":"我们可以把年龄相仿的一群人称为“一代人”（generation）。你可能会觉得，要理解不同世代的人或与他们达成共识并不容易。在今天的《六分钟英语》中，我们要了解一个将最年长的人——也就是退休老人——和最年幼的人，也就是上幼儿园的孩子聚集在一起的项目。我们将探讨的问题是：“我们可以从不同世代的人身上学到什么？”","s":31.3,"e":61.66,"wt":[0,18,48,82,124,146,188,213,290,291,372,429,454,485,502,526,553,582,654,670,762,767,807,839,887,919,927,1003,1066,1110,1158,1196,1211,1246,1297,1325,1356,1368,1388,1428,1444,1474,1480,1524,1550,1580,1618,1730,1782,1838,1875,1899,1937,1970,1994,2025,2058,2078,2107,2128,2190,2207,2252,2304,2324,2342,2454,2478,2494,2518,2561,2580,2661,2692,2712,2731,2771,2792,2858,2965]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Let's start with our quiz question, Georgie. What generation would someone born in 1982 be in? Is it: a) Generation X, b) Millennial, or c) Gen Z.","zh":"先来做道问答吧，Georgie。1982 年出生的人属于哪一代？是：a) X 世代，b) 千禧一代，还是 c) Z 世代？","s":61.66,"e":77.86,"wt":[0,32,42,66,95,138,188,293,336,444,486,522,568,639,768,789,850,875,899,1008,1068,1188,1268,1368,1400,1500,1500]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Phil, I'm going to go with Generation X.","zh":"Phil，我选 X 世代。","s":78.32,"e":81.9,"wt":[0,0,78,78,95,119,165,273]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"OK. Well, we will find out the answer at the end of the programme.","zh":"好，我们节目最后再来揭晓答案。","s":81.95,"e":85.92,"wt":[0,40,69,92,116,140,162,183,226,240,261,283,296,317]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK. Phil, so you said we're going to look at a project which gets small children interacting with older retired people. So, how does it work?","zh":"好的 Phil，你刚才说我们要介绍一个让幼儿和退休老人互动的项目。它具体是怎么运作的呢？","s":85.92,"e":95.16,"wt":[0,52,79,86,120,136,162,198,213,244,252,266,325,362,389,446,540,586,613,643,704,755,770,797,835,853]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Well, in Chester, in the northwest of England, a nursery has opened up in a retirement village. There's the usual nursery staff to look after the children, but the older residents can join in for activities as well. There's a library, a cafeteria and a garden for the children and the older people to share, and some of the older people or 'grand-friends', as the children call them, lead some of the activities, like dance or crafts. And presenter of BBC World Service podcast People Fixing the World, Myra Anubi, went to visit the special nursery and spoke to some of the participants.","zh":"嗯，在英格兰西北部的切斯特，有一家幼儿园开在了养老社区里面。这里有常规的幼师负责照顾孩子，但老年居民也可以一起来参加活动。这里有供孩子和老人们共享的图书馆、餐厅和花园，一些被孩子们称为“爷爷奶奶朋友”的老人还会带大家做舞蹈或手工等活动。BBC 国际台播客《改变世界的人们》的主持人 Myra Anubi 走访了这家特别的幼儿园，并采访了其中的几位参与者。","s":95.17,"e":134.47,"wt":[0,41,56,113,125,148,216,232,346,375,429,451,483,507,515,533,604,681,726,748,787,835,871,885,914,950,984,1052,1085,1104,1141,1207,1235,1255,1273,1303,1347,1389,1459,1493,1523,1595,1603,1675,1699,1707,1763,1782,1803,1849,1849,1873,1931,1933,1965,2070,2097,2126,2142,2165,2213,2253,2268,2371,2387,2429,2491,2519,2569,2599,2599,2607,2641,2709,2749,2799,2835,2947,2960,3029,3057,3091,3125,3182,3223,3265,3303,3319,3359,3397,3456,3485,3499,3533,3576,3606,3667,3691,3725,3750,3777,3789,3815]},{"speaker":"Myra Anubi","en":"Ian was once a fighter pilot, and getting involved in the nursery has helped him fill a void .","zh":"Ian 曾经是一名战斗机飞行员，参与这家幼儿园的活动帮助他填补了内心的空虚（fill a void）。","s":134.47,"e":140.68,"wt":[0,30,46,76,103,136,221,245,270,311,337,349,409,435,479,505,536,551,581]},{"speaker":"Ian","en":"It was after my wife died last year. It seemed I had a big, big hole to fill. And the people down in the nursery, they phoned me up and said, \"We were doing such and such a thing. Why don't you come and join us?\" And that's what I've been doing. So, yes, and I've enjoyed it. And I find it very interesting to see how they develop.","zh":"那是在去年我妻子去世之后。我感觉心里像是空了一大块需要填补。然后幼儿园的工作人员给我打来电话说：“我们正在做某某活动，您要不要过来一起参加？”后来我就一直来参加。是的，我很乐在其中，看着孩子们一点点成长变化，我觉得非常有意思。","s":140.68,"e":166.28,"wt":[0,20,46,84,108,130,138,178,266,300,394,402,430,438,460,478,518,539,581,612,766,796,820,896,996,1054,1086,1108,1116,1126,1142,1153,1158,1158,1188,1215,1236,1261,1272,1321,1339,1369,1384,1396,1422,1448,1485,1574,1574,1574,1589,1614,1814,1914,1940,1948,1973,2020,2124,2182,2236,2236,2236,2300,2346,2376,2436,2452,2482]},{"speaker":"Myra Anubi","en":"One of the things I heard from the grapevine is that you help the children make paper planes.","zh":"我听小道消息说（heard from the grapevine），有一件事就是您会教孩子们折纸飞机。","s":166.28,"e":171.2,"wt":[0,12,22,50,70,96,138,138,138,214,247,270,290,316,338,382,417,457]},{"speaker":"Ian","en":"That's right.","zh":"没错。","s":171.28,"e":171.99,"wt":[0,46]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Myra says that the nursery helped Ian fill a void after his wife sadly died. If you fill a void , you find something to help you feel better after a loss.","zh":"Myra 说，在 Ian 的妻子不幸去世后，这家幼儿园帮他“填补了空虚”（fill a void）。fill a void 的意思是在经历失去之后，找到某件事来让自己好受一些。","s":172.18,"e":181.86,"wt":[0,36,62,84,108,154,194,225,252,259,289,327,348,387,417,478,532,532,540,592,592,592,609,675,708,723,753,776,805,864,903,915]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Ian explains that after his wife died, staff at the nursery would phone him to tell him about the daily activities, which he refers to as such and such . And that's an expression that we use to talk about something without giving a specific example.","zh":"Ian 解释说，妻子去世后，幼儿园工作人员常会打电话告诉他每天的活动安排，他用 such and such（这样那样的事）来泛指。我们在谈论某件事而不想举具体例子时，就可以用这个表达。","s":181.86,"e":197.87,"wt":[0,28,90,123,180,188,221,334,378,394,420,420,449,490,514,542,563,601,633,657,689,774,806,823,862,884,948,974,1004,1021,1044,1044,1054,1077,1152,1158,1185,1204,1219,1249,1286,1355,1406,1448,1469,1518]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"It sounds like Ian has enjoyed spending time with the children at the nursery, and Myra tells him she's heard from the grapevine that he's been teaching the children to make paper planes.","zh":"听起来 Ian 很喜欢在幼儿园和孩子们待在一起，Myra 还告诉他，自己听小道消息说（heard from the grapevine）他一直在教孩子们折纸飞机。","s":197.87,"e":207.92,"wt":[0,31,46,70,101,109,159,215,232,245,269,283,319,347,401,420,444,475,494,525,565,607,617,688,710,744,759,803,827,867,883,905,945]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Yes, hear from the grapevine is an expression, meaning that something is a rumour. And we also say, hear on the grapevine .","zh":"是的，hear from the grapevine 是一个固定表达，表示某事是道听途说的传闻。我们也可以说 hear on the grapevine。","s":207.92,"e":216.28,"wt":[0,72,132,136,167,264,284,298,368,386,424,474,486,534,586,606,616,644,682,713,746,756,801]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Well, this nursery sounds like a really lovely project, Phil. What is the impact it's having on the older and younger people?","zh":"嗯，这家幼儿园听起来真是个很棒的项目，Phil。它对老人和孩子产生了什么样的影响呢？","s":216.28,"e":223.84,"wt":[0,36,62,119,144,154,180,233,273,330,378,403,417,438,479,513,550,580,588,628,646,703]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Well, the charity Ready Generations, which runs the nursery, says it works with universities to study the effects of mixing generations on health and wellbeing, and they say they've seen positive results. And there have been studies around the world on projects that encourage different generations to mix, and they found that they can help reduce loneliness, improve how healthy and well people feel, and even boost learning and creativity. People Fixing the World host Myra spoke to Liz Ludden, co-founder of the nursery in Chester, about the differences that she's noticed since it opened.","zh":"嗯，运营这家幼儿园的慈善机构 Ready Generations 表示，他们正与大学合作研究跨代共处对健康和福祉的影响，并且已经看到了积极的成果。世界各地对鼓励跨代共处项目的研究也表明，这类项目能减少孤独感、改善人们的身心健康状况，甚至还能激发学习力和创造力。《改变世界的人们》主持人 Myra 采访了切斯特这家幼儿园的联合创始人 Liz Ludden，请她谈谈开园以来注意到的变化。","s":223.91,"e":261.83,"wt":[0,51,64,116,168,248,282,327,336,409,435,449,483,529,596,605,635,654,698,712,747,816,828,865,883,951,968,994,1012,1072,1099,1156,1231,1315,1315,1315,1373,1383,1465,1489,1489,1553,1553,1576,1641,1675,1751,1772,1818,1846,1886,1909,1957,1969,1992,2022,2068,2159,2212,2235,2297,2332,2360,2407,2453,2479,2507,2546,2615,2632,2781,2855,2855,2873,2921,2981,3044,3075,3089,3108,3208,3228,3254,3261,3309,3327,3453,3453,3494,3533,3562,3601,3676,3710,3731]},{"speaker":"Myra Anubi","en":"And as I realised, these interactions also seem to be breaking down stereotypes .","zh":"而且正如我注意到的，这些互动似乎也在“打破刻板印象”（breaking down stereotypes）。","s":261.83,"e":267.15,"wt":[0,19,34,54,114,168,240,268,300,326,334,397,414,485]},{"speaker":"Liz Ludden","en":"Your first thought would be how lively and energetic young children can be, and what we found is so can older people. And so sometimes, you know, it's the older people that we're saying, \"Can we just calm this down a little bit?\"","zh":"大家的第一反应通常是小孩子有多活泼好动、精力充沛，但我们发现老年人其实也可以一样活力满满。所以有时候，反而是我们对老人们说：“大家能不能稍微安静一点点？”","s":267.15,"e":281.82,"wt":[0,28,67,105,113,163,210,253,284,339,377,449,463,559,573,589,613,661,696,707,728,764,891,923,977,1025,1035,1059,1059,1103,1103,1146,1173,1214,1235,1247,1264,1290,1320,1356,1371,1384,1423]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Myra says the nursery breaks down stereotypes . This means it provides evidence that common beliefs about a group of people are not true.","zh":"Myra 说这家幼儿园“打破了刻板印象”（breaks down stereotypes）。这意思是用事实证明人们对某一群体的固有看法其实并不准确。","s":281.89,"e":290.21,"wt":[0,26,59,77,129,173,239,285,315,331,363,381,434,489,519,555,599,619,643,661,695,729,749,773]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"For example, a stereotype about generations is that younger people are lively , meaning they're loud and have lots of energy, whereas older people are not that lively .","zh":"比如，关于不同世代的一个刻板印象就是年轻人总是“活泼好动”（lively），也就是闹腾又精力旺盛，而老年人没那么活泼。","s":290.21,"e":301.86,"wt":[0,26,91,115,182,215,293,311,344,392,429,465,494,513,563,629,644,666,700,733,747,829,880,921,961,980,1002,1040,1102]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Right. But in the nursery, Liz says it's often the older people who have lots of energy and are being noisy rather than the children. The staff even have to tell them to calm down sometimes!","zh":"没错。但在那家幼儿园里，Liz 说精力充沛、闹腾得不行的往往是老人们，而不是孩子们。工作人员有时候甚至得提醒他们冷静一点！","s":301.86,"e":313.2,"wt":[0,3,29,46,72,150,182,216,238,270,309,328,367,387,421,445,460,499,515,539,569,616,657,684,704,790,811,847,880,894,913,940,960,974,1012,1030]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"So, Georgie, is that something that you found in your choir?","zh":"那 Georgie，你在你们合唱团里也发现这种情况了吗？","s":313.2,"e":316.92,"wt":[0,80,80,146,190,222,229,244,290,297,328]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Actually, yes. The older people in my choir are big characters. It's often them who are dragging us all to the pub...!","zh":"其实真是这样！我合唱团里的老人们都特别有个性、超爱玩。拉着大家一起去酒吧的往往都是他们……！","s":317.14,"e":323.86,"wt":[0,64,96,117,148,178,192,213,254,266,285,369,395,442,454,477,518,542,569,585,598,620]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"OK. Georgie, I think it's time to look at the answer for that question. I asked if someone was born in 1982, which generation would they be from?","zh":"好了 Georgie，现在该来看看刚才那道问答的答案了。我问你，如果有人出生于 1982 年，他们属于哪一代？","s":324.5,"e":334.8,"wt":[0,20,70,90,108,138,138,178,178,178,232,274,284,284,404,438,473,487,547,572,606,674,756,788,871,923,944,966]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"I said Generation X.","zh":"我选的是 X 世代。","s":334.8,"e":337.16,"wt":[0,12,60,183]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"OK. Well, actually, I'm afraid the answer was b) – they're a Millennial, just like you!","zh":"好吧，其实恐怕答案是 b)——他们是千禧一代，就像你一样！","s":337.16,"e":342.62,"wt":[0,63,96,157,179,206,222,224,272,288,316,351,357,429,454,479]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Ah...! OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we learned in this programme, starting with generation , which is a group of people who are of a similar age.","zh":"啊……！好吧，让我们回顾一下本期节目学到的词汇，先从 generation 开始，它指年龄相仿的一代人。","s":343.31,"e":352.8,"wt":[0,120,154,185,267,267,298,310,354,373,403,453,500,525,587,615,651,661,673,703,732,761,783,805,821,837,899]},{"speaker":"Fill a void","en":"means find something to help you feel better after experiencing loss.","zh":"Fill a void 意思是经历失落后，找到某件事来填补空虚、让自己好受些。","s":354.14,"e":359.1,"wt":[0,46,94,94,114,146,176,207,259,311,379]},{"speaker":"Such and such","en":"is a way to talk about something without giving a specific example.","zh":"Such and such 用来泛指某件事，而不必举出具体例子。","s":359.78,"e":363.87,"wt":[0,12,30,54,72,94,99,166,212,246,254,321]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"If you hear something on the grapevine , you hear a rumour.","zh":"如果你 hear something on the grapevine，就是听到了小道消息或传闻。","s":363.87,"e":368.43,"wt":[0,15,42,78,169,178,210,280,315,334,367,375]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"The expression break down stereotypes means find evidence that common beliefs about a group of people are untrue.","zh":"短语 break down stereotypes 意思是用事实证明对某一群体的固有偏见并不属实，即打破刻板印象。","s":368.43,"e":375.17,"wt":[0,16,79,116,147,221,261,293,349,365,405,450,477,485,512,525,571,600]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"And lively means loud and with lots of energy.","zh":"而 lively 意思是热闹活泼、精力充沛的。","s":375.17,"e":379.23,"wt":[0,25,91,136,231,231,245,288,314]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Once again, our six minutes are up, but you can test what you've learned with the worksheet on our website. Go to bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"六分钟又到了，不过你可以登录我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 做练习题来检测学习效果。","s":379.23,"e":386.8,"wt":[0,20,60,77,94,135,152,181,210,217,235,261,283,301,325,341,359,425,441,465,515,531,551]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Thanks for joining us and goodbye.","zh":"感谢收听，再见。","s":386.82,"e":388.93,"wt":[0,38,57,88,117,140]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Goodbye!","zh":"再见！","s":388.93,"e":389.82,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0pmkqml.mp3"},{"epNum":3,"id":"ep-261105","date":"2026-11-05","title":"Could vaccines cure cancer?","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-261105","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/261105_6min_could_vaccines_cure_cancer_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/261105_6_minute_english_could_vaccines_cure_cancer_transcript.pdf","summary":"When was the last time you had a vaccination, and what was it for? Many of us get vaccinations when we are children or when we go travelling, but did you know that scientists have been developing vaccines that can be personalised to help your body keep itself clear of some specific cancer types? Georgie and Phil discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"According to the World Health Organisation, what is the most common cancer worldwide?","options":["a) lung cancer","b) skin cancer","c) stomach cancer"],"topic":"健康医学","titleZh":"疫苗能治愈癌症吗？","summaryZh":"你上一次打疫苗是什么时候，打的是什么疫苗？我们大多数人在童年或出国旅行时都会接种疫苗，但你知道吗？科学家一直在研发个体化疫苗，帮助人体清除特定类型的癌细胞。Georgie 和 Phil 将探讨这一前沿研究并讲解新词汇。","questionZh":"根据世界卫生组织的数据，全球最常见的癌症是哪一种？","optionsZh":["a) 肺癌","b) 皮肤癌","c) 胃癌"],"answer":{"letter":"a","explanationZh":"Georgie 在节目最后公布了答案：全球最常见的癌症是肺癌（lung cancer）。据世界卫生组织估计，2024 年全球约有 240 万例肺癌病例。"},"vocab":[{"word":"a long time coming","defEn":"taking a long time to achieve","pos":"短语","zh":"姗姗来迟；历经漫长等待才实现","exampleEn":"I guess this moment has been quite a long time coming , hasn't it?","ipa":"/ə lɒŋ taɪm ˈkʌmɪŋ/"},{"word":"false dawn","defEn":"situation where something appears to be improving, when actually it isn't","pos":"短语","zh":"虚幻的曙光；空欢喜一场","exampleEn":"There's been some false dawns in cancer vaccines.","ipa":"/fɔːls dɔːn/"},{"word":"space","defEn":"field of research","pos":"n.","zh":"领域；研究/行业方向","exampleEn":"I mean, there's been a lot of work in this space , and it's all been very encouraging...","ipa":"/speɪs/"},{"word":"breakthrough","defEn":"significant progress in something","pos":"n.","zh":"突破；重大进展","exampleEn":"...so it's very exciting that there's this breakthrough – apparent breakthrough – that's come through today.","ipa":"/ˈbreɪkθruː/"},{"word":"prime (something)","defEn":"get (something) ready","pos":"v.","zh":"使……做好准备；激活（免疫系统等）","exampleEn":"So, it's a way of priming the immune system to the cancer.","ipa":"/praɪm/"},{"word":"promise","defEn":"positive potential","pos":"n.","zh":"潜力；希望；前景","exampleEn":"And actually, seeing that this extraordinary technology is, sort of, starting to reach its promise – this is very exciting for everybody.","ipa":"/ˈprɒmɪs/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"side effects","zh":"副作用（医疗、政策讨论中均极高频，雅思写作聊科技或药物利弊必备）","contextEn":"If you think about many existing cancer treatments, such as radiotherapy and chemotherapy, these have quite a few side effects."},{"en":"work out","zh":"弄清楚、查明、算出来（英式口语和学术访谈中高频替代 figure out）","contextEn":"So, you sequence the DNA of the cancer cell to work out how it's different from a normal cell."},{"en":"make a big difference","zh":"产生重大影响、带来很大改变（雅思口语表达“某事很有意义/作用很大”的地道表达）","contextEn":"Now, that sounds like some really clever science here. Could it make a big difference, Georgie?"},{"en":"translate into","zh":"转化为、落到……上（科研、商业语境常指把实验/想法转化为实际成果）","contextEn":"I mean, there's been a lot of work in this space , and it's all been very encouraging, particularly in the animal models, but getting this to translate into humans can be a much harder, longer process..."}],"sentences":[{"en":"But the idea is that a cancer vaccine would help the body's natural immune system help fight the disease, which could have fewer side effects and also be more effective.","zh":"但它的核心思路是，癌症疫苗能够协助人体自身的天然免疫系统去对抗疾病，这不仅可能副作用更少，而且疗效也会更好。","coreEn":"The idea is that a cancer vaccine would help the body's natural immune system help fight the disease.","coreZh":"核心思路是癌症疫苗能帮助人体天然免疫系统对抗疾病。","cars":[{"text":"which could have fewer side effects and also be more effective","hook":"逗号 + 关系词 which","noteZh":"指代前面“用疫苗激活自身免疫系统抗癌”这件事，补充说明它的两大优势：副作用更少、效果更好"}],"tipZh":"逗号后的 which 经常不单指某一个名词，而是把前面整件事打包指代，用来顺手补一句评价或优势。"},{"en":"I mean, there's been a lot of work in this space , and it's all been very encouraging, particularly in the animal models, but getting this to translate into humans can be a much harder, longer process, so it's very exciting that there's this breakthrough – apparent breakthrough – that's come through today.","zh":"我是说，这个领域已经开展了大量研究，成果都非常鼓舞人心，尤其是在动物模型实验中；但要将这些成果转化到人体上，往往是一个艰难得多、漫长得多的过程，因此今天能出现这一突破——至少目前看来是重大突破——实在令人振奋。","coreEn":"There's been a lot of work, and it's all been very encouraging, but getting this to translate into humans can be a much harder, longer process, so it's very exciting that there's this breakthrough.","coreZh":"这一领域已有很多鼓舞人心的研究，但转化到人体要难得多，所以今天的突破非常令人兴奋。","cars":[{"text":"in this space","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"限定研究范围：在这个领域里"},{"text":"particularly in the animal models","hook":"逗号补充修饰","noteZh":"精准缩小范围：尤其是在动物实验阶段令人鼓舞"},{"text":"getting this to translate into humans","hook":"-ing 短语作主语","noteZh":"在 but 之后充当新句子的主语：“把这成果转化到人体上”这件事"},{"text":"– apparent breakthrough –","hook":"破折号插入语","noteZh":"科学家严谨的自我修正：强调是“目前看来的突破”"},{"text":"that's come through today","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"修饰 breakthrough，说明是“今天传出来的”突破"}],"tipZh":"超长口语采访句看着吓人，其实只是 and → but → so 串起的四节小火车；抓住转折词 but 和结果词 so，逻辑立刻清清楚楚。"},{"en":"It's used to target any cancer cells that have been spread around the body, to stop the cancer from coming back, and the vaccine is made specially for each patient by looking at the cancerous cells in the tumour that's been removed.","zh":"它用于精准瞄准已经扩散到身体各处的癌细胞，防止癌症复发；这种疫苗是根据已被切除肿瘤中的癌细胞，为每位患者量身定制的。","coreEn":"It's used to target any cancer cells, and the vaccine is made specially for each patient.","coreZh":"它用来瞄准癌细胞，而且这种疫苗是为每位患者专门定制的。","cars":[{"text":"that have been spread around the body","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"限定目标癌细胞：那些已经扩散到全身的癌细胞"},{"text":"to stop the cancer from coming back","hook":"不定式 to do（表目的）","noteZh":"说明清除这些癌细胞的最终目的：防止复发"},{"text":"by looking at the cancerous cells in the tumour","hook":"介词短语（by + -ing 表方式）","noteZh":"解释“量身定制”是如何做到的：通过分析肿瘤里的癌细胞"},{"text":"that's been removed","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"修饰前面的 tumour，说明是“已经被切除的”肿瘤"}],"tipZh":"科普说明文高频套路：be used to do（用来干嘛）+ by doing（通过什么方式做到），抓住这两个路标就能秒懂技术原理。"},{"en":"And then, particularly for the bits of the cancer that's different on the surface of the cell, you take those bits of DNA and then you put them into the RNA vaccine, and then they'll make these bits of proteins that get recognised by the immune system.","zh":"然后，特别是针对癌细胞表面与正常细胞不同的那些片段，你会提取出那部分 DNA 放进 RNA 疫苗里，接着它们就会生成能被免疫系统识别的蛋白质片段。","coreEn":"You take those bits of DNA and put them into the RNA vaccine, and they'll make these bits of proteins.","coreZh":"你提取那些 DNA 片段放进 RNA 疫苗，它们就会制造出蛋白质片段。","cars":[{"text":"particularly for the bits of the cancer that's different on the surface of the cell","hook":"介词短语 + 关系词 that","noteZh":"先交代筛选条件：专门针对癌细胞表面发生变异的部分"},{"text":"that get recognised by the immune system","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"修饰前面的 proteins，说明这些蛋白质的作用是“能被免疫系统认出来”"}],"tipZh":"遇到步骤流程讲解（and then... and then...），别被专业词 DNA/RNA 吓到，直接按动作顺序抓动词：take → put → make → get recognised。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Hi Phil.","zh":"嗨，Phil。","s":0,"e":0.43,"wt":[0,0]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Hi Georgie.","zh":"嗨，Georgie。","s":0,"e":0.25,"wt":[0,0]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Phil, have you had many vaccines in your life? So, these are injections to protect you from diseases.","zh":"Phil，你这辈子打过很多疫苗吗？就是用来预防疾病的注射针剂。","s":0.25,"e":6.22,"wt":[0,18,47,68,89,118,176,190,219,265,288,314,336,411,425,476,497,523]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Oh, I've had loads. I had all the ones you have when you're a child, but I've also had some for travelling and working abroad so... yeah, I've had lots.","zh":"哦，我打过一大堆。小时候该打的疫苗我都打全了，后来为了出国旅行和工作又打了不少，所以……对，打过很多。","s":6.35,"e":15.04,"wt":[0,23,65,103,203,226,243,261,288,303,321,345,359,359,407,478,483,500,522,548,563,580,653,663,704,749,757,789,806,822]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK. Well, many of us will have had vaccines to stop us getting common diseases such as flu or Covid 19, but could injections help us fight more complicated diseases too? In this episode of 6 Minute English, we'll be looking at some new research that has scientists very excited, and we'll be asking the question, 'Could vaccines cure cancer?'","zh":"好的。我们很多人都打过预防流感或新冠等常见疾病的疫苗，但打针也能帮我们对抗更复杂的疾病吗？在本期《六分钟英语》中，我们将关注一项令科学家十分振奋的新研究，并探讨一个问题：“疫苗能治愈癌症吗？”","s":15.2,"e":37.18,"wt":[0,23,96,126,141,156,186,216,240,296,304,333,349,384,426,516,540,560,592,612,649,762,769,807,870,894,914,944,975,1046,1088,1165,1181,1196,1244,1257,1277,1318,1380,1398,1409,1436,1452,1479,1499,1568,1588,1626,1704,1756,1850,1858,1870,1887,1922,1931,1984,2027,2083,2123]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK. Let's start with a quiz question, Phil. There are lots of different types of cancer affecting many parts of the body, but according to the World Health Organisation, what is the most common cancer worldwide? Is it: a) lung cancer, b) skin cancer, or c) stomach cancer?","zh":"好，我们先来做道问答，Phil。癌症有很多种，会影响身体的不同部位，但根据世界卫生组织的数据，全球最常见的癌症是哪一种？是：a) 肺癌，b) 皮肤癌，还是 c) 胃癌？","s":38.68,"e":56.36,"wt":[0,0,74,106,126,146,196,210,314,321,336,356,366,410,428,446,479,549,583,606,616,640,706,713,759,774,789,820,854,953,960,976,1000,1040,1081,1130,1252,1257,1274,1306,1349,1440,1467,1519,1576,1594,1628,1687]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Well, I don't know. I'm going to guess b) skin cancer.","zh":"嗯，我不太清楚。我猜一个 b) 皮肤癌吧。","s":56.58,"e":61.38,"wt":[0,0,4,48,156,231,231,246,290,361,397]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Well, we'll find out the answer at the end of the programme.","zh":"好，节目最后我们来揭晓答案。","s":61.62,"e":64.66,"wt":[0,15,45,70,86,106,143,155,178,191,203,221]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Right. Now, this idea of a vaccine that could cure cancer sounds really interesting. Could you tell us a bit more about it, Georgie?","zh":"好的。不过，能治愈癌症的疫苗这个想法听起来真有意思。Georgie，你能给我们多介绍一下吗？","s":64.93,"e":72.82,"wt":[0,33,61,93,123,123,166,224,243,283,332,416,430,478,579,588,603,623,633,650,653,675,701,718]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Well, scientists have been trying to develop a cancer vaccine for quite a long time. If you think about many existing cancer treatments, such as radiotherapy and chemotherapy, these have quite a few side effects. But the idea is that a cancer vaccine would help the body's natural immune system help fight the disease, which could have fewer side effects and also be more effective. Scientists are particularly hopeful about a new vaccine developed to treat skin cancer. BBC Radio 4 programme Inside Science has been hearing about the latest research. Cancer cell biologist Anne Willis spoke to presenter Tom Whipple.","zh":"嗯，科学家研发癌症疫苗的努力已经持续了相当长一段时间。你想想现有的许多癌症疗法，比如放疗和化疗，都有不少副作用。而癌症疫苗的思路是帮助人体自身的天然免疫系统去抗击疾病，这样不仅副作用可能更少，效果也会更好。科学家们对一种新研发的皮肤癌治疗疫苗尤其寄予厚望。BBC 广播四台的《科学内幕》节目报道了这项最新研究，癌细胞生物学家 Anne Willis 接受了主持人 Tom Whipple 的采访。","s":72.86,"e":109.28,"wt":[0,11,66,90,110,143,154,194,202,239,286,306,339,345,378,431,444,450,484,508,534,577,613,686,708,726,802,825,960,976,1000,1014,1032,1066,1094,1180,1190,1206,1233,1253,1260,1279,1319,1370,1390,1406,1422,1454,1498,1535,1592,1601,1635,1661,1718,1744,1768,1791,1817,1838,1882,1902,1934,1950,1982,2107,2140,2157,2227,2273,2307,2314,2336,2390,2443,2455,2485,2546,2702,2707,2742,2769,2828,2873,2926,2947,2975,3016,3016,3068,3112,3206,3231,3269,3317,3344,3398,3404,3458,3514,3542]},{"speaker":"Tom Whipple","en":"I guess this moment has been quite a long time coming , hasn't it? There's been some false dawns in cancer vaccines.","zh":"我想这一刻真是“姗姗来迟”（a long time coming），对吧？癌症疫苗领域过去曾出现过几次“虚幻的曙光”（false dawns）。","s":109.43,"e":115.94,"wt":[0,18,37,67,101,123,149,149,183,231,280,310,329,348,363,363,379,413,442,471,497,525]},{"speaker":"Anne Willis","en":"I mean, there's been a lot of work in this space , and it's all been very encouraging, particularly in the animal models, but getting this to translate into humans can be a much harder, longer process, so it's very exciting that there's this breakthrough – apparent breakthrough – that's come through today.","zh":"我是说，这个领域（space）已经做了大量工作，成果都非常鼓舞人心，尤其是在动物模型实验中；但要把它转化到人体应用上，过程要艰难得多、漫长得多，所以今天能够取得这一突破（breakthrough）——至少目前看来的重大突破——确实令人非常振奋。","s":115.99,"e":131.44,"wt":[0,18,29,29,33,45,59,81,139,171,205,245,275,288,297,311,330,352,413,473,487,515,560,618,626,657,669,690,737,763,801,833,861,868,897,958,998,1078,1089,1099,1120,1159,1181,1214,1234,1305,1334,1355,1371,1377,1397,1423,1495]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Tom says that the development of the cancer vaccine has been a long time coming , and this means that it's taken a lot of time to achieve.","zh":"Tom 说癌症疫苗的进展“历经漫长等待”（a long time coming），意思是花了很多时间才取得这一成果。","s":131.72,"e":141.44,"wt":[0,1,39,66,90,180,200,220,269,333,372,388,428,479,528,589,628,629,667,700,731,764,794,812,836,852,884,900]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yes. Tom also says there have been some false dawns , an expression meaning that something looks like it's improving when it's actually not.","zh":"没错。Tom 还提到之前有过几次“空欢喜”（false dawns），这个表达指某件事看似正在好转，实则并没有。","s":141.84,"e":150.19,"wt":[0,15,39,120,120,142,176,206,238,281,325,357,373,415,454,476,528,558,582,606,664,692,732,748]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Anne says there's been a lot of work in this space . Space here means field of research – so, in this context, research into cancer vaccines.","zh":"Anne 说“在这个领域”（in this space）已有大量研究。这里的 space 指研究领域——在这段语境中就是指癌症疫苗研究领域。","s":150.19,"e":162.12,"wt":[0,52,94,123,149,167,189,253,307,334,378,430,490,516,557,655,659,679,747,784,790,803,852,927,1004,1052,1099]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Scientists think they might have made a breakthrough . That means they've made significant progress.","zh":"科学家认为他们可能取得了“突破”（breakthrough），意思就是取得了重大进展。","s":162.53,"e":167.84,"wt":[0,38,66,90,120,145,167,175,248,293,297,334,367,402,474]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"And Georgie, these vaccines that we're talking about, they aren't quite like the ones that we're all familiar with, are they? This isn't a vaccine to prevent you from getting skin cancer, is it?","zh":"Georgie，我们聊的这些疫苗跟大家平时熟悉的疫苗不太一样，对吧？它并不是用来预防你得皮肤癌的，是吗？","s":168.1,"e":179.32,"wt":[0,3,76,116,183,216,246,264,394,403,424,449,468,490,501,520,543,557,596,656,667,705,729,758,764,814,825,858,874,929,975,1005,1066,1076]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"No. The vaccine being tested is used to treat people after they've had a tumour removed. It's used to target any cancer cells that have been spread around the body, to stop the cancer from coming back, and the vaccine is made specially for each patient by looking at the cancerous cells in the tumour that's been removed. Anne Willis explained more to BBC Radio 4's Inside Science.","zh":"不是的。目前测试的这种疫苗是用于肿瘤切除手术后的患者。它的作用是瞄准已经扩散到身体各处的癌细胞，防止癌症复发，而且是通过分析切除下来的肿瘤中的癌细胞，为每位患者量身定制的。Anne Willis 在 BBC 广播四台的《科学内幕》中做了进一步解释。","s":179.37,"e":202.44,"wt":[0,16,33,78,107,147,161,191,204,240,287,327,347,361,401,459,548,564,588,602,637,655,692,730,744,763,778,807,847,862,905,916,940,964,1011,1041,1071,1147,1155,1173,1201,1221,1269,1315,1339,1377,1441,1456,1489,1501,1533,1555,1587,1597,1619,1669,1691,1727,1846,1897,1951,1977,2001,2017,2085,2103,2169,2225]},{"speaker":"Anne Willis","en":"So, cancer cells are very different from normal cells. They contain lots of mutations. So, you sequence the DNA of the cancer cell to work out how it's different from a normal cell. And then, particularly for the bits of the cancer that's different on the surface of the cell, you take those bits of DNA and then you put them into the RNA vaccine, and then they'll make these bits of proteins that get recognised by the immune system. So, it's a way of priming the immune system to the cancer.","zh":"癌细胞和正常细胞非常不同，它们包含大量基因突变。所以你会先对癌细胞进行 DNA 测序，弄清楚它和正常细胞有何区别。接着，特别是针对癌细胞表面发生变异的部分，你提取出对应的 DNA 片段放进 RNA 疫苗中，随后它们会合成一些能被免疫系统识别的蛋白质片段。所以，这是一种激活并让免疫系统对癌症做好准备（priming）的方法。","s":202.57,"e":229.09,"wt":[0,16,28,50,78,85,131,152,182,219,237,261,261,307,396,411,420,475,499,515,528,548,589,623,633,654,674,683,699,744,744,777,815,897,904,925,955,967,997,1045,1065,1091,1147,1180,1236,1243,1261,1303,1321,1347,1405,1422,1463,1499,1503,1519,1615,1635,1653,1671,1689,1703,1729,1785,1826,1903,1911,1929,1959,1999,2025,2047,2061,2111,2129,2151,2191,2203,2215,2257,2329,2341,2351,2365,2375,2401,2439,2457,2503,2553,2567,2588]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Anne is saying that the scientists work out what in the cancer cells is different and put these bits into the vaccine.","zh":"Anne 的意思是，科学家会查明癌细胞里有哪些不同的地方，再把这些片段放进疫苗里。","s":229.09,"e":237.62,"wt":[0,29,46,98,135,158,244,283,311,371,383,401,435,466,501,605,613,650,682,719,752,778]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Right. The vaccine then becomes a way of priming the immune system to cancer. If you prime something, you get it ready. The vaccine basically helps the immune system get better at recognising and fighting cancer cells in the body.","zh":"没错。这样一来，疫苗就成了一种让免疫系统针对癌症做好准备（priming）的方式。prime something 就是“使某事物做好准备”。本质上，这种疫苗能让免疫系统更擅长识别并攻击体内的癌细胞。","s":238.01,"e":252.22,"wt":[0,39,62,103,129,173,180,199,212,262,283,315,359,382,496,512,520,553,625,645,663,679,751,760,798,847,873,888,926,959,983,1031,1055,1151,1161,1229,1279,1281,1297,1319]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Now, that sounds like some really clever science here. Could it make a big difference, Georgie?","zh":"听起来这科学原理真是太巧妙了。Georgie，它能带来很大的改变吗？","s":252.56,"e":259.42,"wt":[0,30,71,118,140,190,248,292,318,470,478,492,506,535,546,625]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Well, more trials are needed, but scientists are hopeful. Here's Anne Willis again talking to Tom Whipple, presenter of Inside Science.","zh":"嗯，还需要开展更多临床试验，但科学家们充满希望。下面再来听听 Anne Willis 与《科学内幕》主持人 Tom Whipple 的对话。","s":259.77,"e":268.18,"wt":[0,15,42,87,102,175,179,257,266,349,375,398,435,473,516,529,549,622,683,705,749]},{"speaker":"Tom Whipple","en":"Are you excited?","zh":"你觉得兴奋吗？","s":268.6,"e":269.54,"wt":[0,10,26]},{"speaker":"Anne Willis","en":"Oh, I mean, very! I'm just, you know... anything that will improve lives of cancer patients is always wonderful. And actually, seeing that this extraordinary technology is, sort of, starting to reach its promise – this is very exciting for everybody.","zh":"哦，非常兴奋！我只是觉得……任何能够改善癌症患者生活质量的进展都是了不起的。而且看到这项非凡的技术开始兑现它的“潜力”（promise）——这对所有人来说都无比令人激动。","s":269.61,"e":282.48,"wt":[0,16,18,54,99,107,132,145,182,215,243,278,348,387,391,423,480,496,534,609,626,680,721,757,779,867,921,935,943,954,968,984,998,1022,1058,1079,1097,1106,1124,1160,1173]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"So, Anne is excited. She says that after a long time working on this technology, it's starting to reach its promise . And promise here means positive potential.","zh":"看来 Anne 确实很激动。她说在潜心研究这项技术这么久之后，它终于开始展现其“潜力”（promise）了。这里的 promise 指积极的发展潜力。","s":282.48,"e":294.94,"wt":[0,43,86,142,224,247,287,309,342,357,385,416,476,480,504,616,641,672,691,738,766,817,864,903,954,992,1030,1092]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK. Phil, I think it's time I revealed the answer to my question. I asked you, \"What is the most common form of cancer worldwide?\" You said skin cancer, but the answer is actually lung cancer. In 2024, there were an estimated 2.4 million cases worldwide, according to the World Health Organisation.","zh":"好了 Phil，现在该揭晓我那道问题的答案了。我问你：“全球最常见的癌症是哪一种？”你猜的是皮肤癌，但正确答案其实是肺癌。据世界卫生组织估计，2024 年全球约有 240 万例肺癌病例。","s":295.52,"e":315.6,"wt":[0,0,60,76,90,120,120,168,176,201,252,269,287,392,409,433,457,496,506,530,563,612,630,668,708,808,834,882,901,981,985,1002,1047,1059,1117,1151,1272,1318,1376,1410,1426,1437,1488,1597,1636,1729,1767,1806,1816,1831,1856,1887]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we learned in this programme, starting with the expression a long time coming , which means something has taken a long time to achieve.","zh":"好的，让我们回顾一下本期节目学到的词汇，先从短语 a long time coming 开始，它指某件事花了很长时间才实现。","s":315.76,"e":328.44,"wt":[0,104,160,242,242,270,298,298,298,329,476,517,528,553,638,665,709,806,841,864,905,948,1010,1040,1044,1092,1124,1160,1184]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"A false dawn is when something appears to be improving, when it actually isn't.","zh":"false dawn（虚幻的曙光）指某事看似正在好转，实则并非如此。","s":328.71,"e":333.46,"wt":[0,11,39,63,80,105,168,217,231,249,305,326,336,379]},{"speaker":"Space","en":"can mean field of research.","zh":"Space 在这里可以指研究领域。","s":334.7,"e":337.52,"wt":[0,38,98,142,184]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"A breakthrough refers to making significant progress with something.","zh":"breakthrough（突破）指在某件事上取得重大进展。","s":337.52,"e":341.84,"wt":[0,25,96,96,160,220,278,287,317]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"If you prime something, you get it ready.","zh":"如果你 prime something，意思就是让它做好准备。","s":341.87,"e":345.42,"wt":[0,6,28,137,181,206,234,268]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"And promise can mean positive potential.","zh":"而 promise 可以指积极的潜力、前景。","s":345.45,"e":348.89,"wt":[0,2,67,94,131,205]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Once again, our six minutes are up. Test what you've learned with the worksheet on our website. And that's bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"六分钟又到了。欢迎到我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 做练习题，检测你的学习成果。","s":348.89,"e":357.66,"wt":[0,9,67,92,117,176,201,262,295,313,341,375,391,407,473,492,511,605,609,667]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Thanks for joining us and goodbye.","zh":"感谢收听，再见。","s":357.66,"e":359.41,"wt":[0,34,55,92,109,130]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Bye.","zh":"再见！","s":359.41,"e":359.84,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0pldlfg.mp3"},{"epNum":4,"id":"ep-261029","date":"2026-10-29","title":"Is aircon the answer to a heating world?","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-261029","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/261029_6_minute_english_is_aircon_the_answer_to_a_heating_world_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/261029_6_minute_english_is_aircon_the_answer_to_a_heating_world_transcript.pdf","summary":"Do you ever feel it is too hot to sleep? With climate change gradually increasing world temperatures, people across the world are becoming increasingly affected by hot weather. In many locations, air conditioning is used to help cool down homes and workplaces, but not everyone has access to this. And it isn't just about getting a good night's sleep – extremely hot weather can be a serious health risk. Georgie and Neil discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"Modern air conditioning was invented in 1902, but what was it originally used for?","options":["a) to protect people in hospitals","b) to dry ink","c) to clean air"],"topic":"自然气候","titleZh":"空调是应对全球变暖的解药吗？","summaryZh":"你有没有热得睡不着觉的时候？随着气候变化逐步推高全球气温，世界各地越来越多的人受到高温天气的影响。在许多地方，人们依靠空调为住宅和办公场所降温，但并非所有人都有条件用上空调。这不仅仅关乎睡个好觉——极端高温还可能构成严重的健康风险。Georgie 和 Neil 将探讨这一议题并讲解新词汇。","questionZh":"现代空调发明于 1902 年，它最初是用来做什么的？","optionsZh":["a) 保护医院里的病人","b) 烘干油墨","c) 净化空气"],"answer":{"letter":"b","explanationZh":"Georgie 在节目最后揭晓答案：现代空调最初是用来烘干油墨（to dry ink）的，由 Willis Carrier 于 1902 年在纽约布鲁克林的一家印刷厂发明。"},"vocab":[{"word":"life-saving","defEn":"used to stop people from dying","pos":"adj.","zh":"救命的；挽救生命的","exampleEn":"Actually, air conditioning is life-saving .","ipa":"/ˈlaɪfˌseɪvɪŋ/"},{"word":"inhabit","defEn":"live in","pos":"v.","zh":"居住于；栖居在","exampleEn":"And there are so many large parts of the world where we would not be able to inhabit if we did not have air conditioning.","ipa":"/ɪnˈhæbɪt/"},{"word":"the grid","defEn":"the system or network that distributes electricity across different locations","pos":"n.","zh":"电网；输电系统","exampleEn":"So, you know, we're going to see the grid collapse.","ipa":"/ðə ɡrɪd/"},{"word":"vicious cycle","defEn":"situation where one problem leads to another problem, which then makes the first problem worse, leading to a chain of events where each cause and effect makes the original problem even worse","pos":"短语","zh":"恶性循环","exampleEn":"And we reach a vicious cycle , because then you're in a world where it's getting warmer.","ipa":"/ˌvɪʃəs ˈsaɪkəl/"},{"word":"passive cooling","defEn":"making a building cooler without using a lot of power","pos":"短语","zh":"被动式降温（低能耗自然降温）","exampleEn":"So, one of the most important things is to do passive cooling first.","ipa":"/ˈpæsɪv ˈkuːlɪŋ/"},{"word":"shading","defEn":"blocking the Sun's light and heat","pos":"n.","zh":"遮阳；遮阴（阻挡阳光和热量）","exampleEn":"Some of the most important ones are first to cut solar gain, which is through things like shading .","ipa":"/ˈʃeɪdɪŋ/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"cope with","zh":"应对、处理（困难、压力或恶劣环境）；雅思大小作文高频核心词","contextEn":"Today on 6 Minute English, we're talking about this and whether air conditioning really is the best way to cope with a warming world."},{"en":"go-to solution","zh":"首选方案、默认对策（go-to 作定语非常地道，如 go-to restaurant / go-to choice）","contextEn":"When air conditioning becomes the first, go-to solution for how we deal with the increased, prolonged and extreme heat that we're going to face and are facing, it places stresses across the system that are not able to be met."},{"en":"serious downsides","zh":"严重的弊端/缺点（比 disadvantages 更自然地道，雅思口语和写作利弊题必备）","contextEn":"No. There are some serious downsides. So, one big problem with air conditioning is the amount of energy it uses."},{"en":"have a wild guess","zh":"瞎猜一下、凭直觉蒙一个（日常聊天和口语互动非常鲜活的表达）","contextEn":"And I had a wild guess. I said c) to clean air."}],"sentences":[{"en":"When air conditioning becomes the first, go-to solution for how we deal with the increased, prolonged and extreme heat that we're going to face and are facing, it places stresses across the system that are not able to be met.","zh":"当空调成为我们应对正在面临以及未来将要面临的不断加剧、持续且极端的高温的首选方案时，它会给整个系统带来无法承受的压力。","coreEn":"It places stresses across the system.","coreZh":"它给整个系统带来压力。","cars":[{"text":"When air conditioning becomes the first, go-to solution","hook":"状语从句（When 引导条件/时间）","noteZh":"先铺垫前提：当空调成为第一首选方案时"},{"text":"for how we deal with the increased, prolonged and extreme heat","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"说明是针对什么问题的方案：如何应对加剧、持久且极端的高温"},{"text":"that we're going to face and are facing","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"修饰前面的 heat，强调这种高温不仅将来要面对，现在已经在面对了"},{"text":"that are not able to be met","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"修饰前面的 stresses，补充这些压力是“无法被满足/承接的”"}],"tipZh":"遇到 When... 开头的超长前半句，耐心等逗号后面的真正主语 it 出现；主句其实只有 7 个词（it places stresses across the system），其余全是修饰车厢。"},{"en":"We heard that air conditioning creates emissions, which can lead the world to warm up, which means we then need more air conditioning.","zh":"我们了解到空调会产生碳排放，这会导致地球进一步变暖，从而意味着我们之后需要用更多的空调。","coreEn":"We heard that air conditioning creates emissions.","coreZh":"我们听说空调会产生排放物。","cars":[{"text":"which can lead the world to warm up","hook":"逗号 + 关系词 which","noteZh":"承接前文，说明碳排放导致的直接后果：让地球变暖"},{"text":"which means we then need more air conditioning","hook":"逗号 + 关系词 which（二连推）","noteZh":"继续承接“地球变暖”的结果，推出下一环：我们需要更多空调"}],"tipZh":"连续两个 , which... 是表达“连锁反应/因果链条”的利器，雅思写作写环境或社会恶性循环时可以直接套用。"},{"en":"If people are trying to use more electricity than it can cope with, then the grid can collapse – you get power cuts, which is when you can't get any electricity.","zh":"如果人们的用电量超过了电网所能承受的负荷，电网就会崩溃——也就是发生停电，到时候你根本用不上任何电。","coreEn":"The grid can collapse – you get power cuts.","coreZh":"电网会崩溃——也就是会停电。","cars":[{"text":"If people are trying to use more electricity than it can cope with,","hook":"状语从句（If 引导条件）","noteZh":"先设定触发条件：人们的用电需求超出电网的承受能力"},{"text":"than it can cope with","hook":"比较结构","noteZh":"与前面的 more electricity 搭配，界定超负荷的标准：超过它（电网）能应付的量"},{"text":"which is when you can't get any electricity","hook":"逗号 + 关系词 which","noteZh":"用大白话补充解释 power cuts（停电）具体是什么意思"}],"tipZh":"看到 more A than B can cope with 时，把 cope with 当作整体动作理解成“超过 B 的承受极限”，同时注意破折号后通常是用通俗说法解释专业词（grid collapse → power cuts）。"},{"en":"A vicious cycle , or a vicious circle, is where a solution to a problem actually makes it worse, which means you have to do more and that makes the problem even worse still.","zh":"恶性循环（vicious cycle 或 vicious circle）指的是解决某个问题的办法反而让问题变得更糟，这意味着你不得不采取更多同类措施，结果让问题进一步恶化。","coreEn":"A vicious cycle is where a solution actually makes it worse.","coreZh":"恶性循环就是解决办法反而让问题更糟的情况。","cars":[{"text":"or a vicious circle","hook":"逗号插入语","noteZh":"补充同义说法"},{"text":"to a problem","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"修饰 solution，说明是“针对某个问题的”办法"},{"text":"which means you have to do more and that makes the problem even worse still","hook":"逗号 + 关系词 which","noteZh":"补充恶化后的连锁反应：不得不加码，导致雪上加霜"}],"tipZh":"英语口语做下定义时很喜欢用 ...is where...（……就是指这样一种情况），不一定表示具体地点，阅读和听力中要灵活理解。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Hi Neil.","zh":"嗨，Neil。","s":4.61,"e":5.38,"wt":[0,14]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Hi Georgie.","zh":"嗨，Georgie。","s":7.4,"e":11.59,"wt":[0,89]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"So, Neil, we've just had a really hot summer in the UK. Did you have air conditioning in your house?","zh":"Neil，英国刚经历了一个特别炎热的夏天。你家里装空调了吗？","s":11.59,"e":19.42,"wt":[0,145,215,243,261,277,309,339,361,366,388,421,489,533,594,617,629,685,697,721]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"I didn't have air conditioning in my house. I wish I had, because it was really hot and I couldn't sleep.","zh":"我家里没装空调。我真希望装了，因为实在太热了，根本睡不着。","s":19.42,"e":25.93,"wt":[0,5,36,60,78,151,163,175,226,240,280,308,354,400,413,443,497,534,554,562,605]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"No, I had one of those big fans, which didn't always do its job. But there is a really serious side to this. Heat records continue to be broken around the world. You may be feeling that summers are getting too hot. Have you been using air conditioning more to cope? If so, you're not alone. Experts are predicting that ten new air conditioning units will be installed every second for the next 30 years. Today on 6 Minute English, we're talking about this and whether air conditioning really is the best way to cope with a warming world.","zh":"我也没装，我用的是那种大电扇，但也经常不管用。不过这件事其实有非常严肃的一面。全球各地的高温纪录不断被打破。你可能也觉得夏天越来越热了。你是不是也开始更频繁地开空调来应对？如果是的话，你并不孤单。专家预测，未来 30 年里，全球每秒钟都将新安装 10 台空调。在今天的《六分钟英语》中，我们就来聊聊这个话题，探讨空调是否真的是应对全球变暖的最佳方式。","s":25.93,"e":58.16,"wt":[0,28,35,56,81,91,127,148,190,226,268,313,325,346,399,412,433,441,447,482,522,552,568,618,645,696,755,769,783,827,871,893,959,976,1007,1011,1050,1065,1111,1131,1177,1197,1237,1263,1283,1314,1342,1362,1442,1481,1488,1589,1589,1650,1678,1696,1747,1791,1810,1873,1898,1936,1955,1974,2050,2088,2103,2119,2194,2217,2264,2280,2308,2330,2366,2479,2479,2521,2545,2545,2599,2632,2663,2687,2730,2737,2779,2800,2870,2907,2918,2940,2962,2978,2994,3014,3038,3044,3089]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK. As always, Neil, we'll start with a quiz question. So, modern air conditioning was invented in 1902, but what was it originally used for? Is it: a) to protect people in hospitals, b) to dry ink, or c) to clean air?","zh":"好，和往常一样，Neil，我们先从一道问答开始。现代空调发明于 1902 年，但它最初是用来做什么的？是：a) 保护医院里的病人，b) 烘干油墨，还是 c) 净化空气？","s":58.16,"e":75.24,"wt":[0,0,72,108,138,138,169,205,221,302,360,449,462,476,540,560,623,700,795,810,845,871,888,976,1010,1056,1068,1096,1120,1134,1183,1228,1239,1331,1344,1369,1407,1472,1493,1562,1562,1595]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, I have absolutely no idea, Georgie. I'm going to guess and say c) clean air.","zh":"嗯，我完全没头绪，Georgie。我猜一个 c) 净化空气吧。","s":75.44,"e":81.66,"wt":[0,74,102,140,182,224,252,304,324,346,370,404,425,449,532,540]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK, Neil, we'll find out the answer at the end of the programme.","zh":"好的 Neil，节目最后我们来揭晓答案。","s":81.79,"e":85.14,"wt":[0,13,50,81,114,127,144,182,195,213,232,246,264]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK. Georgie, we've been talking about how uncomfortable the hot weather can make you feel in the summer. Is air conditioning all about comfort, though?","zh":"好的 Georgie，我们刚才一直在说夏天高温让人有多难受。不过，空调难道只是为了图个凉快舒服吗？","s":85.23,"e":93.98,"wt":[0,15,75,97,123,161,197,237,333,349,365,405,438,445,472,486,498,517,625,637,667,682,704,787,789]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"No, it's not just about comfort. Summer heat can be a real health issue as well. It's thought that across Europe, over 30,000 people died as a result of this summer's heat. Dr Radhika Khosla, an expert in climatology, told BBC World Service's The Climate Question how important air conditioning can be:","zh":"不，绝不仅仅是为了舒适。夏季高温同样会引发实实在在的健康危机。据估算，整个欧洲今年夏天就有超过 3 万人死于高温。气候学专家 Radhika Khosla 博士向 BBC 国际台《气候之问》节目讲述了空调有多重要：","s":94.32,"e":114.2,"wt":[0,0,11,32,64,105,186,235,275,291,307,315,346,397,448,453,531,542,582,617,650,695,718,814,848,871,882,890,928,944,976,1016,1107,1111,1162,1221,1235,1279,1294,1400,1432,1456,1496,1560,1576,1636,1707,1713,1766,1783,1854,1871]},{"speaker":"Dr Radhika Khosla","en":"Actually, air conditioning is life-saving . You know, in Singapore, when Lee Kuan Yew was asked what made civilisation possible in the tropics, he said it was air conditioning. And there are so many large parts of the world where we would not be able to inhabit if we did not have air conditioning.","zh":"事实上，空调是“救命的”（life-saving）。在新加坡，当年李光耀被问及是什么让热带地区得以孕育现代文明时，他回答说是空调。世界上还有大片地区，如果没有空调，我们根本无法在那里居住（inhabit）。","s":114.36,"e":132.12,"wt":[0,46,74,83,97,220,308,319,368,405,470,496,512,537,558,580,610,642,754,855,897,910,930,991,1002,1028,1041,1065,1087,1188,1201,1222,1236,1252,1293,1327,1346,1360,1376,1404,1418,1436,1464,1492,1508,1524,1544,1591,1600,1612,1630,1648,1672,1692]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"So, Radhika is saying that air conditioning is life-saving . It literally stops people from dying.","zh":"也就是说，Radhika 认为空调是“挽救生命的”（life-saving），名副其实能防止人因酷暑丧命。","s":132.27,"e":138.88,"wt":[0,17,59,73,115,143,164,252,269,334,381,398,477,529,557,581]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yes, and she makes the point that there are many parts of the world that we would not be able to inhabit – that means live in – without this technology.","zh":"对，她还指出，如果没有这项技术，世界上有很多地方我们根本无法“居住”（inhabit，意思就是 live in）。","s":139.15,"e":147.36,"wt":[0,17,34,51,80,97,127,149,178,196,218,247,264,275,309,325,341,359,381,397,437,448,490,514,545,584,615,621,630,684,715]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK Georgie. So, it sounds like air conditioning has a lot of good things about it. Is it all positive, though?","zh":"好的 Georgie，听起来空调好处多多。那它全都是积极影响吗？","s":147.36,"e":154.18,"wt":[0,22,72,104,116,156,176,192,275,287,294,317,332,366,411,449,487,507,521,590,617]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"No. There are some serious downsides. So, one big problem with air conditioning is the amount of energy it uses. Let's hear more from Radhika talking to The Climate Question:","zh":"不是的，它也有一些严重的弊端。空调的一大问题就是耗电量巨大。我们来听听 Radhika 在《气候之问》中的进一步分析：","s":154.48,"e":164.16,"wt":[0,16,40,56,92,139,209,225,241,266,309,336,356,437,450,470,500,523,560,577,643,660,683,712,729,763,803,814,831,875]},{"speaker":"Dr Radhika Khosla","en":"When air conditioning becomes the first, go-to solution for how we deal with the increased, prolonged and extreme heat that we're going to face and are facing, it places stresses across the system that are not able to be met. So, you know, we're going to see the grid collapse. We're going to see emissions rise. We're going to see all kinds of other impacts that actually are quite detrimental. And we reach a vicious cycle , because then you're in a world where it's getting warmer. We're using air conditioning to be able to protect ourselves. That's leading to more emissions and more energy use. That is then making the world warmer. And then we need more air conditioning.","zh":"当空调成为我们应对正在面临以及未来将要面临的不断加剧、持久且极端高温的首选方案时，它会给整个系统带来难以承受的负荷。因此我们会看到电网（the grid）崩溃，看到碳排放上升，还会看到各种其实相当有害的连锁影响。于是我们就陷入了“恶性循环”（vicious cycle）：世界越来越热，我们用空调来保护自己，这导致了更多能耗与排放，进而让地球更热，然后我们就需要开更多的空调。","s":164.67,"e":203.79,"wt":[0,20,43,123,169,215,261,291,352,376,399,414,453,485,560,665,681,703,754,783,812,848,885,899,928,957,998,1080,1085,1135,1207,1249,1277,1327,1354,1376,1401,1434,1450,1466,1525,1549,1559,1581,1608,1613,1629,1672,1680,1706,1773,1787,1787,1803,1873,1923,1981,2004,2027,2037,2053,2069,2110,2133,2173,2264,2279,2334,2350,2381,2474,2490,2503,2535,2541,2586,2604,2618,2669,2702,2717,2725,2738,2816,2835,2859,2902,2955,2985,3015,3033,3105,3117,3129,3153,3165,3213,3312,3338,3375,3394,3426,3475,3495,3522,3558,3581,3603,3614,3637,3661,3677,3693,3749,3750,3775,3776,3795,3815,3830]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"We heard about the grid . The grid is the network that distributes electricity across towns, cities and countries. If people are trying to use more electricity than it can cope with, then the grid can collapse – you get power cuts, which is when you can't get any electricity.","zh":"我们刚才听到了“电网”（the grid）这个词。the grid 是指把电力输送到城镇、城市乃至全国的供电网络。如果人们的用电量超过电网的承载极限，电网就会崩溃——也就是发生停电，完全用不上电。","s":203.79,"e":221.18,"wt":[0,10,44,78,98,120,149,179,223,237,258,309,333,424,494,538,586,634,650,736,750,793,814,862,871,901,928,1005,1040,1041,1060,1085,1122,1154,1167,1191,1210,1256,1280,1304,1318,1350,1435,1435,1435,1456,1478,1517,1569,1592]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"We heard that air conditioning creates emissions, which can lead the world to warm up, which means we then need more air conditioning. This makes the world even warmer and so on. And Radhika calls this a vicious cycle . A vicious cycle , or a vicious circle, is where a solution to a problem actually makes it worse, which means you have to do more and that makes the problem even worse still.","zh":"我们听到空调会产生碳排放，这会让地球变暖，于是我们又需要更多空调，而这又让地球更热，如此循环往复。Radhika 把这称为“恶性循环”（vicious cycle，也可以说 vicious circle），意思是用来解决问题的办法反而让问题更糟，结果你不得不加倍使用这个办法，导致情况雪上加霜。","s":221.2,"e":245.72,"wt":[0,22,46,70,110,178,182,248,289,306,335,357,400,416,448,491,518,554,571,605,633,664,686,790,814,820,842,880,920,966,1008,1036,1083,1096,1144,1184,1214,1224,1280,1311,1347,1352,1408,1431,1448,1464,1472,1528,1584,1612,1624,1632,1688,1712,1739,1775,1830,1866,1876,1923,1957,1991,2011,2038,2051,2064,2091,2111,2138,2177,2192,2264,2299,2326]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK. Well, that sounds like a bit of a problem. What are the solutions?","zh":"嗯，听起来确实是个棘手的难题。那有什么解决办法吗？","s":246.14,"e":250.12,"wt":[0,24,40,64,69,89,96,119,134,141,217,247,270,293]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Well, Radhika says that there are some things we should try first before using air conditioning, which will still be important. Here she is again talking to The Climate Question.","zh":"嗯，Radhika 表示，在开空调之前我们应该先尝试一些其他办法，虽然空调本身依然很重要。再来听听她在《气候之问》里怎么说。","s":250.19,"e":260.56,"wt":[0,25,61,91,114,140,159,182,209,225,261,293,333,380,435,438,528,548,573,604,619,690,715,738,745,792,832,844,862,906]},{"speaker":"Dr Radhika Khosla","en":"So, one of the most important things is to do passive cooling first. Now, passive cooling is when we take steps to provide thermal comfort that are not energy intensive. Some of the most important ones are first to cut solar gain, which is through things like shading .","zh":"最重要的一点是先采用“被动式降温”（passive cooling）。所谓被动式降温，就是采取不耗费大量能源的方式来提供体感舒适度。其中最关键的做法之一，就是先减少太阳热量的吸收，比如通过“遮阳”（shading）等方式。","s":260.64,"e":281.16,"wt":[0,48,60,68,88,126,147,203,222,243,310,329,398,488,506,551,592,608,632,715,830,888,940,972,1024,1048,1048,1064,1091,1153,1316,1316,1316,1338,1389,1443,1460,1481,1517,1586,1644,1678,1736,1750,1770,1792,1792,1808,1936]},{"speaker":"Passive cooling","en":"is where you cool your house down without using a lot of energy.","zh":"被动式降温（Passive cooling）指的是在不消耗大量能源的前提下给房屋降温。","s":281.16,"e":285.02,"wt":[0,18,18,39,66,100,142,180,231,268,275,297,311]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yes, heat from the sun can be blocked by shading , which could be with trees, curtains or blinds.","zh":"没错，太阳的热量可以通过“遮阳”（shading）来阻挡，比如利用树木、窗帘或百叶窗。","s":285.07,"e":291.16,"wt":[0,24,52,80,101,127,143,158,210,220,261,285,314,340,354,377,438,481,496]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK Georgie. So, passive cooling and shading – that sounds really easy. Is it that simple?","zh":"好的 Georgie，被动式降温和遮阳听起来很简单。真的就这么简单吗？","s":291.36,"e":296.54,"wt":[0,11,44,120,160,162,184,230,257,280,315,351,394,416,449,467]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"No, that's not all we have to do. Radhika also talks about the importance of using sustainable energy to power the air conditioning that we still will need.","zh":"不，我们要做的不止这些。Radhika 还强调了用可持续能源来为我们仍然需要的空调供电的重要性。","s":296.75,"e":305.68,"wt":[0,15,54,73,92,114,135,144,181,215,238,267,296,313,373,397,438,512,539,551,582,603,626,695,722,732,763,788]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK Georgie. It's time, I think, for you to give the answer to the question you asked us.","zh":"好的 Georgie，我觉得现在该由你来公布之前那道问题的答案了。","s":306.08,"e":310.78,"wt":[0,0,72,110,130,150,178,200,216,234,234,234,275,287,309,370,400,424]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yes, Neil. I asked you what modern air conditioning was used for when it was first invented in 1902.","zh":"是的 Neil，我问你现代空调在 1902 年刚发明出来时是用来做什么的。","s":310.99,"e":317.58,"wt":[0,22,70,129,129,129,158,196,215,301,317,352,381,397,405,438,453,508,529]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"And I had a wild guess. I said c) to clean air. But you know what? I'm feeling pretty confident now.","zh":"我当时瞎猜了一个 c) 净化空气。不过你猜怎么着？我现在感觉还挺有信心的。","s":317.71,"e":324.78,"wt":[0,21,33,70,125,175,263,283,283,367,391,425,445,456,467,482,508,533,565,604,661]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Sadly, you are incorrect. The correct answer was b) to dry ink. It was invented by Willis Carrier at a printing company in Brooklyn, New York.","zh":"遗憾的是，你答错了。正确答案是 b) 烘干油墨。它是由 Willis Carrier 在纽约布鲁克林的一家印刷公司发明的。","s":325.07,"e":333.98,"wt":[0,28,51,74,167,190,233,269,293,321,336,359,424,426,446,501,515,560,603,616,622,688,725,738,813,839]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we learned in this programme, starting with life-saving , which describes something that stops people from dying.","zh":"好吧，让我们回顾一下本期节目学到的词汇，先从 life-saving 开始，它用来形容能挽救生命、防止人死亡的事物。","s":334.19,"e":342.75,"wt":[0,29,70,96,116,183,195,246,264,284,333,373,413,481,512,535,598,657,684,722,759,786]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"If it's possible to inhabit somewhere, that means it's possible to live there.","zh":"如果某个地方可以 inhabit，意思就是适合在那里居住。","s":342.75,"e":346.96,"wt":[0,1,25,73,86,136,197,220,249,271,318,329,352]},{"speaker":"The grid","en":"is the system or network that distributes electricity across towns, cities, and countries.","zh":"The grid（电网）是指向城镇、城市和国家输送电力的系统或网络。","s":347.37,"e":353.66,"wt":[0,24,93,93,171,225,255,343,392,431,495,543,560]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"A vicious cycle is where a solution to a problem also makes it worse, meaning the problem just keeps getting even worse as you try to fix it.","zh":"vicious cycle（恶性循环）指的是解决问题的办法反而让问题恶化，意味着你越想解决，问题反而越来越严重。","s":353.75,"e":362.32,"wt":[0,4,53,90,105,142,149,214,224,236,289,321,363,380,452,489,509,559,588,618,666,693,727,740,760,780,793,817]},{"speaker":"Passive cooling","en":"is where you make a building cooler without using a lot of power.","zh":"Passive cooling（被动式降温）指的是在不耗费大量电力的情况下让建筑物降温。","s":363.36,"e":367.79,"wt":[0,21,51,73,102,117,171,216,274,315,323,347,363]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"And shading is blocking the sun's light and heat.","zh":"而 shading（遮阳）指阻挡太阳的光照和热量。","s":367.79,"e":370.99,"wt":[0,11,70,77,136,159,194,232,253]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Once again, our six minutes are up. Test what you've learned with the worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"六分钟又到了。欢迎访问我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 做练习题，检测你的学习成果。","s":370.99,"e":377.46,"wt":[0,14,57,75,93,131,145,200,209,229,259,295,315,336,381,396,416,465]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Thank you for joining us. Goodbye.","zh":"感谢收听，再见。","s":377.47,"e":378.48,"wt":[0,13,27,41,75,85]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Goodbye!","zh":"再见！","s":378.48,"e":378.94,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0pk7c8b.mp3"},{"epNum":5,"id":"ep-261022","date":"2026-10-22","title":"What should you eat for a better night’s sleep?","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-261022","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/261022_6_minute_english_what_should_you_eat_for_a_better_nights_sleep_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/261022_6_minute_english_what_should_you_eat_for_a_better_nights_sleep_transcript.pdf","summary":"Have you ever had the feeling of lying awake at night, unable to get to sleep? Many of us know what it is like to struggle with sleep, especially if we have drunk too much tea or coffee before bed! But did you know that your sleep quality can be affected by other parts of your diet too? Georgie and Phil discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"What percentage of people in the UK say they get less than seven hours sleep per night?","options":["a) 15%","b) 25%","c) 35%"],"topic":"健康医学","titleZh":"想睡个好觉，该吃点什么？","summaryZh":"你有没有经历过夜里躺在床上翻来覆去睡不着的滋味？很多人都有过失眠的困扰，尤其是睡前喝了太多茶或咖啡的时候！但你知道吗？日常饮食中的其他部分同样会影响睡眠质量。Georgie 和 Phil 将探讨饮食与睡眠的关系，并讲解新词汇。","questionZh":"英国有多大比例的人表示自己每晚睡眠不足 7 小时？","optionsZh":["a) 15%","b) 25%","c) 35%"],"answer":{"letter":"c","explanationZh":"Georgie 在节目最后揭晓答案：Phil 答对了！根据 YouGov 的一项调查，35% 的英国成年人表示自己每晚睡眠只有 6 小时甚至更少。"},"vocab":[{"word":"nutrients","defEn":"important substances that all lifeforms need to survive and be healthy","pos":"n.","zh":"营养素；营养物质","exampleEn":"Well, it's less about particular foods and more about particular nutrients .","ipa":"/ˈnjuːtriənts/"},{"word":"saturated fats","defEn":"fats that are thought to be worse for our health, often found in meat and dairy","pos":"短语","zh":"饱和脂肪","exampleEn":"Saturated fat is a type of fat mainly found in meat and dairy that's thought to be bad for your health.","ipa":"/ˈsætʃəreɪtɪd fæts/"},{"word":"deep sleep","defEn":"stage of the sleep cycle when the brain is at its slowest and the body can rest and repair","pos":"短语","zh":"深度睡眠","exampleEn":"And according to Marie-Pierre, eating less saturated fat and more nutrients like fibre is associated with more deep sleep at night.","ipa":"/diːp sliːp/"},{"word":"supplements","defEn":"tablets and other consumables that contain particular nutrients that you might not get in your everyday diet","pos":"n.","zh":"营养补充剂；保健品","exampleEn":"And you may also see adverts for supplements that claim to improve sleep.","ipa":"/ˈsʌplɪmənts/"},{"word":"feed information","defEn":"give lots of information continuously","pos":"短语","zh":"持续灌输/推送信息","exampleEn":"She says we are fed information on social media about magical tablets.","ipa":"/fiːd ˌɪnfəˈmeɪʃən/"},{"word":"magic bullet","defEn":"simple and effective solution, usually one that doesn't exist in reality","pos":"短语","zh":"灵丹妙药；立竿见影的神奇解法（通常并不存在）","exampleEn":"And of course, you know, Marie-Pierre and I know that there is no such magic bullet , unfortunately.","ipa":"/ˈmædʒɪk ˈbʊlɪt/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"struggle to","zh":"很难做到、费力去……（比 find it hard to 更简洁地道，口语写作通用）","contextEn":"Now, Phil, do you ever struggle to get a good night's sleep?"},{"en":"be associated with","zh":"与……有关联（学术和雅思写作表达因果/相关性的黄金句型，严谨又避免说得太绝对）","contextEn":"So, looking at large groups of people over a long period of time, and they found that a healthier diet is associated with better quality sleep."},{"en":"it's less about ... and more about ...","zh":"重点不在于……，而更多在于……（雅思口语 Part 3 纠偏、升华观点的高分句式）","contextEn":"Well, it's less about particular foods and more about particular nutrients ."},{"en":"do very well for oneself","zh":"赚得盆满钵满、混得风生水起（英式委婉幽默说法，暗指“早就发大财了”）","contextEn":"And if there was, I'm sure, you know, we would be... we would be doing very well for ourselves."}],"sentences":[{"en":"But when we talk about nutrients , diets that are higher in fibre, which we find in fruits and vegetables and whole grains, and lower in saturated fats , which we find in more plant-based types of foods, are associated with more deep sleep at night.","zh":"但谈到营养素时，膳食纤维含量较高（可从水果、蔬菜和全谷物中获取）且饱和脂肪含量较低（更多见于植物性饮食）的饮食模式，与夜间更多的深度睡眠相关。","coreEn":"Diets are associated with more deep sleep at night.","coreZh":"（某些）饮食模式与夜间更多深度睡眠相关。","cars":[{"text":"when we talk about nutrients","hook":"状语从句（限定话题）","noteZh":"切换到营养素层面来讨论"},{"text":"that are higher in fibre","hook":"关系词 that（并列条件一）","noteZh":"给 diets 加第一个条件：膳食纤维含量更高的饮食"},{"text":"which we find in fruits and vegetables and whole grains","hook":"逗号插入语 + 关系词 which","noteZh":"顺手注释 fibre 的来源：蔬果和全谷物"},{"text":"and lower in saturated fats","hook":"并列修饰（并列条件二）","noteZh":"给 diets 加第二个条件：饱和脂肪含量更低"},{"text":"which we find in more plant-based types of foods","hook":"逗号插入语 + 关系词 which","noteZh":"顺手注释这种低饱和脂肪饮食的特点：更多见于植物性食物"}],"tipZh":"这句是典型的“主语被拉成 30 个词”的雅思阅读长难句！主语 diets 到谓语 are associated with 之间夹了两层插入语，先一眼找到主语和谓语，再回头看夹心内容。"},{"en":"They've also done some smaller studies looking in more detail at exactly what people are eating and then studying how they sleep in a laboratory.","zh":"他们还开展了一些规模较小的研究，更细致地观察人们究竟吃了什么，然后在实验室里研究他们的睡眠状况。","coreEn":"They've also done some smaller studies.","coreZh":"他们还做了一些规模较小的研究。","cars":[{"text":"looking in more detail at exactly what people are eating","hook":"-ing 修饰","noteZh":"修饰 studies，说明研究的第一步：细致观察受试者到底吃了什么"},{"text":"and then studying how they sleep in a laboratory","hook":"并列 -ing 修饰","noteZh":"由 and then 连接的第二步：接着在实验室监测他们怎么睡"}],"tipZh":"名词 studies 后直接跟 looking... and then studying...，这种并列 -ing 短语把“研究内容”压缩得非常紧凑，读的时候直接理解成“这些研究先……然后……”。"},{"en":"This magical powder or this magical tablet that you can take that will, you know, help you sleep better immediately from tonight.","zh":"这种神奇的粉末或药片，只要你吃下去，从今晚起就能立刻帮你睡得更香。","coreEn":"This magical powder or this magical tablet.","coreZh":"这种神奇的粉末或药片（口语中顺承上文的名词短语）。","cars":[{"text":"that you can take","hook":"关系词 that（第一个限定）","noteZh":"说明是“你能服用的”粉末或药片"},{"text":"that will, you know, help you sleep better immediately from tonight","hook":"关系词 that（第二个限定）","noteZh":"接着夸大它的功效：今晚就能立刻助眠；中间夹着口语填充词 you know"}],"tipZh":"真实英文访谈中经常出现两个 that 从句连用，还会夹杂 you know 这类口语缓冲词；听力中要学会自动过滤 you know，抓紧后面的核心动词 help you sleep better。"},{"en":"But experts say that focusing on our whole diet, rather than one particular fruit or supplement , is going to be the most effective.","zh":"但专家表示，关注我们的整体饮食，而非某一种特定的水果或营养补充剂，才是最有效的做法。","coreEn":"Experts say that focusing on our whole diet is going to be the most effective.","coreZh":"专家说关注整体饮食将会是最有效的。","cars":[{"text":"focusing on our whole diet","hook":"-ing 短语作主语","noteZh":"在 that 从句里担任主语：“关注整体饮食”这件事"},{"text":"rather than one particular fruit or supplement","hook":"逗号插入对比（rather than）","noteZh":"插在主语和 is going to 之间做对比排除：“而不是某一种特定水果或保健品”"}],"tipZh":"A, rather than B, is... 是雅思写作表达“应该重视整体而非局部”的高分句式，记住谓语动词只看前面的 A（单数 is），不受 B 影响。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Hi Georgie.","zh":"嗨，Georgie。","s":0.07,"e":0.72,"wt":[0,20]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Hi Phil. Now, Phil, do you ever struggle to get a good night's sleep?","zh":"嗨，Phil。问你个问题，你有没有睡不好觉的时候？","s":0.92,"e":5.34,"wt":[0,32,111,134,168,183,201,226,286,300,316,329,358,394]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"You know, I do sometimes. If I've been thinking about something a lot, or even worse, if I've had a cup of coffee later than I should, sometimes I can lie there for hours.","zh":"嗯，有时候确实会。如果我满脑子都在琢磨某件事，或者更糟的是咖啡喝得比平时晚了，我有时会在床上干躺好几个小时。","s":5.55,"e":16.56,"wt":[0,7,7,21,75,148,159,200,230,273,337,353,371,472,533,533,585,598,617,627,641,655,690,736,768,787,808,875,903,931,959,987,987,1009]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yes. Well, many of us will be familiar with that feeling of lying in bed, feeling really tired, but just not being able to go to sleep, but what causes us to struggle to sleep and is there anything we can do about it? Well, researchers have for a while been interested in the link between what we eat and how we sleep. Today on 6 Minute English, we'll be talking about this and asking, \"What should you eat for a better night's sleep?\"","zh":"是啊，我们很多人都熟悉那种滋味：躺在床上累得不行，却怎么也睡不着。到底是什么导致我们难以入睡，又有什么办法能改善呢？一段时间以来，研究人员一直对“吃什么”和“睡得好不好”之间的关联很感兴趣。在今天的《六分钟英语》中，我们就来聊聊这个话题，并探讨：“想睡个好觉，该吃点什么？”","s":16.99,"e":42.44,"wt":[0,34,77,95,111,137,149,190,239,253,281,307,337,361,379,419,473,527,576,589,617,643,671,695,695,725,749,823,834,857,887,903,914,955,981,1012,1030,1046,1088,1147,1167,1187,1212,1241,1308,1340,1405,1427,1439,1465,1495,1526,1569,1581,1599,1637,1689,1705,1713,1773,1797,1815,1837,1916,1940,1952,1970,2007,2057,2073,2092,2131,2157,2189,2208,2269,2301,2339,2363,2397,2413,2422,2460,2508]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK. Phil, let's start with a quiz question. So, adults need between 7–9 hours sleep a night to stay healthy, according to experts, but what percentage of people in the UK say they get less than this? Is it: a) 15%, b) 25%, or c) 35%?","zh":"好，Phil，我们先从一道问答开始。专家称成年人每晚需要 7 到 9 小时睡眠才能保持健康，但在英国，有多大比例的人表示自己睡不到这个时长？是：a) 15%，b) 25%，还是 c) 35%？","s":45.4,"e":64.98,"wt":[0,7,92,127,162,192,197,225,311,378,383,420,560,580,616,660,669,720,730,758,807,824,856,963,979,1008,1028,1072,1082,1098,1134,1196,1231,1250,1270,1302,1320,1379,1400,1424,1469,1597,1637,1764,1778,1872]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"OK. I think people find sleeping hard, so I think it's going to be c) 35%.","zh":"好吧，我觉得大家普遍都挺难睡够的，所以我选 c) 35%。","s":65.28,"e":71.94,"wt":[0,0,60,79,197,225,328,411,422,431,431,436,444,448,480,602]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK. Well, we'll find out the answer later in the programme.","zh":"好，我们稍后在节目里揭晓答案。","s":72.35,"e":75.32,"wt":[0,18,31,55,73,89,109,143,197,218,227]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Georgie, you've been looking at the research on this. Do scientists think that what we eat affects how we sleep?","zh":"Georgie，你一直在看这方面的研究。科学家认为我们吃的东西会影响睡眠吗？","s":75.32,"e":82.5,"wt":[0,54,82,107,124,134,168,182,196,323,333,431,476,513,538,553,581,649,668,681]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yes, they do. Scientists have done some long-term studies. So, looking at large groups of people over a long period of time, and they found that a healthier diet is associated with better quality sleep. They've also done some smaller studies looking in more detail at exactly what people are eating and then studying how they sleep in a laboratory.","zh":"是的，他们确实这样认为。科学家开展了一些长期研究，即在很长一段时间内追踪大批人群，结果发现更健康的饮食与更优质的睡眠存在关联。他们还做了一些规模较小的研究，更细致地观察受试者到底吃了什么，再在实验室里研究他们的睡眠状况。","s":82.91,"e":103.18,"wt":[0,15,39,103,129,147,173,201,287,319,326,372,392,428,457,491,552,553,586,619,619,669,727,739,766,787,805,833,857,924,933,1032,1050,1113,1153,1233,1249,1279,1303,1344,1372,1419,1455,1474,1501,1545,1558,1610,1638,1671,1697,1764,1779,1803,1855,1876,1901,1931,1937,1954]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"OK. And in these studies, have they found that particular foods are better for our sleep than others?","zh":"好的。那在这些研究中，他们有没有发现某些特定食物比其他食物更有助于睡眠？","s":103.46,"e":110.7,"wt":[0,9,14,85,138,245,277,317,348,412,488,552,563,605,620,632,661,684]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Well, it's less about particular foods and more about particular nutrients . So, nutrients means important things your body needs to survive and be healthy. For example, protein is a nutrient . BBC World Service programme The Food Chain has been speaking to researcher Professor Marie-Pierre St-Onge about which nutrients are important for sleep.","zh":"嗯，重点与其说是某些特定食物，不如说是某些特定“营养素”（nutrients）。nutrients 指人体维持生存和健康所必需的重要物质，比如蛋白质就是一种营养素。BBC 国际台的《食物链》节目采访了研究员 Marie-Pierre St-Onge 教授，探讨哪些营养素对睡眠至关重要。","s":110.95,"e":131.84,"wt":[0,42,72,105,133,203,247,264,286,320,383,433,472,482,563,609,662,687,707,729,767,780,841,855,880,953,971,1015,1077,1085,1097,1145,1184,1194,1225,1268,1325,1353,1375,1411,1422,1445,1487,1503,1591,1630,1742,1816,1841,1887,1919,1937,1977,2017]},{"speaker":"Marie-Pierre St-Onge","en":"So, diets that look more like a Mediterranean pattern – higher in fruits and vegetables, whole grains, lower in animal-based foods, lower in sugar. But then from our own research, where we evaluate food intakes in the lab, we see different... similar patterns, really. But when we talk about nutrients , diets that are higher in fibre, which we find in fruits and vegetables and whole grains, and lower in saturated fats , which we find in more plant-based types of foods, are associated with more deep sleep at night.","zh":"更接近地中海饮食模式的膳食——富含水果、蔬菜和全谷物，动物性食物较少，糖分也更低。而在我们自己于实验室评估食物摄入量的研究中，也看到了……其实是非常相似的规律。但谈到营养素（nutrients）时，富含膳食纤维（来自蔬果和全谷物）且低饱和脂肪（saturated fats，更常见于植物性食物为主的饮食）的膳食，与夜间更多的深度睡眠（deep sleep）相关。","s":132.18,"e":167.82,"wt":[0,37,84,206,234,262,285,288,367,399,417,449,460,498,525,594,608,744,796,817,937,1014,1062,1092,1197,1213,1249,1272,1305,1316,1384,1406,1457,1572,1628,1644,1664,1700,1737,1744,1764,1904,1936,1955,2025,2046,2097,2101,2138,2162,2238,2276,2310,2310,2336,2404,2442,2505,2522,2532,2557,2577,2591,2613,2658,2680,2706,2761,2777,2813,2833,2880,2900,2917,2934,2950,2973,2989,3007,3067,3100,3164,3321,3331,3374,3378,3396,3426,3448,3494]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Marie-Pierre says that Mediterranean-style diets are associated with good sleep. A Mediterranean diet includes lots of vegetables and less animal-based foods such as meat and dairy.","zh":"Marie-Pierre 说地中海风格的饮食与良好睡眠有关。地中海饮食包含大量蔬菜，以及较少的肉类和乳制品等动物性食物。","s":167.91,"e":179.1,"wt":[0,75,107,125,249,288,304,378,408,451,507,507,590,616,695,704,726,816,826,851,924,957,987,1006,1037,1065]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Yes, and Marie-Pierre's own research shows that some nutrients , such as fibre, are important for sleep and others, such as saturated fats , are less important. Saturated fat is a type of fat mainly found in meat and dairy that's thought to be bad for your health.","zh":"没错，Marie-Pierre 自己的研究也表明，像膳食纤维这样的营养素对睡眠很重要，而像“饱和脂肪”（saturated fats）这类则不太有利。饱和脂肪主要存在于肉类和乳制品中，通常被认为对健康不利。","s":179.27,"e":196.1,"wt":[0,38,72,165,192,268,292,320,342,379,397,417,483,548,564,619,643,688,705,760,843,843,878,895,909,937,965,1072,1152,1181,1200,1209,1257,1267,1307,1348,1373,1395,1415,1419,1504,1529,1529,1555,1587,1611,1621,1644]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Right. And according to Marie-Pierre, eating less saturated fat and more nutrients like fibre is associated with more deep sleep at night. A deep sleep is when you are more fully asleep and your body is able to rest.","zh":"对。而且据 Marie-Pierre 所说，少吃饱和脂肪、多摄入纤维等营养素，与夜间更多的“深度睡眠”（deep sleep）有关。深度睡眠是指你完全熟睡、身体能够得到充分休息与修复的阶段。","s":196.31,"e":210.66,"wt":[0,52,72,125,141,235,278,310,363,417,428,454,513,551,600,609,699,726,769,792,834,850,927,928,955,995,1005,1031,1047,1053,1117,1141,1188,1203,1235,1239,1297,1301,1323]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"So, Georgie, it sounds like thinking about our diets is important if we want to improve our sleep. And now I've heard that certain foods have a lot of nutrients that can be helpful for sleep. Is that true?","zh":"那么 Georgie，听起来如果想要改善睡眠，关注日常饮食确实很重要。我还听说某些特定食物富含助眠营养素，这是真的吗？","s":210.99,"e":224.18,"wt":[0,32,108,119,183,218,265,305,336,396,404,472,487,504,532,579,600,623,672,689,727,763,827,855,900,944,976,980,1016,1023,1105,1127,1141,1162,1193,1205,1255,1263,1287]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Well, there is some limited research on specific foods and their impact on sleep. So, for example, scientists have been studying kiwi fruits and also tart cherry juice. And you may also see adverts for supplements that claim to improve sleep. Supplements are tablets you take, which contain particular nutrients that you might not get in your everyday diet. But experts say that focusing on our whole diet, rather than one particular fruit or supplement , is going to be the most effective. Here's sleep doctor Allie Hare explaining more to BBC World Service programme The Food Chain.","zh":"嗯，关于特定食物对睡眠影响的研究目前还比较有限。例如，科学家一直在研究猕猴桃和酸樱桃汁。你可能还见过号称能改善睡眠的“营养补充剂”（supplements）广告。supplements 指你服用的片剂等产品，含有日常饮食中可能摄取不足的特定营养素。但专家表示，关注整体饮食结构，而不是寄希望于某一种水果或保健品，才是最有效的。下面听听睡眠医生 Allie Hare 在 BBC 国际台《食物链》节目中的进一步解释。","s":224.51,"e":258.56,"wt":[0,32,61,72,92,131,190,218,274,320,336,373,415,433,479,498,517,588,633,647,670,703,735,792,817,864,903,935,1032,1043,1059,1083,1104,1121,1165,1217,1251,1269,1293,1319,1347,1408,1469,1484,1525,1543,1573,1601,1648,1710,1776,1789,1811,1811,1839,1881,1897,1909,1983,2056,2072,2125,2143,2176,2227,2243,2274,2305,2352,2384,2408,2427,2491,2533,2543,2609,2639,2646,2679,2692,2712,2725,2752,2844,2882,2919,2955,2987,3039,3095,3113,3137,3152,3184,3230,3295,3317,3339]},{"speaker":"Allie Hare","en":"I think people are looking for the one supplement often or the... you know, we're fed a lot of these things, aren't we, on social media? This magical powder or this magical tablet that you can take that will, you know, help you sleep better immediately from tonight. And of course, you know, Marie-Pierre and I know that there is no such magic bullet , unfortunately. And if there was, I'm sure, you know, we would be... we would be doing very well for ourselves.","zh":"我觉得人们总是在寻找某一种万能补充剂……社交媒体上也总给我们源源不断地灌输这些东西（we're fed a lot of these things），对吧？好像吃了某种神奇粉末或神奇药片，从今晚起就能立刻睡得香。当然，我和 Marie-Pierre 都很清楚，遗憾的是并不存在这样的“灵丹妙药”（magic bullet）。要是真有的话，我相信我们俩早就发大财了。","s":258.86,"e":286.8,"wt":[0,4,28,66,74,110,142,142,191,269,317,335,384,408,448,448,466,507,528,538,562,580,598,616,625,671,726,784,888,985,992,1021,1072,1118,1134,1134,1172,1206,1230,1293,1329,1392,1410,1410,1437,1509,1562,1586,1630,1638,1642,1694,1719,1762,1876,1884,1894,1920,1934,1934,1972,2004,2037,2096,2117,2130,2216,2226,2238,2240,2258,2284,2440,2466,2472,2474,2479,2485,2492,2528,2612,2612,2612,2655,2675]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Allie says people are looking for a supplement that can help them sleep.","zh":"Allie 说人们总想找到某种能帮自己入睡的补充剂。","s":286.82,"e":291.92,"wt":[0,54,160,188,188,251,270,279,377,390,410,428,454]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yes. She says we are fed information on social media about magical tablets. If you are fed information , you are given lots of it in a continuous way.","zh":"是的。她说社交媒体不断向我们“灌输信息”（fed information），推销各种神奇药片。be fed information 意思是被持续不断地塞入大量信息。","s":292.12,"e":301.92,"wt":[0,39,71,104,112,136,189,252,268,315,367,400,457,547,554,570,598,619,681,707,718,740,770,792,802,826,834,872,924]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"But Allie argues that experts know there's no magic bullet to help you sleep, and a magic bullet refers to a simple and effective solution to a problem that's usually one that doesn't exist. OK. Well, now I think it's time to find out the answer to your quiz question, Georgie.","zh":"但 Allie 指出，专家们都明白并不存在助眠的“灵丹妙药”（magic bullet）。magic bullet 指解决某个问题既简单又立竿见影的神奇办法，但通常并不存在于现实中。好了 Georgie，现在该揭晓你那道问答的答案了吧。","s":302.01,"e":320.38,"wt":[0,22,54,108,177,216,285,323,361,407,473,480,509,541,602,618,625,674,710,752,773,792,834,850,909,925,933,955,1062,1095,1137,1155,1181,1239,1306,1353,1414,1439,1448,1496,1532,1581,1585,1606,1622,1638,1671,1690,1704,1726,1781]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yes. I asked you what percentage of UK adults say they get less than seven hours sleep a night.","zh":"是的。我问你，英国有多大比例的成年人表示自己每晚睡眠不足 7 小时。","s":320.48,"e":326.58,"wt":[0,51,51,107,124,144,200,221,246,315,331,358,387,423,437,494,512,547,564]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"And I said I thought it was c) 35%.","zh":"我猜的是 c) 35%。","s":326.68,"e":330.32,"wt":[0,12,42,72,82,98,112,146,283]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"You are correct, Phil. Yes, 35% of adults say they get six hours or less sleep a night, according to a study by YouGov. OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we learned in this programme, starting with nutrients , which are important things your body needs to survive and be healthy.","zh":"你答对了，Phil！没错，根据 YouGov 的一项调查，35% 的成年人表示每晚睡眠在 6 小时或以下。好了，让我们回顾一下本期节目学到的词汇，先从 nutrients（营养素）开始，它指人体维持生存与健康所需的重要物质。","s":330.63,"e":348.44,"wt":[0,20,40,108,160,203,274,294,357,381,407,421,448,500,509,541,582,590,635,673,679,699,733,759,868,900,933,955,976,1017,1058,1093,1101,1128,1181,1227,1286,1335,1359,1381,1398,1449,1485,1515,1537,1569,1582,1645,1667,1690]},{"speaker":"Saturated fats","en":"are fats mainly found in meat and dairy products that are thought to be bad for our health.","zh":"Saturated fats（饱和脂肪）主要存在于肉类和乳制品中，通常被认为对健康不利。","s":349.84,"e":355.52,"wt":[0,50,85,117,146,167,198,215,251,307,320,333,364,378,396,448,464,490]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"A deep sleep is when your body is fully asleep and able to rest.","zh":"deep sleep（深度睡眠）指身体进入完全熟睡并能够得到充分休息的阶段。","s":355.54,"e":359.98,"wt":[0,10,37,72,77,104,125,160,190,209,264,288,316,334]},{"speaker":"Supplements","en":"are tablets or other things which contain particular nutrients you might not get in your everyday diet.","zh":"Supplements（营养补充剂）是含有日常饮食中可能摄取不到的特定营养素的片剂或其他制品。","s":360.56,"e":366.46,"wt":[0,35,74,100,118,164,187,239,280,332,357,382,400,436,448,480,542]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"To feed information means to give lots of it in a continuous way.","zh":"feed information 意思是持续不断地向人提供、灌输大量信息。","s":366.66,"e":370.58,"wt":[0,17,44,126,155,172,190,210,222,245,252,262,340]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"And a magic bullet is a simple and effective solution, and it's often one that doesn't exist.","zh":"而 magic bullet（灵丹妙药）指简单又特效的解决方案，但往往在现实中并不存在。","s":370.72,"e":376.84,"wt":[0,10,32,71,118,134,142,192,217,286,366,376,402,428,456,484,536]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Once again, our six minutes are up, but don't forget you can test what you've learned with the worksheet on our website. Go to bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"六分钟又到了，别忘了可以前往我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 做练习题，检验学习成果。","s":377.03,"e":384.98,"wt":[0,20,57,74,90,129,143,168,184,211,248,271,286,319,329,358,389,405,427,479,493,511,564,574,591]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Thanks for joining us and goodbye.","zh":"感谢收听，再见。","s":385.31,"e":387.58,"wt":[0,32,52,85,108,129]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Goodbye!","zh":"再见！","s":387.66,"e":388.38,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0phyzz9.mp3"},{"epNum":6,"id":"ep-261015","date":"2026-10-15","topic":"经济消费","title":"Why do people buy gold?","titleZh":"人们为什么买黄金？","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-261015","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/261015_6_minute_english_why_do_people_buy_gold_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/261015_6_minute_english_why_do_people_buy_gold_transcript.pdf","summary":"Many of us own jewellery or other items that are gold coloured, but do you own anything that is made from real gold? Gold is a precious metal, which means that there is a limited supply in the world. This not only makes it expensive, but also means that it holds a relatively stable value. So, why are investors interested in it, and should the rest of us start buying more of it? Georgie and Phil discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","summaryZh":"我们很多人都有金色的首饰或其他物品，但你拥有真正用黄金制成的东西吗？黄金是一种贵金属，这意味着全球供应量有限。这不仅让它价格昂贵，也意味着它的价值相对稳定。那么，为什么投资者对黄金如此感兴趣？普通人又该不该多买一些呢？Georgie 和 Phil 将探讨这个话题，并教你几个实用的新词汇。","question":"How much gold has been extracted from the earth over its history?","questionZh":"纵观人类历史，迄今已从地球上开采出多少黄金？","options":["a) over 50,000 tonnes","b) over 100,000 tonnes","c) over 200,000 tonnes"],"optionsZh":["a) 超过 5 万吨","b) 超过 10 万吨","c) 超过 20 万吨"],"answer":{"letter":"c","explanationZh":"节目结尾 Phil 公布答案时确认，Georgie 猜的“超过 20 万吨（over 200,000 tonnes）”是正确的。"},"vocab":[{"word":"asset","defEn":"something of financial value that can be sold for money","pos":"n.","zh":"资产；有价值的财产","exampleEn":"even though it isn't money, gold is still a financial asset","ipa":"/ˈæset/"},{"word":"safe haven","defEn":"something or somewhere protected from harm or loss","pos":"n. 短语","zh":"避风港；（金融）避险资产","exampleEn":"Gold is a safe haven because it's not connected to one particular government or one particular economy or one particular market","ipa":"/seɪf ˈheɪvən/"},{"word":"out of thin air","defEn":"from nothing or nowhere","pos":"短语","zh":"凭空；无中生有","exampleEn":"It can't be created and fabricated out of thin air through alchemy","ipa":"/aʊt əv θɪn eə/"},{"word":"alchemy","defEn":"ancient study combining elements of chemistry and mysticism, which attempted to do impossible things like transforming ordinary metals into gold","pos":"n.","zh":"炼金术","exampleEn":"As Catherine says, we don't make gold through alchemy .","ipa":"/ˈælkəmi/"},{"word":"ethereal","defEn":"seemingly unreal","pos":"adj.","zh":"虚无缥缈的；仿佛不真实的","exampleEn":"So much of the world we live in now is ethereal .","ipa":"/ɪˈθɪəriəl/"},{"word":"a hankering for","defEn":"a strong desire for","pos":"短语","zh":"对……的强烈渴望/向往","exampleEn":"And in this ethereal world, people have a hankering for something that's solid and real, like gold.","ipa":"/ə ˈhæŋkərɪŋ fɔː/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"in times of crisis","zh":"在危机时期（经济、社会类雅思写作与听力高频正式表达）","contextEn":"And people buy gold in times of crisis because it's thought of as a safe investment."},{"en":"a store of value","zh":"价值储存手段；保值工具（金融与经济领域地道搭配）","contextEn":"so the fact that it's neutral means it's an asset that's perceived to be a store of value over a longer term."},{"en":"hit an all-time high","zh":"创下历史新高（描述价格、数据走势时比 reach the highest price 更地道）","contextEn":"Now, Phil, at the start of this programme, you said that gold prices hit an all-time high in early 2026."},{"en":"more or less","zh":"大致上；差不多（口语中用来缓和绝对语气的高频副词短语）","contextEn":"And Georgie, because we get gold from mines, there's more or less a fixed amount."}],"sentences":[{"en":"Gold is a safe haven because it's not connected to one particular government or one particular economy or one particular market, so the fact that it's neutral means it's an asset that's perceived to be a store of value over a longer term.","zh":"黄金之所以是避险资产，是因为它不与某一个特定政府、经济体或市场挂钩，因此它的中立属性意味着它被视为一种长期的保值资产。","coreEn":"Gold is a safe haven, so the fact means it's an asset.","coreZh":"黄金是避险资产，因此这一事实意味着它是一种资产。","cars":[{"text":"because it's not connected to one particular government or one particular economy or one particular market","hook":"状语从句 because","noteZh":"说明黄金之所以成为避险资产的原因，连用三个 or 并列强调其独立性"},{"text":"that it's neutral","hook":"同位语从句 that","noteZh":"紧跟 the fact，解释“这个事实”具体是指黄金具有中立性"},{"text":"that's perceived to be a store of value over a longer term","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"补充说明 asset 的特征——在较长时期内被人们公认为一种价值储存手段"}],"tipZh":"遇到 the fact that ... means ... 结构时，先把 that 后面补全的“事实内容”打包看成一个整体主语，句子因果逻辑瞬间清晰。"},{"en":"It can't be created and fabricated out of thin air through alchemy , and so this, sort of, controls the supply and sustains an underlying value behind the demand for gold.","zh":"黄金无法通过炼金术凭空制造出来，所以这在某种程度上控制了供应量，并在黄金需求背后支撑起它的内在价值。","coreEn":"It can't be created and fabricated, and so this controls the supply and sustains an underlying value.","coreZh":"它无法被制造出来，因此这控制了供应并支撑起内在价值。","cars":[{"text":"out of thin air through alchemy","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"接在动词后面，说明“以何种方式”制造（凭空、靠炼金术）"},{"text":", sort of,","hook":"逗号插入语","noteZh":"口语缓冲词，意思是“在某种程度上、算是”，听读时可直接跳过不影响主干"},{"text":"behind the demand for gold","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"修饰 underlying value，说明这种内在价值是支撑在“对黄金的需求”背后的"}],"tipZh":"BBC 访谈专家常在主语和动词间插入 sort of / kind of / you know 等口语填充词，听力中学会自动“消音”，紧盯动词 controls 和 sustains。"},{"en":"And there has been a series of world events that have made government banks want to buy more gold, for example the wars in Ukraine and the Middle East, and in politics too, for example the US imposing tariffs on international products.","zh":"此外，一系列国际事件促使各国央行想要购买更多黄金，例如乌克兰和中东的战争，还有政治方面的事件，比如美国对国际商品加征关税。","coreEn":"There has been a series of world events.","coreZh":"发生了一系列世界事件。","cars":[{"text":"that have made government banks want to buy more gold","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"解释这些世界事件带来的直接后果——促使央行增持黄金"},{"text":"for example the wars in Ukraine and the Middle East","hook":"举例补充 for example","noteZh":"列举地缘冲突方面的具体例子"},{"text":"for example the US imposing tariffs on international products","hook":"举例补充 + -ing 修饰","noteZh":"列举政治贸易方面的具体例子，“美国对国际商品征收关税”这一动名词复合结构"}],"tipZh":"长句里连续出现两个 for example 时，它们只是在给前面的 world events 举实例；阅读做主旨题时抓前半句核心结论，做细节题时再回来定位例子。"},{"en":"It's... it's virtual, it's online, it disappears, whereas gold is something solid, something dependable and, you know, maybe something a little bit old fashioned.","zh":"它……它是虚拟的、线上的、转瞬即逝的，而黄金是实实在在的、靠得住的，而且，怎么说呢，也许还有点复古怀旧的感觉。","coreEn":"It's virtual, online, and disappears, whereas gold is something solid, dependable, and old fashioned.","coreZh":"现代世界是虚拟易逝的，而黄金则是实在、可靠且略显复古的。","cars":[{"text":"whereas gold is something solid, something dependable and, you know, maybe something a little bit old fashioned","hook":"转折对比从句 whereas","noteZh":"用 whereas 引出与现代虚拟世界形成鲜明反差的黄金特质"},{"text":", you know,","hook":"逗号插入语","noteZh":"口语停顿过渡词，用来思考措辞或拉近与听众距离，无实义"}],"tipZh":"whereas（然而/而）是雅思听力与阅读极高频的“对比信号词”，一听到 whereas 就要立刻竖起耳朵，后面往往紧跟考点或作者真正强调的对象。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Hi Phil.","zh":"嗨，Phil。","s":0.19,"e":0.86,"wt":[0,3]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Hi Georgie. I've got a question for you. Do you own anything made of gold?","zh":"嗨，Georgie。我有个问题想问你。你有任何黄金做的东西吗？","s":1.09,"e":7.32,"wt":[0,9,98,127,139,143,189,206,317,323,367,421,520,528,542]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Well, at the moment I'm wearing a lot of gold-coloured jewellery, but I'm not sure exactly how much of it is made of gold.","zh":"嗯，我现在戴着不少金色的首饰，但我不太确定里面到底有多少是真金做的。","s":7.6,"e":15.76,"wt":[0,86,100,124,142,170,170,210,222,248,300,374,396,426,440,528,543,600,600,600,608,637,653,669]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"OK. I don't think I own anything made of gold. Beyond jewellery and other personal items, gold is bought and sold by investors and banks. The price of gold has always gone up and down, but in 2026 it reached its highest ever price. In this episode of 6 Minute English, we'll be talking about how gold became a valuable investment and asking the question, why do people buy gold?","zh":"好吧，我想我大概没有任何黄金制品。除了首饰和其他个人物品之外，投资者和银行也会买卖黄金。黄金价格一向起起落落，但在2026年达到了历史最高价。在本期《六分钟英语》中，我们将探讨黄金是如何成为一项高价值投资的，并提出一个问题：人们为什么买黄金？","s":15.76,"e":43.26,"wt":[0,24,78,78,126,138,168,242,248,274,368,372,428,477,552,601,635,662,736,782,794,850,878,903,931,1058,1063,1096,1096,1144,1160,1172,1202,1220,1264,1294,1314,1338,1446,1466,1506,1538,1601,1669,1776,1776,1787,1848,1876,1898,1898,1913,1962,1962,2012,2048,2088,2139,2150,2202,2224,2331,2338,2358,2404,2514,2514,2548,2585,2611]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Let's start with our quiz question, Georgie. Gold is a metal that's extracted or dug up from under the ground in mines. But how much gold has been extracted from the earth? Is it: a) over 50,000 tonnes, b) over 100,000 tonnes, or c) over 200,000 tonnes?","zh":"我们先从每周一问开始吧，Georgie。黄金是一种从地下矿井中开采或挖掘出来的金属。那么迄今为止从地球上总共开采出了多少黄金呢？选项是：a) 超过 5 万吨，b) 超过 10 万吨，还是 c) 超过 20 万吨？","s":45.92,"e":73.08,"wt":[0,0,0,52,90,156,196,334,372,386,416,423,446,562,584,626,648,707,728,738,776,818,924,1044,1070,1112,1166,1186,1240,1334,1334,1335,1472,1472,1538,1640,1710,1771,1932,2036,2072,2157,2255,2316,2350,2448,2646]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Phil, I have absolutely no idea, but I'm going to go with c) 200,000 tonnes.","zh":"Phil，我完全没头绪，不过我打算选 c) 20 万吨。","s":73.36,"e":78.76,"wt":[0,0,18,104,109,184,226,232,233,248,262,274,312,323,490]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"OK. Well, we'll give you the answer at the end of the programme.","zh":"好，我们会在节目最后揭晓答案。","s":78.91,"e":82.54,"wt":[0,4,27,65,90,95,114,170,229,229,234,255,292]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK Phil. So, we've been using gold for thousands of years, but why and how did it start?","zh":"好的，Phil。人类使用黄金已经有几千年历史了，但这一切最初是怎么开始的，又是为什么呢？","s":82.75,"e":88.92,"wt":[0,11,41,56,93,123,147,206,218,282,297,411,411,481,495,495,511,543]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Well, before money was paper or plastic or just numbers in your bank account, a lot of it was actually gold. And experts have discovered gold in coins that are thought to have been made in the 6th Century BC. So, gold coins were made and they were used because the metal was much easier to carry and trade than things like cattle or timber.","zh":"在货币变成纸币、银行卡或仅仅是银行账户里的一串数字之前，很多钱其实就是黄金。专家们已经在被认为铸造于公元前6世纪的硬币中发现了黄金。当时人们铸造并使用金币，是因为这种金属比牲畜或木材等物品更容易携带和交易。","s":89.07,"e":112.8,"wt":[0,13,90,99,123,197,214,289,309,345,345,393,407,416,533,543,557,589,610,656,737,753,841,870,889,1000,1063,1063,1139,1158,1173,1204,1216,1234,1253,1266,1277,1309,1376,1435,1545,1575,1611,1653,1687,1727,1741,1741,1783,1853,1887,1900,1969,1989,2008,2037,2053,2117,2121,2154,2191,2207,2245,2267,2289]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK. But now our money isn't literally made of gold, so why is gold valuable?","zh":"明白了。但现在我们的钱字面上已经不是黄金做的了，那为什么黄金依然值钱呢？","s":112.8,"e":118.32,"wt":[0,40,57,91,149,155,192,258,288,289,354,395,401,417,485]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"It's a good question, but even though it isn't money, gold is still a financial asset , and an asset is something valuable that could be sold for money. And people buy gold in times of crisis because it's thought of as a safe investment. Let's listen to expert Suki Cooper explaining more to the BBC World Service.","zh":"这是个好问题。尽管它不是货币了，但黄金仍然是一种金融资产（asset），所谓“资产”就是有价值、可以变卖换钱的东西。而且人们在危机时期会买入黄金，因为它被视为一种安全的投资。我们来听听专家 Suki Cooper 在 BBC 国际频道上的进一步解释。","s":118.36,"e":140,"wt":[0,12,18,56,94,113,138,183,187,246,318,364,381,444,452,543,556,565,606,633,647,663,758,803,812,822,830,850,874,956,1017,1064,1091,1148,1156,1204,1255,1356,1356,1361,1422,1422,1442,1469,1484,1580,1640,1658,1671,1716,1766,1804,1868,1924,1927,1949,1972,2017]},{"speaker":"Suki Cooper","en":"Gold is a safe haven because it's not connected to one particular government or one particular economy or one particular market, so the fact that it's neutral means it's an asset that's perceived to be a store of value over a longer term.","zh":"黄金是一种避险资产（safe haven），因为它不与某一个特定政府、经济体或市场挂钩，因此它的中立性意味着它被视为一种能够长期保值的资产。","s":140.06,"e":153.6,"wt":[0,20,30,52,78,110,110,156,164,234,268,282,367,426,429,451,529,600,600,646,703,750,773,784,836,850,887,946,998,1008,1027,1058,1092,1096,1112,1144,1170,1192,1222,1253,1274,1275,1310]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Suki describes gold as a safe haven , and a safe haven means something or somewhere that's protected from harm or danger, or in financial terms, from a fall in value.","zh":"Suki 将黄金形容为“避风港/避险资产（safe haven）”，意思是指免受伤害或危险的事物或地方，在金融领域则指能抵御贬值风险的资产。","s":153.6,"e":165.44,"wt":[0,58,136,192,195,200,232,263,285,296,304,336,409,424,488,504,600,624,706,738,760,805,888,891,904,981,1022,1038,1063,1105,1120]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yes, Suki explains that, unlike dollars or pounds for example, gold is not connected to a particular country. It's neutral.","zh":"是的，Suki 解释说，与美元或英镑等货币不同，黄金不依附于任何特定国家，它是中立的。","s":165.44,"e":173.91,"wt":[0,70,114,119,152,218,272,314,328,363,404,453,455,480,574,577,582,668,718,770]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"And Georgie, because we get gold from mines, there's more or less a fixed amount. Unlike paper money, you can't just print more, as Catherine Schenk, professor of economic and social history, explains.","zh":"而且 Georgie，由于黄金是从矿里开采出来的，它的总量大致是固定的。不像纸币那样想多印就能多印。经济与社会史教授 Catherine Schenk 是这样解释的。","s":173.91,"e":187.06,"wt":[0,27,84,153,169,228,240,271,385,385,385,410,442,448,488,589,633,635,713,713,739,751,829,879,884,945,981,1031,1097,1119,1142,1185,1273]},{"speaker":"Catherine Schenk","en":"It can't be created and fabricated out of thin air through alchemy , and so this, sort of, controls the supply and sustains an underlying value behind the demand for gold.","zh":"它无法通过炼金术（alchemy）凭空（out of thin air）制造出来，所以这在某种程度上控制了供应量，并在黄金需求背后支撑起了它的内在价值。","s":187.12,"e":197.31,"wt":[0,21,49,74,112,147,212,237,238,267,289,302,359,392,410,426,455,466,508,546,576,631,658,732,739,829,842,885,901,956,970]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Catherine explains that gold can't be created out of thin air , an expression meaning making something from nothing.","zh":"Catherine 解释说黄金不能“凭空（out of thin air）”造出来，这个表达的意思就是无中生有。","s":197.31,"e":204.46,"wt":[0,63,121,149,178,217,231,285,321,328,354,372,391,418,466,542,563,630,671]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"As Catherine says, we don't make gold through alchemy . Alchemy is an ancient kind of chemistry that attempted to do impossible things, like turn ordinary metals into gold. And because we can't just magically make more gold, when lots of people want to buy gold, the price increases.","zh":"正如 Catherine 所说，我们没法靠炼金术（alchemy）造黄金。炼金术是一种古代化学，试图做到不可能的事情，比如把普通金属变成黄金。正因为我们不能像变魔术一样凭空变出更多黄金，当很多人想买黄金时，价格就会上涨。","s":204.66,"e":225.3,"wt":[0,14,88,118,150,203,238,289,321,359,393,458,458,503,523,559,616,631,696,726,738,755,838,937,959,1024,1063,1106,1144,1190,1276,1279,1299,1345,1380,1462,1498,1540,1582,1622,1662,1665,1704,1740,1758,1794,1856,1882,1935]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK. Understood. Now, Phil, at the start of this programme, you said that gold prices hit an all-time high in early 2026. So, what's going on?","zh":"好，明白了。那么 Phil，节目一开始你说金价在2026年初创下了历史新高。到底是怎么回事？","s":225.38,"e":235.78,"wt":[0,82,210,230,254,262,288,288,334,361,399,444,455,487,524,576,592,616,671,703,748,768,872,875,923,971]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Well, we mentioned that the price of gold increases in times of crisis because government banks want to buy more of it. And there has been a series of world events that have made government banks want to buy more gold, for example the wars in Ukraine and the Middle East, and in politics too, for example the US imposing tariffs on international products. But since that peak in January, the price has dropped a bit.","zh":"我们刚才提到，在危机时期金价会上涨，因为各国央行想增持黄金。而最近发生了一系列国际事件促使央行买进更多黄金，比如乌克兰和中东的战争，还有政治层面的因素，比如美国对国际商品加征关税。不过自一月份的峰值以来，价格已经回落了一点。","s":236,"e":263.22,"wt":[0,0,82,109,110,139,166,230,248,331,363,364,410,480,562,626,640,687,691,713,756,756,838,848,848,890,890,968,1024,1062,1092,1122,1122,1152,1186,1216,1256,1289,1291,1315,1360,1385,1411,1473,1508,1534,1550,1670,1682,1698,1715,1749,1776,1802,1888,1901,1920,1976,1984,2001,2069,2120,2178,2234,2304,2380,2386,2401,2437,2449,2522,2528,2589,2608,2647,2659]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK. And does the fall in the price of gold mean that people won't want to invest in it in the future?","zh":"好，那金价下跌是否意味着人们以后就不想投资黄金了呢？","s":263.46,"e":269.1,"wt":[0,74,92,106,130,152,160,160,160,173,266,298,316,320,346,376,391,456,462,492,497,521]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"No. It's likely that the price will go up again. Experts believe that gold will always be an attractive investment during uncertain times. Let's hear from market strategist John Reade.","zh":"不会，价格很可能会再次上涨。专家认为，在充满不确定性的时期，黄金始终是一项有吸引力的投资。我们来听听市场策略师 John Reade 的看法。","s":269.4,"e":283.2,"wt":[0,23,110,120,151,173,215,240,264,298,418,462,462,513,532,591,614,618,629,687,780,808,839,976,998,1068,1102,1151,1236,1284]},{"speaker":"John Reade","en":"So much of the world we live in now is ethereal . It's... it's virtual, it's online, it disappears, whereas gold is something solid, something dependable and, you know, maybe something a little bit old fashioned. And maybe it's a hankering for traditional values rather than what is super modern and super changeable.","zh":"我们如今身处的这个世界，有太多东西都是虚无缥缈的（ethereal）。它是虚拟的、线上的、说没就没的；而黄金却是实实在在、可靠的东西，甚至可能带着一点复古的感觉。也许这反映出人们对传统价值的强烈渴望（a hankering for），而不是去追逐那些极其摩登又瞬息万变的东西。","s":283.2,"e":302.86,"wt":[0,1,19,20,48,109,115,148,176,200,228,251,271,295,380,419,438,503,530,624,632,672,683,773,856,900,1018,1070,1082,1090,1092,1168,1194,1228,1280,1302,1328,1363,1380,1394,1410,1478,1502,1582,1633,1670,1696,1710,1722,1774,1796,1824,1888]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"John says that much of the modern world is ethereal , meaning it doesn't seem real.","zh":"John 说现代世界的许多事物都是虚无缥缈的（ethereal），意思是看起来不真实。","s":303.12,"e":307.69,"wt":[0,13,42,88,100,117,135,185,214,230,273,296,347,347,411,432]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"And in this ethereal world, people have a hankering for something that's solid and real, like gold. And the expression a hankering for means a strong desire for something.","zh":"在这个虚无缥缈的世界里，人们对像黄金这样实在而真实的东西怀有强烈的渴望（a hankering for）。a hankering for 这个表达的意思就是对某事物有强烈的渴望。","s":307.84,"e":320.06,"wt":[0,42,74,112,187,209,277,287,295,384,404,448,496,536,550,603,637,667,682,698,772,792,882,894,944,973,1066,1096,1158]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK. Phil, I think it's time for the answer to your quiz question.","zh":"好了，Phil，我觉得现在该揭晓你那道竞猜题的答案了。","s":320.06,"e":323.82,"wt":[0,29,62,69,96,122,169,172,204,250,271,274,325]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"OK. Well, I asked how much gold has been mined from the ground to date.","zh":"好的。我问的是迄今为止从地下开采出了多少黄金。","s":324.2,"e":330.32,"wt":[0,32,78,78,128,159,244,306,322,322,410,410,422,460,532]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"I said 200,000 tonnes.","zh":"我选的是 20 万吨。","s":330.36,"e":332.81,"wt":[0,43,75,220]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"And that is the correct answer! OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we learnt in this programme, starting with asset . And an asset is something of financial value that can be sold for money.","zh":"这就是正确答案！好，我们来回顾一下本期学到的词汇，首先是 asset（资产），指具有经济价值、可以出售换钱的东西。","s":333.3,"e":345.52,"wt":[0,30,30,74,92,92,177,268,314,342,342,387,403,472,478,536,569,636,659,700,746,746,800,836,868,882,954,978,1022,1070,1080,1099,1135,1179]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"A safe haven is something or somewhere protected from harm or loss.","zh":"safe haven（避风港/避险资产）指免受伤害或损失的事物或地方。","s":345.52,"e":350.08,"wt":[0,16,66,90,94,157,166,230,284,315,337,349]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"To make something out of thin air is an expression meaning to make something from nothing.","zh":"make something out of thin air（凭空制造某物）是一个习语，意思是无中生有。","s":350.08,"e":355.96,"wt":[0,36,82,122,129,153,206,224,240,275,308,366,384,422,492,523]},{"speaker":"Alchemy","en":"is a kind of ancient chemistry that attempted to do impossible things, like making gold from ordinary metals.","zh":"（炼金术）是一种古代化学，试图做到不可能的事情，比如用普通金属炼出黄金。","s":356.38,"e":363.86,"wt":[0,24,52,60,74,125,190,245,294,312,335,407,462,493,532,568,588,652]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"If something is ethereal , it doesn't seem real.","zh":"如果某事物是 ethereal（虚无缥缈的），意思是它看起来不像真的。","s":363.92,"e":366.9,"wt":[0,2,66,76,141,176,179,260,269]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"And if you have a hankering for something, you have a strong desire for it.","zh":"如果你对某物有 a hankering for（强烈渴望），意思就是你非常想要它。","s":367.6,"e":372.04,"wt":[0,26,44,66,82,98,106,130,218,228,261,269,328,396,418]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Once again, our six minutes are up, and if you have a hankering for more practice, test what you've learned with the worksheet on our website. That's bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"我们的六分钟时间又到了，如果你渴望做更多练习，可以用我们网站上的学习单来检验今天学到的内容，网址是 bbclearningenglish.com。","s":372.27,"e":382.42,"wt":[0,10,44,88,141,141,163,169,208,217,229,258,270,347,350,401,445,477,509,536,565,565,605,633,651,689,719,767]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Thank you for joining us. Goodbye.","zh":"感谢收听，再见。","s":382.48,"e":384.26,"wt":[0,24,26,38,72,123]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Goodbye!","zh":"再见！","s":384.26,"e":385.02,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0pgs15w.mp3"},{"epNum":7,"id":"ep-261008","date":"2026-10-08","topic":"健康医学","title":"Are people drinking less alcohol?","titleZh":"人们现在喝酒变少了吗？","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-261008","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/261008_6_minute_english_are_people_drinking_less_alcohol_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/261008_6_minute_english_are_people_drinking_less_alcohol_transcript.pdf","summary":"When was the last time you had an alcoholic drink? Some people choose to live without drinking any alcohol, but for many others drinking alcohol is a normal part of life. Even so, in recent generations many people have chosen to stop drinking alcohol. Georgie and Neil discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","summaryZh":"你上一次喝酒是什么时候？有些人选择滴酒不沾，而对另一些人来说，喝酒则是日常生活中很平常的一部分。即便如此，近几代人中有越来越多人选择戒酒。Georgie 和 Neil 将探讨这一趋势，并教你几个实用的新词汇。","question":"Many people in the UK and other countries choose to give up alcohol in the new year for the whole of the month of January, but what is the challenge called?","questionZh":"英国及其他国家的许多人会选择在新年伊始的整个一月份戒酒，这项挑战叫什么名字？","options":["a) No-drink New Year","b) Alcohol-free January","c) Dry January"],"optionsZh":["a) 无酒新年（No-drink New Year）","b) 无酒精一月（Alcohol-free January）","c) 戒酒一月（Dry January）"],"answer":{"letter":"c","explanationZh":"节目结尾 Neil 揭晓答案说这项挑战叫做 Dry January（戒酒一月），Georgie 确认回答正确。"},"vocab":[{"word":"sober","defEn":"not drinking alcohol or taking recreational drugs","pos":"adj.","zh":"戒酒的；清醒的（不饮酒或吸食娱乐性药物）","exampleEn":"So, why are more young people choosing to be sober ?","ipa":"/ˈsəʊbə/"},{"word":"societal pressure","defEn":"feeling that you should do something because lots of other people expect you to","pos":"n. 短语","zh":"社会压力；外界的从众压力","exampleEn":"So, Daniel says that when he was younger, he felt societal pressure to drink alcohol.","ipa":"/səˈsaɪətəl ˈpreʃə/"},{"word":"the norm","defEn":"the usual or common thing to do","pos":"n. 短语","zh":"常态；司空见惯的事","exampleEn":"So, Daniel says drinking was the norm at university and after work.","ipa":"/ðə nɔːm/"},{"word":"self-optimisation content","defEn":"videos, posts and other media about being fit, healthy and achieving big goals","pos":"n. 短语","zh":"自我优化/自律提升类内容","exampleEn":"I think we had this, like, rise of self-optimisation content .","ipa":"/ˌselfˌɒptɪmaɪˈzeɪʃən ˈkɒntent/"},{"word":"tout","defEn":"praise something or talk about it a lot to get others to try it","pos":"v.","zh":"大力吹捧；极力推崇","exampleEn":"Millie says being sober gets touted as a way to get up at 5am to exercise.","ipa":"/taʊt/"},{"word":"pushback","defEn":"strong criticism of a trend; people doing the opposite of a trend","pos":"n.","zh":"抵制；反弹；反击潮流的行为","exampleEn":"But still, Millie thinks there's a pushback against self-optimisation content .","ipa":"/ˈpʊʃbæk/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"cut down on","zh":"减少、削减（常用于减少饮酒、糖分、开支等，雅思健康类极高频短语）","contextEn":"Today on 6 Minute English, we're going to find out why many people are choosing to give up or cut down on alcohol, and we'll be asking, \"Is this the end of British drinking culture?\""},{"en":"give it away","zh":"剧透；泄露答案（日常口语中比 tell the answer 地道得多）","contextEn":"Well, I know the answer to this, but I'm not going to give it away."},{"en":"step away from","zh":"抽身离开；主动告别某种习惯或环境（职场与生活表达都常用）","contextEn":"So, I did that and I think Covid lockdown just gave me a bit of confidence to say, \"OK, let me step away from this. It's not necessarily something I want to do moving forward.\""},{"en":"get swept in with","zh":"被卷入其中；被一并裹挟进某种热潮里（生动形象的口语表达）","contextEn":"I think unfortunately sobriety, kind of, got swept in with that and it got touted as this way to, you know, get up at 5am, do a HYROX."}],"sentences":[{"en":"So, today I don't drink at all, but when I was in my early 20s, I think I felt maybe a bit of, like, societal pressure – you know, work drinks or being at uni, where it was the norm to just join in.","zh":"所以，我现在滴酒不沾，但在二十出头的时候，我觉得自己大概感受到了一点社会压力——你知道的，就是同事聚会喝酒或者在大学里，大家自然而然都会一起喝。","coreEn":"Today I don't drink at all, but I felt societal pressure.","coreZh":"如今我滴酒不沾，但当初我感受到了社会压力。","cars":[{"text":"when I was in my early 20s","hook":"状语从句 when","noteZh":"交代时间背景：在自己二十出头那几年"},{"text":"– you know, work drinks or being at uni,","hook":"破折号插入语","noteZh":"举例说明社会压力具体出现在哪些场合（下班聚餐喝酒、读大学）"},{"text":"where it was the norm to just join in","hook":"关系词 where","noteZh":"补充说明在这些场合中，“跟着大家一起喝”就是再正常不过的常态"}],"tipZh":"真实英语口语常带 I think / maybe / like / you know 等缓冲词，拆解时先剥掉这些“情绪填充料”，就能一眼看清 but 前后的今昔对比。"},{"en":"But during the Covid-19 pandemic, when people in the UK were unable to socialise and visit pubs and bars, Daniel's attitude to alcohol changed.","zh":"但在新冠疫情期间，当英国民众无法外出社交、去不了酒馆和酒吧时，Daniel 对酒精的态度发生了转变。","coreEn":"Daniel's attitude to alcohol changed.","coreZh":"Daniel 对酒精的态度改变了。","cars":[{"text":"during the Covid-19 pandemic","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"点出变化发生的大背景时间段"},{"text":"when people in the UK were unable to socialise and visit pubs and bars","hook":"关系词 when","noteZh":"夹在两个逗号之间，具体解释疫情期间的限制状况，为态度转变铺垫原因"}],"tipZh":"英语常把“大时间 + when 从句补充当时情景”放在句首做长铺垫，读到第二个逗号后面才真正出现“车头”主句，切忌在半路迷失主语。"},{"en":"Whilst many people use social media to share their experience of drinking less alcohol or no alcohol at all, some people have been saying that they want to go out more and drink and have fun with friends.","zh":"尽管许多人利用社交媒体分享自己少喝酒甚至滴酒不沾的经历，但也有一些人一直在说，他们想多出去和朋友喝酒寻开心。","coreEn":"Some people have been saying that they want to go out more and drink and have fun.","coreZh":"一些人一直在说，他们想多出去喝酒玩乐。","cars":[{"text":"Whilst many people use social media to share their experience of drinking less alcohol or no alcohol at all","hook":"让步对比从句 Whilst","noteZh":"先铺垫主流现象（很多人在社媒分享戒酒/减酒经历），为后文的反向声音做对比"},{"text":"to share their experience of drinking less alcohol or no alcohol at all","hook":"不定式 to do + 介词短语","noteZh":"在前半句内进一步说明大家用社交媒体的具体目的"}],"tipZh":"Whilst 是英式英语高频书面与播客词（等同于 While），引导“虽然A如此，但B相反”的对照结构，主旨重心通常落在后半句。"},{"en":"I think unfortunately sobriety, kind of, got swept in with that and it got touted as this way to, you know, get up at 5am, do a HYROX.","zh":"我觉得很遗憾，戒酒在某种程度上被卷入了那股热潮，被吹捧成一种——你知道的——能让你早上五点起床、去参加 HYROX 健身赛的方式。","coreEn":"Sobriety got swept in with that and it got touted as this way.","coreZh":"戒酒被卷入了那股热潮，并被吹捧成这样一种方式。","cars":[{"text":", kind of,","hook":"逗号插入语","noteZh":"口语委婉词，表示“某种程度上、有点儿”"},{"text":"to, you know, get up at 5am, do a HYROX","hook":"不定式 to do","noteZh":"修饰 this way，具体说明戒酒被包装成了哪种极端自律的生活方式"}],"tipZh":"口语中经常用 get + 过去分词（got swept in / got touted）替代 be 动词构成被动语态，语气更自然也更强调“无意间落入某种境地”。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Hi Neil.","zh":"嗨，Neil。","s":19.1,"e":19.92,"wt":[0,26]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Hi Georgie. Do you want to hear something quite unusual for me?","zh":"嗨，Georgie。想听一件对我来说挺不寻常的事吗？","s":20.02,"e":23.5,"wt":[0,22,92,94,94,94,102,126,162,224,304,320]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Err, yes!","zh":"呃，想听！","s":23.64,"e":24.66,"wt":[0,34]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"I haven't had an alcoholic drink this year.","zh":"我今年还没喝过一口酒呢。","s":24.82,"e":28.28,"wt":[0,16,72,105,124,192,257,295]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Wow! That is really impressive. How do you feel? Like a new person?","zh":"哇！太厉害了。你感觉怎么样？觉得像脱胎换骨一样吗？","s":28.58,"e":32.92,"wt":[0,26,55,69,113,210,226,236,252,290,318,330,354]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"I do feel very good, I have to say.","zh":"我不得不说，我感觉确实非常棒。","s":33,"e":35.34,"wt":[0,2,16,47,78,124,132,163,179]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"I can't say the same – I did have a few alcoholic drinks at the weekend with my friends. But in many parts of the world, people don't drink alcohol at all. In the UK it's very common – that is until recently. Today on 6 Minute English, we're going to find out why many people are choosing to give up or cut down on alcohol, and we'll be asking, \"Is this the end of British drinking culture?\"","zh":"我可就没法这么说了——周末我还跟朋友喝了几杯呢。不过在世界上的许多地方，人们根本不喝酒。而在英国，喝酒一直非常普遍——至少直到最近都是如此。今天在《六分钟英语》里，我们将探寻为什么越来越多人选择戒酒或减少饮酒，并提出这样一个问题：“英国的饮酒文化要走向终结了吗？”","s":35.39,"e":61.18,"wt":[0,4,36,58,80,109,133,140,161,189,196,217,281,324,338,359,413,439,471,569,587,595,623,647,655,677,721,729,761,801,849,871,961,973,1001,1034,1066,1104,1158,1193,1215,1246,1287,1392,1413,1426,1445,1484,1537,1563,1563,1605,1631,1663,1682,1708,1747,1766,1818,1837,1875,1882,1949,1987,2007,2053,2143,2151,2151,2197,2212,2283,2317,2351,2381,2415,2457,2459]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK. Neil, let's start with a quiz question. Many people in the UK and other countries choose to give up alcohol in the new year for the whole of the month of January, but what is the challenge called? Is it: a) No-drink New Year b) Alcohol-free January, or c) Dry January?","zh":"好的，Neil，我们先来一道竞猜题。在英国和其他国家，许多人在新年伊始会选择整个一月份滴酒不沾，这项挑战叫什么名字？选项是：a) 无酒新年（No-drink New Year），b) 无酒精一月（Alcohol-free January），还是 c) 戒酒一月（Dry January）？","s":62.72,"e":83.64,"wt":[0,22,60,92,92,133,145,193,318,358,380,380,426,455,497,514,598,634,651,672,701,760,776,792,827,860,876,906,944,956,972,982,1036,1138,1180,1221,1225,1245,1304,1368,1380,1410,1475,1533,1568,1652,1678,1788,1886,1914,1952,2008]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, I know the answer to this, but I'm not going to give it away.","zh":"嗯，这道题的答案我知道，但我可不会提前剧透。","s":83.64,"e":87.64,"wt":[0,48,108,108,112,160,176,208,232,260,280,308,313,338,352]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK. Well, then our listeners can find out the answer at the end of the programme.","zh":"好吧，那我们的听众可以在节目最后揭晓答案。","s":87.64,"e":91.44,"wt":[0,33,55,77,93,143,159,184,209,215,246,256,271,286,296,311]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Yes, they can. So, Georgie, I hear all the time that younger people don't drink as much alcohol today as they used to in the past. Is that true?","zh":"没错。那么 Georgie，我经常听说现在的年轻人不像过去那样爱喝酒了，这是真的吗？","s":91.44,"e":101.62,"wt":[0,27,63,120,136,210,218,251,276,301,351,373,430,479,518,548,572,609,666,707,723,748,774,792,808,836,911,918,947]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Well, in the UK, surveys suggest that 16 to 24-year-olds are more likely to be non-drinkers than the older generations.","zh":"嗯，在英国，调查显示16至24岁的年轻人比老一辈更有可能滴酒不沾。","s":101.76,"e":110.68,"wt":[0,52,69,95,176,190,251,286,344,382,496,517,572,587,601,630,723,744,768,808]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK. So, why are more young people choosing to be sober ? That means not to drink alcohol.","zh":"明白了。那为什么越来越多年轻人选择戒酒（sober）呢？这个词意思就是不喝酒。","s":110.97,"e":117.22,"wt":[0,41,64,88,112,144,184,239,293,299,315,346,381,414,455,480,496,537]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Well, the BBC World Service podcast What in the World have been investigating this. Presenter Hannah Gelbart met three young people who choose to be sober for some alcohol-free drinks to find out more. Here's Daniel's story:","zh":"BBC 国际频道的播客《What in the World》一直在调查这个问题。主持人 Hannah Gelbart 约了三位选择戒酒的年轻人一起喝无酒精饮品，想一探究竟。下面是 Daniel 的故事：","s":117.24,"e":131.84,"wt":[0,45,68,91,129,182,235,268,292,302,332,356,380,454,502,554,590,636,660,699,738,788,818,855,872,889,931,956,990,1091,1142,1159,1193,1217,1282,1307,1340]},{"speaker":"Daniel","en":"Yeah. So, today I don't drink at all, but when I was in my early 20s, I think I felt maybe a bit of, like, societal pressure – you know, work drinks or being at uni, where it was the norm to just join in. I think you'd probably get a few weird glances if you're like, \"Yeah, I'm not going to join in at all.\" So, I did that and I think Covid lockdown just gave me a bit of confidence to say, \"OK, let me step away from this. It's not necessarily something I want to do moving forward.\"","zh":"是的。我现在滴酒不沾，但二十出头的时候，我觉得自己大概感受到了一点社会压力（societal pressure）——你知道的，下班和同事小酌，或者在大学里，跟着大家一起喝是常态（the norm）。如果你说“嗯，我完全不打算喝”，可能还会招来几道异样的目光。所以我当时也就跟着喝了，而新冠封控期恰好给了我一点底气，让我可以说：“好，让我从这种习惯里抽身吧，这未必是我以后想继续做的事。”","s":131.98,"e":153.84,"wt":[0,23,34,60,66,93,116,131,163,179,201,208,222,233,244,271,320,325,352,369,406,432,436,451,456,463,513,540,555,568,592,619,657,669,700,712,730,761,773,802,826,863,886,915,928,947,952,977,1002,1042,1057,1062,1074,1092,1115,1124,1142,1157,1172,1187,1202,1227,1238,1262,1266,1278,1316,1328,1334,1353,1378,1384,1400,1434,1465,1522,1598,1617,1626,1630,1646,1655,1702,1727,1743,1822,1822,1822,1822,1838,1858,1893,1913,1927,1983,2028,2032,2058,2070,2082,2133]},{"speaker":"Hannah Gelbart","en":"So, how many years have you been sober for?","zh":"那你已经戒酒多少年了？","s":154,"e":156.12,"wt":[0,8,20,36,56,72,84,117,156]},{"speaker":"Daniel","en":"I would say it's been six years now.","zh":"算起来现在已经有六年了。","s":156.12,"e":157.94,"wt":[0,7,45,84,106,129,146,176]},{"speaker":"Hannah Gelbart","en":"Six years?","zh":"六年了？","s":157.94,"e":158.22,"wt":[0,3]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"So, Daniel says that when he was younger, he felt societal pressure to drink alcohol. Societal pressure is feeling like you need to do something because lots of people around you are doing it.","zh":"Daniel 说他年轻时感受到了喝酒的社会压力（societal pressure）。所谓社会压力，就是因为周围很多人都在做某件事，而觉得自己也应该跟着做的感觉。","s":158.04,"e":171.16,"wt":[0,172,221,253,280,300,318,348,370,412,440,514,536,556,602,682,754,793,806,852,878,897,923,936,949,1008,1054,1080,1093,1132,1171,1190,1209,1262]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Right. So, Daniel says drinking was the norm at university and after work. The norm is a way of saying that something is normal or common.","zh":"没错，Daniel 说在大学和下班后喝酒是常态（the norm）。the norm 用来表达某件事很正常、司空见惯。","s":171.34,"e":180.02,"wt":[0,48,82,116,142,182,198,226,254,270,350,378,426,493,512,547,553,560,583,598,638,668,676,716,784,816]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"But during the Covid-19 pandemic, when people in the UK were unable to socialise and visit pubs and bars, Daniel's attitude to alcohol changed. After that, he decided to be sober and he hasn't had a drink for about six years.","zh":"但在新冠疫情期间，当英国民众无法外出社交、去不了酒馆和酒吧时，Daniel 对酒精的态度发生了变化。在那之后，他决定戒酒，到现在已经大约六年没碰过酒了。","s":180.32,"e":195.72,"wt":[0,26,58,74,174,264,284,290,300,346,382,414,446,471,532,556,592,621,642,685,742,822,833,909,1006,1042,1062,1080,1120,1136,1164,1197,1215,1227,1263,1281,1287,1318,1359,1404,1442]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"So, Georgie, it seems like many young people don't drink at all and lots of them share their experience of being sober on social media. But recently there's been a bit of a change, hasn't there?","zh":"这么说来，Georgie，似乎很多年轻人完全不喝酒，不少人还在社交媒体上分享自己的戒酒经历。不过最近风向好像有点变了，对吧？","s":195.88,"e":207.58,"wt":[0,14,70,91,126,154,182,220,256,292,338,358,386,416,462,468,500,539,578,610,648,662,726,756,794,876,899,950,985,1014,1018,1032,1044,1050,1099,1135]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yes. Whilst many people use social media to share their experience of drinking less alcohol or no alcohol at all, some people have been saying that they want to go out more and drink and have fun with friends. They argue we're too worried about fitness and wellness, and that drinking some alcohol to have a good time is fine. Here's Millie talking more about this to BBC World Service podcast What in the World:","zh":"是的。尽管很多人用社交媒体分享自己少喝酒或不喝酒的经历，但也有一些人开始说他们想多出去和朋友喝酒找乐子。他们认为我们对健身和养生过于焦虑了，其实喝点酒开心一下也没什么问题。来听听 Millie 在 BBC 国际频道《What in the World》播客里是怎么说的：","s":207.76,"e":232.46,"wt":[0,38,84,115,161,184,230,272,288,320,356,408,430,474,518,577,592,607,661,676,714,800,800,820,850,912,926,944,960,976,990,1014,1049,1068,1100,1119,1144,1163,1188,1252,1277,1309,1352,1386,1416,1448,1492,1511,1562,1581,1606,1657,1682,1726,1738,1763,1769,1794,1838,1850,1928,1970,2012,2062,2090,2125,2153,2167,2188,2223,2272,2323,2351,2384,2406]},{"speaker":"Millie","en":"I think we had this, like, rise of self-optimisation content . I think unfortunately sobriety, kind of, got swept in with that and it got touted as this way to, you know, get up at 5am, do a HYROX. And, you know, for many of us, that's probably not our reality with not drinking. It's that we do it to feel a bit more sane or, you know, things like that. And so, I think now we are entering this, like, almost pushback era of that content and you're seeing celebrities... Like, it's not just alcohol – you're seeing more celebrities smoking and, like, posting that on their Instagram and not worrying if it's brand-friendly.","zh":"我觉得之前兴起了一股“自我优化类内容（self-optimisation content）”的热潮。很遗憾，戒酒在某种程度上被卷入了其中，被吹捧（tout）成一种能让你早上五点起床、去参加 HYROX 健身赛的方式。但对我们很多人来说，不喝酒的真实情况其实并不是那样。我们不喝酒只是为了让自己头脑更清醒、心态更平稳，诸如此类。所以我觉得我们现在正进入一个对这类内容产生反弹（pushback）的时代，你能看到名人们……不只是喝酒，你看到越来越多名人抽烟，还把照片发到 Instagram 上，根本不在乎这会不会影响商业形象。","s":232.66,"e":260.09,"wt":[0,16,16,30,52,88,124,162,178,318,341,362,367,392,443,489,505,514,536,566,584,610,628,640,664,692,728,740,760,778,787,794,806,826,839,852,898,926,937,985,990,994,1000,1015,1036,1046,1066,1097,1139,1152,1169,1205,1226,1241,1300,1346,1358,1363,1376,1389,1402,1429,1436,1456,1483,1510,1531,1548,1576,1576,1591,1650,1668,1680,1686,1717,1735,1747,1765,1828,1842,1866,1902,1950,1968,1980,2004,2047,2066,2101,2190,2204,2228,2262,2280,2288,2313,2328,2348,2366,2384,2420,2470,2483,2501,2532,2550,2559,2581,2630,2640,2660,2688,2696,2718]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK. So, Millie thinks that being sober is associated with certain kinds of self-optimisation content , where people talk about fitness, diet and having big goals for themselves.","zh":"明白，Millie 认为戒酒如今和某些“自我优化类内容（self-optimisation content）”捆绑在了一起，这类内容谈论的都是健身、饮食控制和给自己定下宏大目标。","s":260.76,"e":271.84,"wt":[0,41,55,96,137,165,199,233,252,335,354,407,438,459,586,619,639,676,721,751,787,855,886,916,950,961,996,1017]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yes, and Millie says being sober gets touted as a way to get up at 5am to exercise. In other words, people talk about it a lot and try to encourage people to do it.","zh":"是的，而且 Millie 说戒酒被大力吹捧（tout）成早上五点起床锻炼的秘诀。换句话说，人们反复宣扬这件事，试图鼓动大家都去做。","s":271.84,"e":283,"wt":[0,45,72,126,162,207,252,288,348,356,364,388,408,441,446,460,496,580,654,666,696,742,742,763,799,813,820,842,864,886,950,976,1026,1041,1066]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"But Millie says that being sober isn't always about being super fit and healthy. For her, it's about being sane.","zh":"但 Millie 表示，戒酒并不总是为了追求极致的健康和身材。对她而言，这只是为了保持理智清醒。","s":283.1,"e":290.36,"wt":[0,18,52,86,112,144,183,216,262,294,331,368,390,412,486,504,550,580,614,660]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"But still, Millie thinks there's a pushback against self-optimisation content . Pushback means a criticism of the content, and people are pushing back by doing the opposite. For example, lots of celebrities post about themselves drinking and smoking.","zh":"不过，Millie 认为现在出现了针对“自我优化类内容”的抵制潮（pushback）。pushback 意为对某种趋势的强烈批评，人们通过反其道而行之来表达抵制，比如很多名人发帖展示自己喝酒抽烟。","s":290.56,"e":306.28,"wt":[0,19,64,103,142,174,193,248,279,420,466,508,580,586,640,696,712,734,780,804,845,865,913,940,953,1002,1014,1094,1116,1181,1202,1224,1290,1326,1348,1382,1424,1450]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, Georgie, I've decided to try to give up alcohol for a whole year, but I don't think everybody should do that if they don't want to. It's really up to the individual.","zh":"嗯，Georgie，我已经决定尝试整整一年不喝酒，但如果别人不想这么做，我也绝不认为每个人都该照做。这完全取决于个人选择。","s":306.56,"e":316.04,"wt":[0,18,91,117,165,178,198,211,238,253,299,318,325,359,399,419,425,472,550,587,610,632,650,664,678,700,722,740,766,821,832,843,860]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yeah, I still enjoy having a few drinks with my friends. I think it's just all about balance, really. OK. Neil, it's time to reveal the answer to this week's question. I asked, \"What was the name given for when people give up alcohol in January?\"","zh":"是啊，我还是挺喜欢和朋友小酌几杯的。我觉得归根结底在于把握平衡。好了，Neil，现在该揭晓本周竞猜题的答案了。我问的是：“人们在一月份戒酒的挑战叫什么名字？”","s":316.06,"e":331.08,"wt":[0,26,32,62,92,128,134,152,188,212,224,284,290,320,344,368,386,424,477,558,582,626,658,678,694,730,748,786,810,835,872,942,955,1025,1068,1083,1108,1141,1182,1207,1240,1276,1276,1328,1397,1413]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Yes. And the answer is Dry January.","zh":"是的，答案就是 Dry January（戒酒一月）。","s":331.08,"e":334.62,"wt":[0,47,75,100,164,235,258]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"That is correct. Well done! Let's recap the vocabulary we learned in this programme, starting with sober , which describes someone who doesn't drink alcohol or take recreational drugs.","zh":"完全正确，答得好！我们来回顾一下本期学到的词汇，首先是 sober（戒酒的/清醒的），用来形容不饮酒或不吸食娱乐性药物的人。","s":334.8,"e":345.84,"wt":[0,18,31,78,100,140,173,208,224,280,295,348,364,391,466,508,536,564,585,620,683,732,753,798,837,886,900,928,1032]},{"speaker":"Societal pressure","en":"is the feeling that you should do something because lots of other people expect you to.","zh":"（社会压力）是指因为周围很多人期望你这么做，而让你觉得自己应该去做的那种感觉。","s":347.14,"e":352.24,"wt":[0,8,24,61,82,102,138,152,215,264,292,305,359,405,446,450]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Saying something is the norm is a way to say it's usual or common.","zh":"说某件事是 the norm（常态），意思是这件事很平常、很普遍。","s":352.6,"e":356.44,"wt":[0,41,74,88,116,152,164,170,188,206,252,268,329,356]},{"speaker":"Self-optimisation content","en":"means videos and posts about being fit, healthy and achieving big goals.","zh":"（自我优化类内容）指关于保持健康体魄、追求养生以及实现宏大目标的视频和帖子。","s":359.14,"e":364.84,"wt":[0,39,90,108,162,194,238,287,354,402,464,490]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"If somebody touts an idea or lifestyle, they talk about it a lot and try to convince people to do it.","zh":"如果某人 tout（大力吹捧）某种理念或生活方式，意思是他们反复宣扬并试图说服大家去效仿。","s":364.98,"e":371.42,"wt":[0,17,78,116,138,170,186,278,296,314,348,361,367,387,407,429,444,517,556,574,594]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"And pushback is strong criticism of a trend, and people doing the opposite.","zh":"而 pushback（反弹/抵制）是指对某种趋势的强烈批评，以及人们反其道而行之的行为。","s":371.68,"e":377.32,"wt":[0,28,102,120,173,248,252,278,328,344,379,436,484]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Once again, our six minutes are up, but don't forget to test what you've learned with the worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"我们的六分钟时间又到了，别忘了去我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 下载学习单，检验一下今天学到的知识。","s":377.54,"e":385.22,"wt":[0,27,60,74,88,122,136,155,169,192,221,231,250,269,297,331,350,395,452,467,490,560]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Thank you for joining us and goodbye.","zh":"感谢收听，再见。","s":385.22,"e":386.94,"wt":[0,34,40,66,76,98,114]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Goodbye!","zh":"再见！","s":386.94,"e":387.39,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0pfllv6.mp3"},{"epNum":8,"id":"ep-261001","date":"2026-10-01","topic":"心理社交","title":"Why does heartbreak hurt so much?","titleZh":"为什么心碎会这么痛？","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-261001","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/261001_6_minute_english_why_does_heartbreak_hurt_so_much_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/261001_6_minute_english_why_does_heartbreak_hurt_so_much_transcript.pdf","summary":"Anyone who has had their heart broken will know how horrible it feels. But did you know that the pain you feel is not just imaginary – it registers in the same part of the brain that processes physical pain. So, why do we feel this pain? And if it feels so bad, what is the point of being in love in the first place? Neil and Georgie discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","summaryZh":"经历过心碎的人都知道那有多难受。但你知道吗？你感受到的痛苦绝非凭空想象——它激活的正是大脑中处理身体疼痛的同一区域。那么，我们为什么会感到这种痛？既然失恋这么痛苦，人最初为什么要坠入爱河呢？Neil 和 Georgie 将探讨这一话题，并教你几个实用的新词汇。","question":"Which theory, developed by psychologists in the 1950s, describes a link between children's early bonds with their parents and their romantic relationships as adults?","questionZh":"20世纪50年代心理学家提出了一项理论，指出儿童早期与父母的情感纽带会影响成年后的恋爱关系，这项理论叫什么？","options":["a) contraction theory","b) attachment theory","c) superstition theory"],"optionsZh":["a) 收缩理论（contraction theory）","b) 依恋理论（attachment theory）","c) 迷信理论（superstition theory）"],"answer":{"letter":"b","explanationZh":"节目结尾 Neil 确认 Georgie 很有把握选出的 b) 依恋理论（attachment theory）正是正确答案。"},"vocab":[{"word":"heartbreak","defEn":"feeling of intense, painful sadness after the end of a relationship","pos":"n.","zh":"心碎；失恋之痛","exampleEn":"Now, heartbreak is the feeling of sadness we get when we lose someone we love.","ipa":"/ˈhɑːtbreɪk/"},{"word":"addicted to","defEn":"unable to cut a connection with someone or something because you depend on it or want more of it all the time","pos":"短语","zh":"对……上瘾；极度迷恋/依赖","exampleEn":"Nature gave us these systems to be addicted to each other – for reproduction, for protection.","ipa":"/əˈdɪktɪd tuː/"},{"word":"orientated/oriented towards","defEn":"focused on someone, so that you think about them before others","pos":"短语","zh":"以……为重心；全心朝向某人","exampleEn":"You're oriented completely toward the other person.","ipa":"/ˈɔːriənteɪtɪd təˈwɔːdz/"},{"word":"social bonds","defEn":"close relationships between family, friends and romantic partners","pos":"n. 短语","zh":"社会纽带；亲密的人际关系","exampleEn":"Having really strong social bonds increases your likelihood that you'll survive from just about anything","ipa":"/ˈsəʊʃəl bɒndz/"},{"word":"longevity","defEn":"length of time you will live","pos":"n.","zh":"长寿；寿命长度","exampleEn":"And it overall is the number one predictor of longevity .","ipa":"/lɒnˈdʒevəti/"},{"word":"ex","defEn":"previous romantic partner","pos":"n.","zh":"前任（前男友/前女友/前伴侣）","exampleEn":"Well, scientists have been looking at this by asking people to look at photos of their exes , their former partners, and scanning their brains.","ipa":"/eks/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"light up","zh":"（脑部扫描中的区域）亮起、被激活（脑科学和心理类科普高频地道用法）","contextEn":"People were shown statements about themselves – for example, Neil is kind – and the part of the brain we use to think about ourselves lit up."},{"en":"come with","zh":"伴随……而来（比 be caused by / accompany 更自然的口语与写作表达）","contextEn":"So, Neil, are all of these brain changes that come with being in love actually good for us?"},{"en":"equivalent to","zh":"等同于；相当于（健康、数据对比类雅思阅读与写作高频搭配）","contextEn":"So, if you are lonely and you don't have bonds , then your mortality risk goes up in a way that's equivalent to being obese or smoking."},{"en":"overlap with","zh":"与……重叠、交叉（精准表达脑区、时间或观点重合的动词短语）","contextEn":"So, if you look at the parts of the brain that light up, they overlap with the same brain regions that are activated when you feel visceral pain – when you feel actual, you know... like you get a burn, or you break a... you break a bone."}],"sentences":[{"en":"And they found that looking at someone you're in love with activates a part of the brain that also reminds us to eat and drink.","zh":"他们发现，注视着你爱的人会激活大脑中同样负责提醒我们吃饭喝水的那块区域。","coreEn":"They found that looking at someone activates a part of the brain.","coreZh":"他们发现，看着某人会激活大脑的某个区域。","cars":[{"text":"you're in love with","hook":"省略关系词的定语从句","noteZh":"紧贴在 someone 后面，限定是“你爱着的”那个人"},{"text":"that also reminds us to eat and drink","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"补充说明这块脑区原本的生理功能——提醒我们进食和饮水"}],"tipZh":"看到 looking at someone you're in love with activates... 别被一串短词绕晕：整个 -ing 动名词短语在做从句主语，找到谓语动词 activates 就能瞬间一分为二。"},{"en":"When showing the same statements about their partner, the same part of the brain was active – not the part of the brain we generally use to think about others.","zh":"当向他们展示关于伴侣的同样描述时，活跃的依然是同一块脑区——而不是我们通常用来思考他人的那块脑区。","coreEn":"The same part of the brain was active.","coreZh":"同一块脑区处于活跃状态。","cars":[{"text":"When showing the same statements about their partner","hook":"-ing 修饰 / 时间状语","noteZh":"交代实验条件：展示关于伴侣的相同语句时"},{"text":"– not the part of the brain we generally use to think about others","hook":"破折号补充说明 + 省略关系词定语","noteZh":"反向对比，点出真正令人惊讶之处——大脑把伴侣归入了“自己人”而非“他人”的区域"}],"tipZh":"雅思心理学阅读题极爱考实验对照结果，破折号后的 not ... 部分往往直接点破研究的核心反转结论。"},{"en":"Having really strong social bonds increases your likelihood that you'll survive from just about anything, whether that be cancer, depression, etc.","zh":"拥有非常牢固的社会情感纽带，会提高你从几乎任何困境中挺过来的几率，无论是癌症、抑郁症还是其他疾病。","coreEn":"Having strong social bonds increases your likelihood.","coreZh":"拥有牢固的社交纽带会提高你的几率。","cars":[{"text":"that you'll survive from just about anything","hook":"同位语从句 that","noteZh":"紧跟 likelihood，说明具体是哪方面的几率（几乎熬过一切病痛的存活率）"},{"text":"whether that be cancer, depression, etc.","hook":"让步补充从句 whether","noteZh":"举例涵盖范围，说明不论癌症还是抑郁症都包含在内"}],"tipZh":"whether that be A, B, etc. 是英语访谈和学术演讲中很地道的“无论A还是B”列举句式，其中的 be 用原形虚拟语气，听读时记住即可。"},{"en":"So, if you look at the parts of the brain that light up, they overlap with the same brain regions that are activated when you feel visceral pain – when you feel actual, you know... like you get a burn, or you break a... you break a bone.","zh":"所以，如果你观察大脑中亮起的那些区域，它们与你感到切肤剧痛时被激活的脑区是重合的——也就是当你真真切切感到……比如被烫伤，或者摔断骨头时的那种痛。","coreEn":"They overlap with the same brain regions.","coreZh":"它们与同一批大脑区域重叠。","cars":[{"text":"if you look at the parts of the brain that light up","hook":"状语从句 if","noteZh":"引出观察对象：失恋者大脑中被激活亮起的区域"},{"text":"that are activated when you feel visceral pain","hook":"关系词 that + 状语从句 when","noteZh":"说明另一组脑区的属性：感到内脏/切肤剧痛时被激活的区域"},{"text":"– when you feel actual, you know... like you get a burn, or you break a... you break a bone","hook":"破折号解释说明","noteZh":"用大白话例子（烫伤、骨折）把专业词 visceral pain 翻译得通俗易懂"}],"tipZh":"遇到生僻学术词（如 visceral pain）别慌，BBC 嘉宾和雅思听力常在后面用破折号、or 或 like 给出生活化的通俗解释，顺着往后听就能猜出词义。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Hi Georgie.","zh":"嗨，Georgie。","s":4.49,"e":5.4,"wt":[0,14]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Hi Neil.","zh":"嗨，Neil。","s":7.77,"e":11.13,"wt":[0,84]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Now, I want you to think back to the last time you had your heart broken. How did it feel?","zh":"现在，我想请你回想一下上一次心碎的经历。那是什么感觉？","s":11.13,"e":19.26,"wt":[0,164,227,287,299,329,356,427,466,485,509,539,557,579,608,656,707,731,749,771]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"It was a couple of years ago. And I just remember a really intense, sad emotion. But it also sometimes feels like a physical pain in your stomach, doesn't it?","zh":"那是几年前的事了。我只记得一种极其强烈的悲伤情绪。而且有时候肚子也会像真的在隐隐作痛一样，对吧？","s":19.5,"e":28.85,"wt":[0,6,16,38,38,48,84,166,174,188,214,228,278,302,386,457,530,542,558,589,654,698,730,738,790,822,826,843,885,910]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"It does. It's like real pain, isn't it? And today, in 6 Minute English, we're talking about heartbreak . Now, heartbreak is the feeling of sadness we get when we lose someone we love. And that pain you feel, it's not just imaginary. In this podcast, we'll be understanding how our brains change when we fall in love and asking the question, why does heartbreak hurt so much?","zh":"确实如此，就像真实的身体疼痛一样，不是吗？今天在《六分钟英语》里，我们要聊的就是心碎（heartbreak）。心碎是指失去所爱之人时感到的极度悲伤。而且你感受到的那种痛，并不只是心理想象。在本期播客中，我们将了解坠入爱河时大脑会发生怎样的变化，并探讨一个问题：为什么心碎会这么痛？","s":28.9,"e":52.26,"wt":[0,15,36,36,90,140,166,166,266,274,301,312,328,361,408,433,470,500,560,602,616,690,707,727,784,796,849,863,898,916,938,975,990,1030,1094,1108,1137,1166,1188,1234,1276,1302,1337,1462,1466,1490,1546,1566,1578,1652,1671,1698,1765,1806,1834,1858,1882,1902,1952,1964,2000,2019,2089,2112,2140,2180,2232,2284]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"And as usual, we'll be learning some useful new vocabulary. You can find the full list and the definitions at bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"像往常一样，我们也会学习一些实用的新词汇。你可以在 bbclearningenglish.com 上找到完整词汇表和释义。","s":52.6,"e":61.16,"wt":[0,24,40,105,120,128,172,193,237,259,364,376,395,422,443,477,499,516,546,626,640]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Georgie, we're going to start with a quiz question. Are you ready?","zh":"Georgie，我们先从竞猜题开始，准备好了吗？","s":61.52,"e":64.36,"wt":[0,32,50,62,80,105,124,130,154,226,238,256]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"I am very ready.","zh":"完全准备好了。","s":64.54,"e":66.08,"wt":[0,22,41,82]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"So, in the 1950s, psychologists noticed a link between children's early bonds with their parents and their romantic relationships as adults, but what is this theory called? Is it: a) contraction theory, b) attachment theory, or c) superstition theory?","zh":"20世纪50年代，心理学家注意到儿童早期与父母的情感纽带，和他们成年后的恋爱关系之间存在联系。这项理论叫什么名字？选项是：a) 收缩理论（contraction theory），b) 依恋理论（attachment theory），还是 c) 迷信理论（superstition theory）？","s":66.28,"e":85.22,"wt":[0,12,25,45,152,272,334,346,408,447,516,548,586,606,646,708,724,760,827,862,938,1000,1020,1052,1068,1100,1147,1228,1240,1260,1290,1410,1484,1566,1638,1696,1704,1774,1831]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"I think it's b) attachment theory.","zh":"我觉得是 b) 依恋理论（attachment theory）。","s":85.4,"e":87.82,"wt":[0,6,45,75,97,182]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK. Well, we will find out at the end of the programme.","zh":"好，我们会在节目最后揭晓答案。","s":88,"e":91.2,"wt":[0,35,62,80,108,130,156,176,188,198,204,238]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"What's going on in our brains when we fall in love?","zh":"当我们坠入爱河时，大脑里究竟发生了什么？","s":91.4,"e":94.54,"wt":[0,40,66,90,104,141,192,210,232,248,270]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, scientists have scanned people's brains while they're looking at a photo of their romantic partner. And they found that looking at someone you're in love with activates a part of the brain that also reminds us to eat and drink.","zh":"科学家在受试者看着恋爱伴侣照片时扫描了他们的大脑。结果发现，注视着你爱的人会激活大脑中同样负责提醒我们吃饭喝水的那块区域。","s":94.76,"e":109.16,"wt":[0,35,98,123,167,217,268,288,318,326,336,396,444,456,490,544,624,634,659,686,715,760,774,824,846,860,880,932,990,999,1029,1045,1067,1110,1136,1172,1224,1256,1272,1315,1344]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"And do scientists know why being in love impacts our brain in this way?","zh":"科学家知道为什么谈恋爱会以这种方式影响大脑吗？","s":109.24,"e":113.52,"wt":[0,17,30,95,124,182,220,240,280,315,339,364,376,400]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"So, it's all about survival. Our brains have evolved to be this way. Dr Lucy Brown explains more to the BBC World Service.","zh":"这一切都关乎生存，我们的大脑就是这样进化出来的。Lucy Brown 博士向 BBC 国际频道做了进一步解释。","s":113.72,"e":122.6,"wt":[0,7,37,60,113,190,208,254,289,330,330,366,386,470,498,531,562,618,654,664,682,733,772]},{"speaker":"Dr Lucy Brown","en":"This is a survival system. Nature gave us these systems to be addicted to each other – for reproduction, for protection. You're oriented completely toward the other person. It's like hunger or thirst.","zh":"这是一套生存系统。大自然赋予我们这些机制，让我们彼此“上瘾（addicted to）”——为了繁衍，也为了互相保护。你的注意力会完全朝向对方（oriented completely toward），这就像饥饿或口渴一样本能。","s":122.8,"e":139.08,"wt":[0,14,30,40,89,156,228,274,298,360,467,482,502,554,568,604,639,664,689,846,878,1000,1039,1113,1182,1229,1253,1293,1348,1376,1418,1498,1520]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Lucy says our brains make us addicted to our romantic partners. If you're addicted to something, you want more of it. You can literally be addicted to things like drugs or alcohol, but we also use it metaphorically to talk about things we love.","zh":"Lucy 说我们的大脑会让我们对伴侣“上瘾（addicted to）”。如果你对某件事上瘾，你就会一直想要更多。字面上你可以对毒品或酒精上瘾，但我们也常常用它来比喻我们深爱的事物。","s":139.08,"e":154.06,"wt":[0,31,64,88,136,176,184,256,267,290,352,444,445,473,509,519,572,595,626,657,672,720,730,748,804,816,868,880,916,940,996,1011,1116,1132,1151,1179,1204,1214,1322,1330,1358,1396,1422,1449]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"She says that romantic partners are oriented to each other, which in British English we say orientated , and it means they are pointed towards each other. They think of each other before other people.","zh":"她说恋爱伴侣彼此“以对方为重心（oriented to）”，在英式英语中我们常说 orientated，意思就是两人目光朝向彼此，遇事总是先想到对方再想到别人。","s":154.16,"e":165.96,"wt":[0,16,40,64,113,164,184,256,262,290,340,371,383,426,472,492,520,602,640,652,665,699,729,753,792,839,866,930,950,974,986,1008,1060,1096,1132]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Neil, is there anything else scientists have discovered from scanning the brains of people in love?","zh":"Neil，科学家通过扫描热恋人群的大脑，还有别的发现吗？","s":166.32,"e":171.52,"wt":[0,23,31,59,91,113,184,196,284,305,350,370,402,420,434,478]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, being in love doesn't just change the way we think. It changes our whole identity. A 2013 study showed us more about this. People were shown statements about themselves – for example, Neil is kind – and the part of the brain we use to think about ourselves lit up. When showing the same statements about their partner, the same part of the brain was active – not the part of the brain we generally use to think about others.","zh":"嗯，恋爱不仅改变我们的思维方式，还会重塑我们的整个自我认同。2013年的一项研究进一步揭示了这一点。研究人员向受试者展示关于他们自己的描述——比如“Neil 很善良”——这时大脑中用来思考自我的区域亮了起来。而当展示关于他们伴侣的同样描述时，活跃的居然还是同一块脑区，而不是我们通常用来思考他人的脑区。","s":171.92,"e":201.66,"wt":[0,37,62,82,113,155,184,216,232,248,272,340,354,426,460,524,656,660,757,797,845,861,893,926,1000,1031,1059,1089,1153,1185,1236,1260,1284,1348,1380,1408,1470,1524,1533,1550,1573,1584,1601,1632,1646,1668,1681,1716,1747,1832,1879,1980,2000,2039,2056,2093,2162,2191,2227,2284,2298,2328,2358,2373,2397,2428,2454,2501,2532,2547,2569,2604,2614,2636,2680,2694,2760,2790,2803,2840,2860]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"So, Neil, are all of these brain changes that come with being in love actually good for us?","zh":"那么 Neil，伴随恋爱而来的所有这些大脑变化，真的对我们有好处吗？","s":201.84,"e":207.04,"wt":[0,24,64,84,108,117,150,183,242,262,288,310,332,352,386,436,458,482]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, studies suggest that good social connections help manage stress and support our immune system. Professor Zoe Donaldson explained more to the BBC World Service.","zh":"研究表明，良好的人际联结有助于调节压力并增强我们的免疫系统。Zoe Donaldson 教授在 BBC 国际频道上做了更详细的解释。","s":207.16,"e":217.96,"wt":[0,47,106,160,182,208,241,320,345,394,444,466,508,534,583,660,719,736,800,848,880,891,912,952,992]},{"speaker":"Professor Zoe Donaldson","en":"Having really strong social bonds increases your likelihood that you'll survive from just about anything, whether that be cancer, depression, etc. And it overall is the number one predictor of longevity . So, if you are lonely and you don't have bonds , then your mortality risk goes up in a way that's equivalent to being obese or smoking.","zh":"拥有非常牢固的社会情感纽带（social bonds），能提高你从几乎任何困境中挺过来的几率，不管是癌症、抑郁症还是其他疾病。总体而言，它是预测长寿（longevity）的头号指标。所以如果你感到孤独、缺乏亲密纽带，你的死亡风险就会上升，其危害程度等同于肥胖或吸烟。","s":218.24,"e":238.54,"wt":[0,39,78,108,148,200,248,260,308,318,342,370,394,417,439,488,510,510,558,629,729,804,825,844,911,931,960,1019,1052,1100,1100,1179,1240,1256,1268,1287,1305,1345,1363,1378,1404,1430,1461,1484,1510,1556,1630,1680,1704,1744,1756,1762,1784,1814,1830,1846,1876,1938,1966]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"So, having strong social bonds or close relationships with other people makes you more likely to survive disease, according to Zoe.","zh":"也就是说，根据 Zoe 的说法，拥有牢固的社会纽带（social bonds），也就是与他人建立亲密关系，会让你更有可能战胜疾病。","s":238.64,"e":246.82,"wt":[0,31,79,128,180,220,233,268,346,364,410,460,494,514,541,588,599,651,705,760,774]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Yes, it's an important factor in our longevity , which means how long we live. In fact, Zoe says, being lonely and not having good social bonds increases your mortality risk, your risk of dying, by as much as being obese or smoking.","zh":"没错，这是影响我们长寿（longevity，即寿命长短）的一个重要因素。事实上，Zoe 说，孤独以及缺乏良好的人际纽带会让死亡风险大幅上升，程度堪比肥胖或吸烟。","s":247.04,"e":262.06,"wt":[0,30,58,72,127,174,185,211,269,298,320,354,374,394,408,482,496,540,569,602,643,700,716,735,776,801,840,884,949,981,1054,1120,1146,1160,1182,1222,1238,1254,1256,1298,1348,1392,1412]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK. So, what happens when these bonds are broken, when we experience heartbreak ?","zh":"明白。那么当这些纽带断裂、我们经历心碎（heartbreak）时，又会发生什么呢？","s":262.24,"e":266.64,"wt":[0,24,45,72,113,128,154,182,210,278,286,305,359,406]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, scientists have been looking at this by asking people to look at photos of their exes , their former partners, and scanning their brains. They found that heartbreak activates parts of the brain we use when we're in danger or in pain. Professor Zoe Donaldson explains more.","zh":"科学家通过让受试者观看前任（exes，也就是过去伴侣）的照片并扫描他们的大脑，研究了这个问题。他们发现，心碎会激活我们在身处危险或遭受疼痛时才会用到的脑区。Zoe Donaldson 教授给出了进一步说明。","s":266.64,"e":284.12,"wt":[0,28,80,105,123,154,170,200,212,263,310,326,358,386,427,451,492,524,552,576,614,680,703,756,786,878,896,926,952,1015,1076,1110,1118,1137,1166,1182,1209,1247,1272,1311,1344,1362,1384,1484,1538,1574,1636,1660]},{"speaker":"Professor Zoe Donaldson","en":"It's literal pain in your brain. So, if you look at the parts of the brain that light up, they overlap with the same brain regions that are activated when you feel visceral pain – when you feel actual, you know... like you get a burn, or you break a... you break a bone.","zh":"这是大脑中实打实的疼痛。如果你观察大脑中亮起的那些区域，它们与你感到切肤剧痛时被激活的脑区是重合的——也就是当你真真切切感受到，比如被烫伤，或者摔断骨头时的那种痛。","s":284.2,"e":298.48,"wt":[0,32,72,113,121,147,216,228,238,257,278,288,306,334,344,361,384,411,439,460,491,551,568,596,604,674,758,768,801,842,842,890,920,983,1001,1016,1028,1054,1072,1094,1104,1127,1140,1162,1172,1198,1226,1226,1227,1227,1228,1270,1316,1366]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK. So, now it's time for the answer to the quiz question I asked you. It was about a theory developed by psychologists in the 1950s, which says that children's early relationships with their parents impact their relationships as adults. What's the name of the theory?","zh":"好了，现在该揭晓我之前问你的竞猜题答案了。题目问的是20世纪50年代心理学家提出的一项理论，该理论认为儿童早期与父母的关系会影响成年后的亲密关系。这项理论叫什么名字？","s":298.84,"e":316.52,"wt":[0,48,64,88,120,156,200,217,256,266,284,321,364,389,414,484,494,515,550,560,601,668,676,760,771,776,902,922,946,971,1029,1058,1140,1158,1200,1285,1338,1384,1436,1504,1616,1642,1658,1672,1682,1704]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"I was quite confident about this, wasn't I, Neil? I said attachment theory.","zh":"我对这道题挺有信心的，不是吗，Neil？我答的是依恋理论（attachment theory）。","s":316.68,"e":320.92,"wt":[0,2,19,48,105,129,162,188,200,238,240,284,352]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, you had a reason to be confident because you are correct.","zh":"嗯，你确实有理由自信，因为你答对了。","s":321.08,"e":324.46,"wt":[0,37,59,78,84,124,137,150,224,246,264,286]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yay! OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we learnt in this programme, starting with heartbreak , which is the feeling you experience after you lose someone you love.","zh":"太棒了！好，我们来回顾一下本期学到的词汇，首先是 heartbreak（心碎），指你失去深爱之人后所感受到的剧烈悲伤。","s":324.52,"e":334.2,"wt":[0,44,90,128,224,224,272,284,332,341,366,423,474,499,550,574,588,602,634,662,694,766,797,818,848,890,908]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"If you're addicted to someone or something, you want more of it all the time.","zh":"如果你对某人或某事 addicted to（上瘾），意思就是你时刻都渴望得到更多。","s":334.36,"e":338.72,"wt":[0,8,40,84,95,134,147,206,224,254,278,288,320,336,364]},{"speaker":"Oriented","en":"or orientated towards is used metaphorically to mean that someone or something is your main focus.","zh":"（oriented 或 orientated towards）在比喻义上表示某人或某事是你的核心关注点。","s":339.6,"e":346.74,"wt":[0,25,128,184,198,228,340,349,383,417,468,494,546,546,568,636]},{"speaker":"Social bonds","en":"are the close relationships we have with family, friends and romantic partners.","zh":"（社会纽带）指我们与家人、朋友以及恋爱伴侣之间的亲密关系。","s":347.72,"e":352.44,"wt":[0,19,41,72,163,176,202,228,278,320,344,398]},{"speaker":"Longevity","en":"means how long you'll live.","zh":"（长寿/寿命）指一个人能活多长时间。","s":353.44,"e":355.24,"wt":[0,42,66,92,102]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"And an ex is a past romantic partner that you are no longer in a relationship with.","zh":"而 ex（前任）指已经分手的过去恋爱伴侣。","s":355.36,"e":360.68,"wt":[0,10,34,59,76,85,123,200,276,292,306,330,352,392,402,415,492]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Once again, our six minutes are up, but you can test what you've learned with the worksheet on our website. Go to bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"我们的六分钟时间又到了，你可以用我们网站上的学习单来检验所学内容，请访问 bbclearningenglish.com。","s":360.88,"e":368.32,"wt":[0,22,58,80,105,136,148,182,191,206,221,243,261,292,330,348,366,412,426,449,500,513,534]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Thanks for joining us and goodbye.","zh":"感谢收听，再见。","s":368.82,"e":370.64,"wt":[0,20,36,70,96,114]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Goodbye.","zh":"再见！","s":370.64,"e":371.24,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0pdc7mz.mp3"},{"epNum":9,"id":"ep-260924","date":"2026-09-24","topic":"健康医学","title":"Why do we itch?","titleZh":"我们为什么会发痒？","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260924","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260924_6_minute_english_why_do_we_itch_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260924_6_minute_english_why_do_we_itch_transcript.pdf","summary":"Have you ever had an itch that you really wanted to scratch, but couldn't? Feeling itchy is unpleasant, and the feeling of finally scratching that itch can be very satisfying! But why do we feel itchy in the first place? And is it possible to catch an itch from someone else? Neil and Georgie discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","summaryZh":"你有没有过那种非常想挠却又不能挠的痒？发痒的感觉很不舒服，而终于挠到痒处的那一刻又格外爽快！可我们一开始为什么会发痒呢？“痒”真的会像感冒一样传染给别人吗？Neil 和 Georgie 将探讨这一话题，并教你几个实用的新词汇。","question":"Listen to the recording of scratching on the programme (at 1:38). What is being scratched?","questionZh":"听节目中（1分38秒处）的一段抓挠录音。录音里正在挠的是什么？","options":["a) an arm","b) a beard","c) some jeans"],"optionsZh":["a) 一条手臂","b) 胡子","c) 牛仔裤"],"answer":{"letter":"b","explanationZh":"节目结尾专家 Henning Holle 揭晓，录音里挠的确实是有毛发的皮肤——具体来说是胡子（a beard），Georgie 猜对了。"},"vocab":[{"word":"itch / itchy","defEn":"unpleasant feeling on your skin / feeling something unpleasant on your skin","pos":"n./v. ; adj.","zh":"发痒、痒感 / 发痒的、让人发痒的","exampleEn":"Now an itch is that unpleasant feeling you get on your skin that you have to scratch with your fingers.","ipa":"/ɪtʃ/ · /ˈɪtʃi/"},{"word":"scratch","defEn":"rub your nails back and forth against something, often to relieve an itch","pos":"v./n.","zh":"挠；抓（痒）","exampleEn":"When you feel an itch , you often scratch it with your fingers by rubbing your nails on the skin.","ipa":"/skrætʃ/"},{"word":"unpack","defEn":"explain in clear detail","pos":"v.","zh":"剖析；详细拆解说明","exampleEn":"There's lots of science to unpack here.","ipa":"/ʌnˈpæk/"},{"word":"parasite","defEn":"animal, plant or fungus that lives on another lifeform and feeds on it","pos":"n.","zh":"寄生虫；寄生物","exampleEn":"by scratching the skin, we remove parasites or plants or anything that does not belong on the skin.","ipa":"/ˈpærəsaɪt/"},{"word":"spontaneously","defEn":"without being asked; without thinking","pos":"adv.","zh":"自发地；不由自主地","exampleEn":"They also started to scratch themselves more spontaneously .","ipa":"/spɒnˈteɪniəsli/"},{"word":"catch (an illness/feeling)","defEn":"get an illness or feeling from someone else","pos":"v.","zh":"染上；被传染（疾病或某种感觉）","exampleEn":"So, the answer is basically yes, you can catch a scratch .","ipa":"/kætʃ/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"funny you should ask","zh":"说来真巧 / 你这么一问正好（极地道的英式口语搭话，对方正好问到你此刻的切身经历）","contextEn":"Well, funny you should ask, because I'm actually wearing quite an itchy jumper at the moment and it's making me want to scratch my skin."},{"en":"for no particular reason","zh":"无缘无故地；没什么特别原因（雅思口语与日常交流高频自然表达）","contextEn":"So, Neil, I mentioned that my jumper was a bit itchy but sometimes I feel itchy for no particular reason."},{"en":"get rid of","zh":"摆脱；清除掉（比 remove 更地道灵活的高频动词短语）","contextEn":"Sonja explains that when our skin feels itchy , we scratch it, and that helps to get rid of parasites and other harmful things on our skin."},{"en":"spot on","zh":"完全准确；一针见血（英式口语常用夸赞，表示猜得或说得非常精准）","contextEn":"It is definitely hairy skin and Anand was spot on."}],"sentences":[{"en":"Now an itch is that unpleasant feeling you get on your skin that you have to scratch with your fingers.","zh":"所谓“痒”，就是你皮肤上产生的那种必须用手指去挠的不舒服的感觉。","coreEn":"An itch is that unpleasant feeling.","coreZh":"痒就是那种不舒服的感觉。","cars":[{"text":"you get on your skin","hook":"省略关系词的定语从句","noteZh":"第一重限定：说明这种感觉发生在你皮肤上"},{"text":"that you have to scratch with your fingers","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"第二重限定：进一步说明这种感觉会迫使你用手指去抓挠"}],"tipZh":"给一个词下定义时，英语常在核心名词（feeling）后连续挂两节修饰车厢，阅读时顺着修饰同一个名词理解即可，不要把第二个 that 误当成修饰 skin。"},{"en":"Well, recently I was bitten by a mosquito and, as you know, when you get bitten by a mosquito, it's really itchy .","zh":"嗯，最近我被蚊子咬了一口，而且你知道的，被蚊子咬了之后真的特别痒。","coreEn":"Recently I was bitten by a mosquito and it's really itchy.","coreZh":"最近我被蚊子咬了，而且真的很痒。","cars":[{"text":", as you know,","hook":"逗号插入语","noteZh":"拉近听众共鸣的口语插入语，意思是“你也知道的”"},{"text":"when you get bitten by a mosquito","hook":"状语从句 when","noteZh":"补充说明发痒触发的条件情景，用 get bitten 替代 be bitten 更显口语自然"}],"tipZh":"雅思口语讲个人经历时，用 and, as you know, when ... 这种自然衔接，能让叙述显得从容又地道。"},{"en":"Sonja explains that when our skin feels itchy , we scratch it, and that helps to get rid of parasites and other harmful things on our skin.","zh":"Sonja 解释说，当皮肤发痒时我们会去挠它，而这一动作有助于清除皮肤上的寄生虫和其他有害物质。","coreEn":"Sonja explains that we scratch it, and that helps to get rid of parasites and other harmful things.","coreZh":"Sonja 解释说我们会去挠皮肤，而这有助于清除寄生虫和其他有害物。","cars":[{"text":"when our skin feels itchy ,","hook":"状语从句 when","noteZh":"夹在 explains that 后面，先给出触发条件（皮肤发痒时）"},{"text":"to get rid of parasites and other harmful things on our skin","hook":"不定式 to do + 介词短语","noteZh":"说明挠痒这个动作带来的实际防御效果和作用部位"}],"tipZh":"宾语从句里常会再嵌套一个 when/if 条件从句（explains that when A, B），抓住主干逻辑“发痒→抓挠→清害物”就能轻松跟上语速。"},{"en":"Well, that's interesting because Dr Henning Holle wanted to study whether just thinking about itching makes us more likely to feel itchy .","zh":"嗯，这很有意思，因为 Henning Holle 博士想要研究：仅仅是想到“发痒”这件事，是否就会让我们更容易觉得痒。","coreEn":"That's interesting because Dr Henning Holle wanted to study whether something makes us more likely to feel itchy.","coreZh":"这很有意思，因为 Henning Holle 博士想研究某件事是否让我们更容易发痒。","cars":[{"text":"whether just thinking about itching makes us more likely to feel itchy","hook":"宾语从句 whether + -ing 作主语","noteZh":"完整交代研究课题：“仅仅想着发痒”作为主语，带动动词 makes"},{"text":"to feel itchy","hook":"不定式 to do","noteZh":"接在 more likely 后构成高频搭配 be likely to do（更有可能做某事）"}],"tipZh":"遇到 study/investigate whether + 动名词短语 + 单数动词（makes），一定要把 just thinking about itching 整体打包当成主语来看，这是科普科研类雅思阅读的经典句式。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Hello and welcome to 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Neil.","zh":"大家好，欢迎收听 BBC 英语教学的《六分钟英语》。我是 Neil。","s":0.24,"e":4.86,"wt":[0,11,32,81,102,116,158,219,249,267,336,384,411]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"And I'm Georgie. Find the vocabulary, definitions and a free worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"我是 Georgie。欢迎访问我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com，获取本期词汇、释义及免费学习单。","s":4.96,"e":12.88,"wt":[0,1,25,112,142,152,224,312,352,368,400,464,472,496,562]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"So, Georgie we're going to talk about something a little different today. We're talking about itching . Now an itch is that unpleasant feeling you get on your skin that you have to scratch with your fingers. What sort of things make you itch ?","zh":"那么 Georgie，今天我们要聊一个有点特别的话题——发痒（itching）。所谓“痒（itch）”，就是皮肤上产生的那种不得不拿手指去挠（scratch）的不舒服感觉。哪些东西会让你觉得痒？","s":14.24,"e":31.28,"wt":[0,0,76,94,110,134,162,206,234,246,246,315,384,408,440,520,566,608,634,654,697,714,814,854,928,959,984,998,1042,1099,1128,1132,1152,1185,1262,1271,1302,1379,1410,1441,1456,1502,1533,1556,1620]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Well, funny you should ask, because I'm actually wearing quite an itchy jumper at the moment and it's making me want to scratch my skin. What about you?","zh":"说来真巧，我现在身上正穿着一件挺扎人发痒（itchy）的毛衣，弄得我一直想挠皮肤。你呢？","s":31.28,"e":39.8,"wt":[0,56,76,90,90,162,165,182,256,315,336,352,392,432,440,448,516,520,542,582,595,622,634,680,693,744,771,811]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, recently I was bitten by a mosquito and, as you know, when you get bitten by a mosquito, it's really itchy . I had to scratch loads and loads.","zh":"嗯，最近我被蚊子咬了一口，你也知道，被蚊子咬了之后真的奇痒无比（itchy），我忍不住挠了好多好多下。","s":40,"e":50.99,"wt":[0,72,112,123,157,225,249,258,442,490,504,526,578,604,608,625,664,680,688,760,808,827,853,883,890,911,925,976,1027,1032]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Oh, I hate it when that happens.","zh":"哦，我最讨厌那样了。","s":50.99,"e":52.7,"wt":[0,11,18,47,61,92,119]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"It was horrible. In this podcast, we're going to hear from some experts about why we itch and how scratching can be good for us.","zh":"确实太难受了。在本期播客中，我们将听听专家解释我们为什么会发痒，以及抓挠如何对我们有益。","s":52.85,"e":61.5,"wt":[0,11,19,91,104,131,180,208,241,260,280,306,339,405,451,475,504,583,587,605,675,696,710,738,759]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"And as usual, we'll be learning some useful new vocabulary.","zh":"照例，我们也会学习一些实用的新词汇。","s":61.72,"e":65.46,"wt":[0,24,56,96,114,144,164,196,244,304]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK. Well, we're going to start with a quiz question of course, Georgie. It's a bit of a different one today. Dr Henning Holle studies the psychology of itching , and he played some scratching sounds to the guests on the BBC World Service programme CrowdScience. Now the question is, what is being scratched ? You have three choices: a) an arm, b) a beard, or c) some jeans.","zh":"好的，Georgie，我们当然还是先从竞猜题开始。今天的题目有点不一样。Henning Holle 博士研究发痒心理学，他在 BBC 国际频道《CrowdScience》节目上给嘉宾播放了几段抓挠的录音。现在的问题是：录音里正在挠的是什么？你有三个选项：a) 一条手臂，b) 胡子，还是 c) 牛仔裤？","s":65.68,"e":96.32,"wt":[0,4,32,67,102,116,151,179,186,219,270,291,328,382,394,398,416,424,432,472,520,580,632,705,769,842,872,984,1000,1081,1128,1128,1128,1189,1240,1338,1416,1424,1440,1488,1504,1528,1566,1592,1649,1715,1832,1853,1888,1929,2028,2096,2120,2192,2237,2272,2294,2334,2369,2456,2499,2543,2608,2732,2782,2864,2900,2968,2968]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK. Well, let's listen... Well, that sounded a bit hairy, so I'm going to go with b) a beard.","zh":"好，我们来听一下……嗯，听起来毛茸茸的，所以我选 b) 胡子。","s":96.54,"e":111.2,"wt":[0,19,46,61,1038,1056,1090,1090,1112,1126,1189,1192,1220,1267,1286,1305,1355,1378,1386]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, we'll find out at the end of the programme.","zh":"好，我们会在节目最后揭晓答案。","s":111.36,"e":113.92,"wt":[0,5,40,73,92,106,142,150,169,186]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"So, Neil, I mentioned that my jumper was a bit itchy but sometimes I feel itchy for no particular reason. So, what's going on there?","zh":"那么 Neil，刚才我提到我的毛衣有点扎得痒，但有时候我也会无缘无故觉得痒，这又是怎么回事呢？","s":114,"e":124.24,"wt":[0,27,64,126,172,212,240,308,319,340,344,454,480,496,570,592,656,672,692,754,771,874,896,896,942]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, that's something that the listeners of BBC World Service programme CrowdScience also wondered. They wanted to know the answer to that. The presenter, Anand Jagatia, spoke to itching expert Sonja Ständer to find out more. Let's listen.","zh":"嗯，BBC 国际频道《CrowdScience》节目的听众也很好奇这个问题，他们都想知道答案。主持人 Anand Jagatia 采访了瘙痒研究专家 Sonja Ständer，来一探究竟。我们一起来听听。","s":124.24,"e":141.28,"wt":[0,0,76,86,126,144,213,243,253,293,348,418,496,592,656,676,680,694,710,736,758,780,854,894,982,1058,1144,1194,1200,1264,1352,1372,1480,1508,1508,1560,1622,1622]},{"speaker":"Anand Jagatia","en":"There's lots of science to unpack here. Sonja, what actually is itchiness ?","zh":"这里面有很多科学知识值得剖析（unpack）。Sonja，发痒究竟是怎么一回事？","s":141.28,"e":145.7,"wt":[0,38,52,61,103,111,136,196,250,264,318,345,400]},{"speaker":"Sonja Ständer","en":"Yeah. So, by scratching the skin, we remove parasites or plants or anything that does not belong on the skin. And so, we start to scratch at the very same area where we itch and remove it. And it can no longer damage the skin and harm ourselves.","zh":"是的。通过抓挠皮肤，我们可以清除寄生虫（parasites）、植物碎屑或任何不属于皮肤表面的东西。因此我们会在发痒的同一位置开始抓挠并将其清除，这样它就无法再损伤皮肤、危害我们的身体了。","s":145.7,"e":164.98,"wt":[0,33,48,64,146,169,190,218,278,353,368,422,472,526,543,570,594,625,640,674,778,804,837,857,909,928,1048,1048,1049,1130,1131,1183,1238,1246,1366,1366,1415,1431,1455,1471,1508,1511,1560,1615,1633,1666,1690,1756]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Anand says there's a lot of science to unpack about itchiness . Unpack here means explain.","zh":"Anand 说关于发痒有很多科学原理需要“剖析（unpack）”。这里的 unpack 就是详细解释的意思。","s":164.98,"e":172.44,"wt":[0,54,95,118,126,150,198,233,250,301,343,402,447,495,529,632]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Sonja explains that when our skin feels itchy , we scratch it, and that helps to get rid of parasites and other harmful things on our skin. Parasites are animals or plants that live on another animal and feed from them.","zh":"Sonja 解释说，当皮肤发痒时我们会去挠它，这有助于清除皮肤上的寄生虫（parasites）和其他有害物。寄生虫就是寄生在其他生物身上并从中汲取营养的动物或植物。","s":172.68,"e":188.46,"wt":[0,44,85,100,140,161,220,238,262,280,300,362,432,448,448,475,492,517,542,618,636,661,703,772,824,828,876,974,1038,1076,1094,1095,1146,1181,1216,1290,1342,1346,1428,1468,1477]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"So, Neil, itching is our skin's way of protecting itself against harmful parasites . And I have to say, all of this talk of itching and scratching is making me feel a bit itchy right now.","zh":"这么说来，Neil，发痒其实是皮肤保护自己免受有害寄生虫侵害的一种方式。而且我不得不说，聊了这么多发痒和抓挠，我现在自己都有点觉得痒了。","s":188.64,"e":199.6,"wt":[0,8,33,90,107,131,180,205,227,304,354,402,456,521,571,576,577,603,616,635,654,667,693,719,732,795,797,862,875,914,927,960,970,988,1040,1068]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, that's interesting because Dr Henning Holle wanted to study whether just thinking about itching makes us more likely to feel itchy .","zh":"嗯，这很有意思，因为 Henning Holle 博士就曾专门研究过：仅仅是想到“发痒”，是否就会让我们更容易觉得痒。","s":199.6,"e":210.08,"wt":[0,33,82,172,218,245,294,370,399,415,464,520,580,648,668,730,774,791,826,879,897,934,987]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Henning and his team studied what happened when people watched videos of itching and scratching .","zh":"Henning 和他的团队研究了人们观看发痒和挠痒视频时的反应。","s":210.08,"e":215.94,"wt":[0,13,39,65,112,160,204,224,264,287,344,389,406,460,484,544]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Let's listen to him speaking to BBC World Service programme CrowdScience.","zh":"我们来听听他在 BBC 国际频道《CrowdScience》节目中的分享。","s":215.94,"e":220.14,"wt":[0,39,86,102,142,188,205,229,282,342,384]},{"speaker":"Dr Henning Holle","en":"And what we found in this study was, yes, these scratching videos indeed made people feel itchier . They also started to scratch themselves more spontaneously . So, the answer is basically yes, you can catch a scratch .","zh":"我们在这项研究中发现，没错，这些挠痒视频确实会让人觉得更痒。他们还会更频繁地不由自主（spontaneously）抓挠自己。所以答案基本上是肯定的——抓痒确实会“传染（catch）”。","s":221.52,"e":236.9,"wt":[0,14,14,22,70,87,124,178,246,248,348,388,442,472,528,558,588,631,670,702,796,801,890,934,992,1008,1086,1132,1192,1210,1248,1280,1328,1350,1376,1406,1434,1455,1499]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"In Henning's study, when people watched videos of scratching , they scratched themselves spontaneously . If you do something spontaneously , you do it without being asked or without thinking.","zh":"在 Henning 的研究中，人们看挠痒视频时会不由自主地（spontaneously）挠自己。如果你做某件事是 spontaneously，意思就是没人要求、不假思索就自发去做了。","s":236.9,"e":250.22,"wt":[0,16,90,136,164,214,281,346,350,427,462,469,542,615,692,738,753,776,791,860,937,966,984,1001,1014,1066,1126,1153,1173,1244]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"So, Henning concludes that yes, you can catch a scratch , just like you might catch an illness. Catch in this context means get from someone else.","zh":"因此 Henning 得出结论：没错，抓痒确实会被“传染（catch）”，就像你会染上某种疾病一样。这里的 catch 意思是从别人那里沾染上。","s":250.46,"e":261.62,"wt":[0,20,91,174,212,255,284,322,372,377,418,442,462,482,514,534,566,584,664,691,705,741,784,820,896,932,932]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK. Georgie, I think it's time for the answer to my quiz question. We had a clip of people scratching and you had to decide what is being scratched .","zh":"好了，Georgie，现在该揭晓我那道竞猜题的答案了。刚才我们放了一段抓挠的录音，你要判断正在挠的是什么。","s":261.68,"e":274.86,"wt":[0,22,72,88,133,176,194,212,248,260,278,312,403,487,508,539,549,656,690,738,780,811,842,873,994,1102,1125,1146,1203,1268]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"I said that was a beard.","zh":"我猜的是胡子。","s":274.86,"e":276.82,"wt":[0,35,68,68,74,100]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK. Well, let's see if the people who are listening to this on the programme CrowdScience agreed.","zh":"好，我们来看看《CrowdScience》节目里听这段录音的嘉宾们是不是也这么认为。","s":277.28,"e":284.64,"wt":[0,57,102,158,201,219,256,288,304,320,361,368,392,404,434,448,544]},{"speaker":"Sonja Ständer","en":"It sounds like hairy skin so I would go for the scalp.","zh":"听起来像是有毛发的皮肤，所以我猜是头皮。","s":284.64,"e":287.82,"wt":[0,22,22,50,96,132,154,161,201,236,256,274]},{"speaker":"Anand Jagatia","en":"Yeah, I think it's beard or something because it does sound like there's hair there.","zh":"嗯，我觉得是胡子之类的，因为听起来那里确实有毛发。","s":287.82,"e":291.82,"wt":[0,23,76,76,128,128,184,220,220,227,246,275,298,337,372]},{"speaker":"Dr Henning Holle","en":"It is definitely hairy skin and Anand was spot on. So, it is indeed a beard scratch that we were listening to.","zh":"绝对是有毛发的皮肤，Anand 猜得完全准确！我们刚才听到的确实是挠胡子的声音。","s":291.99,"e":297.92,"wt":[0,9,15,89,129,157,179,225,238,268,283,300,313,328,372,380,425,473,481,497,537,573]},{"speaker":"Anand Jagatia","en":"Wow!","zh":"哇！","s":297.92,"e":298.14,"wt":[0]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"So I was right!","zh":"这么说我猜对了！","s":298.14,"e":299.33,"wt":[0,78,88,88]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"You were. Congratulations!","zh":"没错，恭喜你！","s":299.33,"e":301.31,"wt":[0,24,107]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we learned in this programme, starting with itch , which is an unpleasant feeling on your skin. And something can be described using the adjective itchy .","zh":"好了，我们来回顾一下本期学到的词汇，首先是 itch（发痒/痒感），指皮肤上那种不舒服的感觉；还可以用形容词 itchy 来形容发痒或让人发痒的东西。","s":301.31,"e":314.48,"wt":[0,79,79,111,135,221,237,301,309,336,397,466,500,551,595,595,595,610,703,757,775,809,853,869,913,943,949,1029,1063,1099,1161,1234]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"When you feel an itch , you often scratch it with your fingers by rubbing your nails on the skin.","zh":"当你觉得痒时，通常会用手指去 scratch（挠），也就是用指甲在皮肤上来回摩擦。","s":314.48,"e":321.5,"wt":[0,24,56,56,108,108,108,120,171,240,256,272,296,379,384,446,486,535,554,578]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"If you unpack an idea or scientific concept, you explain it in clear detail.","zh":"如果你 unpack（剖析）一个观点或科学概念，意思就是把它清晰详尽地解释清楚。","s":321.5,"e":327.32,"wt":[0,0,0,55,128,164,210,230,306,322,370,394,415,474]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"A parasite is an animal or plant that lives on another animal and feeds on it.","zh":"parasite（寄生虫/寄生物）是指寄生在其他生物身上并以其为食的动物或植物。","s":327.32,"e":333.62,"wt":[0,36,110,120,162,190,218,254,280,316,336,404,404,474,474,496]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"If you do something spontaneously , you do it without being asked or without thinking.","zh":"如果你 spontaneously（自发地/不由自主地）做某事，意思是在没人要求或不假思索的情况下就做了。","s":333.62,"e":339.1,"wt":[0,19,22,62,109,183,211,234,249,281,319,354,397,412,467]},{"speaker":"Catch","en":"can be used to mean get from someone else, such as an illness. For example, you can catch a cold.","zh":"（catch，染上/被传染）可以用来表示从别人那里沾染上某种疾病或感觉，例如“catch a cold（感冒）”。","s":339.54,"e":346.67,"wt":[0,23,60,78,102,174,176,211,273,316,342,359,393,465,489,553,576,602,640,654]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Once again, our six minutes are up, but you can test what you've learned with the worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"我们的六分钟时间又到了，你可以去我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 用学习单检验今天学到的内容。","s":346.67,"e":354.38,"wt":[0,65,81,118,171,192,210,222,240,258,276,315,324,360,403,427,445,499,511,529,581]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Thank you for joining us and goodbye.","zh":"感谢收听，再见。","s":354.38,"e":356.3,"wt":[0,30,51,72,110,126,147]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Goodbye.","zh":"再见！","s":356.3,"e":356.75,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0pc618w.mp3"},{"epNum":10,"id":"ep-260917","date":"2026-09-17","topic":"心理社交","title":"Is rejection good for us?","titleZh":"被拒绝其实对我们有好处吗？","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260917","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260917_6_minute_english_is_rejection_good_for_us_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260917_6_minute_english_is_rejection_good_for_us_transcript.pdf","summary":"How would you feel about going up to a stranger on the street and asking them for a cheeky favour? If the idea makes you nervous, then don't worry – you are not alone! But recently, some people have been filming themselves doing this, as part of a social media trend called 'rejection therapy'. It's not an officially recognised form of therapy, but some have found that it improves their confidence. Georgie and Neil discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","summaryZh":"如果在街上走向一个陌生人，厚着脸皮请对方帮个有点出格的小忙，你会是什么感觉？如果光是想想就让你紧张，别担心——你并不孤单！但最近，一些人开始拍视频记录自己这么做，这是社交媒体上兴起的一股叫“拒绝疗法（rejection therapy）”的风潮。它并非官方认可的心理疗法，但有人发现这确实能提升自信。Georgie 和 Neil 将探讨这一现象，并教你几个实用的新词汇。","question":"Which of these things did not happen to the animator Walt Disney before he became successful?","questionZh":"动画大师沃尔特·迪士尼在成名之前，以下哪件事并没有发生在他身上？","options":["a) he lost his job as an artist","b) his first business ran out of money","c) his first full length film was a failure"],"optionsZh":["a) 他丢掉了画师的工作","b) 他的第一家公司资金耗尽破产了","c) 他的第一部长篇电影遭遇了失败"],"answer":{"letter":"c","explanationZh":"节目结尾 Neil 揭晓正确答案是 c)：迪士尼的第一部长篇动画电影《白雪公主和七个小矮人》并没有失败，反而大获成功。"},"vocab":[{"word":"rejection","defEn":"saying no to someone; refusing an idea or suggestion","pos":"n.","zh":"拒绝；回绝","exampleEn":"So, rejection means saying no to someone or refusing to accept their idea or suggestion.","ipa":"/rɪˈdʒekʃən/"},{"word":"spike","defEn":"increase suddenly","pos":"v.","zh":"骤增；飙升","exampleEn":"And actually, walking up to the first person, I felt my adrenaline really, really spiking .","ipa":"/spaɪk/"},{"word":"socially anxious","defEn":"worried about speaking to other people, or being judged when around others","pos":"adj. 短语","zh":"社交焦虑的；社恐的","exampleEn":"I'm not a socially anxious person – I think I'm really confident.","ipa":"/ˈsəʊʃəli ˈæŋkʃəs/"},{"word":"come easier","defEn":"be easier to achieve","pos":"短语","zh":"变得更容易做到；更得心应手","exampleEn":"And once I'd done one, the second one came easier and the third one came even easier.","ipa":"/kʌm ˈiːziə/"},{"word":"perfectionism","defEn":"feeling that everything you do needs to be perfect","pos":"n.","zh":"完美主义","exampleEn":"What we do know is that there is an increase in perfectionism .","ipa":"/pəˈfekʃənɪzəm/"},{"word":"appraise","defEn":"judge","pos":"v.","zh":"评价；评判","exampleEn":"Young people are also worried about how people will appraise them, which means judge them.","ipa":"/əˈpreɪz/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"have a go at","zh":"试一试；亲自尝试某事（英式英语超高频日常短语，比单纯用 try 更地道）","contextEn":"Hannah Gelbart, host of the BBC World Service podcast What in the World, had a go at the trend."},{"en":"get behind the wheel","zh":"握方向盘；亲自开车（生动形象的日常口语习语）","contextEn":"Can I drive your cab? Can I? Can I get behind the wheel?"},{"en":"face one's fears","zh":"直面恐惧（心理学及个人成长类雅思口语/写作高频地道搭配）","contextEn":"Coming somewhere else to try and face my fears... maybe do a bit of shopping."},{"en":"boost one's confidence","zh":"增强自信心（比 increase confidence 更有表现力的地道动宾搭配）","contextEn":"But people who post the videos say they boost their confidence."}],"sentences":[{"en":"And there are some criticisms of the videos because they involve filming your interactions with strangers, which people might not want.","zh":"此外，这类视频也招致了一些批评，因为它们涉及把你与陌生人的互动拍下来，而路人可能并不愿意出镜。","coreEn":"There are some criticisms of the videos.","coreZh":"针对这些视频存在一些批评意见。","cars":[{"text":"because they involve filming your interactions with strangers","hook":"状语从句 because + -ing 宾语","noteZh":"说明招致批评的原因——视频涉及拍摄与陌生人的互动过程"},{"text":", which people might not want","hook":"关系词 which","noteZh":"指代前面“被拍进视频”这件事，点出隐私与意愿问题"}],"tipZh":"involve doing sth.（涉及做某事）和逗号后的非限定性 which 从句（指代前面整件事），是雅思大作文“分析网络现象利弊”时的黄金组合。"},{"en":"And so, I wonder whether that might make people more shy of speaking to strangers – worried that the people will, you know, not appraise them in a good way, and so doing something like this to try to counter that might feel like a positive step to people, that they're trying to do something.","zh":"因此我在想，这会不会让人们更不敢和陌生人搭话——担心别人不会给自己好评；所以像这样主动去做点什么来对抗这种焦虑，在人们眼里可能就像迈出了积极的一步，说明他们正在努力做出改变。","coreEn":"I wonder whether that might make people more shy, and so doing something like this might feel like a positive step.","coreZh":"我在想这是否会让人更害羞，因此像这样做点什么或许会让人觉得是积极的一步。","cars":[{"text":"of speaking to strangers","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"接在 shy 后面，限定是在“与陌生人交谈”这件事上感到胆怯"},{"text":"– worried that the people will, you know, not appraise them in a good way,","hook":"破折号插入语 + -ed 修饰","noteZh":"解释为什么不敢开口：内心担忧他人不会正面评价自己"},{"text":"to try to counter that","hook":"不定式 to do","noteZh":"交代做“拒绝疗法”的目的——试图对抗前面提到的完美主义与社交恐惧"},{"text":", that they're trying to do something","hook":"同位语补充 that","noteZh":"进一步解释“积极的一步”给人的心理感受：觉得自己正在采取行动"}],"tipZh":"遇到 50 词以上的长访谈句，直接用标点符号（破折号、逗号）和逻辑词（and so）切分成三段消化：先看前半句焦虑成因，再看后半句行动意义。"},{"en":"Deliberately looking for rejection might be an extreme way to become more confident, but it can lead to surprising discoveries.","zh":"刻意去寻找被拒绝的体验，或许是提升自信的一种极端方式，但它也可能带来意想不到的收获。","coreEn":"Deliberately looking for rejection might be an extreme way, but it can lead to surprising discoveries.","coreZh":"刻意找拒绝或许是一种极端方式，但它能带来意外发现。","cars":[{"text":"Deliberately looking for rejection","hook":"-ing 短语作主语","noteZh":"把“刻意去寻求被拒”这个动作打包成句子的主语"},{"text":"to become more confident","hook":"不定式 to do","noteZh":"修饰 an extreme way，说明这种极端方式所要达成的目标"}],"tipZh":"“A might be ..., but it can lead to ...”是典型的让步转折句型，非常适合雅思写作结尾段做客观平衡的总结。"},{"en":"Yes, and Hannah found that most strangers she spoke to were friendly and happy to help, so she didn't really experience rejection much at all.","zh":"是的，而且 Hannah 发现她搭讪的大多数陌生人都很友善、乐于助人，所以她其实根本没怎么体验到被拒绝。","coreEn":"Hannah found that most strangers were friendly and happy to help, so she didn't really experience rejection.","coreZh":"Hannah 发现大多数陌生人都很友善且乐于帮忙，所以她其实没怎么被拒绝。","cars":[{"text":"she spoke to","hook":"省略关系词的定语从句","noteZh":"紧贴在 most strangers 后面，限定是“她开口交谈过的”那些陌生人"},{"text":"so she didn't really experience rejection much at all","hook":"结果分句 so","noteZh":"点出实验的反转结果：本来是去体验被拒，结果几乎没人拒绝她"}],"tipZh":"英语里两个名词/代词直接撞在一起（most strangers she spoke to），90% 是省略了关系代词 whom/that 的后置定语，直接当成“她搭过话的陌生人”来读。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Hello and welcome to 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Georgie.","zh":"大家好，欢迎收听 BBC 英语教学的《六分钟英语》。我是 Georgie。","s":0.32,"e":5.38,"wt":[0,6,29,83,98,118,167,224,261,280,343,413,430]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"And I'm Neil. Don't forget you can find vocabulary, definitions and a worksheet for free on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"我是 Neil。别忘了你可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 上免费获取本期词汇、释义和学习单。","s":5.58,"e":14.48,"wt":[0,16,35,90,120,160,178,205,222,290,362,386,420,477,508,548,570,598,666]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"So Neil, today we're talking about rejection . So, rejection means saying no to someone or refusing to accept their idea or suggestion. Neil, how do you feel about rejection ?","zh":"那么 Neil，今天我们要聊的话题是“拒绝（rejection）”。所谓拒绝，就是对别人说“不”，或者回绝对方的想法或建议。Neil，你怎么看待被拒绝这件事？","s":15.04,"e":28.72,"wt":[0,36,93,141,189,256,307,367,412,431,498,548,560,582,602,672,740,780,798,836,880,930,951,1070,1106,1126,1138,1159,1184,1207,1298]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, nobody likes rejection . But I think as you get older, you get used to it. And probably it's quite a good thing to experience rejection , even if it doesn't feel nice.","zh":"嗯，没人喜欢被拒绝。不过我觉得随着年龄增长，人会慢慢习惯它。而且经历一些拒绝说不定还挺有好处的，哪怕当下感觉并不好受。","s":28.72,"e":42.84,"wt":[0,102,115,183,265,328,360,374,433,456,458,512,606,619,641,656,680,714,736,821,856,856,870,900,997,1084,1100,1156,1184,1232,1254,1264,1296,1346]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yeah, I agree. We can't always get what we want, can we?","zh":"对，我同意。我们不可能总是如愿以偿，不是吗？","s":42.84,"e":45.86,"wt":[0,26,35,116,126,151,183,196,216,225,261,271]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"We can't, no. In this podcast, we'll be learning about a new internet trend called rejection therapy and how people are using it to help them to be more confident. OK. Let's start with our quiz question, Georgie. Lots of famous people have faced rejection in their lives before they became successful, but which of these things did not happen to the animator Walt Disney before he became successful? Was it: a) he lost his job as an artist, b) his first business ran out of money, or c) his first full length film was a failure?","zh":"确实不能。在本期播客中，我们将了解一种叫做“拒绝疗法（rejection therapy）”的新兴网络潮流，以及人们如何借此提升自信。好了，Georgie，我们先来一道竞猜题。许多名人在成功之前都曾在人生中屡遭拒绝，但在动画大师沃尔特·迪士尼成名之前，以下哪件事并没有发生在他身上？选项是：a) 他丢掉了画师的工作，b) 他的第一家公司资金耗尽，还是 c) 他的第一部长篇电影遭遇了失败？","s":45.86,"e":86.7,"wt":[0,30,70,134,145,168,220,247,266,310,354,362,397,450,496,554,598,657,682,710,757,790,824,841,858,892,926,943,960,998,1318,1318,1394,1416,1434,1460,1516,1550,1662,1692,1730,1788,1792,1868,1889,2000,2028,2055,2096,2145,2186,2226,2332,2356,2384,2406,2450,2484,2500,2551,2600,2636,2654,2718,2752,2810,2859,2877,2931,2994,3006,3038,3115,3133,3172,3194,3220,3238,3272,3352,3440,3466,3514,3538,3565,3594,3618,3662,3681,3776,3810,3854,3880,3900,3934,3963,4001]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"I've got no idea, but I'm going to go with a) he lost his job as an artist.","zh":"我完全没头绪，但我打算选 a) 他丢掉了画师的工作。","s":86.74,"e":92.02,"wt":[0,31,56,72,121,144,160,160,174,244,277,310,329,359,384,407,423,443]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK. Well, we'll find out the answer at the end of the programme.","zh":"好，我们会在节目最后揭晓答案。","s":92.2,"e":95.8,"wt":[0,13,54,88,116,132,160,199,213,240,255,264,290]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK Neil. So, most people don't like being rejected, but one thing to try might be something called rejection therapy. It's a social media trend where people record themselves talking to strangers and sometimes being rejected.","zh":"好的，Neil。大多数人都不喜欢被拒绝，但或许可以试一试所谓的“拒绝疗法”。这是社交媒体上的一股风潮，人们拍下自己找陌生人搭话、有时遭到拒绝的过程。","s":96.16,"e":112.74,"wt":[0,16,56,80,112,160,200,236,277,367,399,430,482,518,552,584,618,670,684,751,828,855,859,908,940,977,1014,1064,1114,1200,1258,1272,1410,1468,1540,1549]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK. That sounds quite interesting, but let's find out how it works in practice. Hannah Gelbart, host of the BBC World Service podcast What in the World, had a go at the trend. Let's see how she got on.","zh":"听起来挺有意思的，我们来看看实际操作起来是什么样。BBC 国际频道《What in the World》播客的主持人 Hannah Gelbart 亲自试了试这股风潮。来听听她的进展如何。","s":113.18,"e":127.19,"wt":[0,15,45,90,133,225,247,284,314,336,358,372,421,430,554,556,694,722,722,722,802,802,863,938,967,981,1005,1052,1068,1096,1104,1114,1130,1242,1244,1328,1352,1352,1357]},{"speaker":"Hannah Gelbart","en":"Hey, can I come sit down at your table?","zh":"嘿，我能坐你这桌吗？","s":127.19,"e":129.85,"wt":[0,36,60,74,104,128,158,176,206]},{"speaker":"Stranger","en":"Yeah.","zh":"可以啊。","s":129.85,"e":130.4,"wt":[0]},{"speaker":"Hannah Gelbart","en":"Hey, how's it going?","zh":"嘿，近来怎么样？","s":130.4,"e":131.83,"wt":[0,35,65,84]},{"speaker":"Stranger","en":"Good, thank you.","zh":"挺好的，谢谢。","s":131.83,"e":133.09,"wt":[0,42,76]},{"speaker":"Hannah Gelbart","en":"How's your day gone? Good afternoon. How are you doing? Can I drive your cab? Can I? Can I get behind the wheel?","zh":"今天过得怎么样？（切换场景）下午好，你好吗？我能开一下你的出租车吗？能吗？我能坐上驾驶座开开吗？","s":133.09,"e":140.88,"wt":[0,29,59,83,138,168,245,269,293,318,378,402,416,450,480,530,554,593,618,631,656,695,719]},{"speaker":"Cab Driver","en":"Don't be silly!","zh":"别胡闹了！","s":140.88,"e":141.96,"wt":[0,30,48]},{"speaker":"Hannah Gelbart","en":"Don't be silly...! Coming somewhere else to try and face my fears... maybe do a bit of shopping. I was wondering, can I have a nap in one of these beds? Can I just try it out? Wow – I didn't expect that one to be a yes!","zh":"“别胡闹了”……换个地方继续直面恐惧……也许去逛逛街吧。我想问一下，我能在你们这些床上小睡一会儿吗？我就试躺一下行吗？哇——我真没想到这个请求对方竟然答应了！","s":141.96,"e":153.92,"wt":[0,30,48,108,108,156,174,188,196,226,250,284,454,474,480,488,488,510,612,612,614,644,648,654,678,684,702,723,732,743,775,816,834,840,863,882,894,934,948,963,964,994,1025,1046,1062,1072,1082,1086]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Well, it seems like people were pretty nice to Hannah in those examples.","zh":"嗯，在这些例子里，大家对 Hannah 似乎都还挺友善的。","s":154,"e":157.68,"wt":[0,0,2,32,40,83,111,154,182,196,239,253,292]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Yes. And she talks with psychologist Claudia Hammond about how she felt during the experiment.","zh":"是的。之后她还和心理学家 Claudia Hammond 聊了聊自己在这次尝试中的感受。","s":157.76,"e":163.88,"wt":[0,48,68,90,120,184,234,282,352,376,394,424,470,502,544]},{"speaker":"Hannah Gelbart","en":"And actually, walking up to the first person, I felt my adrenaline really, really spiking . I'm not a socially anxious person – I think I'm really confident. But that first thing really scared me. And once I'd done one, the second one came easier and the third one came even easier. And you know, by the fourth time I didn't even mind it whether they said yes or no.","zh":"其实走向第一个人的时候，我感觉自己的肾上腺素真的在疯狂飙升（spiking）。我并不是一个有社交焦虑（socially anxious）的人——我觉得自己其实非常自信。但第一次尝试真的吓到我了。不过迈出第一步之后，第二次就变得更容易（came easier），第三次就更轻松了。你知道吗，到了第四次的时候，我甚至根本不在乎对方说行还是不行了。","s":163.92,"e":181.9,"wt":[0,0,58,92,106,116,138,176,210,224,242,266,350,386,412,445,474,504,504,509,553,591,621,640,648,672,688,721,781,803,819,846,880,932,948,978,995,1023,1041,1061,1158,1178,1194,1212,1240,1240,1243,1260,1290,1308,1332,1363,1402,1430,1432,1460,1470,1487,1516,1546,1550,1581,1601,1621,1656,1694,1708,1730,1744,1762]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Hannah says that when she walked up to the first stranger in the video, she felt her adrenaline spiking . If something spikes , it increases sharply.","zh":"Hannah 说视频里她走向第一个陌生人时，感到肾上腺素飙升（spiking）。如果某样东西 spike，意思就是急剧上升。","s":182.12,"e":191.48,"wt":[0,13,40,67,94,114,154,168,187,201,240,289,303,322,372,388,412,436,534,568,599,666,673,706,727,747,801]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Hannah is not generally a socially anxious person. Someone who is socially anxious is nervous or worried about speaking to other people.","zh":"Hannah 平时并不是一个有社交焦虑（socially anxious）的人。socially anxious 指在和别人交谈时容易紧张或担忧。","s":191.52,"e":200.98,"wt":[0,50,50,114,122,182,206,271,360,414,439,455,546,592,616,682,712,741,775,829,843,877]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Each time Hannah tried to speak to a new person, she says, it came easier . Come easier is an informal way to say something is easier to achieve.","zh":"Hannah 说，每多找一个陌生人搭话，这件事就变得更顺手（came easier）。come easier 是一种非正式的口语表达，意思是更容易做到。","s":201.14,"e":211.32,"wt":[0,14,40,84,114,134,162,174,196,213,278,313,355,377,422,474,526,558,622,622,660,676,705,722,796,828,845,898,915]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Now, this social media trend is called rejection therapy, but it's not a proper form of therapy. It hasn't been tested.","zh":"不过，这股社交媒体潮流虽然叫“拒绝疗法”，但它并不是正规的心理疗法，也没有经过科学验证。","s":211.52,"e":220.08,"wt":[0,19,54,106,152,192,213,269,346,440,448,472,496,503,552,584,604,684,696,747,776]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"And there are some criticisms of the videos because they involve filming your interactions with strangers, which people might not want.","zh":"此外，这类视频也招致了一些批评，因为它们涉及拍摄你与陌生人的互动，而路人可能并不希望被拍。","s":220.1,"e":228.22,"wt":[0,30,51,71,97,162,176,196,238,270,296,342,381,406,508,519,602,640,687,708,734]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"But people who post the videos say they boost their confidence. So, why do people want to ask for rejection ?","zh":"但发布这类视频的人表示，这能增强他们的自信心。那么，人们为什么会主动去求拒绝呢？","s":228.35,"e":236.56,"wt":[0,13,45,68,95,116,217,237,275,305,341,445,445,515,543,595,595,645,645,660,751]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Psychologist Claudia Hammond has some ideas, which she shared with Hannah on the BBC World Service podcast What in the World.","zh":"心理学家 Claudia Hammond 有一些见解，她在 BBC 国际频道《What in the World》播客中和 Hannah 分享了自己的看法。","s":236.56,"e":244.5,"wt":[0,71,122,172,196,236,304,316,337,380,409,448,458,478,507,548,593,648,673,685,703]},{"speaker":"Claudia Hammond","en":"What we do know is that there is an increase in perfectionism . People are more worried now, younger people, about how other people are judging them. And so, I wonder whether that might make people more shy of speaking to strangers – worried that the people will, you know, not appraise them in a good way, and so doing something like this to try to counter that might feel like a positive step to people, that they're trying to do something. Also, they're fun to watch.","zh":"我们确实知道的是，完美主义（perfectionism）倾向正在上升。现在的人们，尤其是年轻人，更加在意别人怎么评判自己。所以我在想，这会不会让人更不敢和陌生人说话——担心别人不会正面评价（appraise）自己；因此像这样主动做点什么去对抗这种焦虑，在人们眼里就像迈出了积极的一步，证明自己在努力采取行动。而且，这类视频看着也挺有意思的。","s":244.72,"e":270.74,"wt":[0,0,0,60,82,96,96,108,124,198,256,290,350,386,392,406,426,471,489,523,575,594,609,636,669,691,720,782,792,797,854,854,902,936,966,999,1053,1098,1136,1146,1184,1205,1275,1310,1331,1360,1381,1426,1458,1478,1510,1534,1582,1582,1582,1589,1614,1720,1770,1850,1884,1926,1926,1968,1988,2013,2032,2072,2096,2119,2136,2160,2200,2270,2274,2302,2304,2317,2343,2366,2373,2380,2432,2468,2510,2528,2540]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Claudia says there's an increase in perfectionism among young people. Perfectionism is the feeling that everything you do needs to be perfect.","zh":"Claudia 说年轻人中的完美主义（perfectionism）正在增多。完美主义就是觉得自己做的每件事都必须尽善尽美的心态。","s":270.96,"e":280.44,"wt":[0,23,55,108,122,185,200,301,340,379,486,543,557,577,626,656,740,760,787,828,845,862]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Young people are also worried about how people will appraise them, which means judge them.","zh":"年轻人也很担心别人会如何评价（appraise）自己，这个词就是评判的意思。","s":280.68,"e":286.78,"wt":[0,18,64,88,117,170,208,231,277,311,368,419,456,516,530]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Deliberately looking for rejection might be an extreme way to become more confident, but it can lead to surprising discoveries.","zh":"刻意去寻找被拒绝的体验，或许是提升自信的一种极端方式，但它也可能带来意想不到的发现。","s":286.96,"e":295.36,"wt":[0,33,99,128,216,250,266,278,331,360,372,408,432,522,552,553,573,599,662,683]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yes, and Hannah found that most strangers she spoke to were friendly and happy to help, so she didn't really experience rejection much at all.","zh":"是的，Hannah 就发现她搭话的大多数陌生人都很友善且乐于帮忙，所以她其实根本没怎么尝到被拒绝的滋味。","s":295.44,"e":303.82,"wt":[0,52,52,112,136,166,198,226,251,293,312,336,383,402,431,451,522,552,553,592,631,688,746,768,784]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK. Georgie, I think it's time now for the answer to my quiz question. I asked you which rejection Walt Disney didn't experience before becoming successful?","zh":"好了，Georgie，现在该揭晓我那道竞猜题的答案了。我问你，沃尔特·迪士尼在成名之前没有经历过哪一种挫折？","s":303.86,"e":315.8,"wt":[0,32,94,117,156,194,224,244,256,302,314,332,374,428,516,548,575,614,654,786,799,848,954,981,1084,1097]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yes, and I went for a) I don't think he lost his job as an artist.","zh":"是的，我选了 a)，我觉得他没有丢掉画师的工作。","s":316.18,"e":321.2,"wt":[0,21,49,57,96,124,166,173,209,245,259,288,310,332,346,360]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, actually, the answer was c) his first full length film, Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs, was not a failure. It was a huge success.","zh":"嗯，其实正确答案是 c)，他的第一部长篇电影《白雪公主和七个小矮人》并没有失败，反而大获成功。","s":321.68,"e":330.66,"wt":[0,32,53,87,144,188,232,256,296,322,382,416,451,498,512,550,552,602,632,640,646,714,730,747,750,788]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Wow! That was his first film? That's really impressive.","zh":"哇！那竟然是他的第一部电影？太了不起了。","s":330.9,"e":334.02,"wt":[0,30,51,69,87,117,158,193,228]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"It is.","zh":"确实如此。","s":334.02,"e":334.9,"wt":[0,20]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we learned in this programme, starting with rejection , which means saying no or refusing an idea or suggestion.","zh":"好了，我们来回顾一下本期学到的词汇，首先是 rejection（拒绝），意思是对某人说“不”或回绝某个想法、建议。","s":335.04,"e":345.12,"wt":[0,0,32,56,140,168,188,248,268,296,379,424,456,538,578,599,638,685,701,764,780,796,828,898]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"The verb spike means increase suddenly.","zh":"动词 spike（骤增/飙升）意思是突然大幅上升。","s":345.12,"e":348.38,"wt":[0,2,32,110,174,230]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Someone who is socially anxious is nervous or worried about speaking to other people.","zh":"socially anxious（有社交焦虑的）形容人在与他人交谈时容易紧张或焦虑。","s":348.42,"e":354.2,"wt":[0,30,45,65,146,222,239,307,329,366,396,438,449,476]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"If something comes easier , it's easier to achieve.","zh":"如果某件事 comes easier（变得更容易），意思就是它更容易做到。","s":354.32,"e":358.06,"wt":[0,32,41,87,134,164,202,268,278]},{"speaker":"Perfectionism","en":"is the feeling that everything you do needs to be perfect.","zh":"（完美主义）是指觉得自己做的每一件事都必须尽善尽美的心态。","s":358.99,"e":363.04,"wt":[0,20,38,85,112,167,187,221,258,273,288]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"And if you appraise someone, you judge them.","zh":"如果你 appraise（评价）某人，意思就是对他们做出评判。","s":363.04,"e":366.42,"wt":[0,48,68,88,156,183,215,268]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Once again, our six minutes are up, but you can test what you've learned with the free worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"我们的六分钟时间又到了，你可以用我们网站 bbclearningenglish.com 上的免费学习单检验今天学到的内容。","s":366.56,"e":374.6,"wt":[0,16,53,71,92,134,155,184,202,220,240,260,280,296,356,368,386,415,470,486,520,570]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Thank you for joining us and goodbye.","zh":"感谢收听，再见。","s":374.96,"e":376.98,"wt":[0,0,2,32,66,82,108]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Goodbye.","zh":"再见！","s":377.12,"e":377.66,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0pb0qlf.mp3"},{"epNum":11,"id":"ep-260910","date":"2026-09-10","title":"Can apps teach you a language?","titleZh":"手机应用能教会你一门语言吗？","topic":"文化教育","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260910","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260910_6_minute_english_can_apps_teach_you_a_language_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260910_6_minute_english_can_apps_teach_you_a_language_transcript.pdf","summary":"Have you ever used Duolingo, Babbel, Busuu or perhaps another language-learning app? How did you get on? Some people love the convenience of these apps – you can learn on the go, like during your commute to work, rather than committing to a certain time each week, like learning in a class. But are they teaching the languages effectively? Neil and Pippa discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","summaryZh":"你用过多邻国（Duolingo）、Babbel、Busuu 或其他语言学习应用吗？效果怎么样？很多人喜欢这类应用的便利性——可以随时随地学，比如在上班通勤路上，而不用像上课那样每周固定抽出时间。但它们真的能有效地教会你一门语言吗？尼尔和皮帕将探讨这个话题，并教你一些新词汇。","question":"Which of these is not currently a language you can learn using Duolingo?","questionZh":"以下哪一种目前不是你可以在多邻国（Duolingo）上学习的语言？","options":["a) Latin","b) Klingon","c) Elvish"],"optionsZh":["a) 拉丁语","b) 克林贡语","c) 精灵语"],"answer":{"letter":"c","explanationZh":"音频结尾皮帕揭晓答案：你不能在多邻国上学精灵语（Elvish），但可以学拉丁语和《星际迷航》里的虚构语言克林贡语。"},"vocab":[{"word":"implicit","defEn":"suggested or understood without being directly stated","pos":"adj.","zh":"隐含的；潜移默化的（不直接挑明的）","exampleEn":"And many of these apps say they focus on teaching language for situations and implicit learning, rather than directly teaching language rules such as grammar.","ipa":"/ɪmˈplɪsɪt/"},{"word":"explicit","defEn":"communicated clearly and directly","pos":"adj.","zh":"明确的；直接讲授的","exampleEn":"There is quite a bit of evidence that some explicit teaching of, for example, grammar and vocabulary is helpful.","ipa":"/ɪkˈsplɪsɪt/"},{"word":"immersion","defEn":"experience of being completely surrounded by something","pos":"n.","zh":"沉浸（式体验）；完全置身其中","exampleEn":"So, adults can't realistically achieve full immersion in a language like children do.","ipa":"/ɪˈmɜːʃən/"},{"word":"key finding","defEn":"most important conclusion or discovery from a piece of research","pos":"n. 短语","zh":"关键发现；核心研究结论","exampleEn":"The key finding was that in some of the tests, written grammar tests, the Duolingo learners actually improved a bit more...","ipa":"/kiː ˈfaɪndɪŋ/"},{"word":"promising","defEn":"showing signs of being positive","pos":"adj.","zh":"有希望的；前景乐观的","exampleEn":"Liz says the study is promising, which means it shows signs of being positive.","ipa":"/ˈprɒmɪsɪŋ/"},{"word":"one particular","defEn":"one specific individual thing or person","pos":"短语","zh":"某一个特定的","exampleEn":"So, it's really promising, but of course it's one particular language, in one particular app, against one particular school.","ipa":"/wʌn pəˈtɪkjələ/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"be exposed to","zh":"接触到；置身于……环境中（雅思写作/口语讨论语言或媒体环境的高频地道搭配）","contextEn":"There is a very big difference between being a child who gets constantly exposed to this world of language, compared with trying to learn a language a few hours a week."},{"en":"Got you.","zh":"明白了 / 懂你的意思了（非常自然的英美日常口语回应，相当于 I understand）","contextEn":"Got you. So, it would be helpful to try to mimic what children do, which is full immersion, but realistically you're not going to get to those levels."},{"en":"moving forwards","zh":"展望未来；今后（职场和访谈里极其常用的过渡短语，表示“往后看/接下来”）","contextEn":"Probably moving forwards, some combination of human teaching and these platforms to give us that repetition is probably going to be the right direction."},{"en":"keep up","zh":"跟上（节奏、语速或进度）","contextEn":"Try listening on 0.5 speed or in short sections if you find it too difficult to keep up."}],"sentences":[{"en":"There is a very big difference between being a child who gets constantly exposed to this world of language, compared with trying to learn a language a few hours a week.","zh":"作为一名时刻置身于这个语言世界中的孩子，与每周只抽几小时尝试学一门语言，这两者之间有着非常大的差别。","coreEn":"There is a very big difference between being a child compared with trying to learn a language.","coreZh":"做（处于该环境中的）孩子和尝试学一门语言之间有非常大的差别。","cars":[{"text":"who gets constantly exposed to this world of language","hook":"关系词 who","noteZh":"补充说明这是怎样的孩子——“时刻接触并置身于这个语言世界里的”孩子"},{"text":"compared with trying to learn a language a few hours a week","hook":"-ed 修饰（对比结构）","noteZh":"引出对比的另一方，并加上频率“每周学几小时”"}],"tipZh":"看到 between A, compared with B 虽然在书面严格语法里略显口语化（正常多用 between A and B），但听力里专家常这样自然对比，抓准对比双方即可。"},{"en":"Indeed, Liz Wonnacott has seen one study she thinks could be interesting though, which compares two groups of Spanish speakers learning English – one using an app and one attending classes – for a similar amount of time.","zh":"确实，不过丽兹·沃纳科特看过一项她认为很有意思的研究，这项研究对比了两组学习英语的西班牙语母语者——一组用手机应用，另一组去上课——学习时长相近。","coreEn":"Liz Wonnacott has seen one study, which compares two groups of Spanish speakers for a similar amount of time.","coreZh":"丽兹看过一项研究，该研究对比了学习时长相近的两组西班牙语母语者。","cars":[{"text":"she thinks could be interesting though","hook":"省略 that 的定语从句","noteZh":"紧贴在 one study 后，说明“她觉得可能有意思”"},{"text":"which compares two groups of Spanish speakers learning English","hook":"关系词 which + -ing 修饰","noteZh":"解释这项研究的具体内容，其中 learning English 修饰这两组人"},{"text":"– one using an app and one attending classes –","hook":"破折号插入语","noteZh":"顺手拆解这两组人分别是怎么学的，读长句时可以先跳过去看后面的时长"}],"tipZh":"雅思阅读和听力里经常出现双破折号插入语，先把它当作括号“剥掉”，就能立刻把 compares two groups ... 和后面的 for a similar amount of time 连起来。"},{"en":"The key finding was that in some of the tests, written grammar tests, the Duolingo learners actually improved a bit more, while actually in a test where they were looking at listening the classroom learners appeared to improve more.","zh":"核心发现是，在某些测试中——也就是书面语法测试里——多邻国学习者进步反而稍微多一点，而在一项考察听力的测试中，课堂学习者看起来进步更大。","coreEn":"The key finding was that the Duolingo learners improved a bit more, while the classroom learners appeared to improve more.","coreZh":"核心发现是：多邻国学习者进步多一点，而课堂学习者（在另一项上）看起来进步更大。","cars":[{"text":"in some of the tests, written grammar tests,","hook":"介词短语 + 同位语补充","noteZh":"限定第一个结论的范围，并顺口把“某些测试”具体化为“书面语法测试”"},{"text":"while actually in a test where they were looking at listening","hook":"对比连词 while + 关系词 where","noteZh":"用 while 转折引出另一组表现，where 从句说明这是“考察听力的测试”"}],"tipZh":"while 放在句中常表“然而、对比”，是雅思听力 Part 3/4 和阅读匹配题的超高频信号词，前后通常各对应一组实验结果。"},{"en":"Apps can help us learn some parts of a language, but in this study they weren't as successful at improving listening skills, and so Liz concludes that combining learning in apps with some other ways of studying is the best way to try to improve your language skills.","zh":"应用可以帮助我们学习语言的某些部分，但在这项研究中，它们在提升听力技能方面没那么成功，因此丽兹得出结论：把应用学习与其他学习方式结合起来，才是努力提升语言技能的最佳途径。","coreEn":"Apps can help us, but they weren't as successful, and so Liz concludes that combining learning in apps with other ways is the best way.","coreZh":"应用能帮到我们，但没那么成功，因此丽兹总结说把应用和其他方式结合才是最好的方法。","cars":[{"text":"at improving listening skills","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"说明“没那么成功”具体指哪方面——提升听力技能"},{"text":"combining learning in apps with some other ways of studying","hook":"-ing 动名词短语作主语","noteZh":"整个短语打包当作 that 从句的主语：“把应用学习和其他学习方式结合起来”"},{"text":"to try to improve your language skills","hook":"不定式 to do","noteZh":"修饰 the best way，说明这是“用来提升语言技能的”最佳方式"}],"tipZh":"遇到长动名词作主语（combining ... with ... is ...），一眼扫到后面的单数动词 is 就能迅速切出主干，这类结构也很适合用在雅思大作文结论段。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Hello and welcome to 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Neil.","zh":"大家好，欢迎收听 BBC 学英语的《六分钟英语》。我是尼尔。","s":2.56,"e":12.22,"wt":[0,548,582,600,622,652,682,724,758,820,856,892,920]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"And I'm Pippa. Apps like Duolingo, Babbel and Busuu all aim to help you learn a language, but do they really help? We'll be trying to answer that question in today's episode of 6 Minute English, and as usual we'll be learning some useful new vocabulary too.","zh":"我是皮帕。像多邻国、Babbel 和 Busuu 这类应用都旨在帮你学语言，但它们真的有用吗？今天的《六分钟英语》我们将试着回答这个问题，照例也会学习一些实用的新词汇。","s":12.34,"e":27.74,"wt":[0,28,51,102,132,162,237,282,305,335,412,438,454,454,454,487,494,582,603,622,661,719,790,806,826,850,865,895,915,958,974,1021,1075,1083,1102,1141,1186,1205,1218,1250,1283,1295,1347,1373,1412,1431,1496]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Yes, and don't forget to head to our website for a transcript of this episode and a free worksheet to test what you've learnt. It's all at bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"没错，也别忘了去我们的网站获取本期节目的文本和免费练习题，检验一下你的学习成果。都在 bbclearningenglish.com。","s":27.81,"e":37.12,"wt":[0,13,31,61,97,109,133,145,163,205,223,229,291,307,331,387,411,419,451,497,509,533,557,593,651,686,711,728]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"OK. Neil, let's start with a quiz question. Which of these is not a language you can learn using Duolingo? Is it: a) Latin, b) Klingon, or c) Elvish?","zh":"好的。尼尔，我们先来道小测验题。以下哪一种不是你可以在多邻国上学的语言？是：a) 拉丁语，b) 克林贡语，还是 c) 精灵语？","s":37.28,"e":49.41,"wt":[0,22,67,104,142,172,179,209,296,333,347,384,400,440,456,504,520,536,586,632,738,738,758,834,916,956,1037,1060,1094]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"I'm going to say c) Elvish.","zh":"我猜是 c) 精灵语。","s":49.41,"e":52.62,"wt":[0,34,45,69,113,203]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Well, we'll find out at the end of the programme. Now, people might be familiar with the app Duolingo. And then there are other apps which say they can help you learn a language quickly – Bussuu and Babbel, for example.","zh":"好，我们节目最后揭晓答案。大家对多邻国这款应用可能都很熟悉，此外还有其他号称能帮你快速学语言的应用——比如 Busuu 和 Babbel。","s":52.72,"e":65.04,"wt":[0,21,56,85,106,120,141,162,176,197,283,354,354,383,397,464,472,496,520,621,632,653,679,695,721,742,768,784,805,821,842,859,884,889,936,997,1032,1057,1082,1131,1156]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"And listeners might be familiar with how they tend to work. You get short lessons which test you on a repeated set of words. Pippa Right. And many of these apps say they focus on teaching language for situations and implicit learning, rather than directly teaching language rules such as grammar. If something is implicit , it's suggested but not communicated directly.","zh":"听众朋友们对它们通常的运作方式可能也很熟悉：你会做一些简短的课程，反复测试一组词汇。皮帕：没错。而且很多这类应用都声称自己侧重于情景语言教学和隐性学习（implicit learning），而不是直接讲授语法之类的语言规则。如果某件事是 implicit（隐含的），意思就是靠暗示体会，而不是直接说明。","s":65.04,"e":86.54,"wt":[0,16,66,94,105,152,176,192,216,240,256,318,333,354,390,440,474,500,526,536,547,605,626,640,686,713,740,761,790,804,840,869,889,919,956,969,1032,1072,1096,1190,1205,1316,1372,1406,1438,1500,1500,1555,1592,1626,1643,1709,1720,1776,1790,1834,1857,1883,1943,1963,1983,2064]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Yes. So, if you think about how children learn to speak their first language, it's mostly implicit – they don't have lessons. But can this work for adults?","zh":"是的。你想想小孩子是怎么学说母语的，基本都是潜移默化的隐性学习——他们不用上课。但这套方法对成年人管用吗？","s":86.54,"e":95.82,"wt":[0,14,27,40,60,94,128,202,203,237,250,284,318,362,423,446,490,543,571,600,636,665,746,766,786,813,840,860]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"BBC Radio 4 programme Sliced Bread has been investigating the evidence behind language learning apps. Presenter Greg Foot spoke to language learning expert Professor Elizabeth Wonnacott, or Liz, about implicit learning.","zh":"BBC 广播四台的节目《Sliced Bread》一直在调查语言学习应用背后的科学依据。主持人格雷格·富特就隐性学习采访了语言学习专家伊丽莎白·沃纳科特教授（简称丽兹）。","s":95.82,"e":108.91,"wt":[0,64,64,152,152,184,222,246,344,378,402,450,508,545,592,634,687,711,738,770,786,826,866,906,962,1012,1074,1090,1182,1230,1284]},{"speaker":"Professor Elizabeth Wonnacott","en":"There is a very big difference between being a child who gets constantly exposed to this world of language, compared with trying to learn a language a few hours a week. There is quite a bit of evidence that some explicit teaching of, for example, grammar and vocabulary is helpful.","zh":"作为一名时刻置身于这个语言世界中的孩子，与每周只抽几小时尝试学一门语言，这两者之间有着非常大的差别。有相当多的证据表明，针对语法和词汇等方面进行一些明确直接的教学（explicit teaching）是很有帮助的。","s":108.91,"e":126.32,"wt":[0,41,57,65,97,121,203,258,309,325,371,389,421,502,559,574,608,647,663,733,779,802,837,853,877,885,970,971,988,1017,1023,1063,1092,1103,1132,1138,1155,1166,1213,1245,1290,1381,1437,1477,1501,1541,1579,1593,1660,1673]},{"speaker":"Greg Foot","en":"Got you. So, it would be helpful to try to mimic what children do, which is full immersion , but realistically you're not going to get to those levels.","zh":"明白了。也就是说，试着模仿孩子那样全面沉浸式学习（full immersion）会很有帮助，但现实中成年人根本达不到那种程度。","s":126.32,"e":135.83,"wt":[0,24,48,62,80,108,135,192,208,232,248,287,320,360,384,413,424,456,506,530,618,712,712,733,774,790,814,830,871]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Liz says that while children learn their first language implicitly by being constantly surrounded by the language, adults are often learning for only a few hours a week.","zh":"丽兹说，孩子因为时刻被语言包围，所以能潜移默化地学会母语，但成年人往往每周只学几个小时。","s":135.83,"e":145.62,"wt":[0,21,49,73,115,157,190,221,256,308,374,387,420,486,552,564,584,651,685,707,744,797,823,839,861,884,919,927]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Yes. So, for adults trying to learn a new language, research has shown that some explicit teaching is helpful. Explicit is the opposite of implicit . So, for example, explicit grammar teaching would be somebody explaining a grammar rule to you.","zh":"是的。因此对于试图学习新语言的成年人来说，研究表明进行一些明确讲授（explicit teaching）是有益的。explicit 是 implicit 的反义词。举个例子，明确讲授语法就是有人直接给你解释一条语法规则。","s":145.74,"e":159.89,"wt":[0,15,29,50,93,136,150,185,192,213,283,326,342,370,394,426,482,535,546,619,678,693,715,774,789,821,848,863,890,946,1001,1050,1105,1127,1138,1195,1254,1272,1308,1332,1354]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Right. So, adults can't realistically achieve full immersion in a language like children do. Immersion means being completely surrounded by something.","zh":"没错。所以成年人现实中没法像孩子那样实现完全的语言沉浸（full immersion）。immersion 的意思就是完全被某种事物包围。","s":159.89,"e":168.54,"wt":[0,37,51,95,131,234,283,307,378,389,402,457,497,507,551,623,649,667,723,771,805]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"OK. So, a mixture of the implicit teaching of the apps and some explicit teaching – for example, learning grammar rules – is useful. But Neil, there's limited research about just how effective these apps can be at teaching you a language.","zh":"好的。看来把应用的隐性教学和一些显性教学（比如学语法规则）结合起来是很有用的。不过尼尔，关于这些应用教语言究竟有多有效，目前的研究还很有限。","s":168.77,"e":182.06,"wt":[0,15,30,103,103,106,177,191,253,275,303,339,371,400,458,485,499,515,581,625,661,681,695,718,758,778,805,851,898,952,985,1019,1051,1091,1131,1153,1175,1190,1205,1253,1253,1289]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Indeed, Liz Wonnacott has seen one study she thinks could be interesting though, which compares two groups of Spanish speakers learning English – one using an app and one attending classes – for a similar amount of time. She explains more to BBC Radio 4 programme Sliced Bread.","zh":"确实如此，不过丽兹·沃纳科特看过一项她认为很有意思的研究，它对比了两组学习英语的西班牙语母语者——一组用应用，另一组去上课——两组的学习时长相近。她在 BBC 广播四台节目《Sliced Bread》里做了进一步解释。","s":182.42,"e":199.68,"wt":[0,50,76,130,152,190,214,254,270,318,342,354,424,462,494,545,564,602,614,658,709,766,811,837,879,908,924,947,970,993,1063,1097,1117,1140,1147,1201,1246,1263,1326,1346,1401,1430,1446,1502,1542,1584,1624,1667]},{"speaker":"Professor Elizabeth Wonnacott","en":"The key finding was that in some of the tests, written grammar tests, the Duolingo learners actually improved a bit more, while actually in a test where they were looking at listening the classroom learners appeared to improve more. So, it's really promising , but of course it's one particular language, in one particular app, against one particular school.","zh":"核心发现（key finding）是，在某些测试——也就是书面语法测试中，多邻国学习者进步反而略多一些，而在一项考察听力的测试中，课堂学习者看起来进步更大。所以这确实很有希望（promising），但当然，这只是针对某一种特定语言、某一款特定应用，与某一所特定学校做的对比。","s":199.68,"e":220.02,"wt":[0,20,40,86,104,116,136,150,166,188,237,283,329,368,389,432,481,530,579,585,603,688,722,722,722,724,748,778,808,824,856,882,938,960,1026,1080,1092,1156,1206,1258,1272,1300,1344,1436,1482,1504,1512,1544,1574,1589,1659,1708,1722,1750,1774,1828,1890,1911,1981]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Liz says the key finding from the research is that the app users improved more on written tests, but the classroom learners improved more on listening tests. A key finding is the most important conclusion of a piece of research.","zh":"丽兹说，这项研究的核心发现（key finding）是应用用户在书面测试中进步更大，而课堂学习者在听力测试中进步更大。key finding 指的是一项研究中最重要的结论。","s":220.12,"e":233.44,"wt":[0,21,52,68,110,149,172,198,242,260,274,304,338,373,429,457,471,520,555,576,604,670,713,766,792,805,864,916,922,942,988,1001,1021,1100,1106,1180,1196,1213,1240,1254]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Liz says the study is promising , which means it shows signs of being positive.","zh":"丽兹说这项研究结果很有希望（promising），意思是它显现出积极向好的迹象。","s":233.44,"e":238,"wt":[0,21,49,70,106,120,163,184,220,256,270,306,342,356,392]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Yes. However, Liz points out the limits of the study. It's only looking at one particular language, one particular app and one particular language school. The repetition of one particular here means specific or individual, and Liz would like to see research on more apps, more types of teaching and also more different languages.","zh":"是的。不过，丽兹也指出了这项研究的局限性：它只研究了某一种特定（one particular）语言、某一款特定应用和某一所特定语言学校。这里反复使用 one particular，意思是“具体的、单个的”，丽兹希望能看到针对更多应用、更多教学方式以及更多不同语言的研究。","s":238.06,"e":257.37,"wt":[0,16,68,90,134,156,178,222,237,258,295,323,352,404,426,458,514,584,598,665,685,705,725,792,846,906,926,993,1006,1026,1098,1139,1166,1223,1237,1309,1330,1351,1387,1415,1429,1450,1502,1521,1549,1586,1607,1634,1650,1704,1719,1747,1775,1837]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Apps can help us learn some parts of a language, but in this study they weren't as successful at improving listening skills, and so Liz concludes that combining learning in apps with some other ways of studying is the best way to try to improve your language skills.","zh":"应用可以帮助我们学习语言的某些部分，但在这项研究中，它们在提高听力技能方面没那么成功，因此丽兹得出结论：将应用学习与其他学习方法结合起来，才是努力提升语言能力的最佳途径。","s":257.37,"e":273.18,"wt":[0,36,57,85,99,133,161,195,215,222,279,295,311,335,383,406,449,474,531,566,621,657,750,773,788,810,887,918,991,1036,1045,1073,1101,1129,1164,1192,1206,1263,1277,1298,1326,1347,1361,1382,1396,1446,1474,1531]},{"speaker":"Professor Elizabeth Wonnacott","en":"Probably moving forwards, some combination of human teaching and these platforms to give us that repetition is probably going to be the right direction.","zh":"展望未来，把真人教学和能为我们提供高频重复练习的这类平台结合起来，很可能才是正确的方向。","s":273.3,"e":282.16,"wt":[0,55,103,168,200,289,305,345,414,429,454,502,518,534,542,568,624,698,744,744,744,749,771,802]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"As teachers, we'd also suggest doing things that help you improve different skills – speaking, listening, reading and writing. So, listening to podcasts or trying to practise speaking with a language exchange partner could be things to combine with learning in apps. Find what works for you.","zh":"作为老师，我们也建议你多做一些能全面提升听、说、读、写各项技能的事情。比如，听播客或者试着和语伴练习口语，都可以和应用学习搭配起来。找到最适合自己的方式。","s":282.16,"e":298.56,"wt":[0,15,76,106,136,192,233,261,285,306,327,370,422,447,463,524,592,635,653,723,736,773,784,835,896,896,952,956,1011,1037,1043,1097,1151,1199,1233,1256,1296,1322,1361,1389,1434,1459,1487,1515,1543,1578,1599]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"I think it's time to find out the answer to the quiz question, Neil. I asked you which language you can't learn on Duolingo... and you were right. It's c) Elvish, but you can learn Latin and Klingon, the fictional language from the TV series Star Trek, on Duolingo. And if you did want to improve your Elvish, we did find an app specifically for that.","zh":"我觉得该揭晓测验题的答案了，尼尔。我问你哪门语言不能在多邻国上学……你答对了！是 c) 精灵语。不过你在多邻国上确实可以学拉丁语和克林贡语——也就是电视剧《星际迷航》里的虚构语言。如果你真的想精进精灵语，我们还真找到了专门教它的应用呢。","s":298.56,"e":320.38,"wt":[0,7,42,69,97,111,139,160,184,215,240,258,293,363,424,432,476,502,546,616,642,685,729,746,815,841,880,896,941,966,1030,1102,1130,1146,1174,1194,1236,1262,1316,1348,1402,1402,1415,1448,1487,1528,1560,1589,1598,1704,1704,1704,1713,1735,1764,1778,1829,1858,1901,1915,1937,1966,1980,2002,2104,2137]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Right. Let's recap the vocabulary we learned in this episode. Implicit means suggested but not communicated directly.","zh":"好了，我们来回顾一下本期学到的词汇。implicit 意思是隐含的、暗示而未直接明说的。","s":320.49,"e":327.84,"wt":[0,26,61,96,117,187,200,239,259,292,364,423,463,527,543,578,656]},{"speaker":"Explicit","en":"is the opposite of implicit . It means something is communicated directly.","zh":"explicit（明确的）是 implicit 的反义词，意思是某事被直接清楚地表达出来。","s":328.45,"e":333.28,"wt":[0,14,36,95,109,141,168,182,219,291,304,387]},{"speaker":"Immersion","en":"is the state of being fully surrounded by something.","zh":"immersion（沉浸）是指完全被某种事物包围的状态。","s":333.8,"e":336.88,"wt":[0,13,33,66,79,112,145,211,224]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"The key finding of a research study is the most important conclusion of the research.","zh":"一项研究的 key finding（核心发现）指的是该研究最重要的结论。","s":336.88,"e":342.12,"wt":[0,28,56,96,112,128,175,221,230,251,279,342,412,425,447]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"And the phrase one particular means specific or individual.","zh":"而短语 one particular 的意思是“某一个具体的、特定的”。","s":342.12,"e":345.93,"wt":[0,21,42,84,105,180,212,289,300]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Once again, our six minutes are up. Test what you've learned with the worksheet on our website.","zh":"我们的六分钟时间又到了。欢迎用我们网站上的练习题测试一下你的学习成果。","s":345.93,"e":351.52,"wt":[0,26,58,129,163,189,189,247,273,287,311,311,334,359,407,423,449]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"And if you're interested to hear the full episode of Sliced Bread about language-learning apps and the science behind them, there's a link in the notes below this episode. Try listening on 0.5 speed or in short sections if you find it too difficult to keep up.","zh":"如果你想收听《Sliced Bread》关于语言学习应用及其背后科学的完整节目，本期节目下方的简介里有链接。如果觉得太难跟不上，可以试试 0.5 倍速或者分段收听。","s":351.52,"e":366.08,"wt":[0,14,26,62,76,88,113,132,157,201,213,244,276,308,416,434,453,472,518,557,583,628,634,660,673,692,724,756,782,856,874,928,958,1021,1126,1140,1164,1212,1246,1260,1282,1304,1322,1358,1364,1379,1410]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Bye for now!","zh":"暂别啦，拜拜！","s":366.08,"e":366.96,"wt":[0,21,44]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Goodbye!","zh":"再见！","s":366.96,"e":367.42,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0p8w5by.mp3"},{"epNum":12,"id":"ep-260903","date":"2026-09-03","title":"Climate change and extreme weather","titleZh":"气候变化与极端天气","topic":"自然气候","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260903","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260903_6_minute_english_storms_and_climate_change_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260903_6_minute_english_storms_and_climate_change_transcript.pdf","summary":"Every year we see damage, death and destruction caused by hurricanes, cyclones and typhoons - tropical storms. Is climate change making this impact worse? Climate change may be making this destruction worse. Phil is joined by BBC weather presenter and meteorologist Sarah Keith-Lucas to learn more about this and teach you some new vocabulary. The clips from this episodes are from our new weather vocabulary series: How we talk about the weather . Watch the videos to learn how to talk about the weather from BBC weather forecasters and presenters","summaryZh":"每年我们都会看到飓风、气旋和台风等热带风暴带来的破坏、死亡与灾难。气候变化正在加剧这种影响吗？答案很可能是肯定的。菲尔邀请了 BBC 气象主播兼气象学家莎拉·基思-卢卡斯一起深入探讨这个话题，并教你一些新词汇。本期节目的音频片段来自我们全新的天气词汇系列《我们如何谈论天气》，观看这些视频可以跟 BBC 气象预报员和主播学习地道的天气表达。","question":"By how much has Earth's temperature increased since 1970. Is it?","questionZh":"自 1970 年以来，地球的平均气温上升了多少？","options":["a) Around 0.5C","b) Around 1C","c) Around 2C"],"optionsZh":["a) 约 0.5 摄氏度","b) 约 1 摄氏度","c) 约 2 摄氏度"],"answer":{"letter":"b","explanationZh":"莎拉在节目结尾揭晓：自 1970 年以来地球陆地和海洋表面的平均气温大约上升了 1 摄氏度，而北极等地区的升温速度则是全球平均水平的三倍。"},"vocab":[{"word":"likelihood","defEn":"how probable something is to happen","pos":"n.","zh":"可能性；概率","exampleEn":"Darren said that if a storm moves more slowly, there will be more likelihood of flooding.","ipa":"/ˈlaɪklihʊd/"},{"word":"landslide","defEn":"where earth, rocks, etc move quickly downhill","pos":"n.","zh":"山体滑坡；塌方","exampleEn":"Yes, landslides are where a large amount of earth or rock moves down a slope quickly.","ipa":"/ˈlændslaɪd/"},{"word":"mudslide","defEn":"a type of landslide where liquid mud moves quickly downhill","pos":"n.","zh":"泥石流","exampleEn":"Mudslides tend to be faster and more liquid.","ipa":"/ˈmʌdslaɪd/"},{"word":"storm surge","defEn":"a sudden rise in sea level that moves inland","pos":"n. 短语","zh":"风暴潮","exampleEn":"A storm surge is a sudden rise in the sea level, that can move inland causing destruction.","ipa":"/stɔːm sɜːdʒ/"},{"word":"sweep in","defEn":"move in quickly and powerfully","pos":"动词短语","zh":"席卷而来；猛烈涌入","exampleEn":"If something sweeps in, it moves in very fast and very powerfully.","ipa":"/swiːp ɪn/"},{"word":"casualty","defEn":"someone killed or injured in an accident, attack or incident","pos":"n.","zh":"伤亡人员；受害者","exampleEn":"So, we had the word casualty, and this is used to talk about people killed or injured by an accident, a military attack, or in this case an extreme weather event.","ipa":"/ˈkæʒuəlti/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"over the long term","zh":"从长远来看；在长期内（雅思大作文探讨气候、经济等趋势的高频表达）","contextEn":"Today I'll explain how a change in the climate over the long term can be seen in the weather."},{"en":"slow down","zh":"放慢速度；减速","contextEn":"Scientists also think storms are slowing down."},{"en":"loss of life","zh":"人员伤亡 / 生命损失（比 deaths 更正式客观，新闻与雅思学术写作常用）","contextEn":"What else causes loss of life in a hurricane, typhoon or cyclone?"},{"en":"lead to","zh":"导致；引发（最实用的因果衔接短语之一）","contextEn":"Just a small increase in average temperature can lead to a much bigger increase in extreme events such as heatwaves, wildfires and floods."}],"sentences":[{"en":"They're not moving across as quickly, and that means if a storm is in the same area for longer, the winds will be stronger for longer, and the rain will last for longer producing more flooding and more likelihood of flooding and landslides and also mudslides .","zh":"它们移动过境的速度不像以前那么快了，这意味着如果一场风暴在同一个地区停留更长时间，强风就会持续更久，降雨也会持续更久，从而引发更多洪涝，也大大增加洪水、山体滑坡和泥石流发生的可能性。","coreEn":"They're not moving across as quickly, and that means the winds will be stronger and the rain will last for longer.","coreZh":"它们移动没那么快了，这意味着风会持续更强、雨会下得更久。","cars":[{"text":"if a storm is in the same area for longer","hook":"状语从句 if","noteZh":"给出前提条件：风暴在同一个地方滞留更久"},{"text":"producing more flooding and more likelihood of flooding and landslides and also mudslides","hook":"-ing 修饰（表顺带结果）","noteZh":"补充说明强风和持久降雨带来的连锁后果：更多洪涝以及更高的滑坡、泥石流风险"}],"tipZh":"英文常在主句结尾挂一个 -ing 分词短语（producing ...）顺带引出自然结果，拆句时把它看成“从而造成……”即可，无需回溯重读。"},{"en":"That potentially could mean that a storm surge which is one element of a storm that produces more casualties than any other, those storm surges could become even more dangerous.","zh":"这可能意味着，风暴潮——也就是风暴中比其他任何因素都造成更多人员伤亡的环节——这些风暴潮可能会变得更加危险。","coreEn":"That could mean that those storm surges could become even more dangerous.","coreZh":"这可能意味着风暴潮会变得更危险。","cars":[{"text":"which is one element of a storm","hook":"关系词 which","noteZh":"顺口给“风暴潮”下定义：它是风暴里的一个组成部分"},{"text":"that produces more casualties than any other","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"套娃式补充：这个组成部分造成的伤亡比其他任何因素都多"}],"tipZh":"真人访谈中经常出现“主语说完插了一大段解释，怕听众忘了又重复一遍主语”（a storm surge ... those storm surges），听力里遇到这种重复别慌，反而帮你重新锚定主干！"},{"en":"A storm surge is where the sea level rises quickly pushed by the strong winds caused by a storm.","zh":"风暴潮是指海平面在风暴引发的强风推动下迅速抬升的现象。","coreEn":"A storm surge is where the sea level rises quickly.","coreZh":"风暴潮就是海平面迅速上升的情况。","cars":[{"text":"pushed by the strong winds","hook":"-ed 修饰（表被动原因）","noteZh":"说明海平面迅速抬升是被什么推起来的——强风"},{"text":"caused by a storm","hook":"-ed 修饰（连环后置修饰）","noteZh":"紧接着修饰 the strong winds，说明这是“由风暴引发的”强风"}],"tipZh":"英语解释自然现象非常爱用连续的过去分词后置修饰（pushed by ... caused by ...），翻译成中文时倒过来顺着说成“在风暴引发的强风推动下”就会非常地道。"},{"en":"Some places are warming much quicker than this such as the Arctic that's warming 3 times faster than the average rate.","zh":"有些地区的升温速度比这快得多，比如北极，它的升温速度是全球平均水平的三倍。","coreEn":"Some places are warming much quicker than this.","coreZh":"有些地方升温比这快得多。","cars":[{"text":"such as the Arctic","hook":"举例短语 such as","noteZh":"顺手举出具体例子——北极地区"},{"text":"that's warming 3 times faster than the average rate","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"紧跟在 the Arctic 后面量化说明快了多少：比平均速度快三倍"}],"tipZh":"雅思听力里经常先抛一个宏观平均数，紧接着用 such as + 倍数对比（3 times faster than ...）给出局部极值，做填空或选择题时要特别留心这种数据对比。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Hello, this is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Phil. It seems that we're hearing about more and more terrible weather, but how exactly is climate change affecting extreme weather? I'm lucky today to be joined by BBC Weather forecaster, meteorologist and presenter, Sarah Keith-Lucas. Hi Sarah.","zh":"大家好，这里是 BBC 学英语的《六分钟英语》，我是菲尔。我们似乎越来越频繁地听到恶劣天气的消息，但气候变化究竟是如何影响极端天气的呢？今天我很荣幸请到了 BBC 气象预报员、气象学家兼主播莎拉·基思-卢卡斯。嗨，莎拉。","s":2.54,"e":27.34,"wt":[0,576,588,616,642,674,710,742,800,829,905,927,1006,1016,1051,1062,1095,1142,1186,1216,1238,1270,1328,1382,1458,1485,1545,1560,1617,1643,1692,1730,1786,1810,1852,1892,1908,1924,1964,1989,2018,2069,2134,2208,2235,2291,2322,2418,2436]},{"speaker":"Sarah Keith-Lucas","en":"Hi Phil. It's great to be here.","zh":"嗨，菲尔。很高兴来到这里。","s":27.4,"e":29.05,"wt":[0,18,56,78,106,116,128]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Sarah's here to help us understand what's happening with extreme weather. She'll also help explain some of the words and expressions used in a new series of BBC Learning English videos all about weather and climate and the language we use to talk about them. You can find the link to them on our webpage, or in the show notes for this podcast. Sarah, let's start with a really important question: what's the difference between climate and weather?","zh":"莎拉今天来帮我们了解极端天气究竟是怎么回事。她还会帮忙讲解 BBC 学英语全新系列视频里用到的一些词汇和表达，这套视频专门讲天气、气候以及我们谈论它们时使用的语言。你可以在我们的网页或本播客的节目简介里找到链接。莎拉，我们先从一个非常重要的问题开始：气候（climate）和天气（weather）有什么区别？","s":29.05,"e":54.23,"wt":[0,40,62,73,95,115,183,240,287,312,355,415,452,477,502,545,570,582,607,646,680,747,774,787,793,813,849,867,887,942,979,1039,1055,1096,1130,1148,1191,1209,1227,1277,1289,1307,1319,1351,1374,1407,1419,1431,1455,1478,1496,1508,1533,1545,1563,1606,1618,1630,1648,1673,1704,1722,1747,1815,1879,1918,1944,1970,1976,2016,2075,2151,2187,2207,2277,2326,2436,2451]},{"speaker":"Sarah Keith-Lucas","en":"That's a great question, Phil. The weather is what you see and feel every day. Climate is more long-term. Today I'll explain how a change in the climate over the long term can be seen in the weather.","zh":"这是个很好的问题，菲尔。天气是你每天看到和感受到的状况，而气候则更偏向长期趋势。今天我会解释，长期的气候变化是如何体现在日常天气里的。","s":54.23,"e":67.06,"wt":[0,40,46,79,146,193,213,265,281,297,321,361,385,434,470,577,587,605,641,740,809,833,873,897,905,953,961,986,1030,1060,1082,1112,1142,1164,1179,1209,1224,1246]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"As always, we will also be learning some key vocabulary to talk about the topic. And, Sarah, it's traditional for us to set a quiz question. Have you got one for us?","zh":"和往常一样，我们也会学习一些讨论这个话题的核心词汇。还有，莎拉，按传统我们会出一道小测验题。你为我们准备好了吗？","s":68.95,"e":78.46,"wt":[0,15,59,75,112,142,161,207,230,247,305,321,345,369,385,462,478,525,552,628,649,663,677,697,705,737,797,821,841,857,881,905]},{"speaker":"Sarah Keith-Lucas","en":"Yes, I have! By how much has Earth's temperature increased since 1970. Is it? a) Around 0.5C b) Around 1C c) Around 2C","zh":"准备好了！自 1970 年以来，地球的气温上升了多少？是：a) 约 0.5 摄氏度，b) 约 1 摄氏度，还是 c) 约 2 摄氏度？","s":78.55,"e":92.59,"wt":[0,31,38,91,106,129,159,205,258,342,411,457,608,626,689,725,906,1019,1051,1191,1257,1313,1374]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"I don't know, about 2 degrees?","zh":"我不太确定，大概 2 度吧？","s":92.59,"e":95.92,"wt":[0,21,68,133,187,211]},{"speaker":"Sarah Keith-Lucas","en":"I'll give the answer at the end of the programme, so listen to the end to find out.","zh":"我会在节目最后公布答案，所以请听到最后来揭晓吧。","s":95.92,"e":100.5,"wt":[0,16,54,70,104,113,128,144,152,168,237,249,302,318,336,368,388,412]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Now, we're going to talk about tropical storms. What do we mean by 'tropical storms', Sarah?","zh":"下面我们来聊聊热带风暴。莎拉，我们说的“热带风暴”是指什么？","s":100.72,"e":107.14,"wt":[0,31,62,94,106,136,167,241,314,350,368,386,422,492,564,592]},{"speaker":"Sarah","en":"Well, those are hurricanes, cyclones and typhoons. They're large rotating storm systems formed over warm tropical waters. These are actually the same, we just use different names depending on where in the world they form. Hurricanes are found in the Atlantic, Cyclones in the Indian Ocean and Typhoons in the West Pacific. As the world warms, climate scientists think that these storms will get more destructive.","zh":"其实就是飓风、气旋和台风。它们是在温暖的热带海域上空形成的大型旋转风暴系统。这三者本质上是一样的，只是根据它们在世界上形成的海域不同而叫法不同：大西洋的叫飓风，印度洋的叫气旋，西太平洋的叫台风。随着全球变暖，气候科学家认为这些风暴的破坏力会越来越强。","s":107.27,"e":134.42,"wt":[0,40,69,104,195,261,285,386,419,461,528,550,578,628,661,697,753,880,902,918,967,991,1036,1049,1075,1095,1154,1193,1233,1257,1294,1307,1334,1367,1401,1478,1514,1530,1553,1567,1613,1706,1776,1785,1810,1853,1896,1922,1992,2009,2035,2070,2161,2185,2201,2241,2306,2344,2412,2446,2473,2509,2548,2569,2595,2622]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"In one of our new Learning English for weather videos, meteorologist Darren Bett tells us about another worrying change.","zh":"在我们全新的天气英语学习视频中，气象学家达伦·贝特还告诉了我们另一个令人担忧的变化。","s":134.52,"e":142.88,"wt":[0,11,34,49,72,95,157,211,234,288,351,428,483,519,563,581,625,688,758]},{"speaker":"Darren Bett","en":"Scientists also think storms are slowing down. They're not moving across as quickly, and that means if a storm is in the same area for longer, the winds will be stronger for longer, and the rain will last for longer producing more flooding and more likelihood of flooding and landslides and also mudslides .","zh":"科学家们还认为风暴正在减速。它们移动过境的速度不像以前那么快，这意味着如果一场风暴在同一个地区停留更久，强风持续的时间就会更长，降雨也会下得更久，从而引发更多洪涝，也增加了洪水、山体滑坡（landslides）以及泥石流（mudslides）发生的可能性（likelihood）。","s":142.96,"e":165.12,"wt":[0,96,152,192,253,269,319,368,417,438,481,524,544,607,628,662,696,710,741,788,804,818,846,892,936,968,1053,1068,1102,1129,1142,1197,1217,1278,1298,1322,1352,1380,1408,1424,1513,1577,1609,1680,1716,1746,1827,1843,1899,1927,2016,2047,2088,2161]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Darren said that if a storm moves more slowly, there will be more likelihood of flooding. Likelihood is the noun that we use to say how probable, or how likely something is to happen. Darren also told us about landslides and mudslides .","zh":"达伦说，如果风暴移动变慢，发生洪涝的可能性（likelihood）就会更高。likelihood 是一个名词，用来表示某事发生的概率有多大。达伦还提到了山体滑坡（landslides）和泥石流（mudslides）。","s":165.12,"e":181.84,"wt":[0,47,80,114,126,149,208,236,264,321,357,380,399,427,504,528,629,706,721,743,773,803,817,840,855,877,904,960,976,1016,1089,1134,1146,1178,1258,1307,1339,1371,1392,1457,1536,1562,1624]},{"speaker":"Sarah Keith-Lucas","en":"Yes, landslides are where a large amount of earth or rock moves down a slope quickly. Mudslides tend to be faster and more liquid. Both of these things can happen after particularly heavy rain and can cause damage to buildings. People can also be killed or injured by them.","zh":"是的，landslide（山体滑坡）是指大量泥土或岩石快速顺坡滑落；而 mudslide（泥石流）往往速度更快，含水量更高、更呈流态。这两者都可能在特大暴雨后发生，不仅会损毁建筑物，还可能造成人员伤亡。","s":181.84,"e":202.39,"wt":[0,42,127,152,194,202,248,280,304,352,376,457,491,516,533,576,661,738,772,789,806,863,888,937,1068,1068,1070,1118,1175,1203,1264,1311,1382,1416,1455,1477,1499,1538,1582,1600,1745,1783,1807,1839,1855,1902,1918,1973,1989]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"What else causes loss of life in a hurricane, typhoon or cyclone?","zh":"在飓风、台风或气旋中，还有什么会导致人员丧生？","s":202.39,"e":206.39,"wt":[0,21,49,91,119,133,160,175,182,259,308,322]},{"speaker":"Sarah Keith-Lucas","en":"Well, something else that is very dangerous is a storm surge. Let's hear again from my colleague Darren Bett.","zh":"另一个非常危险的因素是风暴潮（storm surge）。我们再来听听我同事达伦·贝特的介绍。","s":206.39,"e":213.12,"wt":[0,25,71,81,97,123,193,211,225,259,319,414,440,465,496,521,533,589,626]},{"speaker":"Storm surge","en":", that's that body of water that gets swept inland and produces widespread flooding. Climate change is warming our seas, and the sea levels are rising. That potentially could mean that a storm surge which is one element of a storm that produces more casualties than any other, those storm surges could become even more dangerous.","zh":"风暴潮（storm surge），指的是被卷向内陆并引发大范围洪涝的那股海水。气候变化正在使海洋升温，海平面也在上升。这可能意味着，作为风暴中比其他任何因素都造成更多人员伤亡（casualties）的环节，风暴潮可能会变得更加危险。","s":213.74,"e":239.72,"wt":[0,50,74,98,130,155,187,208,244,286,341,365,434,555,639,699,750,767,827,852,890,906,945,1030,1034,1130,1161,1262,1322,1353,1425,1466,1494,1537,1580,1622,1640,1716,1727,1744,1752,1802,1818,1921,1946,2027,2059,2083,2147,2187,2227,2282,2306,2395,2431,2476]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"So, we had the word casualty , and this is used to talk about people killed or injured by an accident, a military attack, or in this case an extreme weather event.","zh":"这里我们听到了 casualty（伤亡人员）这个词，它用来指在事故、军事袭击，或像本例中的极端天气事件中受伤或遇难的人。","s":239.84,"e":249.57,"wt":[0,22,44,77,110,160,212,240,248,272,280,304,312,336,361,402,452,469,528,545,564,642,648,696,728,744,752,767,800,817,861,914]},{"speaker":"Sarah Keith-Lucas","en":"Darren talked about storm surges . A storm surge is where the sea level rises quickly pushed by the strong winds caused by a storm. This water comes onto land with an enormous amount lot of force, and storm surges can be up to 10 metres high. Buildings get destroyed, people can drown.","zh":"达伦谈到了风暴潮（storm surges）。风暴潮是指海平面在风暴引发的强风推动下迅速上升。这些海水以极其巨大的冲击力涌上陆地，风暴潮最高可达 10 米。建筑会被摧毁，人们也可能溺亡。","s":249.57,"e":271.08,"wt":[0,47,90,131,187,244,302,308,349,391,407,449,474,499,541,587,708,753,763,776,834,873,889,899,955,1051,1090,1138,1175,1204,1234,1251,1276,1339,1370,1389,1411,1456,1483,1528,1582,1611,1635,1643,1675,1724,1765,1823,1898,1923,2027,2059,2083]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"We heard about the storm sweeping inland. Something sweeping in is moving quickly and powerfully. And you can hear more from Darren in the videos on our website. Sarah, how about the answer to the quiz question you asked earlier?","zh":"我们听到了风暴“席卷内陆（sweeping inland）”。如果某物 sweep in，意思就是它迅猛而有力地涌入。大家可以在我们网站的视频里听到更多达伦的讲解。莎拉，该揭晓你之前出的测验题答案了吧？","s":271.08,"e":285.02,"wt":[0,11,49,84,110,148,225,284,332,388,411,426,477,525,549,631,655,680,703,735,772,796,840,850,871,913,927,948,1018,1067,1088,1124,1142,1179,1191,1212,1253,1290,1307,1336]},{"speaker":"Sarah Keith-Lucas","en":"OK, Phil. So, I asked by how much has the world's average temperature increased since 1970.","zh":"好的，菲尔。我刚才问的是，自 1970 年以来全球平均气温上升了多少。","s":285.04,"e":292.16,"wt":[0,37,104,135,152,200,232,263,312,335,359,397,442,514,573,608]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"And I thought that it was around two celsius.","zh":"我猜的是大约 2 摄氏度。","s":292.23,"e":295.58,"wt":[0,25,35,73,99,113,151,213,243]},{"speaker":"Sarah Keith-Lucas","en":"It's actually around one degree celsius. 1C is just an average across all of earth's land and ocean surface. Some places are warming much quicker than this such as the Arctic that's warming 3 times faster than the average rate. Just a small increase in average temperature can lead to a much bigger increase in extreme events such as heatwaves, wildfires and floods.","zh":"实际上大约是 1 摄氏度。这 1 摄氏度只是整个地球陆地和海洋表面的平均值。有些地方升温比这快得多，比如北极的升温速度是平均水平的三倍。平均气温哪怕只上升一点点，也会导致热浪、野火和洪水等极端天气事件大幅增加。","s":295.76,"e":319.5,"wt":[0,24,88,144,184,224,343,375,391,423,439,494,541,565,584,632,672,688,728,815,834,873,892,928,960,994,1016,1071,1092,1106,1127,1169,1211,1261,1296,1336,1374,1400,1416,1456,1503,1522,1528,1560,1612,1625,1670,1744,1762,1787,1799,1805,1832,1889,1940,1965,2022,2076,2110,2127,2222,2288,2316]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Oh wow, so that is an important difference. OK, let's recap the vocabulary we've learned, starting with likelihood , which is noun that we use to talk how probable or how likely something is to happen.","zh":"哇，原来这背后差别这么大。好了，我们来回顾一下今天学到的词汇，首先是 likelihood（可能性），这个名词用来表示某事发生的概率有多大。","s":319.59,"e":332.86,"wt":[0,19,39,54,84,99,114,181,281,321,364,409,425,473,506,581,629,683,742,769,807,831,864,885,900,921,935,1000,1021,1079,1097,1145,1193,1233,1241,1257]},{"speaker":"Landslides","en":"and mudslides which can occur after heavy rainfall make earth or mud move downhill very fast.","zh":"landslides（山体滑坡）和 mudslides（泥石流）可能在强降雨后发生，导致泥土或泥浆高速顺坡滑落。","s":333.75,"e":341.5,"wt":[0,26,104,148,174,225,257,289,386,420,465,491,527,570,656,699]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"A storm surge is a sudden rise in the sea level, that can move inland causing destruction.","zh":"storm surge（风暴潮）是指海平面突然上升，并涌向内陆造成破坏。","s":341.52,"e":347.49,"wt":[0,8,51,96,117,128,167,200,216,231,249,291,325,350,383,436,496]},{"speaker":"Sarah Keith-Lucas","en":"If something sweeps in, it moves in very fast and very powerfully.","zh":"如果某物 sweep in（席卷而来），意思是它以极快的速度和强大的力量涌入。","s":347.49,"e":352.95,"wt":[0,16,105,168,188,208,259,283,323,379,403,443]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"And finally, a casualty is someone who is injured or killed by an accident, attack, or something like an extreme weather event. Once again, our six minutes are up.","zh":"最后，casualty（伤亡人员）是指在事故、袭击或极端天气事件中受伤或丧生的人。我们的六分钟时间又到了。","s":352.95,"e":362.96,"wt":[0,18,77,85,152,169,228,253,273,321,345,385,405,425,490,523,537,600,623,641,693,739,787,801,870,885,905,953,973]},{"speaker":"Sarah Keith-Lucas","en":"Goodbye!","zh":"再见！","s":363.01,"e":363.68,"wt":[0]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Thank for joining us today, Sarah! Bye!","zh":"谢谢你今天的加入，莎拉！拜拜！","s":363.76,"e":365.84,"wt":[0,29,43,74,85,109,152]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0p7p91r.mp3"},{"epNum":13,"id":"ep-260827","date":"2026-08-27","title":"How do we describe smells?","titleZh":"我们该如何形容气味？","topic":"文化教育","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260827","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260827_6_minute_english_smells_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260827_6_minute_english_smells_worksheet_.pdf","summary":"What is the worst smell in the world? And what is the best smell? Are there any smells that you like, but that your friends or family hate? Smells are all around us all the time, but the English language is often quite general in the way it describes smells, as opposed to other senses like sounds or tastes. But why is this? Neil and Georgie discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","summaryZh":"世界上最难闻的气味是什么？最好闻的又是什么？有没有哪种气味你很喜欢，但朋友或家人却讨厌？气味无时无刻不围绕着我们，然而与声音或味觉等其他感官相比，英语在形容气味时往往非常笼统。这究竟是为什么呢？尼尔和乔治亚将探讨这个问题，并教你一些新词汇。","question":"What is the word for the smell of old books?","questionZh":"英文中表示“旧书气味”的词是哪一个？","options":["a) vellichor","b) petrichor","c) nidor"],"optionsZh":["a) vellichor","b) petrichor","c) nidor"],"answer":{"letter":"a","explanationZh":"尼尔在节目末尾确认正确答案是 a) vellichor（旧书店的怀旧书香气）；b) petrichor 是雨后泥土的清香，c) nidor 则是烹饪肉类和油脂散发的气味。"},"vocab":[{"word":"stinky (also: reeking, honking, humming, miffy, funky)","defEn":"(informal) having a bad smell","pos":"adj.","zh":"发臭的；难闻的（口语）","exampleEn":"I could say, \"Oh this perfume you think is really fragrant, I think is stinky,\" and both can be true.","ipa":"/ˈstɪŋki/"},{"word":"fragrant","defEn":"having a sweet or pleasant smell","pos":"adj.","zh":"芬芳的；香气扑鼻的","exampleEn":"I might think a perfume is stinky, an adjective meaning bad smelling, while Neil might find the same perfume fragrant, meaning pleasant smelling.","ipa":"/ˈfreɪɡrənt/"},{"word":"subjective","defEn":"based on your own personal opinions and feelings","pos":"adj.","zh":"主观的（基于个人感受与看法的）","exampleEn":"Asifa thinks this is because, unlike tastes and colours, smell words are subjective – they're based on personal opinions and feelings rather than objective truth.","ipa":"/səbˈdʒektɪv/"},{"word":"visual","defEn":"related to seeing","pos":"adj.","zh":"视觉的；与看有关的","exampleEn":"Asifa's research shows that, here in the UK at least, people talk about visual or auditory things much more than they talk about smells.","ipa":"/ˈvɪʒuəl/"},{"word":"auditory","defEn":"related to hearing","pos":"adj.","zh":"听觉的；与听有关的","exampleEn":"The adjective visual means related to seeing, and auditory means related to hearing.","ipa":"/ˈɔːdɪtəri/"},{"word":"shut (something) down","defEn":"stop something","pos":"动词短语","zh":"叫停；打断；压制（谈话或行为）","exampleEn":"When children start talking about smells, especially unpleasant ones, parents often react by shutting the conversation down.","ipa":"/ʃʌt daʊn/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"can't beat (something)","zh":"没有什么比得上……（日常口语夸赞某事物“绝佳、无可替代”的地道说法）","contextEn":"Well, I don't think you can beat the smell of a freshly opened packet of ground coffee."},{"en":"that's not the case for ...","zh":"……的情况并非如此（雅思写作/口语里非常干净利落的转折表达）","contextEn":"Words like spicy, sweet or salty create a common idea that we can all agree on, but that's not the case for smells."},{"en":"remark on","zh":"评论；提起；说到（比 talk about 更精准，常指顺口评价某现象）","contextEn":"Young children in the UK are often interested in the sense of smell and they'll remark on it, and the parent will shut it down."},{"en":"dedicated to","zh":"专门用于……的；致力于……的","contextEn":"Along with other European languages, English doesn't have much vocabulary dedicated to the different qualities of actual smells."}],"sentences":[{"en":"One smell many people love is the smell of old books and the sentimental feeling it gives them for second-hand bookshops.","zh":"有一种气味深受许多人喜爱，那就是旧书的气味，以及它带给人们的对二手书店的那种怀旧情感。","coreEn":"One smell is the smell of old books and the sentimental feeling.","coreZh":"有一种气味就是旧书的味道以及那种怀旧感。","cars":[{"text":"many people love","hook":"省略 that 的定语从句","noteZh":"修饰 One smell，说明这是“很多人喜欢的”一种气味"},{"text":"it gives them for second-hand bookshops","hook":"省略 that 的定语从句 + 介词短语","noteZh":"修饰 the sentimental feeling，说明这种怀旧感是“它带给人们对二手书店的”情感"}],"tipZh":"一个句子里连续出现两处省略 that 的定语从句（smell [that] many people love / feeling [that] it gives them），快速识别“名词紧跟主语+动词”的组合就能一眼剥离。"},{"en":"Words like spicy, sweet or salty create a common idea that we can all agree on, but that's not the case for smells.","zh":"像“辣的”、“甜的”或“咸的”这类词能建立起我们大家都有共识的概念，但形容气味的词却不是这样。","coreEn":"Words create a common idea, but that's not the case for smells.","coreZh":"这些词建立起共同概念，但气味并非如此。","cars":[{"text":"like spicy, sweet or salty","hook":"介词短语 like","noteZh":"举例说明是指哪些味觉词汇"},{"text":"that we can all agree on","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"修饰 a common idea，解释这个共同概念是“大家都能达成一致的”"}],"tipZh":"用 that's not the case for ... 做对比收尾，既避免重复前面一整句话，又显得表达地道紧凑，雅思写作 Task 2 可直接套用。"},{"en":"So, if we compare how often people talk about visual things, or auditory things, or tastes or smells, what we find is we talk a lot about what we see and very rarely about what we smell.","zh":"因此，如果我们对比人们谈论视觉事物、听觉事物、味觉或嗅觉的频率，我们会发现大家经常聊看到的东西，却极少谈论闻到的气味。","coreEn":"What we find is we talk a lot about what we see and very rarely about what we smell.","coreZh":"我们的发现是：我们常聊看到的，很少聊闻到的。","cars":[{"text":"if we compare how often people talk about visual things, or auditory things, or tastes or smells","hook":"状语从句 if + 宾语从句 how often","noteZh":"给出研究对比的前提：比较人们谈论这四种感官体验的频率"},{"text":"what we find","hook":"主语从句 what","noteZh":"把“我们发现的结果”作为车头主语，突出后面的核心结论"}],"tipZh":"学术访谈和雅思听力 Part 4 极其喜欢用 What we find is ... 来引出实验结论，听到这个信号词时务必竖起耳朵抓后面的对比内容。"},{"en":"Along with other European languages, English doesn't have much vocabulary dedicated to the different qualities of actual smells.","zh":"与其他欧洲语言一样，英语中并没有太多专门用来形容气味本身不同特质的词汇。","coreEn":"English doesn't have much vocabulary.","coreZh":"英语里没有太多（这类）词汇。","cars":[{"text":"Along with other European languages,","hook":"介词短语（句首伴随状语）","noteZh":"先交代范围：并非只有英语这样，其他欧洲语言也同样如此"},{"text":"dedicated to the different qualities of actual smells","hook":"-ed 修饰","noteZh":"后置修饰 vocabulary，限定是“专门用于描述实际气味不同特质的”词汇"}],"tipZh":"过去分词短语 dedicated to ... 紧跟在名词后作定语，比写成 which is dedicated to ... 更精炼，是雅思阅读高分句和写作升级的利器。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Hello, this is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Neil.","zh":"大家好，这里是 BBC 学英语的《六分钟英语》，我是尼尔。","s":2.54,"e":11.92,"wt":[0,560,574,596,622,646,696,734,788,814,873,887]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"And I'm Georgie. In English, there are many ways to describe smells. Poet Michael Rosen wanted to find words describing bad smells, and asked listeners of his BBC Radio 4 programme Word of Mouth for some suggestions.","zh":"我是乔治亚。在英语里，有很多方式可以形容气味。诗人迈克尔·罗森想搜集一些形容臭味的词，于是向他主持的 BBC 广播四台节目《Word of Mouth》的听众征集建议。","s":12,"e":25.44,"wt":[0,18,44,108,119,171,198,217,240,263,274,328,426,447,492,534,564,592,620,657,714,752,800,820,845,890,908,924,985,1015,1038,1109,1140,1155,1194,1217,1248]},{"speaker":"Michael Rosen","en":"Fusty, reeking, toiking, honking, humming, funky...","zh":"发霉的、恶臭熏天的、馊臭的、臭烘烘的、发臭的、怪味难闻的……","s":25.44,"e":28.66,"wt":[0,53,120,170,232,282]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Smells are everywhere, whether they're bad smells like rotten eggs and sweaty feet, or pleasant ones like lavender and freshly baked bread. What smells do you especially like or dislike, Georgie?","zh":"气味无处不在，无论是像臭鸡蛋、汗脚这样难闻的味道，还是像薰衣草、刚出炉的面包那样怡人的香气。乔治亚，你有没有特别喜欢或特别讨厌的气味？","s":28.86,"e":41.56,"wt":[0,38,64,120,136,168,186,235,267,316,347,371,421,469,485,550,582,614,678,701,780,830,922,959,1002,1016,1037,1109,1138,1152,1215]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Well, I really enjoy the smell of leather, but something I really love is the smell of rain, which has a name. Do you know it, Neil?","zh":"嗯，我非常喜欢皮革的味道，不过我最爱的是雨后的气味，它甚至有一个专门的名字。尼尔，你知道叫什么吗？","s":41.58,"e":50.66,"wt":[0,40,48,96,136,160,200,216,288,312,384,392,440,472,488,512,558,583,637,689,720,730,806,818,836,850,852]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Erm, I don't think I do actually.","zh":"呃，说实话我好像不知道。","s":50.75,"e":53.38,"wt":[0,42,46,91,136,145,181]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"It's called petrichor. It's that smell of the ground just after it's rained.","zh":"它叫 petrichor，就是刚下过雨后地面散发出来的那种泥土清香。","s":53.5,"e":57.55,"wt":[0,26,58,120,140,160,186,197,206,266,304,336,352]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Mmm.","zh":"嗯，真不错。","s":57.55,"e":57.96,"wt":[0]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Neil, what about you? Which smells do you like?","zh":"尼尔，你呢？你喜欢哪些气味？","s":57.96,"e":60.58,"wt":[0,42,62,87,120,152,184,198,221]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, I don't think you can beat the smell of a freshly opened packet of ground coffee.","zh":"嗯，我觉得没有什么能比得上刚拆开一包现磨咖啡粉时的那股香味了。","s":60.72,"e":65.97,"wt":[0,34,41,76,104,118,142,170,200,236,248,258,307,349,391,405,447]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Ooh, that's a good one. In this episode, we'll be learning the language of smells using some useful new words and phrases. And remember, you can find all the vocabulary plus a quiz and worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"哦，那个味道确实很棒。在本期节目中，我们将通过一些实用的新词和短语来学习描述气味的语言。记住，你可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 找到全部词汇、小测验和练习题。","s":65.97,"e":79.66,"wt":[0,37,73,83,83,185,194,209,243,261,268,299,315,355,373,427,467,486,527,547,581,601,671,690,729,741,767,790,805,820,871,915,926,977,994,1063,1078,1101,1167]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"But first, I have a question for you, Georgie. One smell many people love is the smell of old books and the sentimental feeling it gives them for second-hand bookshops. In fact, there's even a word for it, but what is it? Is it: a) vellichor, b) petrichor, or c) nidor?","zh":"但首先我有个问题要考考你，乔治亚。有一种气味深受许多人喜爱，那就是旧书的气味，以及它带给人们对二手书店的那种怀旧感。其实英语里甚至有一个专门的词来形容它，是哪一个呢？a) vellichor，b) petrichor，还是 c) nidor？","s":79.72,"e":98.57,"wt":[0,17,58,73,82,108,152,178,202,286,312,346,373,413,440,453,473,507,530,546,583,605,676,708,759,773,810,839,861,941,1036,1049,1092,1107,1135,1144,1181,1206,1256,1280,1306,1322,1394,1406,1434,1458,1568,1598,1711,1730,1759]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Well, we already know that petrichor is the smell of the ground after rain. I'm going to go with a) vellichor.","zh":"嗯，我们已经知道 petrichor 是雨后泥土的气味了。那我选 a) vellichor。","s":98.57,"e":106.41,"wt":[0,43,60,119,149,187,253,267,295,345,372,398,437,483,563,577,604,618,630,664,682]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, we'll find out at the end of the programme. Listening to Michael Rosen's list – funky, reeking, humming and the rest – you might think English is good at describing smells, but in fact, all those words really mean is bad smelling.","zh":"好，我们节目最后揭晓。听着迈克尔·罗森念的那一串词——funky、reeking、humming 等等——你可能会觉得英语很擅长形容气味，但实际上，这些词归根结底表达的都是“难闻、发臭”。","s":106.41,"e":122,"wt":[0,51,94,128,154,171,191,201,201,219,335,371,391,425,475,527,572,616,679,736,755,774,794,811,830,884,913,961,982,1003,1028,1087,1173,1197,1216,1261,1283,1320,1357,1397,1440,1453,1480]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"In English, we often describe smells by comparing them to other objects. For example, something nice might smell like a rose, while something bad might smell like rotten eggs, but there are few words to describe the actual smell itself.","zh":"在英语中，我们常常通过把气味类比成其他物体来进行描述。比如，好闻的东西可能闻起来像玫瑰，难闻的东西可能闻起来像臭鸡蛋，但用来直接形容气味本身的词却寥寥无几。","s":122.2,"e":137.2,"wt":[0,20,60,92,128,176,210,234,294,316,348,383,456,480,543,562,586,626,666,698,706,754,794,850,886,920,960,984,1028,1104,1118,1142,1168,1192,1231,1247,1300,1334,1381,1420]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Oxford University psychologist Asifa Majid has researched the subject of smell extensively. Here, she tells Michael Rosen a little of what she's found out on BBC Radio 4 programme Word of Mouth.","zh":"牛津大学心理学家阿西法·马吉德对嗅觉这一课题做过广泛研究。下面她在 BBC 广播四台节目《Word of Mouth》中向迈克尔·罗森分享了自己的一些发现。","s":137.26,"e":149.64,"wt":[0,34,107,190,230,266,288,361,383,432,447,484,594,631,653,690,742,779,786,831,846,868,894,904,972,987,1009,1045,1068,1154,1174,1188]},{"speaker":"Asifa Majid","en":"So, if we think about things like stinky or fragrant , they're definitely telling us that something is good or bad. But unlike colour terms, it doesn't really seem to be about the object it's describing. It seems to be a subjective thing. I could say, \"Oh this perfume you think is really fragrant , I think is stinky ,\" and both can be true.","zh":"如果我们想想 stinky（难闻的）或 fragrant（芬芳的）这类词，它们无疑是在告诉我们某物好闻还是难闻。但与颜色词不同，它们似乎并不真正指向被描述物体的客观属性，而更像是一种主观的（subjective）东西。我完全可以说：“哦，你觉得特别香（fragrant）的这款香水，我觉得臭死了（stinky）”，而且我们俩说的都可以是对的。","s":149.77,"e":170.74,"wt":[0,8,21,34,63,89,97,145,210,223,308,353,389,405,451,475,501,537,563,583,605,645,665,705,801,801,855,855,890,917,930,943,993,1006,1045,1071,1143,1159,1177,1177,1177,1234,1287,1359,1368,1417,1458,1479,1495,1637,1655,1696,1712,1741,1768,1783,1801,1819,1839,1916,1943,1969,1995,2011,2039]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Describing smell is not like describing other senses. I might think a perfume is stinky , an adjective meaning bad smelling, while Neil might find the same perfume fragrant , meaning pleasant smelling. Both can be true. The point is that neither stinky nor fragrant say anything specific about the perfume's actual smell.","zh":"形容气味和形容其他感官不同。我可能觉得一款香水很臭（stinky，这个形容词意思是气味难闻），而尼尔可能觉得同一款香水很芬芳（fragrant，意思是气味怡人）。两种说法都可以成立。关键在于，无论是 stinky 还是 fragrant，都没有具体说明这款香水到底是什么味道。","s":170.74,"e":190.9,"wt":[0,54,88,132,160,194,242,282,346,354,393,418,440,484,508,548,574,594,650,694,730,806,832,883,910,926,952,992,1044,1108,1142,1160,1210,1290,1316,1332,1354,1410,1428,1459,1471,1496,1536,1577,1595,1650,1682,1724,1778,1818,1842,1914,1962]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"With our sense of taste, it's different. Words like spicy, sweet or salty create a common idea that we can all agree on, but that's not the case for smells. Asifa thinks this is because, unlike tastes and colours, smell words are subjective – they're based on personal opinions and feelings rather than objective truth.","zh":"但味觉就不一样了。像辣、甜、咸这样的词能建立起我们大家公认的概念，可气味却不是这样。阿西法认为，这是因为与味觉和颜色不同，气味词汇是主观的（subjective）——它们基于个人观点和感受，而非客观事实。","s":191,"e":211.28,"wt":[0,18,44,72,102,153,181,260,270,300,404,450,469,512,563,576,628,652,684,700,724,748,788,826,840,864,864,898,926,952,1028,1060,1099,1133,1144,1192,1235,1278,1299,1363,1399,1435,1506,1535,1549,1598,1634,1648,1722,1770,1791,1849,1892,1921,1982]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"This might explain why our attitude to talking about smells is so different. Here's Asifa again, sharing some examples of how we talk about smells with BBC Radio 4.","zh":"这或许能解释为什么我们对谈论气味的态度如此不同。我们再来听阿西法向 BBC 广播四台分享一些关于人们如何谈论气味的例子。","s":211.5,"e":221.89,"wt":[0,20,57,103,122,141,193,206,252,285,324,337,350,433,489,547,589,641,671,724,746,768,783,813,850,895,920,947,984]},{"speaker":"Asifa Majid","en":"So, if we compare how often people talk about visual things, or auditory things, or tastes or smells, what we find is we talk a lot about what we see and very rarely about what we smell. Young children in the UK are often interested in the sense of smell and they'll remark on it, and the parent will shut it down . They'll say, \"That's disgusting! Put it down.\"","zh":"因此，如果我们比较人们谈论视觉（visual）事物、听觉（auditory）事物、味觉或嗅觉的频率，就会发现我们经常谈论看到的东西，却极少谈论闻到的东西。英国的小孩子往往对嗅觉很感兴趣，也会顺口提起，但家长通常会立刻制止（shut it down），说：“太恶心了！快放下。”","s":221.89,"e":245.86,"wt":[0,11,27,43,157,193,193,217,250,291,341,391,407,474,523,540,591,613,676,697,721,753,772,788,820,828,852,892,924,940,964,988,1020,1068,1109,1140,1166,1257,1307,1372,1388,1412,1521,1555,1595,1629,1641,1641,1653,1669,1709,1733,1789,1852,1862,1877,1900,1971,1971,2001,2032,2047,2066,2091,2091,2163,2224,2267,2281,2307]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Asifa's research shows that, here in the UK at least, people talk about visual or auditory things much more than they talk about smells. The adjective visual means related to seeing, and auditory means related to hearing. The adjective for things related to smelling is olfactory.","zh":"阿西法的研究表明，至少在英国，人们谈论视觉（visual）或听觉（auditory）事物的频率远高于谈论气味。形容词 visual 表示与视觉有关的，auditory 表示与听觉有关的；而表示与嗅觉有关的形容词是 olfactory。","s":245.93,"e":263.86,"wt":[0,44,100,135,177,205,219,240,254,268,317,359,387,422,464,487,541,575,604,633,662,689,719,743,827,851,921,963,1007,1049,1079,1137,1154,1217,1256,1311,1326,1405,1424,1475,1493,1527,1566,1578,1627,1672]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Maybe this is because of how we're raised by our parents. When children start talking about smells, especially unpleasant ones, parents often react by shutting the conversation down . The phrase to shut something down means to stop it.","zh":"也许这和我们从小受父母的教育方式有关。当孩子开始聊起气味——尤其是难闻的气味时，父母常常会直接叫停对话（shutting the conversation down）。短语 shut something down 的意思就是“制止、停止某事”。","s":264.18,"e":277.86,"wt":[0,34,48,74,74,116,136,178,200,216,253,320,340,377,400,442,466,518,583,648,687,732,764,796,809,861,880,961,1011,1078,1098,1130,1152,1184,1226,1266,1288,1314,1330]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Along with other European languages, English doesn't have much vocabulary dedicated to the different qualities of actual smells. There are many ways of talking about smells, especially unpleasant ones, but only a few words which describe actual smells themselves. A good example of a word which does this is musty – a damp, stale smell you might find in an old bookshop. And Neil, that reminds me of your question.","zh":"和其他欧洲语言一样，英语里并没有太多专门用来形容气味本身不同特质的词汇。谈论气味（尤其是臭味）的方法有很多，但真正描述气味本身的词却寥寥无几。一个很好的例子是 musty（发霉味的）——就是你在旧书店里可能会闻到的那种潮湿、陈旧的味道。哦，尼尔，这让我想起了你的问题。","s":278.12,"e":303.62,"wt":[0,22,42,80,133,201,245,284,306,326,401,457,482,495,554,610,626,665,723,755,774,800,826,839,885,917,982,1042,1100,1152,1175,1200,1210,1231,1266,1301,1357,1399,1441,1541,1545,1577,1624,1634,1658,1684,1712,1720,1778,1806,1860,1900,1907,1936,1973,2010,2032,2072,2122,2136,2156,2186,2298,2319,2356,2381,2426,2438,2450,2475]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"That's right. I asked you what the word is, which means the smell of old books.","zh":"没错。我问你哪个词表示旧书的气味。","s":303.72,"e":309.32,"wt":[0,32,80,91,124,144,170,191,218,231,264,304,340,400,424,460]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"I said it was vellichor.","zh":"我猜的是 vellichor。","s":309.37,"e":311.77,"wt":[0,61,61,76,115]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, Georgie, you are absolutely right.","zh":"乔治亚，你完全答对了！","s":311.77,"e":314.84,"wt":[0,43,121,147,173,262]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yay!","zh":"耶！","s":314.84,"e":315.22,"wt":[0]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Petrichor, as you said earlier, is the smell of the earth after rain. And nidor is the smell of cooking meat and fat. OK. It's time for a recap of the vocabulary we've learned. The adjective stinky means foul smelling, while the adjective fragrant means pleasant smelling.","zh":"正如你之前说的，petrichor 是雨后大地的气息，而 nidor 是烹饪肉类和油脂的气味。好了，我们来回顾一下学过的词汇。形容词 stinky 意思是发臭难闻的，而 fragrant 意思是芬芳宜人的。","s":315.22,"e":333.18,"wt":[0,70,85,108,139,209,222,247,278,292,324,363,402,461,483,514,529,554,611,624,687,723,750,806,833,860,888,909,916,950,964,985,1058,1095,1176,1195,1252,1315,1350,1438,1508,1508,1556,1569,1639,1674,1752]},{"speaker":"Subjective","en":"things are based on your own personal opinions and feelings rather than objective facts.","zh":"subjective（主观的）事物是基于你个人的观点和感受，而非客观事实。","s":334.08,"e":339.56,"wt":[0,40,60,87,99,120,140,206,258,281,343,389,420,482]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"The adjective visual describes things related to seeing, while auditory means related to hearing. Things related to smelling are called olfactory.","zh":"形容词 visual 用来形容与视觉相关的事物，auditory 表示与听觉相关的，而与嗅觉相关的叫做 olfactory。","s":339.66,"e":349.06,"wt":[0,16,68,115,168,204,245,262,356,390,442,470,515,526,602,646,680,714,776,803,840]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"And finally, the phrasal verb to shut something down means to stop it. Once again, our six minutes are up, but there's still time to head over to our website, bbclearningenglish.com, for a quiz and worksheet containing all the vocabulary we've discussed. See you again soon! But for now, it's goodbye.","zh":"最后，动词短语 shut something down 意思是制止、叫停某事。我们的六分钟时间又到了，但你仍然可以访问我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com，做做包含今天所有讨论词汇的小测验和练习题。我们很快再见！现在就先说再见啦。","s":349.16,"e":366.24,"wt":[0,20,72,90,138,138,192,219,264,298,326,348,362,428,448,476,488,526,560,578,603,622,665,696,716,733,759,783,795,814,880,1101,1114,1120,1145,1163,1219,1281,1299,1317,1379,1416,1508,1522,1538,1572,1614,1628,1648,1686,1692]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Goodbye!","zh":"再见！","s":366.24,"e":367.14,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0p6j2hz.mp3"},{"epNum":14,"id":"ep-260820","date":"2026-08-20","title":"Sharing the road with driverless cars","titleZh":"与无人驾驶汽车共享道路","topic":"科技未来","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260820","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260820_6_minute_english_sharing_the_road_with_driverless_cars_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260820_6_minute_english_sharing_the_road_with_driverless_cars_transcript.pdf","summary":"Imagine crossing a road and turning to see a car heading towards you... without a driver! While this might seem scary, driverless cars are already operating safely in many cities around the world. These autonomous cars contain huge amounts of technology to help them drive alongside other road users carefully. But will humans behave as carefully around them? Can we communicate clearly with these 'robotaxis', or are we simply incompatible? Phil and Pippa discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","summaryZh":"想象一下，你正在过马路，转头却看见一辆车朝你驶来……而且车里没有司机！虽然这听起来有点吓人，但无人驾驶汽车已经在全球许多城市安全运营。这些自动驾驶汽车搭载了大量技术，帮助它们在其他道路使用者身边谨慎行驶。但人类在它们周围会同样谨慎吗？我们能与这些“自动驾驶出租车”顺畅沟通吗，还是彼此根本格格不入？菲尔和皮帕将讨论这一话题，并教你一些新词汇。","question":"Which was the first city to have fully driverless robotaxis on the streets?","questionZh":"哪座城市是全球第一个在街道上运营全无人驾驶出租车的城市？","options":["a) Philadelphia","b) Phoenix","c) San Francisco"],"optionsZh":["a) 费城","b) 菲尼克斯（凤凰城）","c) 旧金山"],"answer":{"letter":"b","explanationZh":"菲尔在节目结尾揭晓：美国亚利桑那州的菲尼克斯（Phoenix）早在 2017 年就成为全球首个允许无人类安全员的自动驾驶汽车在公共道路上运营的城市。"},"vocab":[{"word":"(make) eye contact","defEn":"look directly into someone's eyes","pos":"短语","zh":"眼神交流；目光接触","exampleEn":"Usually, a driver makes eye contact with me. They look directly into my eyes and then wave me across.","ipa":"/aɪ ˈkɒntækt/"},{"word":"wave","defEn":"raise your hand and move it from side to side as a greeting or to communicate with someone","pos":"v.","zh":"挥手；招手示意","exampleEn":"If you're outside a vehicle right now, you can see the driver inside the vehicle, you can wave to them...","ipa":"/weɪv/"},{"word":"alert","defEn":"warn someone of potential danger","pos":"v.","zh":"提醒；向……发出警报","exampleEn":"It's important to alert people to driverless cars. To alert somebody means to warn them about a potential danger.","ipa":"/əˈlɜːt/"},{"word":"animation","defEn":"display of moving images","pos":"n.","zh":"动画；动态图像显示","exampleEn":"Some cars do this with animations – displays of moving images.","ipa":"/ˌænɪˈmeɪʃən/"},{"word":"glance","defEn":"quick and brief look","pos":"n.","zh":"一瞥；匆匆看一眼","exampleEn":"They only have time for a glance. A glance at something means a quick look.","ipa":"/ɡlɑːns/"},{"word":"jaywalking","defEn":"(American English) crossing the road where it is not allowed or without taking care","pos":"n.","zh":"乱穿马路（美式英语）","exampleEn":"In South Korea, for example, jaywalking is illegal, whereas in the UK, you know, we tend to jaywalk a lot.","ipa":"/ˈdʒeɪwɔːkɪŋ/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"as yet","zh":"到目前为止；至今（常用于否定句，相当于 so far / yet，书面与英式口语皆常用）","contextEn":"Luckily, Phil, you usually arrive safely at work and as yet there are no driverless cars in London."},{"en":"get used to","zh":"习惯于；适应（注意后面接名词或 doing）","contextEn":"I think maybe as we get more used to these technologies, we'll get more used to how to deal with them and when to cross."},{"en":"in terms of","zh":"就……而言；在……方面（雅思口语和写作极其常用的话题限定短语）","contextEn":"What kind of signals worked in terms of alerting pedestrians and runners and cyclists to the intentions of the vehicle?"},{"en":"when it came to","zh":"当谈到……；一涉及到……（由 when it comes to 变来的过去时态，极适合做对比过渡）","contextEn":"The animations – they work well for pedestrians, but when it came to runners and cyclists, they have much less time to make their decisions, so if you do a quick glance at a vehicle, you don't really see the animation ."}],"sentences":[{"en":"But when you come to an autonomous vehicle, there's no person in the driving seat, so how do you negotiate whether they're going to go, whether they're going to let you go?","zh":"但当你面对一辆自动驾驶汽车时，驾驶座上没有人，那你要怎么和它“商量”到底是它先走，还是它让你先过呢？","coreEn":"There's no person in the driving seat, so how do you negotiate?","coreZh":"驾驶座上没人，你该怎么协商通行？","cars":[{"text":"when you come to an autonomous vehicle,","hook":"状语从句 when","noteZh":"设定具体场景：当你面对一辆自动驾驶汽车时"},{"text":"whether they're going to go, whether they're going to let you go","hook":"并列宾语从句 whether","noteZh":"具体说明需要协商的内容：究竟是车先走，还是车让你先走"}],"tipZh":"这里 negotiate 不是商业谈判，而是指交通参与者在路口通过默契“协调谁先走”；两个 whether 并列省去 or，口语感非常强。"},{"en":"The animations – they work well for pedestrians, but when it came to runners and cyclists, they have much less time to make their decisions, so if you do a quick glance at a vehicle, you don't really see the animation .","zh":"动态画面对行人效果很好，但一轮到跑步者和骑行者，他们做决定的时间要少得多，所以如果你只是匆匆瞥一眼车辆，根本看不清画面内容。","coreEn":"The animations work well for pedestrians, but runners and cyclists have much less time, so you don't really see the animation.","coreZh":"动态画面对行人有效，但跑步者和骑行者时间少得多，因此根本看不清画面。","cars":[{"text":"when it came to runners and cyclists,","hook":"状语从句 when it came to","noteZh":"把讨论对象从行人切换到跑步者和骑行者"},{"text":"to make their decisions","hook":"不定式 to do","noteZh":"修饰 much less time，说明是“用来做决定的”时间"},{"text":"if you do a quick glance at a vehicle,","hook":"状语从句 if","noteZh":"给出日常动作条件：只是匆匆扫一眼车子"}],"tipZh":"这个长句由 but 和 so 串起“对比 + 因果”两条清晰逻辑链，雅思听力中常考“为什么某方案不适合骑行者”，答案就藏在 but 后面。"},{"en":"Unlike Britain, jaywalking is rare in South Korea, making the country safer for autonomous cars.","zh":"与英国不同，乱穿马路在韩国很少见，这使得该国对自动驾驶汽车来说更加安全。","coreEn":"Jaywalking is rare in South Korea.","coreZh":"乱穿马路在韩国很少见。","cars":[{"text":"Unlike Britain,","hook":"介词短语 Unlike","noteZh":"放在句首迅速拉出对比对象——英国"},{"text":"making the country safer for autonomous cars","hook":"-ing 修饰（表顺带结果）","noteZh":"点出前面这一现象自然带来的结果：让韩国对自动驾驶汽车更安全"}],"tipZh":"“Unlike A, B ..., making ...”是极高性价比的雅思写作句型，一句话同时完成对比陈述和结果推演。"},{"en":"Phoenix, Arizona, was the first city in the world to have self-driving cars operating on public roads without a human safety driver, and that was in 2017.","zh":"美国亚利桑那州的菲尼克斯是全球第一个让自动驾驶汽车在没有人类安全员的情况下在公共道路上运营的城市，而那是在 2017 年。","coreEn":"Phoenix was the first city in the world to have self-driving cars operating, and that was in 2017.","coreZh":"菲尼克斯是全球首个运营自动驾驶汽车的城市，那是在 2017 年。","cars":[{"text":"to have self-driving cars operating on public roads","hook":"不定式 to do + -ing 修饰","noteZh":"修饰 the first city，说明“第一”具体指什么：拥有在公共道路上运行的自动驾驶汽车"},{"text":"without a human safety driver","hook":"介词短语 without","noteZh":"补充关键限定条件：完全没有人类安全员随车"}],"tipZh":"英语里表达“第一个做某事的……”，固定在序数词名词（the first city）后面接不定式 to do，是听力细节题和阅读定位的高频结构。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Hello, this is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Phil.","zh":"大家好，这里是 BBC 学英语的《六分钟英语》，我是菲尔。","s":2.56,"e":13,"wt":[0,594,624,666,696,730,770,810,884,904,964,992]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"And I'm Pippa.","zh":"我是皮帕。","s":13.1,"e":14.22,"wt":[0,26,54]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Here at 6 Minute English, we love discussing new tech. One of the most controversial technologies of recent years is driverless cars, also known as self-driving cars, autonomous cars or robotaxis.","zh":"在《六分钟英语》，我们很喜欢探讨新科技。近年来最具争议的技术之一就是无人驾驶汽车，也被称为自动驾驶汽车、自主驾驶车或自动驾驶出租车。","s":14.29,"e":28.57,"wt":[0,26,43,69,122,163,200,236,325,351,413,425,435,437,505,609,617,657,701,735,809,863,957,995,1033,1048,1173,1236,1277,1309,1353]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Many people say they wouldn't feel safe in a car without a human driver, but there are concerns from other road users too – pedestrians, runners and cyclists. How will driverless cars interact with them? It's a problem Professor Stephen Brewster, from Glasgow University, has been working on. He explained more to BBC World Service programme Tech Life.","zh":"很多人表示坐在没有人类司机的车里会觉得不安全，但其他道路使用者——行人、跑步者和骑行者——也有担忧。无人驾驶汽车将如何与他们互动？格拉斯哥大学的斯蒂芬·布鲁斯特教授一直在研究这个问题。他向 BBC 国际台节目《Tech Life》作了进一步解释。","s":29.72,"e":52.9,"wt":[0,30,70,98,122,144,198,238,246,272,295,328,352,384,462,482,488,552,578,608,634,668,708,742,778,906,956,988,1080,1095,1121,1176,1210,1260,1282,1341,1364,1371,1423,1489,1541,1599,1628,1680,1760,1775,1795,1832,1912,1928,1988,2012,2032,2071,2102,2154,2190,2235]},{"speaker":"Stephen Brewster","en":"If you're outside a vehicle right now, you can see the driver inside the vehicle, you can wave to them, they can see you, you can make eye contact . But when you come to an autonomous vehicle, there's no person in the driving seat, so how do you negotiate whether they're going to go, whether they're going to let you go?","zh":"现在如果你在车外，你能看见车里的司机，你可以向他们挥手（wave），他们也能看见你，你们可以进行眼神交流（make eye contact）。但当你面对一辆自动驾驶汽车时，驾驶座上没有人，那你要怎么和它协调，判断到底是它先走，还是让你先过呢？","s":52.9,"e":68.6,"wt":[0,6,30,61,71,94,112,124,134,152,164,180,205,230,242,286,296,310,337,350,378,398,422,446,446,456,472,492,526,578,625,654,662,674,690,690,728,728,834,864,885,920,928,948,978,1040,1054,1078,1094,1118,1264,1300,1306,1347,1363,1402,1425,1447,1462,1469,1482,1514]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"On my walk to work, I need to cross the road. Usually, a driver makes eye contact with me. They look directly into my eyes and then wave me across. To wave means to raise your hand and move it from side to side. It's a gesture for saying hello, or a way of telling a person to do something.","zh":"我走路去上班的路上需要过马路。通常司机会和我有眼神交流（makes eye contact），他们会直视我的眼睛，然后挥手（wave）示意我过去。wave 的意思是举起手左右摆动，既可以是打招呼的手势，也可以用来示意别人去做某件事。","s":68.7,"e":87.3,"wt":[0,18,36,68,87,120,129,158,176,178,198,302,304,348,390,424,453,499,525,551,570,603,655,681,694,720,739,774,826,843,958,975,1009,1052,1069,1112,1146,1180,1206,1238,1256,1282,1306,1342,1404,1420,1445,1492,1515,1552,1592,1606,1613,1634,1648,1697,1704,1746,1760,1774]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Luckily, Phil, you usually arrive safely at work and as yet there are no driverless cars in London. But, Phil, how would you feel about crossing that road in front of a robotaxi?","zh":"幸运的是，菲尔，你平时都能平安到公司，而且伦敦目前还没有无人驾驶汽车。不过菲尔，如果要在自动驾驶出租车前面过那条马路，你会是什么感觉？","s":87.55,"e":99.33,"wt":[0,41,95,123,177,221,269,284,315,338,353,376,425,460,475,571,610,634,737,768,814,837,851,873,917,967,1007,1037,1069,1091,1119,1119,1153]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Well, maybe a little bit nervous. I might wait for it to go and go behind it or something.","zh":"嗯，可能会有点紧张。我大概会等它先开过去，然后从它车后绕过去之类的。","s":100.2,"e":104.88,"wt":[0,17,49,55,93,112,169,175,207,232,251,263,275,287,306,318,356,368,380]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Mmm... yeah, I get that. I think maybe as we get more used to these technologies, we'll get more used to how to deal with them and when to cross. But in this episode, we investigate the tech helping driverless cars communicate on the roads. We'll learn some useful new words and phrases, and as usual you'll find all the vocabulary on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"嗯……是的，我能理解。我觉得也许随着我们对这些技术越来越熟悉，我们就会更习惯怎么应对它们以及什么时候过马路。本期节目中，我们就来探究帮助无人驾驶汽车在道路上与人沟通的技术。我们会学一些实用的新词和短语，照例你可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 找到全部词汇。","s":104.98,"e":129.9,"wt":[0,35,86,94,119,187,192,232,274,291,308,333,367,401,418,460,550,554,628,663,697,714,739,770,792,822,842,856,860,898,992,1007,1021,1050,1100,1180,1193,1214,1243,1293,1364,1393,1472,1486,1526,1608,1634,1664,1700,1736,1768,1790,1814,1880,1907,1921,1957,1999,2027,2048,2069,2156,2170,2200,2270]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"But first, I have a question for you, Pippa. As you mentioned, we don't have self-driving cars here in London yet. So, which was the first city to have fully driverless robotaxis on the streets? Was it: a) Philadelphia, b) Phoenix, or c) San Francisco?","zh":"不过先考考你一个问题，皮帕。正如你提到的，我们伦敦现在还没有自动驾驶汽车。那么，哪座城市是第一个在街头运营全无人驾驶出租车的城市？是：a) 费城，b) 菲尼克斯（凤凰城），还是 c) 旧金山？","s":130.16,"e":149.94,"wt":[0,28,76,142,142,192,207,236,264,356,370,392,408,462,496,518,568,592,608,624,674,798,811,858,886,914,961,999,1018,1056,1103,1198,1283,1302,1330,1440,1450,1476,1499,1632,1678,1757,1779,1801,1835]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"I'm going to say b) Phoenix because I think it's quite a car-friendly city.","zh":"我选 b) 菲尼克斯，因为我觉得那是一座对汽车非常友好的城市。","s":149.97,"e":156.32,"wt":[0,25,67,91,126,235,285,288,298,349,389,440,463,560]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"OK. Well, we will find out the answer, but at the end of the programme. Because accidents are often caused by not seeing the oncoming danger, Professor Brewster wants to make driverless cars more visible. He discussed his work with BBC Tech Life's presenter Chris Vallance.","zh":"好的，我们会在节目最后揭晓答案。由于事故往往是因为没有察觉迎面而来的危险，布鲁斯特教授希望让无人驾驶汽车的意图更加显眼易见。他与 BBC《Tech Life》主持人克里斯·瓦朗斯探讨了自己的研究工作。","s":156.49,"e":174.96,"wt":[0,25,59,76,110,143,159,185,245,270,287,312,337,354,379,463,519,543,567,605,637,691,732,771,783,856,919,973,1032,1063,1079,1111,1183,1215,1255,1339,1357,1379,1405,1429,1469,1517,1549,1639,1725,1749]},{"speaker":"Chris Vallance","en":"What kind of signals worked in terms of alerting pedestrians and runners and cyclists to the intentions of the vehicle?","zh":"在提醒（alerting）行人、跑步者和骑行者注意车辆意图方面，哪种信号最有效？","s":175.22,"e":183.8,"wt":[0,26,30,92,156,211,229,260,293,366,467,494,538,580,666,672,692,757,772,794]},{"speaker":"Stephen Brewster","en":"We tested two things. We tested animations and fixed, coloured light rings. The animations – they work well for pedestrians, but when it came to runners and cyclists, they have much less time to make their decisions, so if you do a quick glance at a vehicle, you don't really see the animation .","zh":"我们测试了两种方案：动态画面（animations）和固定的彩色光环。动态画面对行人效果很好，但一轮到跑步者和骑行者，他们做决定的时间要少得多，所以如果你只是匆匆瞥一眼（glance）车辆，根本看不清画面内容。","s":183.9,"e":199.76,"wt":[0,12,41,56,86,108,172,261,287,331,402,440,522,541,579,596,612,636,636,658,726,738,746,762,780,800,848,912,990,1003,1016,1034,1052,1070,1079,1097,1119,1174,1176,1186,1196,1208,1228,1268,1300,1308,1328,1373,1392,1406,1438,1438,1478,1539]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"It's important to alert people to driverless cars. To alert somebody means to warn them about a potential danger. Some cars do this with animations – displays of moving images. For example, a green man walking to signal it's safe to cross.","zh":"提醒（alert）人们注意无人驾驶汽车非常重要。alert somebody 意思是向某人警示潜在的危险。有些汽车通过动画（animations）——即动态图像显示——来做到这一点，比如显示一个正在行走的绿人，示意可以安全过马路。","s":199.76,"e":216.3,"wt":[0,28,98,133,177,230,248,318,402,419,458,520,558,574,605,636,670,682,750,824,856,884,901,922,959,1019,1047,1102,1121,1165,1240,1262,1302,1326,1358,1405,1464,1483,1516,1524,1542,1564]},{"speaker":"Animations","en":"alert pedestrians but are not suitable for runners and cyclists, who are travelling too fast to react. They only have time for a glance . A glance at something means a quick look. So, instead Stephen's team used coloured light rings, similar to traffic lights, to warn cyclists and runners.","zh":"动画（animations）可以提醒行人，却不适合跑步者和骑行者，因为他们行进速度太快，来不及反应，只来得及匆匆瞥一眼（glance）。glance 的意思就是快速看一眼。因此，斯蒂芬的团队改用类似红绿灯的彩色光环来警示骑行者和跑步者。","s":217.46,"e":236.7,"wt":[0,48,119,137,155,175,221,239,281,354,354,365,383,458,482,518,544,634,652,682,710,718,732,798,838,879,884,965,1004,1041,1078,1097,1132,1212,1230,1282,1342,1372,1402,1463,1501,1539,1592,1607,1660,1705,1720,1757,1827,1850]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Culture is another problem. The meaning of body language can change. For example, hand waving can mean 'please proceed' in one culture, but 'thank you' in another.","zh":"文化差异是另一个难题。肢体语言的含义可能会因文化而异。比如，挥手在一种文化里可能表示“请先行”，而在另一种文化里却表示“谢谢”。","s":236.88,"e":248,"wt":[0,76,108,158,230,252,288,304,337,382,410,470,496,556,590,634,662,701,752,812,829,854,942,969,1005,1026,1040]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Ammar Al-Taie is a researcher at the Advanced Institute of Science and Technology in South Korea. Here, Ammar tells BBC World Service's Tech Life why Korean culture is especially suited to self-driving cars.","zh":"阿马尔·阿尔-塔伊是韩国科学技术院（KAIST）的一名研究员。下面，阿马尔向 BBC 国际台《Tech Life》解释了为什么韩国文化特别适合自动驾驶汽车。","s":248,"e":262.92,"wt":[0,35,76,89,156,164,177,197,252,314,327,375,395,464,477,534,633,673,706,752,780,826,900,937,974,1058,1126,1126,1208,1233,1296,1325,1428]},{"speaker":"Ammar Al-Taie","en":"In South Korea, for example, jaywalking is illegal, whereas in the UK, you know, we tend to jaywalk a lot.","zh":"比如在韩国，乱穿马路（jaywalking）是违法的，而在英国，你知道，我们经常习惯乱穿马路。","s":262.96,"e":268.64,"wt":[0,15,44,72,102,143,184,232,264,302,326,352,373,390,418,433,456,474,516,532]},{"speaker":"Jaywalking","en":"is an American English word. It means crossing the road at a place where crossing is not allowed or without taking care. Unlike Britain, jaywalking is rare in South Korea, making the country safer for autonomous cars.","zh":"jaywalking（乱穿马路）是一个美式英语词，指在不允许过街的地方或未加注意的情况下横穿马路。与英国不同，乱穿马路在韩国很少见，这使得韩国对自动驾驶汽车而言更加安全。","s":269.65,"e":283.8,"wt":[0,16,32,97,154,213,228,261,313,332,358,371,377,410,443,495,508,527,573,586,632,679,775,809,873,938,953,983,998,1035,1072,1117,1139,1191,1228,1250,1321]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"We've heard a lot about making driverless cars safer, and with plans to introduce them to London later this year, we'll soon see how safe they really are.","zh":"关于如何让无人驾驶汽车更安全，我们已经听到了很多介绍，随着今年晚些时候伦敦计划引入无人驾驶汽车，我们很快就能亲眼看看它们到底有多安全了。","s":284,"e":294.22,"wt":[0,26,26,76,101,137,181,254,283,376,399,428,428,486,493,537,550,603,647,678,719,763,799,826,853,889,925,978]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Now, isn't it time to reveal the answer to your question, Phil?","zh":"现在，菲尔，是不是该揭晓你那道题的答案了？","s":294.42,"e":297.93,"wt":[0,14,47,60,87,100,140,159,200,213,240,294]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"I asked you which city was the first to have self-driving cars on the road, and you said it was Phoenix... and that is the correct answer. Phoenix, Arizona, was the first city in the world to have self-driving cars operating on public roads without a human safety driver, and that was in 2017. Let's recap the vocabulary we've learned, starting with eye contact – looking directly into someone's eyes.","zh":"我问你哪座城市第一个让自动驾驶汽车上路，你猜的是菲尼克斯……这就是正确答案！亚利桑那州的菲尼克斯是全球第一个让无人类安全员的自动驾驶汽车在公共道路上运营的城市，而且那是在 2017 年。下面我们回顾一下学过的词汇，首先是 eye contact（眼神交流）——直视某人的眼睛。","s":297.93,"e":324.92,"wt":[0,4,38,60,96,125,147,169,205,271,271,335,364,378,400,429,451,483,529,544,575,649,680,807,819,835,848,957,999,1051,1073,1095,1132,1161,1176,1199,1235,1250,1279,1367,1396,1471,1493,1528,1566,1603,1618,1656,1699,1743,1751,1751,1757,1829,1945,1985,2031,2054,2067,2099,2191,2211,2259,2315,2358,2383,2437,2513,2551,2607]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"To wave at someone is a way of saying hi, by raising your hand and waving it from side to side.","zh":"wave at someone（向某人挥手）是打招呼的一种方式，即举起手左右摆动。","s":325.08,"e":330.88,"wt":[0,10,39,53,104,118,125,147,161,205,266,304,304,313,342,364,408,422,451,498,514]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"If you alert someone, you warn them of potential danger.","zh":"如果你 alert（提醒、警示）某人，就是警告对方有潜在危险。","s":330.98,"e":334.5,"wt":[0,16,34,75,127,149,178,207,221,288]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"An animation is a display made up of moving images.","zh":"animation（动画）是由动态图像组成的画面显示。","s":334.6,"e":338.36,"wt":[0,16,96,108,124,187,218,240,259,313]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"A glance is a quick, brief look at something.","zh":"glance（一瞥）是指对某物快速、短暂地看一眼。","s":338.37,"e":341.56,"wt":[0,5,60,79,88,135,182,219,238]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"And finally, the American English term jaywalking means crossing the road where you're not supposed to. Once again, our six minutes are up. If you'd like to hear more programmes about technology, you'll find plenty on our tech topic page, all on our website. And there you'll also find a quiz and worksheet for this episode. Visit bbclearningenglish.com. We'll see you again soon, but for now it's goodbye.","zh":"最后，美式英语词 jaywalking（乱穿马路）指在不该过马路的地方横穿马路。我们的六分钟时间又到了。如果你想收听更多科技类节目，可以在我们网站的科技专题页找到很多内容，同时还有本期节目的小测验和练习题。请访问 bbclearningenglish.com。我们很快再见，现在先道别啦。","s":341.86,"e":366.92,"wt":[0,15,65,86,143,193,228,292,378,384,405,432,474,496,516,572,638,669,702,722,742,789,806,844,859,878,896,912,936,978,1018,1060,1116,1156,1192,1236,1249,1268,1311,1340,1370,1392,1407,1429,1544,1562,1562,1586,1664,1664,1704,1734,1762,1773,1795,1838,1948,1956,2214,2238,2252,2252,2280,2314,2332,2356,2382,2408]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Goodbye!","zh":"再见！","s":366.92,"e":367.37,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0p5h6cg.mp3"},{"epNum":15,"id":"ep-260813","date":"2026-08-13","title":"Who does the housework?","titleZh":"家里谁来做家务？","topic":"职场生活","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260813","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260813_6_minute_english_who_does_the_housework_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260813_6_minute_english_who_does_the_housework_transcript.pdf","summary":"Unloading the dishwasher, mopping the floor, cleaning the loo... what is your most hated household chore? We all know there are jobs around the house which have to be done regularly, even if they are sometimes boring or unpleasant. But who tends to do the most amount of jobs in your household? And is there anyone who isn't pulling their weight...? Pippa and Neil discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","summaryZh":"清空洗碗机、拖地、刷马桶……你最讨厌的家务活是什么？我们都知道家里总有一些必须定期处理的琐事，哪怕它们枯燥乏味甚至令人不快。但在你家里，通常都是谁干的活最多？有没有人没有尽到自己的那份力？皮帕和尼尔将探讨这个话题，并教你一些新词汇。","question":"According to a survey conducted by Good Housekeeping magazine earlier in 2026, what is the most hated household chore in Britain?","questionZh":"根据《Good Housekeeping》杂志 2026 年初进行的一项调查，英国人最讨厌的家务活是哪一项？","options":["a) deep cleaning the oven","b) scrubbing the toilet","c) cleaning the windows"],"optionsZh":["a) 深度清洁烤箱","b) 刷马桶","c) 擦窗户"],"answer":{"letter":"c","explanationZh":"尼尔在节目结尾揭晓：英国人最讨厌的家务甚至不是刷烤箱，而是 c) 擦窗户（cleaning the windows），调查中有 23% 的受访者选择了这一项。"},"vocab":[{"word":"chores","defEn":"tasks around the home, such as cleaning, washing and ironing, that have to be done regularly","pos":"n. (复数常用)","zh":"家务活；日常琐事","exampleEn":"When it comes to household chores, jobs like cleaning, washing clothes or looking after the kids, it's often women who do most of the work.","ipa":"/tʃɔːz/"},{"word":"fair share","defEn":"reasonable amount","pos":"n. 短语","zh":"应尽的一份；合理份额","exampleEn":"So, you're a family man, Neil. Do you do your fair share of household chores?","ipa":"/feə ʃeə/"},{"word":"women's work","defEn":"negative term for the boring and repetitive tasks which are traditionally or stereotypically done by women","pos":"n. 短语","zh":"“女人的活儿”（带贬义或刻板印象的说法，指繁琐重复的家务）","exampleEn":"On Rote, cleaning, cooking and sweeping are considered women's work – a negative term for boring and repetitive tasks which are traditionally done by women.","ipa":"/ˈwɪmɪnz wɜːk/"},{"word":"division of labour","defEn":"way of organising work where different tasks are done by different groups of people","pos":"n. 短语","zh":"劳动分工","exampleEn":"Many cultures have a division of labour – a way of organising work where different tasks are done by different groups of people.","ipa":"/dɪˈvɪʒən əv ˈleɪbə/"},{"word":"take a breather","defEn":"(idiom) stop what you are doing for a short time to rest","pos":"习语","zh":"喘口气；歇一小会儿","exampleEn":"Sometimes mums need to take a breather – an informal way of saying stop and rest a while.","ipa":"/teɪk ə ˈbriːðə/"},{"word":"pull your weight","defEn":"work as hard as everyone else doing the same job","pos":"习语","zh":"尽到自己的本分；出自己应出的一份力","exampleEn":"Anamarija also thinks women appreciate it when their husbands pull their weight.","ipa":"/pʊl jɔː weɪt/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"when it comes to","zh":"当谈到……；一说到……（雅思口语开启观点的王牌开场短语）","contextEn":"When it comes to household chores , jobs like cleaning, washing clothes or looking after the kids, it's often women who do most of the work."},{"en":"take some of the pressure off","zh":"分担一部分压力；帮某人减压（职场与生活都极为实用）","contextEn":"Project worker Anamarija Sočo leads workshops encouraging dads and sons to take some of the pressure off mums."},{"en":"result in","zh":"导致；带来……结果（比 cause 更中性，正面负面结果都能接）","contextEn":"Both these NGO projects agree about one thing – men doing more to help around the house results in happier homes, even if that means cleaning the toilet now and then."},{"en":"now and then","zh":"偶尔；时不时地（地道口语表达，相当于 occasionally / from time to time）","contextEn":"Both these NGO projects agree about one thing – men doing more to help around the house results in happier homes, even if that means cleaning the toilet now and then."}],"sentences":[{"en":"Figures from UN Women, an organisation promoting gender equality, suggest that every day women around the world spend three hours more than men on unpaid care and domestic work.","zh":"致力于促进性别平等的联合国妇女署的数据显示，全球女性每天在无偿照护和家务劳动上花费的时间比男性多出三个小时。","coreEn":"Figures suggest that women spend three hours more than men on unpaid care and domestic work.","coreZh":"数据显示，女性在无偿照护和家务上比男性多花三小时。","cars":[{"text":"from UN Women, an organisation promoting gender equality,","hook":"介词短语 + 逗号同位语插入语","noteZh":"先交代数据来源（联合国妇女署），再顺手用同位语说明这是一家促进性别平等的机构"},{"text":"every day women around the world","hook":"时间状语 + 地点修饰","noteZh":"限定频率（每天）和范围（全世界的女性）"}],"tipZh":"雅思阅读和听力里引用机构数据时，几乎必在机构名后跟一个双逗号同位语解释背景；阅读时一眼跨过插入语直奔主干动词 suggest，速度会快一倍。"},{"en":"It is expected that the tasks done in the house, like holding the child, feeding the child, washing dishes, cooking, will all be done by the mother.","zh":"人们理所当然地认为，家里的活儿——比如抱孩子、喂孩子、洗碗、做饭——全都应该由母亲来做。","coreEn":"It is expected that the tasks will all be done by the mother.","coreZh":"人们默认这些活儿全都会由母亲来做。","cars":[{"text":"done in the house","hook":"-ed 修饰","noteZh":"修饰 the tasks，限定是在“家里做的”活儿"},{"text":", like holding the child, feeding the child, washing dishes, cooking,","hook":"逗号插入语（like 举例）","noteZh":"列举四项具体家务，剥离后主语 the tasks 和谓语 will all be done 立刻对齐"}],"tipZh":"看到主语和谓语之间插了一长串 like A, B, C, D 的举例，先把这节“举例车厢”打包跳过去，主语和后面的被动谓语就能无缝衔接。"},{"en":"You might think this only happens in traditional societies, but research suggests that women in wealthy countries also spend much more time on unpaid care work than men.","zh":"你可能会以为这只发生在传统社会中，但研究表明，富裕国家的女性在无偿照护工作上花费的时间也比男性多得多。","coreEn":"You might think this only happens in traditional societies, but research suggests that women also spend much more time than men.","coreZh":"你或许以为这只发生在传统社会，但研究表明（富裕国家的）女性花的时间也比男性多得多。","cars":[{"text":"in wealthy countries","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"限定女性所在地区——富裕国家，与前半句的“传统社会”形成强烈反差"},{"text":"on unpaid care work","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"说明多花的时间具体用在了什么事情上（spend time on sth）"}],"tipZh":"“You might think ..., but research suggests ...”是典型的先破后立结构，雅思大作文反驳常见误解（让步段）时拿来即用，非常自然有力。"},{"en":"Both these NGO projects agree about one thing – men doing more to help around the house results in happier homes, even if that means cleaning the toilet now and then.","zh":"这两个非政府组织的项目在一点上达成共识——男性多分担家务会带来更幸福的家庭，哪怕这意味着偶尔得刷刷马桶。","coreEn":"Both these NGO projects agree about one thing – men doing more results in happier homes.","coreZh":"这两个项目都认同一点：男性多干点活会带来更幸福的家庭。","cars":[{"text":"men doing more to help around the house","hook":"动名词复合结构（作主语）+ 不定式 to do","noteZh":"整块充当 results in 的主语：“男性多出力帮忙分担家务这件事”"},{"text":"even if that means cleaning the toilet now and then","hook":"让步状语从句 even if","noteZh":"幽默补充极端情况：就算偶尔要刷马桶也值得"}],"tipZh":"注意 men doing more ... 后面接的是单数动词 results in，因为主语不是 men（男人们），而是“男性多干活这件事”（动名词结构视为单数）！"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Hello, this is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Neil.","zh":"大家好，这里是 BBC 学英语的《六分钟英语》，我是尼尔。","s":2.54,"e":11.9,"wt":[0,566,578,604,632,660,704,742,790,820,869,887]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"And I'm Pippa. When it comes to household chores , jobs like cleaning, washing clothes or looking after the kids, it's often women who do most of the work. Here, two female listeners of BBC World Service programme People Fixing the World share their experiences. Do they sound familiar to you?","zh":"我是皮帕。说到家务活（household chores）——比如打扫卫生、洗衣服或者照顾孩子——通常大部分活儿都是女性在干。下面有两位 BBC 国际台节目《People Fixing the World》的女性听众分享了她们的经历，听起来是不是很耳熟？","s":12.02,"e":30.1,"wt":[0,22,48,136,148,168,192,211,262,289,307,334,366,430,475,527,538,591,614,638,699,729,768,796,812,838,856,868,890,976,1013,1031,1080,1128,1141,1160,1192,1237,1295,1334,1372,1391,1423,1455,1487,1623,1632,1656,1696,1708,1746]},{"speaker":"Listener 1","en":"I do everything. I do all the house chores , I do the food shopping, I do all the cooking, I clean the bathroom.","zh":"什么都是我干。所有的家务活（house chores）都是我做，我买菜、我做所有的饭、我还打扫浴室。","s":30.16,"e":37.79,"wt":[0,5,17,84,89,100,117,134,162,195,216,276,308,324,346,417,421,440,469,498,595,604,650,682]},{"speaker":"Listener 2","en":"Are the chores done equally between me and my husband? No, they're not done equally. I do most of the chores .","zh":"我和我丈夫平摊家务（chores）吗？不，完全不平均。大部分家务都是我做的。","s":37.79,"e":46.61,"wt":[0,28,55,103,133,178,222,234,253,271,365,453,477,508,545,641,659,681,713,727,757,819]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"So, you're a family man, Neil. Do you do your fair share of household chores ?","zh":"尼尔，你是顾家好男人。你在家会分担你应尽的那一份（fair share）家务吗？","s":46.61,"e":51.93,"wt":[0,10,56,64,111,149,185,205,225,251,285,317,353,372,399,465]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"I think so. My wife works part time and I work full time, so when I'm not working, I do things, and when she's not working, she does things.","zh":"我觉得算是吧。我妻子做兼职，我做全职，所以我不用上班的时候我来干，她不用上班的时候她来干。","s":51.93,"e":62.28,"wt":[0,5,53,106,126,166,217,265,307,333,347,387,437,501,527,567,661,699,724,753,788,816,829,854,877,902,958,976,1001]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Yeah, it's similar in my house, so me and my husband try to split things, but it's difficult, isn't it?","zh":"是啊，我家也差不多，我和我丈夫尽量分着干，但真的挺难做到的，对吧？","s":62.64,"e":69.26,"wt":[0,38,68,123,138,153,215,230,245,268,283,338,361,376,415,500,524,556,604,626]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"It is.","zh":"确实挺难的。","s":69.42,"e":70.06,"wt":[0,24]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Of course, no one enjoys cleaning the toilet or taking out the bins, but there is a serious side to the issue. Figures from UN Women, an organisation promoting gender equality, suggest that every day women around the world spend three hours more than men on unpaid care and domestic work. So, in this episode, we'll be hearing about two projects helping husbands and wives share the housework more fairly.","zh":"当然，没人喜欢刷马桶或者倒垃圾，但这个问题背后有着严肃的一面。致力于推动性别平等的联合国妇女署的数据显示，全球女性每天在无偿照护和家务劳动上比男性多花三个小时。因此在本期节目中，我们将了解两个帮助夫妻更公平地分担家务的项目。","s":70.18,"e":95.14,"wt":[0,12,69,83,104,147,205,226,269,283,326,347,368,428,448,472,491,497,550,572,587,609,668,721,751,766,808,825,916,990,1039,1104,1158,1178,1214,1249,1285,1320,1342,1370,1421,1457,1493,1518,1542,1570,1584,1627,1662,1688,1739,1794,1807,1820,1847,1907,1940,1953,2000,2038,2058,2112,2159,2220,2238,2279,2320,2344,2418,2451]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"And as usual, you'll find a quiz and a worksheet to go with this episode on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"照例，你可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 上找到与本期节目配套的小测验和练习题。","s":95.48,"e":102.34,"wt":[0,19,31,74,108,118,138,168,180,193,249,261,273,294,323,367,379,398,452]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"But now I have a question for you, Pippa. According to a survey conducted by Good Housekeeping magazine earlier this year, what is the most hated household chore in Britain? Is it: a) deep cleaning the oven, b) scrubbing the toilet, or c) cleaning the windows?","zh":"不过现在我有个问题要考考你，皮帕。根据《Good Housekeeping》杂志今年早些时候做的一项调查，英国人最讨厌的家务活是什么？是：a) 深度清洁烤箱，b) 刷马桶，还是 c) 擦窗户？","s":102.94,"e":122,"wt":[0,14,34,40,66,72,125,145,176,234,292,308,318,360,423,437,466,540,603,644,658,752,793,811,838,874,920,1002,1048,1066,1166,1178,1190,1274,1315,1338,1368,1466,1491,1575,1603,1677,1695,1766,1788,1816]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Hmm. I'm going to say deep cleaning the oven because I really hate that job. I hardly ever do it.","zh":"嗯，我选深度清洁烤箱，因为我实在太讨厌那活儿了，我几乎从来不干。","s":122.12,"e":128.7,"wt":[0,74,112,128,158,204,254,276,300,330,342,404,404,436,472,532,539,581,609,624]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"I don't like any of those, but we'll find out the answer at the end of the programme. Now, the first project we're going to look at is on the remote Indonesian island of Rote. Here, men traditionally work as rice farmers or fishermen, while women look after the home, the children and older people. But with few washing machines and no running water, these domestic chores take a long time.","zh":"这三样我都不喜欢，不过我们节目最后揭晓答案。首先我们要看的第一个项目位于印度尼西亚偏远的罗地岛（Rote）。在那里，男性传统上以种稻或捕鱼为生，而女性负责照顾家庭、孩子和老人。但由于几乎没有洗衣机也没有自来水，这些家务活非常耗时。","s":128.78,"e":154.04,"wt":[0,50,90,122,146,162,196,242,282,312,330,360,398,424,436,450,450,466,620,634,652,687,714,714,726,744,760,786,814,856,898,938,1005,1045,1066,1206,1236,1255,1341,1367,1380,1406,1453,1465,1524,1557,1590,1616,1648,1667,1716,1742,1812,1840,1886,1986,2006,2028,2044,2082,2126,2142,2153,2194,2266,2284,2343,2394,2424,2436,2470]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"The NGO ARUNGI runs a project trying to change attitudes about the role of the island's women. They work alongside community leaders like Mama Shilpa, who spoke with BBC World Service reporter Ade Mardiyati.","zh":"非政府组织 ARUNGI 正在开展一个项目，试图改变人们对岛上女性角色的观念。他们与玛玛·希尔帕等社区领袖合作，她接受了 BBC 国际台记者阿德·马尔迪亚蒂的采访。","s":154.18,"e":167.46,"wt":[0,18,42,150,180,214,254,254,302,302,370,410,434,466,482,506,556,624,650,692,748,808,840,866,890,950,969,1001,1026,1045,1096,1147,1206,1230]},{"speaker":"Mama Shilpa","en":"It is expected that the tasks done in the house, like holding the child, feeding the child, washing dishes, cooking, will all be done by the mother.","zh":"人们理所当然地认为，家里的活儿——比如抱孩子、喂孩子、洗碗、做饭——全都应该由母亲来做。","s":167.6,"e":178.96,"wt":[0,12,32,98,131,156,189,222,238,263,390,436,436,488,542,549,584,678,718,800,868,920,955,973,1009,1027,1054]},{"speaker":"Ade Mardiyati","en":"Shilpa tells me these are all considered women's work . And you might be thinking, \"Well, the man is busy in the fields, so it's just a fair division of labour .\" But Shilpa tells me women also help out in the fields.","zh":"希尔帕告诉我，这些全都被视作“女人的活儿（women's work）”。你可能会想：“好吧，男人在田里忙活，这只是合理的劳动分工（division of labour）嘛。”但希尔帕告诉我，女人们同样也要下田干活。","s":179.05,"e":193.86,"wt":[0,43,83,100,141,165,189,255,281,327,387,397,413,413,463,550,613,623,640,657,691,707,729,799,816,849,883,891,925,979,997,1042,1087,1112,1154,1197,1210,1259,1298,1337,1366,1385,1414]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"On Rote, cleaning, cooking and sweeping are considered women's work – a negative term for boring and repetitive tasks which are traditionally done by women.","zh":"在罗地岛，打扫、做饭和扫地被视作 women's work（女人的活儿）——这是一个带有贬义色彩的说法，指传统上由女性承担的枯燥重复的工作。","s":194.06,"e":203.74,"wt":[0,20,63,136,187,209,268,290,364,415,438,458,465,524,561,587,621,642,714,750,786,807,892,918,942]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Many cultures have a division of labour – a way of organising work where different tasks are done by different groups of people. But on Rote this means women work even harder, helping out with the farming and fishing too.","zh":"许多文化中都有劳动分工（division of labour）——也就是把不同任务分配给不同群体来完成的组织方式。但在罗地岛，这意味着女性更加辛苦，因为她们还得帮忙种地和捕鱼。","s":204.06,"e":217.42,"wt":[0,28,76,104,122,177,190,223,244,251,271,336,353,380,414,476,527,537,558,568,615,638,656,706,732,749,800,835,879,923,957,993,1080,1102,1114,1114,1134,1182,1234,1256]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"You might think this only happens in traditional societies, but research suggests that women in wealthy countries also spend much more time on unpaid care work than men. One such country is Croatia, where the NGO Status M run their Superdads project. Project worker Anamarija Sočo leads workshops encouraging dads and sons to take some of the pressure off mums. Here, she explains the benefits of this to the BBC World Service.","zh":"你可能会以为这只发生在传统社会，但研究表明，富裕国家的女性在无偿照护工作上花的时间也远多于男性。克罗地亚就是其中之一，那里的非政府组织 Status M 发起了“超级奶爸（Superdads）”项目。项目工作人员安娜玛丽亚·索乔主持工作坊，鼓励爸爸和儿子们帮妈妈分担一些压力。下面她向 BBC 国际台解释了这样做的好处。","s":217.52,"e":246.72,"wt":[0,14,40,74,100,134,177,189,257,320,343,392,441,466,509,522,587,652,704,744,782,822,842,884,938,964,968,996,1110,1126,1150,1202,1226,1322,1322,1342,1426,1472,1502,1532,1582,1668,1766,1780,1846,1933,1974,2010,2075,2155,2184,2205,2234,2248,2277,2305,2328,2346,2398,2421,2474,2520,2592,2605,2628,2690,2705,2736,2760,2784,2808,2849]},{"speaker":"Anamarija Sočo","en":"Mothers have, you know, of course, more space for their careers, or hobbies, or just simply to rest – I mean, just to take a breather . Women tend to especially appreciate men who do these kinds of chores . And when they start pulling their weight , then they jokingly say that, \"Uh yeah, women tend to nag less.\"","zh":"妈妈们当然就会有更多空间去发展事业、培养爱好，或者干脆休息一下——我的意思是，喘口气（take a breather）。女性往往特别欣赏愿意做这类家务（chores）的男性。而当男人们开始尽自己应尽的一份力（pulling their weight）时，他们就会开玩笑说：“嗯，对啊，女人们好像也没那么爱唠叨了。”","s":246.94,"e":270.1,"wt":[0,48,97,121,170,186,226,232,264,278,318,442,484,545,562,595,659,671,697,720,730,756,756,780,792,808,887,952,974,1018,1099,1193,1314,1354,1397,1402,1436,1474,1480,1560,1640,1667,1690,1713,1742,1783,1812,1841,1860,1885,1913,1968,1988,2034,2055,2127,2160,2166,2197,2238]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Sometimes mums need to take a breather – an informal way of saying stop and rest a while.","zh":"有时候妈妈们需要喘口气（take a breather）——这是一个口语表达，意思是停下来歇一小会儿。","s":270.8,"e":276.22,"wt":[0,22,90,110,138,156,169,210,232,244,304,321,338,389,423,446,470,490]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Anamarija also thinks women appreciate it when their husbands pull their weight . If you pull your weight , you work as hard as everyone else.","zh":"安娜玛丽亚还认为，当丈夫尽到自己的那份责任（pull their weight）时，妻子会非常感激。pull your weight 的意思是和大家一样卖力干活，不偷懒。","s":276.39,"e":284.52,"wt":[0,59,96,137,172,235,257,287,311,360,384,414,441,469,481,499,524,547,576,595,614,638,650,674,686,755]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Both these NGO projects agree about one thing – men doing more to help around the house results in happier homes, even if that means cleaning the toilet now and then. And speaking of cleaning the toilet, what was the answer to your quiz question, Neil?","zh":"这两个非政府组织的项目在一点上不谋而合——男性多分担家务会让家庭更幸福，哪怕这意味着偶尔要刷刷马桶。说到刷马桶，尼尔，你刚才那道测验题的答案是什么？","s":284.82,"e":299.61,"wt":[0,31,66,90,149,186,223,245,279,304,326,363,392,406,435,479,501,545,591,602,650,700,729,743,771,808,864,884,922,940,954,1024,1043,1083,1094,1143,1156,1205,1229,1247,1265,1302,1314,1338,1362,1412]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"I asked you what chore is the most hated in Britain.","zh":"我问你英国人最讨厌的家务活是哪一项。","s":299.61,"e":303.72,"wt":[0,5,47,90,133,177,194,220,255,299,316]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"I thought it was cleaning the oven.","zh":"我猜的是清洁烤箱。","s":303.85,"e":305.96,"wt":[0,5,47,60,80,131,149]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, I'm afraid that's not the right answer. People hate cleaning the windows even more than scrubbing the oven. Apparently, 23% of the people who did the survey said cleaning the windows. OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we've learned, starting with chores – household tasks like cleaning, washing and ironing that have to be done regularly.","zh":"恐怕没答对。相比刷烤箱，人们更讨厌擦窗户。调查显示，有 23% 的受访者选择了擦窗户。好了，我们来回顾一下学过的词汇，首先是 chores（家务活）——像打扫、洗衣和熨烫这类需要定期处理的家庭琐事。","s":305.96,"e":327.7,"wt":[0,35,56,99,142,164,184,222,286,329,358,408,438,489,517,564,584,630,652,718,744,840,854,875,918,939,960,981,1024,1052,1126,1143,1228,1263,1298,1334,1355,1427,1463,1512,1564,1599,1638,1663,1728,1771,1796,1878,1900,1932,1976,1996,1998,2010,2048,2096]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"If you do your fair share of work, you do a reasonable amount.","zh":"如果你完成了自己 fair share（应尽的那份）工作，意思就是你承担了合理的分量。","s":327.82,"e":332,"wt":[0,18,34,58,90,124,156,182,216,216,262,292,364]},{"speaker":"Women's work","en":"is a negative term for the boring and repetitive tasks which are stereotypically done by women.","zh":"women's work（“女人的活儿”）是一个带贬义色彩的说法，指刻板印象中由女性承担的枯燥重复的杂活。","s":332.92,"e":339.14,"wt":[0,0,43,96,122,142,161,204,222,289,322,355,375,493,527,548]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"The division of labour is a way of organising work where different tasks are done by different groups of people.","zh":"division of labour（劳动分工）是指由不同群体分别承担不同任务的工作组织方式。","s":339.28,"e":345.28,"wt":[0,18,64,79,115,127,133,151,163,224,248,278,333,363,381,405,417,490,516,540]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"The idiom take a breather means to stop what you're doing for a short time and rest.","zh":"习语 take a breather 意思是暂时停下手头的事，喘口气休息一下。","s":345.36,"e":350.2,"wt":[0,18,53,86,92,152,182,199,226,242,274,278,288,332,368,404,432]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"And finally, if you pull your weight , you work as hard as everyone else doing the same job. Once again, our six minutes are up, but remember, you can find a free transcript including all the vocabulary on our website, at bbclearningenglish.com. Goodbye for now.","zh":"最后，如果你 pull your weight（尽到自己的本分），意思就是你和一起干活的其他人同样努力。我们的六分钟时间又到了，别忘了你可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 上免费获取包含全部词汇的节目文本。暂别啦，再见。","s":350.42,"e":365.41,"wt":[0,13,70,82,101,126,151,181,201,220,246,257,282,294,345,370,402,421,458,560,586,628,646,664,706,718,747,766,802,814,844,866,880,904,970,1018,1040,1055,1125,1139,1160,1209,1223,1428,1454,1474]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Goodbye!","zh":"再见！","s":365.52,"e":366.02,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0p4779b.mp3"},{"epNum":16,"id":"ep-260806","date":"2026-08-06","title":"How do climate scientists make predictions?","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260806","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260806_6_minute_english_how_do_climate_scientists_make_predictions_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260806_6_minute_english_how_do_climate_scientists_make_predictions_transcript.pdf","summary":"Across the world, climate change is already beginning to change the way we live our lives. But how big will that change be and what kinds of change will occur? These are the kinds of questions that scientists have been asking for decades. So, how exactly do they try to find answers to such complex questions about the future? Understanding where we are heading might help humanity prepare for the consequences of climate change. Phil and Pippa discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"Which unit do scientists use to measure the Earth's energy imbalance – the difference between the solar energy entering the Earth's atmosphere and the radiation going back out into space?","options":["a) Fahrenheit","b) Watts per square metre","c) Joules"],"topic":"自然气候","titleZh":"气候科学家是如何做预测的？","summaryZh":"在世界各地，气候变化已经开始改变我们的生活方式。但这种变化会有多大？又会发生哪些类型的变化？几十年来，科学家们一直在思考这类问题。那么，他们究竟是如何尝试解答这些关于未来的复杂问题的？了解我们将走向何方，或许能帮助人类为气候变化的后果做好准备。Phil 和 Pippa 将探讨这一话题，并教你一些实用的新词汇。","questionZh":"科学家使用哪种单位来衡量地球的能量失衡——即进入地球大气层的太阳能与散射回太空的辐射之间的差值？","optionsZh":["a) 华氏度","b) 瓦特每平方米","c) 焦耳"],"answer":{"letter":"b","explanationZh":"NASA 气候科学家 Kate Marvel 在采访中明确提到，科学家用来衡量地球能量失衡的单位是“瓦特每平方米”（Watts per square metre），节目结尾 Pippa 也公布了该正确答案。"},"vocab":[{"word":"prediction","defEn":"statement about what you think will happen in the future","pos":"n.","zh":"预测；预言","exampleEn":"Climate models don't make predictions – they make projections.","ipa":"/prɪˈdɪkʃən/"},{"word":"conditional","defEn":"depending on other factors; not certain","pos":"adj.","zh":"有条件的；视情况而定的","exampleEn":"In other words, climate models are conditional, meaning that they depend on other factors.","ipa":"/kənˈdɪʃənəl/"},{"word":"the thing about...","defEn":"(informal) used to introduce the most important feature or detail to understand about something","pos":"短语","zh":"关于……关键的一点是；……的特点在于","exampleEn":"So, the thing about clouds is that they have this dual effect.","ipa":"/ðə θɪŋ əˈbaʊt/"},{"word":"out of whack","defEn":"(idiom) out of balance; not working properly or broken","pos":"短语","zh":"失衡；不正常；出故障","exampleEn":"So, climate models monitor clouds to accurately measure how much the Earth is out of whack.","ipa":"/aʊt əv wæk/"},{"word":"pro- (something)","defEn":"in favour of (something)","pos":"前缀","zh":"支持……的；赞成……的","exampleEn":"You know, I am very pro-science. I am very pro-doing science.","ipa":"/prəʊ/"},{"word":"(not) on the table","defEn":"(idiom) (not) considered an option or a possibility","pos":"短语","zh":"（不）在考虑范围内；（不）作为可行选项","exampleEn":"I am very pro-doing science because no climate change is unfortunately not on the table.","ipa":"/ɒn ðə ˈteɪbəl/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"in the first place","zh":"首先；一开始；归根结底（常放句尾加强提问或解释原因的语气）","contextEn":"So, how do scientists calculate the effects of climate change in the first place?"},{"en":"play a big part in","zh":"在……中起重要作用（雅思口语写作高频搭配，比 be important 更自然）","contextEn":"We usually associate hot summers with cloudless blue skies, but in fact clouds play a big part in the climate, as Kate Marvel explained to the BBC World Service."},{"en":"for one thing","zh":"首先；一来（口语中用来引出多个理由中的第一个）","contextEn":"For one thing, how can you monitor clouds which are constantly moving and changing?"},{"en":"beyond doubt","zh":"毫无疑问；确凿无疑（常与 prove / know 搭配，增强论述说服力）","contextEn":"Climate models prove beyond doubt that the problem of global warming is created by humans, so we know beyond doubt that the solution is also in our hands."}],"sentences":[{"en":"Scientists monitor climate change using the Earth's energy imbalance – the difference between the solar energy entering the Earth's atmosphere and the radiation going back out into space.","zh":"科学家通过地球的能量失衡来监测气候变化，也就是进入地球大气层的太阳能与散射回太空的辐射之间的差值。","coreEn":"Scientists monitor climate change.","coreZh":"科学家监测气候变化。","cars":[{"text":"using the Earth's energy imbalance","hook":"-ing 修饰","noteZh":"说明科学家监测气候变化所借助的方法/指标"},{"text":"– the difference between the solar energy entering the Earth's atmosphere and the radiation going back out into space","hook":"破折号同位解释","noteZh":"用通俗语言给前面的专业术语 energy imbalance 下定义，内部还挂了两个 -ing 修饰语分别限定太阳能和辐射的流向"}],"tipZh":"雅思科普阅读和听力中，遇到专业术语（如 energy imbalance）别慌，后面紧跟的破折号往往就是白话解释。"},{"en":"Huge amounts of physical and chemical data are fed into supercomputers, which then paint pictures of possible futures on Earth.","zh":"海量的物理和化学数据被输入超级计算机，由它们描绘出地球未来可能出现的种种景象。","coreEn":"Huge amounts of data are fed into supercomputers.","coreZh":"海量数据被输入超级计算机。","cars":[{"text":"physical and chemical","hook":"形容词并列修饰","noteZh":"限定数据的学科类型"},{"text":", which then paint pictures of possible futures on Earth","hook":"关系词 which","noteZh":"顺着主句往下说，补充超级计算机拿到数据之后进一步做了什么"}],"tipZh":"逗号加 which 引导的从句常可按“然后/它们……”顺译，不必硬翻成冗长的“的”字前置定语。"},{"en":"Right now, the unit that scientists use to measure the energy imbalance of the planet – how much it's out of whack – is Watts, like in a light bulb, per square metre.","zh":"目前，科学家用来衡量地球能量失衡程度——也就是失衡有多严重——的单位是每平方米瓦特，就像灯泡的瓦数那样。","coreEn":"The unit is Watts per square metre.","coreZh":"这个单位是瓦特每平方米。","cars":[{"text":"that scientists use to measure the energy imbalance of the planet","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"紧贴主语 the unit，说明这是“用来衡量地球能量失衡的”单位"},{"text":"– how much it's out of whack –","hook":"破折号插入语","noteZh":"口语化的补充解释，把前面的 energy imbalance 翻译成更接地气的“失衡程度”"},{"text":", like in a light bulb,","hook":"逗号插入语","noteZh":"拿灯泡打比方，帮听众秒懂 Watts（瓦特）是什么概念"}],"tipZh":"主语和系动词 is 之间被拉得很长时，先跳过破折号和定语从句，把 the unit ... is Watts 拼起来，句子骨架立刻清晰。"},{"en":"Climate models prove beyond doubt that the problem of global warming is created by humans, so we know beyond doubt that the solution is also in our hands.","zh":"气候模型毫无疑问地证明，全球变暖问题是人类造成的，因此我们也确信无疑，解决办法同样掌握在我们自己手中。","coreEn":"Climate models prove that ..., so we know that ...","coreZh":"气候模型证明了……，所以我们知道……。","cars":[{"text":"beyond doubt","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"插在动词和宾语从句之间，强调“确凿无疑”的程度"},{"text":"that the problem of global warming is created by humans","hook":"that 宾语从句","noteZh":"交代模型证明的具体事实：全球变暖由人类引发"},{"text":"that the solution is also in our hands","hook":"that 宾语从句","noteZh":"与前半句形成因果呼应，点出解决办法也在人类手中"}],"tipZh":"两个分句对称使用 beyond doubt that ...，节奏感极强，非常适合直接借鉴到雅思大作文的结尾段做总结升华。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Hello, this is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Phil.","zh":"大家好，欢迎收听 BBC Learning English 出品的《六分钟英语》。我是 Phil。","s":2.54,"e":12.66,"wt":[0,582,594,630,668,704,742,788,846,878,933,970]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"And I'm Pippa.","zh":"我是 Pippa。","s":12.74,"e":13.86,"wt":[0,28,52]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"This summer is becoming one of the hottest ever with heatwaves across Europe. Here in the UK, a new record for the highest June temperature was set at a sweltering 37.7°C. It seems that year after year, temperatures are going up. In 2015, the COP21 summit agreed to limit global warming to 1.5°C, but since then research shows temperatures exceeding that, leading to warnings of catastrophic damage to Earth's life support systems. In this episode, we'll be discussing how hot the planet could get, using some useful new vocabulary. And remember, you'll find a free transcript to read along with on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"今年夏天正成为有史以来最炎热的夏天之一，热浪席卷整个欧洲。在英国，六月最高气温刷新了纪录，达到了酷热的 37.7 摄氏度。气温似乎一年比一年高。2015 年，联合国气候变化大会（COP21）峰会达成协议，将全球变暖幅度控制在 1.5 摄氏度以内，但此后研究表明气温已经超过了这一上限，引发了关于地球生命支持系统可能遭受灾难性破坏的警告。在本期节目中，我们将探讨地球究竟会变得多热，并学习一些实用的新词汇。别忘了，你可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 找到免费文本，边听边读。","s":14,"e":63.78,"wt":[0,30,70,106,166,166,182,250,304,356,388,472,527,616,620,640,663,683,693,724,786,817,848,902,952,1008,1046,1082,1092,1166,1365,1564,1583,1610,1646,1707,1752,1793,1860,1924,1974,2038,2058,2164,2209,2272,2351,2369,2382,2427,2482,2496,2601,2804,2821,2852,2877,2925,2957,3044,3111,3162,3220,3239,3282,3324,3427,3478,3495,3554,3588,3642,3738,3750,3772,3820,3822,3868,3902,3952,3972,3998,4004,4040,4111,4146,4174,4220,4266,4328,4345,4390,4423,4445,4450,4472,4534,4548,4577,4613,4642,4656,4678,4737]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"But first, I have a question for you, Phil. Scientists monitor climate change using the Earth's energy imbalance – the difference between the solar energy entering the Earth's atmosphere and the radiation going back out into space. But which unit do scientists use for this? Is it: a) Fahrenheit, b) Watts per square metre, or c) Joules?","zh":"不过首先，Phil，我有一个问题要问你。科学家通过地球的能量失衡来监测气候变化——也就是进入地球大气层的太阳能与散射回太空的辐射之间的差值。但科学家是用什么单位来衡量它的呢？是：a) 华氏度，b) 瓦特每平方米，还是 c) 焦耳？","s":64.02,"e":87.71,"wt":[0,32,68,90,102,132,144,168,190,314,364,412,448,481,506,518,579,608,659,684,703,766,810,829,860,898,948,967,1010,1086,1102,1130,1186,1200,1236,1294,1324,1418,1440,1479,1509,1524,1588,1616,1634,1702,1714,1748,1770,1906,2006,2006,2019,2087,2166,2189,2221]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Ooh, I know Joules is something to do with energy, so I'm going to go with c).","zh":"哦，我知道焦耳和能量有关，所以我选 c)。","s":87.71,"e":93.12,"wt":[0,83,99,125,159,181,205,222,239,274,327,344,369,405,417,431,469]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Well, we'll find out the answer at the end of the programme, although careful listeners might hear the answer sooner.","zh":"好吧，我们会在节目最后揭晓答案，不过细心的听众可能会更早就听到答案哦。","s":93.12,"e":100.12,"wt":[0,31,64,90,110,130,170,183,205,223,236,256,316,369,415,489,519,550,573,620]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Ooh!","zh":"哦！","s":100.34,"e":101.13,"wt":[0]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Yes, exciting! So, how do scientists calculate the effects of climate change in the first place? The answer is with models. Huge amounts of physical and chemical data are fed into supercomputers, which then paint pictures of possible futures on Earth. But, as NASA climate scientist Kate Marvel explained to BBC World Service programme The Climate Question, nothing is certain.","zh":"没错，很有意思吧！那么，科学家一开始是如何计算气候变化带来的影响的呢？答案是借助模型。海量的物理和化学数据被输入超级计算机，由它们描绘出地球未来可能出现的种种景象。不过，正如 NASA 气候科学家 Kate Marvel 在 BBC 国际台节目《气候之问》中所解释的那样，一切并非确定无疑。","s":101.13,"e":125.64,"wt":[0,49,179,192,211,224,290,349,368,414,426,473,512,525,543,592,647,671,719,735,767,839,871,927,943,1007,1031,1093,1126,1151,1175,1218,1313,1350,1380,1417,1477,1492,1552,1599,1620,1683,1712,1727,1762,1817,1867,1894,1934,1985,2001,2027,2060,2107,2159,2187,2233,2305,2346,2357]},{"speaker":"Kate Marvel","en":"Climate models don't make predictions – they make projections. Predictions are saying this is what will happen and a projection is conditional . A projection is: \"If we do this, then this will happen.\"","zh":"气候模型不做“预测”，它们做的是“推演”。预测是说“这就是将要发生的事”，而推演是有条件的。推演是说：“如果我们这样做，那么就会发生那样的结果。”","s":125.64,"e":138.78,"wt":[0,34,72,103,176,200,210,235,260,349,418,437,484,509,522,559,596,712,730,774,842,920,923,925,934,1026,1043,1062,1080,1098,1153,1200,1220,1250]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Climate models don't make predictions . Those are statements about what's likely to happen. Instead, they make projections about different possible futures, and these depend on many things. In other words, climate models are conditional , meaning that they depend on other factors. The main factor in climate change, of course, is human activity, especially greenhouse gas emissions.","zh":"气候模型不做 predictions（预测），也就是对未来可能发生之事的断言。相反，它们会对各种不同的未来可能性做出推演，而这些推演取决于诸多因素。换句话说，气候模型是 conditional（有条件的），意思是它们取决于其他因素。当然，气候变化最主要的因素就是人类活动，尤其是温室气体排放。","s":139.18,"e":163.66,"wt":[0,20,58,86,134,183,215,234,261,316,336,370,402,435,496,536,560,602,687,725,795,857,911,934,971,1018,1033,1070,1188,1201,1236,1294,1349,1396,1419,1490,1520,1575,1602,1628,1672,1690,1724,1832,1850,1883,1928,1946,1986,1986,1986,1986,2070,2111,2192,2274,2342,2376]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Another influence on climate models is clouds. We usually associate hot summers with cloudless blue skies, but in fact clouds play a big part in the climate, as Kate Marvel explained to the BBC World Service.","zh":"另一个影响气候模型的因素是云层。我们通常会把炎热的夏天和万里无云的蓝天联系在一起，但实际上云层在气候中起着重要作用，正如 Kate Marvel 向 BBC 国际台解释的那样。","s":163.92,"e":177.28,"wt":[0,62,74,98,154,182,238,332,346,397,463,485,536,564,636,659,697,720,735,818,818,843,850,922,922,922,942,996,1011,1042,1087,1164,1184,1201,1226,1268]},{"speaker":"Kate Marvel","en":"So, the thing about clouds is that they have this dual effect. They both cool the planet by blocking sunlight, and they warm the planet because they have their own greenhouse effect. Right now, the unit that scientists use to measure the energy imbalance of the planet – how much it's out of whack – is Watts, like in a light bulb, per square metre.","zh":"关于云层，关键的一点是它们具有双重效应。它们一方面通过阻挡阳光为地球降温，另一方面又因为自身具有温室效应而让地球升温。目前，科学家用来衡量地球能量失衡程度——也就是它失衡有多严重——的单位是每平方米瓦特，就像灯泡的瓦数那样。","s":177.42,"e":199.46,"wt":[0,14,34,56,66,118,166,202,228,232,296,348,430,455,480,505,522,567,578,638,699,722,753,784,807,853,898,920,944,952,1022,1086,1172,1194,1194,1258,1287,1315,1384,1407,1421,1470,1491,1533,1597,1611,1632,1672,1698,1707,1733,1760,1778,1792,1838,1872,1891,1935,1970,1988,1997,2034,2076,2102,2154]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Kate starts by saying, \" The thing about clouds is.\" She uses the expression the thing about to introduce the most important detail about something. Here, that detail is that clouds both cool the planet by blocking sunlight but also warm it by trapping heat.","zh":"Kate 一开头就说：“The thing about clouds is.” 她用 the thing about 这个表达来引出关于某件事最重要的细节。在这里，这个细节就是云层既能通过阻挡阳光给地球降温，也能通过锁住热量给地球增温。","s":199.68,"e":219.02,"wt":[0,48,92,130,169,180,218,248,298,332,404,434,475,505,607,637,680,753,764,830,848,886,969,1018,1059,1157,1190,1223,1272,1287,1321,1389,1415,1450,1476,1529,1545,1600,1688,1706,1749,1784,1801,1818,1889]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"So, climate models monitor clouds to accurately measure how much the Earth is out of whack . If something is out of whack , it's out of balance and not working properly.","zh":"因此，气候模型会监测云层，以准确衡量地球到底有多 out of whack（失衡）。如果某样东西 out of whack，意思就是它失去平衡、无法正常运转了。","s":219.33,"e":229.9,"wt":[0,59,68,117,174,223,239,315,333,357,379,409,431,467,495,531,572,619,639,655,707,725,743,755,763,797,815,859,879,909,939,975]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Hmm. Climate models sound very complicated to me, Pippa. For one thing, how can you monitor clouds which are constantly moving and changing?","zh":"嗯，Pippa，气候模型听起来非常复杂。首先，云层一直在不断移动和变化，你怎么可能监测得了呢？","s":230.09,"e":239.82,"wt":[0,69,113,168,209,243,281,293,305,329,420,445,503,528,553,578,637,704,723,751,822,864,885]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Yeah. And how much information from around the world would the supercomputers need to make accurate projections? It's a concern that BBC presenter Graihagh Jackson raised with Kate Marvel for BBC World Service programme The Climate Question.","zh":"是啊。而且超级计算机得需要来自世界各地多少信息，才能做出准确的推演呢？BBC 主持人 Graihagh Jackson 在 BBC 国际台节目《气候之问》中就向 Kate Marvel 提出了这个担忧。","s":239.84,"e":254.34,"wt":[0,34,55,76,104,181,209,251,272,307,342,363,461,492,513,539,610,732,750,771,820,848,869,923,972,1024,1055,1080,1099,1140,1160,1180,1214,1262,1324,1344,1392]},{"speaker":"Graihagh Jackson","en":"Surely we need to know the trajectory we're heading in, in order to adapt.","zh":"我们肯定需要了解自己正朝着怎样的轨迹前进，这样才能做出适应。","s":254.58,"e":260.14,"wt":[0,36,51,81,96,126,149,226,264,312,338,363,427,452]},{"speaker":"Kate Marvel","en":"Oh absolutely. You know, I am very pro- science. I am very pro- doing science because no climate change is unfortunately not on the table .","zh":"哦，绝对如此。你知道，我非常支持科学，非常支持开展科学研究，因为很遗憾，“不发生气候变化”已经不在可选范围之内了。","s":260.21,"e":270.4,"wt":[0,17,95,119,168,176,192,225,286,343,349,363,387,435,475,537,611,629,705,749,767,884,911,929,956,985]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Kate is very pro- science. If you're pro- something, it means you're in favour of it. Models are complex, but they're necessary because unfortunately no climate change at all is not on the table , and that's an idiom, meaning it's not an option.","zh":"Kate 非常 pro-science（支持科学）。如果你是 pro- 某事，意思就是你赞成、支持它。模型虽然复杂，但很有必要，因为遗憾的是，完全不发生气候变化已经 not on the table——这是一个习语，意思是“不在考虑范围之内/不是一个选项”。","s":270.4,"e":288.92,"wt":[0,48,72,119,189,270,282,327,393,429,443,478,524,538,584,608,694,722,788,842,860,896,956,1025,1117,1131,1180,1222,1244,1308,1354,1402,1415,1443,1470,1490,1518,1574,1593,1640,1706,1743,1771,1790]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"If all this sounds a bit depressing. Kate's final message is more hopeful. Climate models prove beyond doubt that the problem of global warming is created by humans, so we know beyond doubt that the solution is also in our hands.","zh":"如果这一切听起来有点令人沮丧，Kate 最后传达的信息则更有希望。气候模型毫无疑问地证明，全球变暖问题是人类造成的，因此我们也确信无疑，解决办法同样掌握在我们自己手中。","s":289.17,"e":303.96,"wt":[0,12,31,63,69,105,155,257,291,337,351,379,417,543,555,617,669,703,729,729,747,769,783,825,859,897,947,1005,1087,1098,1113,1144,1191,1211,1211,1233,1279,1351,1383,1403,1430]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Now, Pippa, careful listeners might already know but go ahead and reveal the correct answer to your question.","zh":"好了，Pippa，细心的听众可能早就知道了，不过还是请你揭晓问题的正确答案吧。","s":304.2,"e":311.24,"wt":[0,18,82,130,152,216,270,310,337,351,387,409,452,475,535,577,588,621]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Yes. I asked you, \"Which unit do scientists use to measure the Earth's energy imbalance?\" And the answer was Watts per square metre. And I didn't know that, Phil. Right, it's time to recap the vocabulary we heard during this programme. A prediction is a statement about what you think will happen in the future.","zh":"好的。我问你的是：“科学家用什么单位来衡量地球的能量失衡？”答案是瓦特每平方米。Phil，其实我之前也不知道这个呢。好了，现在来回顾一下本期节目中听到的词汇。prediction 指的是对你认为未来会发生之事的陈述，即预测。","s":311.42,"e":329.22,"wt":[0,33,41,80,104,143,175,191,270,294,310,365,389,443,490,569,587,600,636,658,690,708,744,774,792,798,834,858,888,950,990,1015,1041,1054,1086,1105,1169,1182,1214,1252,1278,1362,1368,1435,1443,1449,1510,1544,1571,1591,1625,1652,1688,1700,1726]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"The adjective conditional means depending on other factors.","zh":"形容词 conditional 的意思是“取决于其他因素的、有条件的”。","s":329.36,"e":333.62,"wt":[0,24,96,200,240,304,328,362]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"You can use the informal expression the thing about to introduce the most important feature or detail of something.","zh":"你可以用非正式表达 the thing about 来引出某件事最重要的特征或细节。","s":333.74,"e":340.6,"wt":[0,14,38,58,78,130,196,221,252,293,306,365,384,410,488,530,544,588,602]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"The phrase out of whack means out of balance and not working properly.","zh":"短语 out of whack 的意思是“失去平衡、无法正常运转”。","s":340.7,"e":345.62,"wt":[0,20,73,106,122,171,213,238,254,313,338,363,422]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"If you're pro- something, you're in favour of it.","zh":"如果你是 pro- 某事物，意思就是你支持它。","s":345.84,"e":348.86,"wt":[0,6,40,74,108,136,158,196,210]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"And if something is not on the table , it's not an option. It's not available to us.","zh":"如果某件事是 not on the table，意思就是它不是一个选项，不在我们可选择的范围内。","s":349.16,"e":355.12,"wt":[0,18,41,70,132,157,173,198,231,256,288,313,329,395,427,452,526,542]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"That's the end of this episode of 6 Minute English. Find a worksheet and quiz on our website to test what you've learned.","zh":"本期《六分钟英语》到此结束。欢迎访问我们的网站获取练习题和小测验，检验你的学习成果。","s":355.24,"e":362.16,"wt":[0,34,55,76,90,118,167,181,201,244,312,326,344,396,413,440,451,482,519,532,559,586,626]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"We'll be back next week with another episode, so make sure you don't miss it.","zh":"我们下周会带来新一期节目，千万别错过。","s":362.17,"e":366.16,"wt":[0,25,37,61,85,109,133,176,235,247,269,292,309,337,360]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Bye for now.","zh":"先再见啦。","s":366.2,"e":367.02,"wt":[0,17,42]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Bye!","zh":"拜拜！","s":367.22,"e":367.6,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0p2ygrc.mp3"},{"epNum":17,"id":"ep-260730","date":"2026-07-30","title":"The Enhanced Games","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260730","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260730_6_minute_english_the_enhanced_games_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260730_6_minute_english_the_enhanced_games_transcript.pdf","summary":"What would the world of athletics look like if athletes were allowed to take drugs? That's what the organisers of The Enhanced Games want to find out. In mainstream sport, taking performance-enhancing drugs is strictly forbidden. It's known as 'doping' and if athletes are caught doing it, they can face fines, suspensions or even bans from professional sport. But in The Enhanced Games, doping is allowed. Is this an insult to sporting tradition or an acceptance of scientific progress? Pippa and Neil discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"How many times did Lance Armstrong win the Tour de France, the famous cycling race, before he was finally caught out for doping?","options":["a) five","b) six","c) seven"],"topic":"文化教育","titleZh":"“增强运动会”：允许用兴奋剂的比赛","summaryZh":"如果允许运动员服用药物，田径和体育世界会变成什么样？这正是“增强运动会”（The Enhanced Games）主办方想要探索的问题。在主流体育赛事中，服用提高运动表现的药物是被严令禁止的，俗称“使用兴奋剂”（doping）；一旦运动员被查出违禁，就会面临罚款、禁赛甚至终身逐出职业体坛的处罚。但在“增强运动会”中，使用兴奋剂是被允许的。这究竟是对体育传统的亵渎，还是对科学进步的接纳？Pippa 和 Neil 将就此展开讨论，并教你一些实用的新词汇。","questionZh":"在最终因使用兴奋剂被查处之前，兰斯·阿姆斯特朗曾赢得过多少次著名的环法自行车赛冠军？","optionsZh":["a) 五次","b) 六次","c) 七次"],"answer":{"letter":"c","explanationZh":"Pippa 在节目结尾公布答案：兰斯·阿姆斯特朗在 1999 年至 2005 年间连续七次赢得环法自行车赛冠军，尽管如今我们知道其中涉及使用违禁药物。"},"vocab":[{"word":"doping","defEn":"(informal) using banned substances to perform better in a sports competition","pos":"n.","zh":"使用兴奋剂；服用违禁药物","exampleEn":"Cheating in sport through the use of performance-enhancing drugs, informally called doping, is normally considered wrong.","ipa":"/ˈdəʊpɪŋ/"},{"word":"it's fair to say...","defEn":"used to introduce a statement which the speaker believes is balanced, reasonable or true","pos":"短语","zh":"平心而论；可以说；公允地说","exampleEn":"I think it's fair to say it's one of the most divisive and controversial events that the world of sport has seen for a long time.","ipa":"/ɪts feə tə seɪ/"},{"word":"divisive","defEn":"causing disagreement and argument between people","pos":"adj.","zh":"引发分歧的；极具争议的","exampleEn":"Dan also describes the games as divisive – an adjective that means causing a lot of disagreement between people.","ipa":"/dɪˈvaɪsɪv/"},{"word":"cross the line","defEn":"start behaving in an unacceptable way","pos":"短语","zh":"越界；逾越底线","exampleEn":"But the critics, of course, say that this crosses an ethical line.","ipa":"/krɒs ðə laɪn/"},{"word":"set a good/bad example","defEn":"behave in a way that encourages others to behave well/badly","pos":"短语","zh":"树立好/坏榜样","exampleEn":"These critics say the Games set a bad example, meaning that they encourage others to behave badly as well.","ipa":"/set ə ɡʊd ɪɡˈzɑːmpəl/"},{"word":"normalise","defEn":"treat something as normal or acceptable when it previously was not","pos":"v.","zh":"使……正常化；把……习以为常","exampleEn":"Well, it's because critics argue the games normalise the use of performance drugs in sport.","ipa":"/ˈnɔːməlaɪz/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"turn ... upside down","zh":"彻底颠覆……；使……翻天覆地（形象表达打破既有规则或格局）","contextEn":"But earlier this year, a sporting event took place which turned the debate about drugs in sport upside down."},{"en":"battle it out","zh":"一决高下；展开激烈角逐（体育和新闻报道高频地道口语）","contextEn":"In fact, some of these competitors are former Olympians – medallists battling it out for money and glory."},{"en":"it's no exaggeration to say","zh":"毫不夸张地说（雅思口语与大作文引出强立场的高分表达）","contextEn":"Now, it's no exaggeration to say that The Enhanced Games are controversial."},{"en":"far from over","zh":"远未结束（比 not over yet 更有力度，适合收束争议性话题）","contextEn":"But with arguments strongly held on both sides, the debate is far from over."}],"sentences":[{"en":"This weekend, elite athletes from around the world are descending on Las Vegas, Nevada, for the first-of-its-kind sporting competition known as The Enhanced Games.","zh":"本周末，来自世界各地的顶尖运动员正齐聚内华达州拉斯维加斯，参加这项史无前例的体育赛事——“增强运动会”。","coreEn":"Elite athletes are descending on Las Vegas.","coreZh":"顶尖运动员正纷纷涌向拉斯维加斯。","cars":[{"text":"from around the world","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"说明这批精英运动员来自世界各地"},{"text":"for the first-of-its-kind sporting competition","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"点明他们奔赴拉斯维加斯的目的——参加这项首创的体育赛事"},{"text":"known as The Enhanced Games","hook":"-ed 修饰","noteZh":"后置说明这项赛事的正式名称"}],"tipZh":"英语新闻喜欢用 descend on（蜂拥而至）替代 go to，再用 known as 引出专有名词，读的时候抓住主干动作就行。"},{"en":"I think it's fair to say it's one of the most divisive and controversial events that the world of sport has seen for a long time.","zh":"我认为平心而论，这是体坛长久以来所见过的最具分歧和争议的赛事之一。","coreEn":"It's one of the most divisive and controversial events.","coreZh":"它是最具分歧和争议的赛事之一。","cars":[{"text":"I think it's fair to say","hook":"观点缓冲开头","noteZh":"委婉、客观地引出接下来的评价，显得理性克制"},{"text":"that the world of sport has seen for a long time","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"限定范围，说明是在“体坛很长一段时间以来所见的”赛事之中"}],"tipZh":"雅思口语 Part 3 表达看法时，用 I think it's fair to say + one of the most ... that ... has seen 可以瞬间拉高语域和流利度。"},{"en":"It's an event where performance-enhancing drugs, banned elsewhere in mainstream sport of course, are actually allowed to be taken by the competitors under medical supervision.","zh":"在这项赛事中，那些在其他主流体育项目中自然是被明令禁止的兴奋剂，竟然被允许让参赛选手在医疗监督下服用。","coreEn":"It's an event where performance-enhancing drugs are allowed to be taken.","coreZh":"这是一个允许服用兴奋剂的赛事。","cars":[{"text":", banned elsewhere in mainstream sport of course,","hook":"逗号插入语","noteZh":"插在主语和谓语之间，对比强调这些药物在其他主流赛事里本是违禁品"},{"text":"by the competitors under medical supervision","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"补充说明服用主体（选手）以及前提条件（在医疗监督下）"}],"tipZh":"看到主语后面紧跟一对逗号夹着的过去分词（banned ...），先整体剥离，就能迅速把 drugs 和后面的 are actually allowed 连起来。"},{"en":"The Games' organisers point out that all drugs are approved by the US Food and Drug Agency and taken openly under medical supervision, which they say makes them safer than when they are taken secretly at competitions elsewhere.","zh":"该赛事的主办方指出，所有药物均经过美国食品药品监督管理局批准，且在医疗监督下公开服用，他们表示这比在其他赛事中偷偷服药要安全得多。","coreEn":"The Games' organisers point out that all drugs are approved and taken openly.","coreZh":"赛事主办方指出，所有药物都经过批准且公开服用。","cars":[{"text":"by the US Food and Drug Agency","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"交代药物审批机构，增加权威背书"},{"text":"under medical supervision","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"交代服药的安全条件"},{"text":", which they say makes them safer than when they are taken secretly at competitions elsewhere","hook":"关系词 which","noteZh":"对前面整件事做评价，中间嵌入 they say 标明是“主办方自称”，并引出与“在其他比赛暗中服药”的对比"}],"tipZh":"which they say makes ... 里藏着插入语 they say，雅思阅读判断题常在这里设坑——注意这是“主办方的说法”，而非客观定论。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Hello, this is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Neil.","zh":"大家好，欢迎收听 BBC Learning English 出品的《六分钟英语》。我是 Neil。","s":2.56,"e":11.74,"wt":[0,556,568,590,618,648,686,724,784,814,860,883]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"And I'm Pippa. Sharing your Netflix password, lying about your age or not paying the bus fare are a few examples of cheating in daily life. But there's one place where cheating is usually considered wrong, and that's sport.","zh":"我是 Pippa。分享你的 Netflix 密码、虚报年龄或者坐公交逃票，这些都是日常生活中“作弊”的例子。但有一个领域，作弊通常被视为绝不可接受的错误，那就是体育运动。","s":11.84,"e":25.94,"wt":[0,28,54,152,194,229,273,336,368,404,431,452,465,487,528,549,581,606,631,639,664,732,749,810,832,870,936,954,978,1005,1029,1055,1101,1113,1173,1252,1292,1310,1354]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Cheating in sport through the use of performance-enhancing drugs, informally called doping , is normally considered wrong. Just ask American cycling legend Lance Armstrong, who was thrown out of the sport for using banned substances. But earlier this year, a sporting event took place which turned the debate about drugs in sport upside down. Here's Asma Khalid from BBC World Service's The Global Story to explain:","zh":"在体育比赛中通过使用提高运动表现的药物来作弊——非正式说法叫 doping（使用兴奋剂）——通常被视为错误的。看看美国自行车传奇人物兰斯·阿姆斯特朗就知道了，他因使用违禁药物而被逐出体坛。但今年早些时候，一场体育赛事的举办彻底颠覆了关于体育用药的争论。下面请听 BBC 国际台《全球故事》节目的 Asma Khalid 为我们解释：","s":25.96,"e":52.04,"wt":[0,46,86,124,124,138,160,212,310,396,456,502,543,569,580,636,690,764,794,819,878,929,973,1010,1092,1106,1120,1162,1180,1196,1220,1256,1282,1312,1375,1488,1504,1543,1565,1598,1603,1647,1674,1709,1734,1768,1809,1829,1870,1904,1938,1952,1986,2027,2093,2133,2165,2214,2247,2272,2313,2395,2420,2469,2510,2526]},{"speaker":"Asma Khalid","en":"This weekend, elite athletes from around the world are descending on Las Vegas, Nevada, for the first-of-its-kind sporting competition known as The Enhanced Games. It's kind of like the Olympics, but only for swimming, sprinting and weightlifting. The aim is to break world records. In fact, some of these competitors are former Olympians – medallists battling it out for money and glory. But there's one big difference...","zh":"本周末，来自世界各地的顶尖运动员正齐聚内华达州拉斯维加斯，参加这项史无前例的体育赛事——“增强运动会”。它有点像奥运会，但只设游泳、短跑和举重三个项目，目的是打破世界纪录。事实上，其中一些参赛选手是前奥运选手——奖牌得主们为金钱和荣耀一决高下。但这项赛事有一个巨大的不同之处……","s":52.48,"e":78.96,"wt":[0,14,60,108,120,152,180,210,236,298,330,382,414,461,528,536,558,674,730,804,854,868,889,945,1008,1053,1100,1114,1129,1145,1198,1214,1235,1251,1304,1362,1378,1472,1494,1516,1532,1560,1583,1614,1677,1689,1726,1751,1765,1795,1854,1883,1921,1967,1989,2086,2132,2146,2176,2208,2248,2277,2348,2368,2434,2492,2574]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"The big difference is that The Enhanced Games allow athletes to use performance-enhancing drugs that are banned in professional sports. In these games, nicknamed The Doping Olympics, steroids, growth hormones and stimulants are all OK. You're a big sports fan, Neil. Would you enjoy watching a sport where the athletes have taken drugs?","zh":"这个巨大的不同在于，“增强运动会”允许运动员使用职业体育中明令禁止的兴奋剂。在这场绰号“兴奋剂奥运会”的比赛中，类固醇、生长激素和兴奋剂全都被允许使用。Neil，你是个铁杆体育迷，你会喜欢看运动员服了药的比赛吗？","s":79.3,"e":100.24,"wt":[0,14,34,102,115,142,162,217,251,285,340,353,373,527,558,590,614,662,678,730,848,863,895,939,997,1015,1054,1152,1222,1270,1314,1342,1380,1416,1486,1530,1572,1579,1599,1642,1677,1730,1736,1749,1836,1836,1839,1871,1903,1922,1974,2000,2032]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"No, I wouldn't. I really don't think that's fair. I think we need to know that all the athletes start and compete from the same level.","zh":"不，我不会。我真的觉得那不公平。我认为我们需要确信所有运动员都是站在同一起跑线上公平竞争的。","s":100.25,"e":109.02,"wt":[0,77,97,107,114,156,191,226,268,319,325,357,370,396,409,435,461,483,505,570,617,645,709,747,775,812]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Yeah, I guess that makes sense. I don't really watch that many sports so I guess I wouldn't notice the difference, but if you're really into sport you care about the rules, right?","zh":"嗯，有道理。我平时看体育比赛不多，估计也看不出什么差别，但如果你真的很迷体育，就会很在意规则，对吧？","s":109.24,"e":118.28,"wt":[0,29,35,66,91,122,171,177,207,244,275,300,325,380,393,399,432,440,529,553,569,640,658,667,695,723,742,774,788,808,828,846,879]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Yes, absolutely. And in this episode we'll dive into the controversial world of The Enhanced Games, learning some useful new words and phrases along the way. And remember, you'll find all the vocabulary, plus a quiz and a worksheet, on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"是的，绝对如此。在本期节目中，我们将深入探讨充满争议的“增强运动会”，同时顺便学习一些实用的新词和短语。别忘了，你可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 找到所有词汇，还有小测验和练习题。","s":118.52,"e":135,"wt":[0,34,132,142,154,181,229,254,275,296,316,413,450,465,488,569,611,671,701,745,768,804,827,879,910,932,988,997,1028,1048,1084,1106,1123,1200,1231,1238,1269,1292,1299,1362,1374,1406,1466]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"But now I have a question for you, Neil. You mentioned the famous sporting cheat Lance Armstrong, but how many times did Lance Armstrong win the famous race, the Tour de France, before he was finally caught out? Was it: a) five, b) six, or c) seven?","zh":"不过现在，Neil，我有个问题要考考你。你刚才提到了著名的体坛作弊者兰斯·阿姆斯特朗，在他最终东窗事发之前，他一共赢过多少次著名的环法自行车赛？是：a) 五次，b) 六次，还是 c) 七次？","s":135.22,"e":154.06,"wt":[0,19,40,47,76,83,141,162,197,247,268,333,354,397,454,491,537,650,667,692,725,767,792,834,909,934,1008,1009,1042,1067,1099,1116,1166,1230,1248,1288,1308,1348,1416,1436,1501,1530,1619,1649,1726,1758,1782]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Ooh... Well, it was a lot, so I think I'll go for c) seven.","zh":"哦……嗯，反正赢了很多次，所以我猜 c) 七次。","s":154.06,"e":159.4,"wt":[0,86,99,123,140,164,222,257,269,331,372,404,436,455]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Well, we'll find out the answer at the end of the programme. Now, it's no exaggeration to say that The Enhanced Games are controversial. After all, organisations like the World Anti- Doping Agency have spent decades fighting performance-enhancing drugs in sport. Here's BBC sports editor Dan Roan on World Service programme The Global Story.","zh":"好的，我们节目最后揭晓答案。毫不夸张地说，“增强运动会”极具争议。毕竟，像世界反兴奋剂机构这样的组织已经花了数十年时间与体育界滥用兴奋剂的行为作斗争。下面来听 BBC 体育编辑 Dan Roan 在国际台《全球故事》节目中的观点。","s":159.4,"e":180.82,"wt":[0,20,52,52,64,112,118,134,172,172,204,228,328,358,382,394,477,489,515,548,575,632,672,713,854,885,913,990,1013,1030,1064,1094,1145,1179,1208,1244,1346,1352,1496,1533,1553,1616,1675,1706,1777,1808,1828,1861,1874,1907,1953,2028,2048,2088]},{"speaker":"Dan Roan","en":"I think it's fair to say it's one of the most divisive and controversial events that the world of sport has seen for a long time. It's an event where performance-enhancing drugs, banned elsewhere in mainstream sport of course, are actually allowed to be taken by the competitors under medical supervision.","zh":"我认为平心而论，这是体坛长久以来所见过的最具分歧和争议的赛事之一。在这项赛事中，那些在其他主流体育项目中自然是被明令禁止的兴奋剂，竟然被允许让参赛选手在医疗监督下服用。","s":180.88,"e":198.54,"wt":[0,5,18,36,36,66,66,66,68,76,88,112,240,312,334,369,410,426,442,466,505,525,552,566,578,605,632,632,670,724,820,909,946,973,1034,1047,1114,1160,1173,1226,1246,1292,1294,1302,1356,1421,1433,1459,1550,1600,1662]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Dan Roan says, \" It's fair to say The Enhanced Games are controversial.\" He uses the phrase it's fair to say to introduce a statement that he considers to be balanced and reasonable – that the games are controversial.","zh":"Dan Roan 说：“平心而论，‘增强运动会’很有争议。”他用短语 it's fair to say 来引出一个他认为客观、公允的陈述——也就是这项赛事充满争议。","s":198.8,"e":211.86,"wt":[0,18,55,77,85,115,145,160,181,204,264,302,324,450,467,497,520,573,596,624,638,659,726,738,745,811,838,852,917,931,945,1002,1023,1084,1112,1128,1154,1191,1213]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Dan also describes the games as divisive – an adjective that means causing a lot of disagreement between people. Much of this disagreement is about the use of performance-enhancing drugs themselves. The Games' organisers point out that all drugs are approved by the US Food and Drug Agency and taken openly under medical supervision, which they say makes them safer than when they are taken secretly at competitions elsewhere. But there are many critics too, as Dan Roan outlined to BBC World Service's The Global Story.","zh":"Dan 还把这项赛事形容为 divisive——这个形容词的意思是“在人们之间引发大量分歧的”。其中很大一部分分歧就集中在兴奋剂本身的使用上。赛事主办方指出，所有药物都经美国食品药品监督管理局批准，并在医疗监督下公开服用，他们称这比在其他赛事中偷偷服药更安全。但批评者也大有人在，正如 Dan Roan 在 BBC 国际台《全球故事》中所概括的那样。","s":212.02,"e":243.36,"wt":[0,31,59,123,144,179,193,239,264,278,342,370,405,455,462,483,496,600,658,750,770,778,805,868,882,909,925,941,952,1066,1116,1194,1214,1248,1315,1350,1370,1397,1417,1454,1474,1529,1541,1562,1575,1612,1628,1657,1700,1722,1822,1856,1896,1936,1982,2018,2047,2069,2105,2134,2186,2204,2224,2234,2256,2300,2350,2375,2436,2536,2552,2578,2608,2636,2688,2739,2748,2770,2806,2864,2878,2899,2935,3001,3020,3067]},{"speaker":"Dan Roan","en":"But the critics, of course, say that this crosses an ethical line , that it sets a dreadful example because it risks normalising doping . It could harm the integrity, therefore, of sport, and it could be dangerous to the health of those taking part.","zh":"当然，批评者表示这逾越了道德底线，树立了一个极其糟糕的榜样，因为它有可能让使用兴奋剂变得正常化。因此，这可能会损害体育的公正诚信，而且还可能危及参赛者的身体健康。","s":243.48,"e":256,"wt":[0,8,26,46,82,154,172,244,244,265,277,320,333,345,370,382,407,413,481,514,552,568,598,664,688,712,724,754,778,796,850,904,920,973,985,996,1025,1036,1088,1099,1116,1144,1158,1182,1214]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"For the Games' critics, doping crosses an ethical line . The phrase to cross the line means to start behaving in a way that's not socially acceptable. These critics say the Games set a bad example , meaning that they encourage others to behave badly as well. Why is that, Pippa?","zh":"在该赛事的批评者看来，使用兴奋剂逾越了道德底线。短语 cross the line 意思是开始做出社会无法接受的行为。这些批评者还说，这项赛事 set a bad example（树立了坏榜样），意思是它会助长其他人也跟着效仿不良行为。为什么这么说呢，Pippa？","s":256.16,"e":272.92,"wt":[0,10,30,80,142,205,248,274,326,353,388,405,438,450,478,495,520,550,573,602,658,666,686,706,742,778,840,938,966,1004,1020,1039,1068,1101,1104,1125,1167,1191,1243,1272,1301,1367,1406,1425,1466,1506,1520,1580,1594,1603,1624]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Well, it's because critics argue the games normalise the use of performance drugs in sport. If you normalise something, you treat it as normal or acceptable when previously it wasn't. But with arguments strongly held on both sides, the debate is far from over. So Neil, have you changed your opinion after hearing both sides of the story?","zh":"嗯，这是因为批评者认为，这项赛事让体育界使用兴奋剂的行为“正常化”了。如果你 normalise 某件事，就是把原本不正常或不被接受的事当作理所当然、可以接受的事来对待。不过由于正反双方都态度坚决，这场争论远未结束。那么 Neil，听完双方的说法后，你改变主意了吗？","s":272.94,"e":292.2,"wt":[0,46,74,116,170,194,222,255,310,328,346,358,424,454,470,566,578,596,650,698,716,720,754,788,820,836,896,920,980,998,1080,1102,1133,1188,1230,1254,1270,1316,1340,1362,1374,1422,1456,1474,1546,1558,1593,1623,1636,1674,1702,1744,1774,1800,1830,1846,1858,1886]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"No, I haven't. I think it's just not fair, and I think if you're not fair in sport, what's the point?","zh":"没有，我没改主意。我觉得这根本就不公平，如果体育竞技都不讲公平了，那还有什么意义呢？","s":292.53,"e":299.06,"wt":[0,27,35,115,123,163,195,227,323,365,365,389,389,395,443,467,499,515,575,595,614]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Well, let's hear the answer to my quiz question, Neil. I asked you how many times Lance Armstrong won the Tour de France, and it was c) seven times, consecutively between 1999 and 2005. Of course, we now know that there were performance-enhancing drugs involved.","zh":"好吧，那我们来听听我刚才那个小测验的答案吧，Neil。我问你兰斯·阿姆斯特朗赢过多少次环法自行车赛，答案是 c) 七次，是在 1999 年到 2005 年间连续夺冠的。当然，我们现在都知道其中涉及了违禁药物。","s":299.36,"e":318.5,"wt":[0,36,60,60,106,118,152,176,204,264,336,344,387,413,439,473,516,558,648,672,704,746,767,831,863,884,954,993,1092,1134,1214,1276,1352,1431,1552,1561,1596,1607,1623,1645,1667,1694,1716,1825,1852]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK. So, now it's time to recap the vocabulary we've learned, starting with doping – using banned drugs to perform better in sporting competitions.","zh":"好的。现在我们来回顾一下今天学到的词汇，首先是 doping——指在体育比赛中使用违禁药物以取得更好成绩。","s":318.6,"e":327.72,"wt":[0,32,45,65,91,118,131,165,185,254,287,335,378,417,442,458,513,538,581,652,659,711,728,797]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"You can use the phrase it's fair to say to introduce a statement you consider to be balanced, reasonable or true.","zh":"你可以用短语 it's fair to say 来引出一个你认为公允、合理或符合事实的说法。","s":327.92,"e":334.3,"wt":[0,12,30,48,68,102,129,152,163,185,196,247,252,303,320,376,390,410,483,552,582]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"If something is divisive , it causes disagreement between people.","zh":"如果某件事是 divisive，意思就是它会在人与人之间引发分歧和争执。","s":334.36,"e":338.34,"wt":[0,16,58,82,125,148,169,210,292,336]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"The idiom cross the line means to start behaving in an unacceptable way.","zh":"习语 cross the line 意思是开始做出不可接受的越界行为。","s":338.46,"e":343.32,"wt":[0,21,59,105,128,166,204,220,259,316,326,351,442]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"If you set a bad example , you behave in a way that encourages others to behave badly.","zh":"如果你 set a bad example（树立坏榜样），就是你的行为会诱导别人也跟着做坏事。","s":343.53,"e":348.62,"wt":[0,9,31,53,60,82,124,148,170,214,221,235,257,286,360,385,407,459]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"And finally, to normalise something means to treat it as normal or acceptable when previously it wasn't.","zh":"最后，normalise 某事物意思是把以前并不正常或不被接受的事情当成正常、可接受的事情来看待。","s":348.82,"e":355.24,"wt":[0,22,80,95,155,215,248,261,294,307,320,360,373,470,474,560,578]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK. Once again, our six minutes are up, but if you'd like to practise more, you'll find a quiz and worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com. See you again soon. But for now, it's goodbye.","zh":"好了，我们的六分钟又到了。不过如果你想多加练习，可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 找到小测验和练习题。很快再见，现在先说再见啦。","s":355.5,"e":366.5,"wt":[0,28,51,80,97,114,154,171,193,210,221,249,272,283,329,363,410,440,447,474,496,561,574,597,648,850,866,880,912,964,976,998,1036,1050]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Bye!","zh":"拜拜！","s":366.7,"e":367.1,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0p1q8ns.mp3"},{"epNum":18,"id":"ep-260723","date":"2026-07-23","title":"Children in warzones","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260723","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260723_6_minute_english_children_in_warzones.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260723_6_minute_english_children_in_warzones_transcript.pdf","summary":"The most recent figure from 2024 says that 520 million children are living in conflict zones. That's about one in every five children worldwide. Some of the discussions in this episode will be about death and trauma. Neil and Becca hear from a journalist who has spent some time with children in warzones and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"PTSD is a type of traumatic stress disorder. But what does the P stand for in PTSD? Is it:","options":["a) pre","b) pro","c) post"],"topic":"心理社交","titleZh":"战区中的儿童","summaryZh":"2024 年的最新数据显示，全球有 5.2 亿名儿童生活在冲突地区，约占全球儿童总数的五分之一。本期节目的部分讨论将涉及死亡和心理创伤。Neil 和 Becca 倾听了一位曾与战区儿童共处过的记者的分享，并教你一些实用的新词汇。","questionZh":"PTSD 是一种创伤性应激障碍。那么 PTSD 中的字母 P 代表什么？","optionsZh":["a) pre（……之前）","b) pro（支持/前）","c) post（……之后）"],"answer":{"letter":"c","explanationZh":"Becca 和 Neil 在节目后半段解释，PTSD 是 post-traumatic stress disorder（创伤后应激障碍）的缩写，其中的 P 代表前缀 post-，意为“在某事件之后发生的”。"},"vocab":[{"word":"hyper-vigilant","defEn":"be very careful or almost too careful to notice things, particularly when there is a possibility of danger","pos":"adj.","zh":"高度警觉的；过度戒备的","exampleEn":"I was hyper-vigilant from the very earliest age, obviously watching out, you know, am I under threat, is there a danger to me here?","ipa":"/ˌhaɪpəˈvɪdʒɪlənt/"},{"word":"be of one mind","defEn":"be part of a group of people that share the same opinion, desire, or viewpoint on a matter","pos":"短语","zh":"意见一致；达成共识","exampleEn":"I mean, they disagree about a few things, but on this they're absolutely of one mind.","ipa":"/biː əv wʌn maɪnd/"},{"word":"confront","defEn":"meet with it or deal with something","pos":"v.","zh":"直面；正视；应对","exampleEn":"Fergal says that children should be encouraged to slowly and carefully try to confront their experiences.","ipa":"/kənˈfrʌnt/"},{"word":"nightmare","defEn":"a scary or upsetting dream","pos":"n.","zh":"噩梦；可怕的经历","exampleEn":"Yes, and a nightmare is a very scary or upsetting dream.","ipa":"/ˈnaɪtmeə/"},{"word":"phenomenally","defEn":"do something in an extreme or surprising way","pos":"adv.","zh":"极其；惊人地；非凡地","exampleEn":"The big lesson is that, given a chance, the human spirit, and especially children, are phenomenally resilient.","ipa":"/fəˈnɒmɪnəli/"},{"word":"resilient","defEn":"able to recover and come back from something difficult or bad","pos":"adj.","zh":"有韧性的；能迅速恢复的","exampleEn":"To be resilient is to be able to recover from and come back from something difficult or bad that's happened.","ipa":"/rɪˈzɪliənt/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"go into detail","zh":"详细说明；展开讲细节（常用于否定形式 won't go into detail，汇报和口语都很常用）","contextEn":"We won't go into detail, but if you think you feel may uncomfortable, you can check the transcript for this episode before listening to it on our website, bbclearningenglish.com."},{"en":"one in every five","zh":"五分之一（雅思听力与阅读数据同义替换的高频表达，对应 20%）","contextEn":"That's about one in every five children worldwide."},{"en":"watch out","zh":"提防；保持警觉；小心（非常实用的日常口语动词短语）","contextEn":"I was hyper-vigilant from the very earliest age, obviously watching out, you know, am I under threat, is there a danger to me here?"},{"en":"given a chance","zh":"只要有机会；一旦给予机会（过去分词作条件状语，简洁有力）","contextEn":"The big lesson is that, given a chance, the human spirit, and especially children, are phenomenally resilient ."}],"sentences":[{"en":"Fergal is a BBC reporter known best for reporting from war zones around the world and, as a result, developed PTSD, a type of stress disorder.","zh":"Fergal 是一位 BBC 记者，以报道全球各地战区新闻而闻名，也因此患上了创伤后应激障碍（PTSD）——一种应激障碍。","coreEn":"Fergal is a BBC reporter and developed PTSD.","coreZh":"Fergal 是一位 BBC 记者，后来患上了 PTSD。","cars":[{"text":"known best for reporting from war zones around the world","hook":"-ed 修饰","noteZh":"紧贴 reporter，补充这位记者最出名的职业背景"},{"text":", as a result,","hook":"逗号插入语","noteZh":"点明前后因果关系——正因为长期在战区采访，才导致后面的病症"},{"text":", a type of stress disorder","hook":"逗号同位语","noteZh":"给专业缩写 PTSD 做简明解释，说明它属于哪类疾病"}],"tipZh":"并列谓语 is ... and developed ... 被长修饰语隔开时，先找准主语 Fergal 对应的两个核心动词，就能快速理顺逻辑。"},{"en":"Researchers may disagree on whether the experiences of children in warzones is improving, but on the point of recovery, they are of one mind on that you can't recover from war trauma if you continue living with it.","zh":"研究人员对于战区儿童的处境是否正在改善或许存在分歧，但在康复问题上，他们一致认为：如果你持续生活在创伤之中，就不可能从战争创伤中走出来。","coreEn":"Researchers may disagree on whether ..., but they are of one mind.","coreZh":"研究人员对……或许意见不一，但他们有一点想法一致。","cars":[{"text":"whether the experiences of children in warzones is improving","hook":"whether 宾语从句","noteZh":"交代研究人员存在分歧的具体议题"},{"text":"on the point of recovery","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"限定话题范围，聚焦到“康复”这一点上"},{"text":"that you can't recover from war trauma if you continue living with it","hook":"从句内容 + if 条件状语从句","noteZh":"道出大家共识的具体内容：持续身处创伤环境就无法康复"}],"tipZh":"雅思阅读和听力特别爱考“虽然在 A 上有分歧，但在 B 上达成共识”（may disagree on ..., but ... are of one mind），转折词 but 后面才是真正的考点。"},{"en":"And so there's a new field of theory, well I mean relatively new field of theory which encourages children to slowly and carefully try to confront obviously not death and massacre again, but things like learning to sleep alone, not having to sleep with a parent because that's what they did during the war because they were so consistently terrified.","zh":"因此出现了一个新的理论领域——确切地说是相对较新的理论领域——它鼓励孩子们缓慢而谨慎地尝试去直面一些事情；显然不是再次面对死亡和大屠杀，而是像学习独自睡觉这样的小事，不必再和父母同睡，因为在战争期间他们长期处于极度恐惧中，一直只能那样睡。","coreEn":"There's a new field of theory which encourages children to try to confront things like learning to sleep alone.","coreZh":"有一个新的理论领域鼓励孩子尝试直面像学习独自睡觉这样的事。","cars":[{"text":", well I mean relatively new field of theory","hook":"口语自我修正插入语","noteZh":"说话人临时措辞更严谨，把“新理论”修正为“相对较新的理论”"},{"text":"obviously not death and massacre again, but things like learning to sleep alone","hook":"not ... but ... 对比结构","noteZh":"先排除极端误解（不是再次直面死亡和屠杀），再点出真正要面对的日常小事"},{"text":"because that's what they did during the war because they were so consistently terrified","hook":"连环状语从句 because","noteZh":"一层套一层解释孩子为什么不敢独自睡觉：战时习惯如此，根源是持续的恐惧"}],"tipZh":"真实英语访谈常夹杂 well I mean 这类自我修正和连环 because，听力时别被口语碎词带偏，盯紧 not ... but ... 的真正核心。"},{"en":"The noun phenomenon, or phenomena in its plural form, refers to an observable fact or event that can be experienced through the senses.","zh":"名词 phenomenon（复数形式为 phenomena）指的是能够通过感官体验到的、可观察的事实或事件。","coreEn":"The noun phenomenon refers to an observable fact or event.","coreZh":"名词 phenomenon 指的是一种可观察的事实或事件。","cars":[{"text":", or phenomena in its plural form,","hook":"逗号插入语","noteZh":"顺带提示该词的不规则复数拼写 phenomena"},{"text":"that can be experienced through the senses","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"进一步界定什么样的事实或事件：必须能通过感官去感知"}],"tipZh":"记住 phenomenon（单数）与 phenomena（复数）的拼写区别，这是雅思学术写作中极易写错的词形。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Hello, this is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Neil.","zh":"大家好，欢迎收听 BBC Learning English 出品的《六分钟英语》。我是 Neil。","s":0.37,"e":4.66,"wt":[0,14,43,58,79,123,194,212,238,303,367,395]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"And I'm Becca.","zh":"我是 Becca。","s":4.82,"e":5.84,"wt":[0,16,36]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Now, today, we're going to be talking about the very serious topic of war and how it affects children.","zh":"今天，我们要探讨一个非常严肃的话题：战争以及它如何影响儿童。","s":5.84,"e":12.58,"wt":[0,43,66,99,132,145,158,205,238,258,292,346,397,429,482,502,522,564,606]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Some of the discussions you hear will mention death and trauma. We won't go into detail, but if you think you feel may uncomfortable, you can check the transcript for this episode before listening to it on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"你即将听到的部分讨论会提及死亡和心理创伤。我们不会展开讲细节，但如果你觉得可能会感到不适，可以先在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 查看本期节目的文本，再决定是否收听。","s":12.96,"e":27.22,"wt":[0,0,32,66,96,140,172,184,246,282,310,388,403,426,430,484,540,552,566,590,616,652,665,676,760,774,794,814,841,894,910,941,980,1041,1062,1080,1110,1150,1168,1258]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Yes. And remember, you'll find all this episode's vocabulary, along with a free worksheet, and many other 6 Minute English episodes on our website. Again, that's bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"没错。别忘了，你可以在我们的网站上找到本期节目的全部词汇、免费练习题以及更多《六分钟英语》往期节目。网址还是 bbclearningenglish.com。","s":27.22,"e":37.88,"wt":[0,72,90,150,176,204,226,259,316,379,406,414,434,469,534,538,574,602,629,662,716,774,790,820,831,838,844]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"The most recent figure from 2024 says that 520 million children are living in conflict zones. That's about one in every five children worldwide.","zh":"2024 年的最新数据显示，有 5.2 亿名儿童生活在冲突地区，这大约相当于全世界每五个孩子中就有一个。","s":38.08,"e":49.62,"wt":[0,26,60,111,152,198,302,336,370,474,566,599,603,661,680,730,840,876,908,934,962,993,1015,1072]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"In this episode, we'll hear from Fergal Keane on the BBC World Service Programme The Global Story. Fergal is a BBC reporter known best for reporting from war zones around the world and, as a result, developed PTSD, a type of stress disorder. We'll be finding out what PTSD stands for at the end of the programme.","zh":"在本期节目中，我们将听到 Fergal Keane 在 BBC 国际台节目《全球故事》中的分享。Fergal 是一位 BBC 记者，最擅长从世界各地的战区发回报道，但也因此患上了 PTSD——一种应激障碍。我们会在节目最后揭晓 PTSD 代表什么。","s":49.72,"e":72.6,"wt":[0,0,46,100,122,146,146,212,262,275,295,315,349,399,482,482,550,639,696,706,720,748,862,889,912,977,1004,1030,1069,1111,1116,1162,1248,1270,1280,1302,1388,1416,1590,1616,1640,1659,1717,1826,1869,1886,1938,1964,1998,2048,2097,2129,2170,2173,2188,2199,2216]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Now, in the clips we'll hear journalist Asma Khalid asks Fergal Keane about his own experience with childhood trauma. Fergal grew up in a home with an alcoholic father. Asma wanted to know how his own trauma affected his reporting on the experiences of children in warzones:","zh":"在接下来的音频片段中，我们将听到记者 Asma Khalid 询问 Fergal Keane 他自己关于童年创伤的经历。Fergal 在一个父亲酗酒的家庭中长大。Asma 想知道他自身的创伤是如何影响他去报道战区儿童经历的：","s":72.9,"e":91.18,"wt":[0,42,51,72,110,140,152,174,205,247,278,321,358,397,417,439,534,564,614,704,755,782,795,810,817,848,879,894,982,1042,1086,1120,1137,1174,1223,1273,1347,1402,1455,1476,1548,1573,1586,1660,1703,1732,1755]},{"speaker":"Fergal Keane","en":"I was hyper-vigilant from the very earliest age, obviously watching out, you know, am I under threat, is there a danger to me here?","zh":"我从很小的时候起就处于高度警觉的状态，显然一直在提防着，心里想着：我是不是正受到威胁？这里对我来说有没有危险？","s":91.18,"e":99.72,"wt":[0,26,48,196,328,350,384,397,474,496,534,570,576,594,600,628,645,697,709,740,745,782,794,806]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Fergal mentions that he was hyper-vigilant because was in dangerous situations as a child. The adjective vigilant means to always be careful to notice things, particularly when there is a possibility of danger.","zh":"Fergal 提到他曾处于 hyper-vigilant（高度警觉）的状态，因为他小时候经常身处危险境地。形容词 vigilant 的意思是时刻小心留意周围动静，尤其是当可能存在危险的时候。","s":99.78,"e":113.46,"wt":[0,44,95,116,132,154,247,308,328,341,403,484,496,539,632,650,705,767,798,810,851,867,921,938,973,1027,1112,1140,1163,1176,1202,1268,1292]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Yes. And the prefix hyper- expresses that there is a lot of or even too much of something. Though neutral, it is often used in negative or critical contexts.","zh":"没错。而前缀 hyper- 表示某事物的程度“非常多”甚至“过度”。虽然它本身是中性的，但常用于负面或批评性的语境中。","s":113.5,"e":124.28,"wt":[0,44,61,84,132,232,255,286,324,338,347,370,400,415,446,474,523,531,632,692,731,744,764,804,836,852,910,933,998]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"So, hyper-vigilant means very, very careful and thoughtful. Hypervigilance, the noun, is a state of extreme alertness and sensitivity to surroundings, possibly of extreme anxiety.","zh":"所以，hyper-vigilant 的意思是极度小心谨慎、时刻防备。它的名词形式 hypervigilance 指的是对周围环境极度警觉和敏感的状态，往往伴随着极度的焦虑。","s":124.42,"e":139.14,"wt":[0,40,122,176,222,438,480,506,602,712,732,778,793,800,837,852,904,970,992,1073,1090,1192,1288,1332,1388]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Now let's hear from Fergal as he talks about research on the experience of children in warzones:","zh":"现在我们来听听 Fergal 谈论关于战区儿童经历的研究：","s":139.62,"e":145.74,"wt":[0,32,56,78,94,154,168,182,231,252,308,322,343,420,434,496,524]},{"speaker":"Fergal Keane","en":"I mean, they disagree about a few things, but on this they' re absolutely of one mind . And that is that you can't recover from war trauma if you're living with continuing trauma.","zh":"我的意思是，他们在某些事情上看法不一，但在这一点上他们完全意见一致：那就是如果你一直生活在持续的创伤中，就无法从战争创伤里走出来。","s":145.74,"e":150.74,"wt":[0,29,44,67,113,132,147,165,200,214,237,268,294,311,368,380,408,413,419,422,427,430,434,438,442,449,453,457,463,466,471,477,482,490]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Fergal mentions that 'they', the researchers, disagree about their findings. But they' re absolutely of one mind when considering the recovery of war trauma. If you are of one mind , you are of a group of people that share the same opinion, desire, or viewpoint on a matter. Researchers may disagree on whether the experiences of children in warzones is improving, but on the point of recovery, they are of one mind on that you can't recover from war trauma if you continue living with it.","zh":"Fergal 提到“他们”，也就是研究人员，对各自的研究发现存在分歧。但在看待战争创伤的康复问题上，他们完全是 of one mind（意见一致）。如果你属于 of one mind，意思就是你和一群人在某件事上持有相同的看法、愿望或观点。研究人员对于战区儿童的处境是否正在改善或许存在分歧，但在康复问题上，他们一致认为：如果你持续生活在创伤之中，就不可能从战争创伤中恢复过来。","s":150.74,"e":182.66,"wt":[0,44,91,117,156,176,262,315,347,381,447,474,529,563,616,632,660,697,751,832,865,920,942,986,1094,1110,1125,1142,1178,1192,1216,1237,1260,1284,1298,1305,1343,1358,1423,1433,1471,1494,1523,1592,1637,1666,1723,1735,1786,1862,1880,1940,1990,2006,2034,2056,2115,2132,2177,2195,2240,2290,2342,2366,2388,2406,2428,2461,2526,2550,2570,2596,2635,2674,2698,2730,2754,2793,2875,2899,2912,2961,2977,3001,3082,3106,3122]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Fergal goes on to talk about some of the ways in which these children might be helped:","zh":"Fergal 接着谈到了或许能帮助这些孩子的一些方法：","s":182.66,"e":187.91,"wt":[0,44,71,85,148,148,165,193,207,231,258,271,307,390,401,437,474]},{"speaker":"Fergal Keane","en":"And so there's a new field of theory, well I mean relatively new field of theory which encourages children to slowly and carefully try to confront obviously not death and massacre again, but things like learning to sleep alone, not having to sleep with a parent because that's what they did during the war because they were so consistently terrified. Now, it sounds like a simple thing, but as anybody who's tried to put a child to sleep, who's had a nightmare , a child who hasn't been traumatised will know it's very difficult.","zh":"因此出现了一个新的理论领域——确切地说是相对较新的理论领域——它鼓励孩子们缓慢而谨慎地尝试去直面一些事情；显然不是再次面对死亡和大屠杀，而是像学习独自睡觉这样的小事，不必再和父母同睡，因为在战争期间他们长期处于极度恐惧中，一直只能那样睡。听起来好像很简单，但任何曾哄过做噩梦的普通孩子睡觉的人——哪怕是没受过战争创伤的孩子——都知道这其实非常困难。","s":187.91,"e":223.56,"wt":[0,24,43,69,83,97,121,131,168,196,199,230,268,282,306,315,397,423,511,579,610,649,675,757,780,797,939,978,989,1040,1060,1159,1199,1233,1301,1350,1381,1399,1425,1589,1613,1615,1637,1681,1697,1735,1917,1927,1959,1971,1985,2007,2025,2047,2072,2093,2103,2103,2149,2209,2275,2315,2320,2366,2397,2407,2456,2515,2531,2553,2567,2589,2611,2629,2721,2749,2769,2791,2867,2887,2887,2929,2976,2999,3045,3049,3067,3093,3111,3169,3193,3291,3313,3363]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Fergal says that children should be encouraged to slowly and carefully try to confront their experiences. To confront something is to meet with it or deal with it. For the children to confront their terrifying experiences, they must start with more simple things, like learning to sleep alone.","zh":"Fergal 说，应当鼓励孩子缓慢而谨慎地尝试 confront（直面）自己的经历。confront 某事就是面对它、处理它。为了让孩子们直面那些可怕的经历，他们必须从更简单的事情入手，比如学会独自睡觉。","s":223.74,"e":240.84,"wt":[0,48,72,99,138,138,192,206,262,297,313,367,384,399,442,527,632,644,692,738,752,770,794,814,830,844,885,906,980,992,1004,1036,1079,1102,1135,1201,1286,1312,1338,1371,1396,1432,1462,1518,1532,1560,1600,1608]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Sleeping alone seems simple enough, but Fergal compares the experiences of a children of in a warzone to that of children who have nightmares .","zh":"独自睡觉听起来挺简单的，但 Fergal 把战区儿童的经历比作做 nightmares（噩梦）的孩子。","s":240.87,"e":249.94,"wt":[0,83,121,159,202,258,286,321,377,398,475,483,489,529,545,566,581,629,636,661,674,729,783,818,870]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Yes, and a nightmare is a very scary or upsetting dream. It can also be used metaphorically to describe unpleasant events or experiences. Now, let's hear Fergal talk about what he has learned from reporting on children in warzones:","zh":"是的，nightmare 指的是非常可怕或令人不安的梦，也就是噩梦。它也可以作比喻用法，用来形容令人不快的事件或经历。现在我们来听 Fergal 谈谈他从报道战区儿童中学到了什么：","s":249.94,"e":265.35,"wt":[0,54,62,90,152,152,184,228,262,297,360,452,466,482,506,518,542,628,671,723,772,798,850,1028,1042,1064,1092,1119,1159,1182,1211,1228,1252,1282,1324,1332,1354,1394,1429]},{"speaker":"Fergal Keane","en":"The big lesson is that, given a chance, the human spirit, and especially children, are phenomenally resilient .","zh":"最大的启示在于，只要给个机会，人类的精神——尤其是孩子们——有着惊人的韧性。","s":265.35,"e":273.68,"wt":[0,46,83,112,126,205,205,251,319,339,371,371,382,456,515,537,657,757]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Fergal describes the children as being phenomenally resilient . The noun phenomenon, or phenomena in its plural form, refers to an observable fact or event that can be experienced through the senses. The adverb phenomenally describes something being done or felt in an extreme or surprising way.","zh":"Fergal 形容这些孩子是 phenomenally resilient（极具韧性的）。名词 phenomenon（复数形式为 phenomena）指的是能够通过感官体验到的、可观察的事实或事件。副词 phenomenally 用来形容某事以极端或令人惊叹的方式发生或被感受到。","s":273.68,"e":294.98,"wt":[0,47,102,126,178,186,240,348,407,452,472,514,626,648,718,734,758,802,832,878,893,908,984,1014,1029,1066,1097,1120,1210,1219,1270,1322,1450,1470,1509,1599,1659,1750,1762,1809,1832,1880,1907,1939,1986,2020,2083]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"To be resilient is to be able to recover from and come back from something difficult or bad that's happened. So, Fergal says that children can be surprisingly positive despite the negative things happening around them. They are phenomenally resilient .","zh":"resilient 的意思是能够从已经发生的困难或糟糕经历中恢复过来、重新振作。因此，Fergal 说尽管周围发生了许多负面的事情，孩子们依然能表现得惊人地积极乐观——他们有着非凡的韧性。","s":295,"e":311.38,"wt":[0,29,44,102,116,132,144,173,236,239,268,290,319,347,379,444,522,564,570,628,744,755,799,829,859,916,932,956,1053,1121,1160,1203,1233,1273,1333,1342,1412,1434,1464,1544,1597]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"A result of these negative things could be PTSD. Earlier we mentioned PTSD and it stands for post-traumatic stress disorder.","zh":"这些负面经历可能导致的一个结果就是 PTSD。前面我们提到过 PTSD，它代表的是 post-traumatic stress disorder（创伤后应激障碍）。","s":311.38,"e":321.62,"wt":[0,43,83,104,157,192,244,272,312,472,504,535,596,661,675,696,736,779,901,967]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Yes, and the P in PTSD stands for post-, as we've just heard. And the prefix post- expresses something that happens after or as a result of the event it's attached to.","zh":"没错，正如刚才听到的，PTSD 中的 P 代表 post-。前缀 post- 表示发生在所修饰事件之后、或由该事件引起的事物。","s":321.62,"e":333.74,"wt":[0,50,60,90,164,186,221,292,311,366,373,410,432,483,507,532,583,619,693,750,788,818,908,925,942,980,1008,1034,1053,1088,1120,1142]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Now, let's recap the vocabulary. We first had hyper-vigilant , that is to be very careful or maybe almost too careful to notice things, particularly when there is a possibility of danger.","zh":"现在我们来回顾一下词汇。第一个是 hyper-vigilant，意思是极其小心、甚至近乎过度谨慎地留意周围事物，尤其是在可能存在危险的情况下。","s":334.22,"e":346.68,"wt":[0,42,67,109,134,248,264,304,328,426,464,480,500,512,544,599,636,668,684,724,792,792,858,894,974,982,1009,1043,1056,1062,1144,1188]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"To be of one mind is to be part of a group of people that share the same opinion, desire, or viewpoint on a matter.","zh":"be of one mind 的意思是和一群人在某件事上持有相同的看法、愿望或观点，即意见一致。","s":346.86,"e":354.42,"wt":[0,36,43,56,80,111,132,144,160,192,208,231,258,272,320,352,390,412,450,520,570,613,654,669,676]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"We had confront something. That is to meet with it or deal with it.","zh":"我们还学了 confront something，意思是直面它、应对它。","s":354.42,"e":358.86,"wt":[0,42,45,108,179,209,231,240,271,302,317,331,363,397]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"A nightmare is a scary or upsetting dream.","zh":"nightmare 是指可怕或令人心神不宁的梦，即噩梦。","s":358.92,"e":362.22,"wt":[0,32,84,96,117,159,175,250]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"To do something phenomenally is to do it in an extreme or surprising way.","zh":"phenomenally 地做某事，意思是以极端或令人惊叹的方式去做。","s":362.22,"e":367.48,"wt":[0,40,44,113,209,231,242,258,275,290,305,363,379,474]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"And to be resilient is to be able to recover and come back from something difficult or bad. That's happened once again. Our six minutes are up. You'll find a quiz and a worksheet to practice the vocabulary we've learnt from this episode on our website BBC Learning English. Goodbye for now.","zh":"最后，resilient 的意思是能够从艰难或糟糕的遭遇中恢复过来、重新振作。时间又到了，我们的六分钟结束了。你可以在 BBC Learning English 网站上找到小测验和练习题，复习本期学到的词汇。先再见啦。","s":367.48,"e":385.04,"wt":[0,42,44,59,132,141,156,220,220,231,328,348,374,398,434,447,514,534,576,597,754,782,798,820,850,850,856,884,931,952,962,1008,1023,1026,1082,1124,1143,1161,1223,1252,1254,1268,1332,1362,1376,1410,1470,1504,1570,1666,1712,1728]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Bye.","zh":"拜拜。","s":385.24,"e":385.58,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0ny2jn5.mp3"},{"epNum":19,"id":"ep-260716","date":"2026-07-16","title":"What's in a footballer's brain?","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260716","mp3":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0ny289g.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260716_6_minute_english_what's_in_a_footballer's_brain_worksheet_.pdf","summary":"Football players are faster than ever before. But in a game where every tiny decision counts, elite clubs are turning to neuroscience to understand how players can think, decide and perform even faster. Neil and Georgie discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"Which part of the brain is best known for its role in processing fear and detecting threats? Is it:","options":["a) the brain stem","b) the amygdala","c) the cerebellum"],"topic":"健康医学","titleZh":"足球运动员的大脑里藏着什么秘密？","summaryZh":"如今的足球运动员比以往任何时候都跑得更快。但在一项每一个微小决策都至关重要的运动中，顶级俱乐部正转向神经科学，以探索球员如何能思考得更快、决策更果断、表现更出色。Neil 和 Georgie 将围绕这一话题展开讨论，并教你一些实用的新词汇。","questionZh":"大脑的哪个部位最以处理恐惧和察觉威胁的功能而闻名？","optionsZh":["a) 脑干","b) 杏仁核","c) 小脑"],"answer":{"letter":"b","explanationZh":"Georgie 在节目最后确认了正确答案是 b) the amygdala（杏仁核），并指出杏仁核与人类对恐惧和焦虑的处理密切相关。"},"vocab":[{"word":"(something) going on","defEn":"something unknown and interesting happening","pos":"短语","zh":"（有某种微妙/有趣的）事情正在发生；暗藏门道","exampleEn":"There's something going on in the brain when the athletes are making quick decisions.","ipa":"/ˈɡəʊɪŋ ɒn/"},{"word":"expertise","defEn":"specialised knowledge or skill gained through study or experience","pos":"n.","zh":"专业知识；专长；精湛技艺","exampleEn":"That reduction in activity in Neymar's cortex reflects that he has absolute expertise in moving his feet.","ipa":"/ˌekspɜːˈtiːz/"},{"word":"conscious","defEn":"involving active thought or attention","pos":"adj.","zh":"有意识的；刻意专注的","exampleEn":"And it's essentially done with very, very little conscious effort.","ipa":"/ˈkɒnʃəs/"},{"word":"driven by","defEn":"influenced by or pushed forward by","pos":"短语","zh":"由……驱动的；受……支配/影响的","exampleEn":"A lot of performance is also driven by your emotional state.","ipa":"/ˈdrɪvən baɪ/"},{"word":"all over the place","defEn":"describes a situation or mental state that is disorganised, chaotic or confused","pos":"短语","zh":"乱成一团；心烦意乱；毫无头绪","exampleEn":"If you're mentally all over the place, it's more difficult to kick the ball accurately.","ipa":"/ɔːl ˈəʊvə ðə pleɪs/"},{"word":"margin","defEn":"a measure of difference, small details of which can affect an outcome","pos":"n.","zh":"微小差距；细微优势；幅度","exampleEn":"And when we talk about margins, those are the little margins that can make all the difference.","ipa":"/ˈmɑːdʒɪn/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"for a reason","zh":"是有原因的；绝非偶然（放在句尾，强调某称呼或现象名副其实）","contextEn":"They call it 'the beautiful game' for a reason."},{"en":"make a lot of sense","zh":"很有道理；合情合理（口语和职场讨论中表示逻辑通顺的高频表达）","contextEn":"It made a lot of sense to go, well, neurologically or cognitively, there's something going on ."},{"en":"by accident","zh":"偶然地；无意中（与 on purpose 相对，适合解释“有意识 vs 无意识”）","contextEn":"Conscious describes something done with awareness, not by accident."},{"en":"make all the difference","zh":"起到决定性作用；使结果大不相同（雅思口语写作强调关键细节的利器）","contextEn":"And when we talk about margins , those are the little margins that can make all the difference."}],"sentences":[{"en":"As we've heard, football players have to make decisions in high-pressure situations, and their performance may be influenced by their emotional state and fear response.","zh":"正如我们所听到的，足球运动员必须在高压情境下做出决策，而他们的表现可能会受到情绪状态和恐惧反应的影响。","coreEn":"Football players have to make decisions, and their performance may be influenced.","coreZh":"足球运动员必须做决策，而且他们的表现可能会受到影响。","cars":[{"text":"As we've heard","hook":"状语从句 as","noteZh":"承上启下，呼应前面已经提到的内容"},{"text":"in high-pressure situations","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"说明做决策时的场景条件——高压之下"},{"text":"by their emotional state and fear response","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"点明影响表现的两个内在心理因素"}],"tipZh":"英语科普常把两个相关事实用 and 串起，前句主动（have to make）、后句被动（may be influenced），读的时候留意视角切换。"},{"en":"They found that his brain was a lot less active than the brains of his peers when he rotated his right ankle.","zh":"他们发现，当他转动右脚踝时，他大脑的活跃程度远低于同龄球员的大脑。","coreEn":"They found that his brain was a lot less active.","coreZh":"他们发现他的大脑活跃度低得多。","cars":[{"text":"than the brains of his peers","hook":"比较结构 than","noteZh":"交代比较对象：同龄/同级别球员的大脑（peers 是雅思高频词，意为“同龄人/同行”）"},{"text":"when he rotated his right ankle","hook":"状语从句 when","noteZh":"限定实验动作场景——转动右脚踝的那一刻"}],"tipZh":"比较级前加 a lot（a lot less active）表示程度差距很大；注意 the brains of his peers 里的 the brains 用来避免重复且保持比较对象对等。"},{"en":"So it could be something like they're not great at storing lots of information, so we're trying to overwhelm them, in a safe environment like training, that's where the development and improvement can happen to hopefully affect when they go out into games or cup competitions.","zh":"比如他们可能不太擅长储存大量信息，所以我们会在训练这种安全的环境中有意让他们信息超载，因为正是在这种时候他们才能获得成长和进步，并有望在真正踏上联赛或杯赛赛场时发挥作用。","coreEn":"We're trying to overwhelm them, and that's where the development and improvement can happen.","coreZh":"我们试着给他们施加超负荷压力，而成长和进步正是在那里发生的。","cars":[{"text":"in a safe environment like training","hook":"逗号插入语","noteZh":"限定施加超负荷压力的前提——在训练这种安全可控的环境里"},{"text":"that's where the development and improvement can happen","hook":"表语从句 where","noteZh":"解释这么做的意义：真正的提升就发生在这个过程里"},{"text":"to hopefully affect when they go out into games or cup competitions","hook":"不定式 to do","noteZh":"点出最终目的——希望将训练效果迁移到正式比赛和杯赛中"}],"tipZh":"口语访谈里常连续用逗号和 so 拼接长句，听的时候按“问题（不擅长存信息）→ 做法（训练时加压）→ 目的（提升比赛表现）”三步切块，立刻通透。"},{"en":"If you don't focus right in those moments, and let's say mentally you're all over the place , then it becomes more difficult to, to have an accurate corner and to try to win the game.","zh":"如果你在那些关键时刻没有集中注意力，比如说你的思绪乱成一团，那么想要开出一脚精准的角球并努力赢下比赛就会变得更加困难。","coreEn":"If you don't focus, then it becomes more difficult to have an accurate corner and try to win.","coreZh":"如果你不专注，那么精准开出角球和争取赢球就会变得更难。","cars":[{"text":"right in those moments","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"副词 right 加强语气，强调“就在那些紧要关头”"},{"text":"and let's say mentally you're all over the place","hook":"口语假设插入 let's say","noteZh":"用 let's say（比如说/假设）进一步具象化“不专注”的心理状态"},{"text":"to have an accurate corner and to try to win the game","hook":"不定式 to do","noteZh":"做形式主语 it 背后的真正主语，说明具体变难的两件事"}],"tipZh":"口语中用 let's say（比方说）引出具体假设非常自然，雅思口语举例论证时用上它能立刻摆脱干巴巴的 for example。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Hello, this is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Georgie.","zh":"大家好，欢迎收听 BBC Learning English 出品的《六分钟英语》。我是 Georgie。","s":142.24,"e":146.96,"wt":[0,98,116,140,166,194,230,264,299,352,407,416]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"And I'm Neil.","zh":"我是 Neil。","s":146.96,"e":147.98,"wt":[0,21,40]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Neil, I'm not a huge fan of football, but I know you are. So, why do you love it so much?","zh":"Neil，我不是什么铁杆球迷，但我知道你是。那你为什么这么喜欢足球呢？","s":148.13,"e":153.21,"wt":[0,20,44,68,125,157,157,157,216,240,246,279,303,327,367,369,383,407,440,456,483]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Oh, where can I start? I think it's the combination of the passion and also the skill and athleticism of the players. They call it 'the beautiful game' for a reason.","zh":"哦，这让我从何说起呢？我觉得是激情与球员们的技巧、体能的完美结合。人们称它为“美丽运动”是有原因的。","s":153.41,"e":167.68,"wt":[0,3,42,66,75,142,147,239,239,291,334,367,374,509,557,657,683,770,835,936,940,971,1127,1147,1147,1189,1213,1244,1291,1303,1313]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Now, football players are faster than ever before, but the game is also about making very quick decisions.","zh":"如今的足球运动员比以往任何时候都跑得更快，但这项运动同样关乎做出极快的决策。","s":167.82,"e":174.32,"wt":[0,36,85,114,138,186,223,240,285,307,329,365,374,404,441,486,519,553]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Yes. To be the best of the best, some football clubs have started looking beyond the body, to the brain. Here's Tom Cooke, Academy Psychologist for Nottingham Forest Football Club, talking to the BBC World Service.","zh":"没错。为了成为顶尖中的顶尖，一些足球俱乐部开始不再局限于体能，而是把目光投向大脑。下面来听诺丁汉森林足球俱乐部青训心理学家 Tom Cooke 接受 BBC 国际台的采访。","s":174.32,"e":188.22,"wt":[0,25,41,59,83,116,136,164,205,218,271,304,333,379,422,462,482,508,521,541,670,688,731,800,861,926,945,1004,1040,1084,1127,1177,1185,1210,1230,1255]},{"speaker":"Tom Cooke","en":"We're watching these athletes make decisions in split instincts, under the highest pressures and the highest intensities. It made a lot of sense to go, well, neurologically or cognitively, there's something going on .","zh":"我们看到这些运动员在最高压力和最高强度下凭瞬间直觉做出决定。于是我们很自然地会想：嗯，从神经层面或认知层面来看，大脑里一定暗藏门道。","s":188.36,"e":199.42,"wt":[0,18,66,96,144,175,222,234,264,318,366,370,408,481,486,510,544,640,654,674,690,702,724,754,770,776,785,878,891,972,1009,1046,1072,1083]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"There's something going on in the brain when the athletes are making quick decisions. If there's something going on , something unknown and interesting is happening. So, what's happening inside the brains of football players? And could brain training help win more games? That's what we'll be hearing about in this programme. And don't forget to find a free transcript at bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"当运动员快速做决策时，他们大脑里正“暗藏门道”（something going on）。如果说 there's something going on，意思就是有某种未知而有趣的事情正在发生。那么，足球运动员的大脑内部究竟在发生什么？大脑训练能否帮助球队赢得更多比赛？这就是我们本期节目要探讨的内容。别忘了去 bbclearningenglish.com 获取免费文本。","s":199.42,"e":222.86,"wt":[0,45,93,122,133,144,161,190,213,230,277,294,338,383,450,463,511,569,602,632,674,726,726,752,816,829,973,986,1031,1083,1126,1146,1180,1204,1250,1313,1334,1380,1406,1464,1491,1522,1552,1647,1680,1694,1714,1740,1770,1796,1812,1844,1906,1921,1946,1977,2010,2015,2026,2050,2127,2131]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"But first, Neil. I have a quiz question for you. As we've heard, football players have to make decisions in high-pressure situations, and their performance may be influenced by their emotional state and fear response. But which part of the brain is best known for its role in processing fear and detecting threats? Is it: a) the brain stem b) the amygdala c) the cerebellum","zh":"不过首先，Neil，我有个小测验问题要问你。正如我们听到的，足球运动员必须在高压情境下做决策，而他们的表现可能受到情绪状态和恐惧反应的影响。那么，大脑的哪个部位最以处理恐惧和察觉威胁而闻名呢？是：a) 脑干，b) 杏仁核，还是 c) 小脑？","s":222.96,"e":247.18,"wt":[0,28,75,112,141,158,161,167,241,269,317,328,364,392,437,472,498,513,538,598,612,693,761,804,815,895,924,944,1013,1014,1049,1112,1140,1164,1197,1253,1275,1310,1338,1352,1382,1408,1422,1450,1495,1512,1535,1567,1597,1670,1706,1734,1768,1868,1882,1908,1958,1974,2026,2070,2132,2140,2274,2294,2324]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, I don't know, but I'm going to guess b) the amygdala because I like the sound of the word.","zh":"嗯，我其实不知道，但我猜 b) 杏仁核，因为我喜欢这个词读起来的感觉。","s":247.41,"e":252.72,"wt":[0,21,35,76,105,130,155,199,216,256,272,297,371,398,402,419,434,459,467,483]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK, well I'll reveal the answer later in the programme. In 2014, a group of Japanesescientists studied the brain of Brazilian football star Neymar. They found that his brain was a lot less active than the brains of his peers when he rotated his right ankle. Here's Holly Bridge, Professor of Neuroscience at the University of Oxford talking about this research to the BBC World Service.","zh":"好吧，我稍后在节目中揭晓答案。2014 年，一组日本科学家研究了巴西足球巨星内马尔的大脑。他们发现，当他转动右脚踝时，他大脑的活跃程度远低于同龄球员的大脑。下面请听牛津大学神经科学教授 Holly Bridge 向 BBC 国际台解读这项研究。","s":252.85,"e":277.36,"wt":[0,18,49,79,128,148,194,233,248,271,367,441,475,484,524,575,688,725,746,773,795,855,905,937,1011,1055,1061,1083,1100,1131,1154,1163,1187,1217,1280,1297,1320,1351,1365,1397,1442,1467,1487,1540,1563,1601,1667,1703,1732,1771,1807,1848,1923,1932,1952,2020,2035,2076,2124,2158,2216,2240,2254,2274,2294,2328]},{"speaker":"Holly Bridge","en":"That reduction in activity in Neymar's cortex reflects that he has absolute expertise in moving his feet. And it's essentially done with very, very little conscious effort.","zh":"内马尔大脑皮层活跃度的降低反映出，他在脚部动作上拥有登峰造极的专长，而且这几乎不需要费多少有意识的精力就能完成。","s":277.36,"e":288,"wt":[0,13,52,70,141,159,229,332,380,389,407,453,496,559,573,615,637,665,685,713,790,822,846,874,903,936,989]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Holly said Neymar has absolute expertise in moving his feet. Expertise means a high level of skill or knowledge gained with experience. Neymar's expertise is shown without much conscious effort. Conscious describes something done with awareness, not by accident. So, if Neymar kicks the ball without much conscious effort, he plays without thinking too much.","zh":"Holly 说内马尔在移动双脚方面拥有极高的 expertise（专长/精湛技艺）。expertise 指的是通过经验积累获得的高超技能或专业知识。内马尔展现这种专长时几乎不需要太多 conscious（有意识的）努力。conscious 形容的是带着自觉意识去做的事情，而不是偶然为之。所以，如果内马尔踢球时不需要太多有意识的努力，说明他根本不用过多思考就能踢出来。","s":288,"e":311.8,"wt":[0,39,72,124,202,286,292,298,346,370,506,568,600,606,631,656,685,720,723,789,828,862,958,1016,1082,1096,1133,1188,1268,1288,1389,1431,1487,1534,1562,1589,1644,1662,1674,1764,1788,1812,1887,1911,1932,1980,2021,2031,2084,2119,2130,2163,2200,2247,2264]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Decades after this research, football clubs are becoming more interested in brain data technology, even exploring whether a player's brain can be trained.","zh":"在这项研究过去许多年后，足球俱乐部对脑数据技术越来越感兴趣，甚至开始探索球员的大脑是否也能接受训练。","s":311.9,"e":320.51,"wt":[0,50,50,81,138,207,227,247,301,328,378,400,442,468,535,576,622,665,682,729,768,782,795]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Here's Tom Cooke, Academy Psychologist for Nottingham Forest Football Club.","zh":"下面来听诺丁汉森林足球俱乐部青训心理学家 Tom Cooke 的介绍。","s":320.51,"e":325.54,"wt":[0,42,59,177,229,271,271,310,350,402]},{"speaker":"Tom Cooke","en":"What we're doing is trying to design sessions but with cognition or neuroscience at the centre of it. So it could be something like they're not great at storing lots of information, so we're trying to overwhelm them, in a safe environment like training, that's where the development and improvement can happen to hopefully affect when they go out into games or cup competitions.","zh":"我们正在做的，是尝试设计以认知或神经科学为核心的训练课。比如他们可能不太擅长储存大量信息，所以我们会在训练这种安全的环境中有意让他们信息超载，因为正是在这种时候他们才能获得成长和进步，并有望在真正踏上联赛或杯赛赛场时发挥作用。","s":325.68,"e":345.49,"wt":[0,0,13,48,57,111,126,186,253,272,312,400,411,476,496,517,552,557,570,583,596,630,645,704,733,734,752,778,845,892,914,932,1028,1029,1064,1094,1106,1160,1184,1198,1202,1228,1286,1314,1381,1403,1424,1438,1490,1504,1556,1570,1597,1607,1656,1704,1736,1748,1752,1789,1804,1839,1852,1874]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Training can help players deal with the pressures of the game. Here's Holly Bridge again, talking about how emotions can affect a player's game.","zh":"训练可以帮助球员应对比赛压力。下面我们再听 Holly Bridge 谈谈情绪会如何影响球员的发挥。","s":345.49,"e":354.26,"wt":[0,57,79,106,175,184,219,233,271,283,299,392,427,453,493,531,577,610,632,688,708,753,761,807]},{"speaker":"Holly Bridge","en":"A lot of performance is also driven by your emotional state. And that is something you can learn to control.","zh":"很大一部分赛场表现也是由你的情绪状态驱动的，而情绪状态是你可以学会去控制的。","s":354.26,"e":362.78,"wt":[0,3,38,49,161,181,221,276,301,372,431,542,542,590,594,614,622,667,702,762]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"How well a player performs is driven by their emotional state. Here, driven by means influenced by or pushed forward by. Holly says that too many emotions can lead to a lack of precision and focus.","zh":"球员发挥得好不好是由他们的情绪状态 driven by（驱动/支配）的。这里的 driven by 意思是受……影响或推动。Holly 说，情绪波动过大可能会导致精准度和专注力的缺失。","s":363.06,"e":375.87,"wt":[0,26,50,74,112,154,164,229,253,288,360,410,438,470,531,566,633,648,673,709,763,802,839,898,901,924,955,1020,1038,1060,1077,1121,1139,1140,1203,1224]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"In a football match, high-pressure scenarios like penalties, corners and free kicks offer rare moments of stillness. Here's Niklas Häusler, founder and CEO of Neuro 11, talking to the BBC World Service. He measures player's brainwaves before these important kicks and teaches them how to stay relaxed.","zh":"在一场足球比赛中，点球、角球和任意球这类高压场景反而提供了难得的片刻静止。下面来听 Neuro 11 创始人兼首席执行官 Niklas Häusler 接受 BBC 国际台的采访。他会在这些关键主罚前测量球员的脑电波，并教他们如何保持放松。","s":375.87,"e":396.28,"wt":[0,13,20,76,112,226,258,286,370,407,428,456,491,526,603,610,618,737,786,841,922,965,998,1025,1043,1096,1189,1202,1220,1247,1291,1318,1413,1425,1493,1517,1602,1637,1659,1716,1749,1775,1814,1842,1857,1873,1898]},{"speaker":"Niklas Häusler","en":"If you don't focus right in those moments, and let's say mentally you're all over the place , then it becomes more difficult to, to have an accurate corner and to try to win the game. And when we talk about margins , those are the little margins that can make all the difference.","zh":"如果你在那些关键时刻没有集中注意力，比如说你的思绪乱成一团，那么想要开出一脚精准的角球并努力赢下比赛就会变得更加困难。而当我们谈到“微小差距”（margins）时，正是这些细微的差距能够决定成败。","s":396.58,"e":408.58,"wt":[0,14,24,24,85,110,123,138,178,198,223,240,286,312,330,340,362,387,405,428,439,491,502,527,542,587,594,604,645,681,690,700,715,729,740,758,775,790,810,820,841,865,893,910,942,952,955,973,998,1012,1022,1036,1049,1056]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"If you're mentally all over the place , it's more difficult to kick the ball accurately. When we describe something as ' all over the place ' we mean that it's chaotic, disorganised or confused. Niklas said that small margins can make all the difference to a game.","zh":"如果你的精神状态 all over the place（乱成一团），就很难把球踢准。当我们用 all over the place 来形容某件事时，意思就是它杂乱无章、混乱不堪或毫无头绪。Niklas 还说，微小的 margins（差距）可以对比赛产生决定性影响。","s":408.64,"e":424.1,"wt":[0,16,58,80,118,160,168,190,207,232,261,310,323,355,370,403,486,515,526,581,636,647,656,676,703,722,754,767,783,813,830,860,928,1018,1033,1130,1182,1205,1235,1271,1322,1346,1400,1413,1425,1460,1470,1512]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yes. A margin is a measure of difference. Here, we're talking about small details that can affect a player's performance or outcome of a match.","zh":"是的。margin 是衡量差异幅度的词。在这里，它指的是能够影响球员表现或比赛胜负的细微细节。","s":424.42,"e":432.64,"wt":[0,38,46,96,112,120,192,204,297,314,334,364,424,443,478,501,520,560,565,617,688,701,746,759,765]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Now, speaking of how emotions can affect performance, I think it's time for the answer to your quiz question Georgie.","zh":"好了，说到情绪如何影响表现，Georgie，我觉得是时候公布你那道小测验的答案了。","s":432.64,"e":439.08,"wt":[0,21,75,90,111,181,187,225,295,301,332,356,381,400,421,457,469,497,549,569]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yes. I asked you which part of the brain is best known for its role in processing fear.","zh":"对。我问你的是，大脑的哪个部位最以处理恐惧的功能而闻名。","s":439.17,"e":444.4,"wt":[0,9,81,103,125,125,144,159,181,219,240,264,307,326,361,371,384,450]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"And I guessed b) the amygdala.","zh":"而我猜的是 b) 杏仁核。","s":444.45,"e":447.16,"wt":[0,19,35,102,124,156]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Which was the correct answer! Well done. The amygdala is associated with our processing of fear and anxiety. OK, time for a vocabulary recap. If there is something going on , something interesting or important is happening.","zh":"答对了！干得漂亮。杏仁核与我们对恐惧和焦虑的处理有关。好了，现在来回顾一下词汇。如果说 there is something going on，意思就是有某种有趣或重要的事情正在发生。","s":447.16,"e":461.92,"wt":[0,46,96,150,169,222,258,319,324,372,388,459,497,510,580,614,636,673,758,793,826,836,866,930,1009,1019,1042,1087,1121,1156,1157,1158,1224,1304,1320,1380,1398]},{"speaker":"Expertise","en":"is specialised knowledge or skill gained through study or experience.","zh":"Expertise（专长/专业知识）指通过学习或经验获得的专门知识或技能。","s":462.68,"e":467.16,"wt":[0,14,72,159,177,198,239,287,321,334]},{"speaker":"Conscious","en":"means involving active thought or attention.","zh":"Conscious（有意识的）意思是需要主动思考或集中注意力的。","s":467.53,"e":470.53,"wt":[0,61,96,137,185,208]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"If something is driven by something, it is influenced by it and pushed forward by it.","zh":"如果某事是 driven by 另一件事，意思就是它受后者影响并由后者推动。","s":470.53,"e":475.76,"wt":[0,1,63,81,128,142,205,219,233,307,321,335,369,401,451,467]},{"speaker":"All over the place","en":"describes a situation or mental state which is disorganised, chaotic or confused.","zh":"All over the place（乱成一团）用来形容杂乱无章、混乱或困惑的局面或心理状态。","s":476.67,"e":481.81,"wt":[0,59,65,124,137,176,210,242,275,353,396,413]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"And finally, a margin is a measure of difference, small details of which can affect an outcome.","zh":"最后，margin 是对差距的衡量，其中的细微之处可能左右最终结果。","s":481.81,"e":487.38,"wt":[0,10,63,75,117,139,142,193,208,296,328,364,380,419,443,475,499]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Once again, our six minutes are up, but remember you can find a free transcript, worksheet and quiz, on our website at bbclearningenglish.com. Goodbye for now.","zh":"我们的六分钟又到了，别忘了你可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 上找到免费文本、练习题和小测验。先再见啦。","s":487.65,"e":496.79,"wt":[0,12,43,62,81,128,144,156,175,225,244,263,296,298,327,416,474,498,529,546,570,609,626,835,871,889]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Goodbye!","zh":"再见！","s":496.82,"e":497.78,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0ny289g.mp3"},{"epNum":20,"id":"ep-260709","date":"2026-07-09","title":"Should we cycle more?","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260709","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260709_6_minute_english_should_we_cycle_more_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260709_6_minute_english_should_we_cycle_more_transcript.pdf","summary":"When did you learn to ride a bike? Perhaps you learnt as a child and have not cycled for a long time. Well don't worry, you can pick it up again quickly – it's just like riding a bike! And if you have never cycled, it's not too late to start. With new cycle tracks and electric bikes, cycling is becoming more and more accessible. Neil and Becca discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"Roughly how many bikes are there in the Netherlands?","options":["a) 20 million","b) 24 million","c) 34 million"],"topic":"职场生活","titleZh":"我们应该多骑自行车吗？","summaryZh":"你是什么时候学会骑自行车的？也许你小时候学过，但已经很久没骑了。别担心，你很快就能重新上手——毕竟骑车这事儿学会了就忘不掉！就算你从来没骑过，现在开始也完全不晚。随着新建自行车道和电动自行车的普及，骑行正变得越来越平易近人。Neil 和 Becca 将探讨这一话题，并教你一些实用的新词汇。","questionZh":"荷兰大约有多少辆自行车？","optionsZh":["a) 2000 万辆","b) 2400 万辆","c) 3400 万辆"],"answer":{"letter":"b","explanationZh":"Becca 在节目结尾揭晓答案：荷兰的自行车确实非常多（超过了 1800 万人口），不过总数大约只有 2400 万辆（24 million），并非 Neil 猜的 3400 万辆。"},"vocab":[{"word":"deeply ingrained","defEn":"firmly established within a person or a culture and unlikely to change","pos":"短语","zh":"根深蒂固的；深入人心的","exampleEn":"The bicycle is deeply ingrained into Dutch culture.","ipa":"/ˈdiːpli ɪnˈɡreɪnd/"},{"word":"take off","defEn":"(phrasal verb) suddenly become popular or successful","pos":"短语","zh":"迅速走红；腾飞；流行起来","exampleEn":"But the rise of cycling started in the industrial era, when bicycle manufacturing took off.","ipa":"/teɪk ɒf/"},{"word":"milestone","defEn":"important event in someone's life or in the development of something","pos":"n.","zh":"里程碑；重要转折点","exampleEn":"This was a really important milestone for cycling in the Netherlands.","ipa":"/ˈmaɪlstəʊn/"},{"word":"quality of life","defEn":"level of enjoyment, comfort and health a person experiences; measure of a person's overall wellbeing","pos":"短语","zh":"生活质量","exampleEn":"Gulf cities like Dubai want a reputation as places with a good quality of life.","ipa":"/ˈkwɒləti əv laɪf/"},{"word":"liveability","defEn":"degree to which a place is suitable or pleasant for living in","pos":"n.","zh":"宜居性；适居程度","exampleEn":"Gulf cities are also interested in liveability – the degree to which somewhere is a pleasant place to live.","ipa":"/ˌlɪvəˈbɪləti/"},{"word":"a copy and paste","defEn":"an exact copy of something, which is then moved and put somewhere else","pos":"短语","zh":"照搬照抄；生搬硬套的复制品","exampleEn":"It's certainly not a copy and paste of what is good in Holland or Denmark, but the key is that we're looking for the principles.","ipa":"/ə ˈkɒpi ənd peɪst/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"roughly speaking","zh":"粗略地说；大致上（常放在估算数字前，避免说法过于绝对）","contextEn":"So, roughly speaking, how many bikes are there in the Netherlands?"},{"en":"be better known for","zh":"以……更为人熟知（用来对比某地或某人的固有印象与新一面）","contextEn":"Our second destination is better known for cars than bicycles: the United Arab Emirates or UAE."},{"en":"what works","zh":"行之有效的方法；管用的做法（极简地道的名词性从句表达，雅思口语写作都很加分）","contextEn":"Martin told BBC World Service's The Inquiry that Dubai is studying countries like the Netherlands not to copy them, but to discover what works."},{"en":"a range of","zh":"一系列的；各种各样的（雅思写作中替换 many / a lot of 的高质量搭配）","contextEn":"Dubai can't simply copy the Netherlands, and a range of measures, including water stations and shaded rest areas, have been created for cycling in the heat."}],"sentences":[{"en":"The Netherlands' cycle network spreads over 95,000 miles, and there are more bikes in the country than its 18 million citizens.","zh":"荷兰的自行车道网络绵延超过 9.5 万英里，全国的自行车数量甚至比其 1800 万公民还要多。","coreEn":"The cycle network spreads over 95,000 miles, and there are more bikes than citizens.","coreZh":"自行车道网络长达 9.5 万英里，且自行车比公民还多。","cars":[{"text":"in the country","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"限定自行车数量统计的范围在荷兰国内"},{"text":"than its 18 million citizens","hook":"比较结构 than","noteZh":"用具体人口数 1800 万作参照，直观衬托荷兰自行车数量之庞大"}],"tipZh":"用 spreads over（绵延/覆盖）描述路网长度非常生动，雅思小作文写地图题或城市设施变化时可以直接借用。"},{"en":"And it went from a toy for rich people to an everyday tool for transportation, and this was a really important milestone for cycling in the Netherlands.","zh":"它从有钱人的玩具变成了日常出行的交通工具，而这对荷兰的骑行发展来说是一个非常重要的里程碑。","coreEn":"It went from a toy to an everyday tool, and this was a milestone.","coreZh":"它从玩具变成了日常工具，这是一个里程碑。","cars":[{"text":"from a toy for rich people to an everyday tool for transportation","hook":"介词短语 from ... to ...","noteZh":"清晰勾勒自行车社会定位的转变轨迹：从奢侈消遣变成大众代步工具"},{"text":"for cycling in the Netherlands","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"限定这个里程碑所对应的领域与国家"}],"tipZh":"go from A to B 是描述事物历史演变最地道简洁的骨架，比长篇大论写“以前是……后来变成了……”利落得多。"},{"en":"Cycling is good for mental as well as physical health, and Dubai's town planners are hoping the 600km of cycle tracks already built will make the city more attractive to live in.","zh":"骑行不仅有益于身体健康，也有益于心理健康；迪拜的城市规划者希望，已经建成的 600 公里自行车道能让这座城市变得更宜居、更具吸引力。","coreEn":"Cycling is good for health, and town planners are hoping the cycle tracks will make the city more attractive.","coreZh":"骑行有益健康，规划者希望自行车道能让城市更有吸引力。","cars":[{"text":"mental as well as physical","hook":"并列修饰 as well as","noteZh":"同时涵盖心理与身体两个维度，且把重心自然落在前面的 mental 上"},{"text":"already built","hook":"-ed 修饰","noteZh":"后置修饰 600km of cycle tracks，说明这些车道是“已经建成的”"},{"text":"to live in","hook":"不定式 to do","noteZh":"紧跟在 attractive 后面，说明在“居住”这件事上更有吸引力（注意介词 in 不能漏）"}],"tipZh":"看到 the 600km of cycle tracks already built will make ... 时，注意 already built 只是后置定语，不是谓语动词，真正的谓语是后面的 will make。"},{"en":"Dubai can't simply copy the Netherlands, and a range of measures, including water stations and shaded rest areas, have been created for cycling in the heat.","zh":"迪拜不能简单地照搬荷兰的模式，因此已经推出了一系列应对高温骑行的措施，包括饮水站和遮阳休息区。","coreEn":"Dubai can't copy the Netherlands, and a range of measures have been created.","coreZh":"迪拜不能照搬荷兰，于是推出了一系列措施。","cars":[{"text":", including water stations and shaded rest areas,","hook":"逗号插入语","noteZh":"举例说明具体推出了哪几项措施，把主语 measures 和谓语 have been created 隔开了"},{"text":"for cycling in the heat","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"交代这些措施针对的使用场景——在酷热天气里骑行"}],"tipZh":"雅思阅读和写作里，主语后紧跟 , including ..., 插入举例非常常见，先跳过插入语抓出 a range of measures have been created，主谓一致和句意一目了然。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Hello, this is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Neil.","zh":"大家好，欢迎收听 BBC Learning English 出品的《六分钟英语》。我是 Neil。","s":7.88,"e":11.94,"wt":[0,39,52,74,102,134,182,221,242,298,354,373]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"And I'm Becca.","zh":"我是 Becca。","s":12.04,"e":13.02,"wt":[0,18,22]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Cycling is a popular activity in Britain.","zh":"骑自行车在英国是一项很受欢迎的活动。","s":13.12,"e":15.78,"wt":[0,84,96,111,156,207,219]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Yes. This year, sales of bicycles, or bikes as they're usually called, are up in Britain. Many people choose cycling because it's good for their health and the environment. And electric bikes are making cycling accessible to more people, including older people.","zh":"没错。今年英国的自行车——大家平时通常简称 bikes——销量有所上升。许多人选择骑车，因为这对健康和环境都有好处。而电动自行车正让骑行惠及更多人，包括老年人。","s":16.02,"e":31.3,"wt":[0,29,58,87,123,137,195,264,264,273,323,374,431,452,466,479,566,589,624,659,700,734,754,772,790,818,850,862,896,984,1007,1050,1080,1102,1150,1204,1238,1252,1302,1404,1418,1462]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"In this episode, we'll visit two countries: one with a long history of cycling and another where it's just getting started. And as usual, we'll be learning some useful new words and phrases. And remember, you'll find all the vocabulary from this episode on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"在本期节目中，我们将探访两个国家：一个有着悠久的骑行历史，另一个才刚刚起步。和往常一样，我们也会学习一些实用的新词和短语。别忘了，你可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 找到本期的全部词汇。","s":31.4,"e":49.58,"wt":[0,13,40,88,121,155,175,256,281,315,323,357,410,433,496,508,553,582,610,646,658,784,803,816,849,881,894,940,968,1004,1031,1050,1078,1166,1190,1236,1270,1302,1302,1358,1390,1410,1474,1486,1500,1538,1586]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"But first, Neil, I have a question about a country that's world famous for its bikes: the Netherlands, also often referred to as Holland. The Netherlands' cycle network spreads over 95,000 miles, and there are more bikes in the country than its 18 million citizens. So, roughly speaking, how many bikes are there in the Netherlands? Is it: a) 20 million, b) 24 million, or c) 34 million?","zh":"不过首先，Neil，我有一个关于“自行车王国”的问题，那就是以自行车闻名世界的荷兰（the Netherlands，也常被称为 Holland）。荷兰的自行车道网络绵延超过 9.5 万英里，全国的自行车数量甚至超过了其 1800 万公民。那么粗略地说，荷兰大约有多少辆自行车呢？是：a) 2000 万辆，b) 2400 万辆，还是 c) 3400 万辆？","s":49.86,"e":78.52,"wt":[0,14,61,98,107,122,140,194,216,234,282,322,356,397,417,437,538,556,654,684,721,766,780,798,896,912,968,984,1034,1080,1108,1280,1340,1352,1352,1364,1388,1416,1424,1480,1488,1500,1546,1570,1622,1771,1798,1856,1939,1964,1997,2039,2064,2106,2122,2147,2270,2284,2304,2338,2400,2454,2534,2602,2640,2694,2729,2799]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, I'm going to say c) 34 million.","zh":"嗯，我猜 c) 3400 万辆。","s":78.85,"e":81.92,"wt":[0,48,75,75,89,113,188,245]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Well, we'll reveal the correct answer later in the programme. Staying in the Netherlands, let's hear from Meredith Glaser, CEO of Amsterdam's Urban Cycling Institute. Here, Meredith tells BBC World Service programme The Inquiry more about Dutch people's love of cycling.","zh":"好的，我们稍后在节目中揭晓正确答案。我们先留在荷兰，听听阿姆斯特丹城市骑行研究所首席执行官 Meredith Glaser 的分享。下面 Meredith 在 BBC 国际台节目《深度探究》中进一步讲述了荷兰人对骑行的热爱。","s":82.21,"e":100.44,"wt":[0,40,73,109,133,180,219,249,259,285,380,413,423,451,517,549,579,615,627,727,756,775,883,934,991,1093,1133,1197,1237,1315,1315,1357,1429,1453,1509,1541,1581,1621,1687,1721,1737]},{"speaker":"Meredith Glaser","en":"The bicycle is deeply ingrained into Dutch culture. But the rise of cycling started in the industrial era, when bicycle manufacturing took off . And it went from a toy for rich people to an everyday tool for transportation, and this was a really important milestone for cycling in the Netherlands.","zh":"自行车已经深深扎根于荷兰文化之中。但骑行的兴起始于工业时代，当时自行车制造业迅速腾飞。它从有钱人的玩具变成了日常出行的交通工具，而这对荷兰的骑行发展来说是一个非常重要的里程碑。","s":100.7,"e":124.68,"wt":[0,30,102,126,190,274,328,382,486,523,552,590,609,677,746,756,786,826,870,957,1020,1090,1132,1182,1263,1284,1302,1318,1364,1422,1458,1590,1632,1688,1768,1804,1858,1894,1952,2046,2064,2076,2076,2108,2142,2154,2252,2288,2310,2320,2342]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Cycling is deeply ingrained into Dutch culture. Deeply ingrained are two words that go together commonly, and it means something that's firmly established within a person or culture, and unlikely to change.","zh":"骑行已经 deeply ingrained（根深蒂固）于荷兰文化之中。deeply ingrained 是很常用的固定搭配，意思是某事物在个人或文化中已经牢牢确立，不太可能改变。","s":124.84,"e":138.84,"wt":[0,48,106,150,224,254,294,352,424,484,508,533,566,592,607,658,724,744,757,790,850,889,957,1030,1030,1086,1094,1152,1192,1256,1276,1310]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"During the 1890s, bike manufacturing took off in the Netherlands. Take off is a phrasal verb meaning to suddenly become popular or successful. Certain brands became symbols of a new, comfortable style of bike. New factories made bikes cheaper to buy. This was a milestone for cycling in the Netherlands. A milestone is an important event in someone's life or in the development of something.","zh":"19 世纪 90 年代，荷兰的自行车制造业 took off（迅速腾飞）。take off 是一个短语动词，意思是突然流行起来或取得成功。某些品牌成了新型舒适自行车的象征，而新建的工厂让自行车价格变得更加亲民。这对荷兰骑行史而言是一个 milestone（里程碑）。milestone 指的是某人人生中或某事物发展过程中的重要事件。","s":139.22,"e":165,"wt":[0,0,44,148,183,258,284,298,306,330,440,456,496,508,522,578,612,626,674,698,746,784,820,990,1024,1068,1120,1158,1170,1193,1226,1300,1322,1346,1404,1429,1494,1526,1538,1568,1588,1732,1746,1760,1781,1840,1859,1905,1918,1937,2039,2044,2109,2124,2139,2207,2244,2259,2314,2356,2371,2386,2408,2484,2506]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Our second destination is better known for cars than bicycles: the United Arab Emirates or UAE. UAE is hoping cycling could be a greener way for visitors to explore tourist attractions in cities like Dubai and Abu Dhabi. Transport consultant Martin Tillman, who lives in the Gulf region, explained more to BBC World Service programme The Inquiry.","zh":"我们的第二个目的地以汽车而非自行车更为人熟知：阿拉伯联合酋长国（简称阿联酋/UAE）。阿联酋希望骑行能成为游客探索迪拜、阿布扎比等城市旅游景点的一种更环保的方式。居住在海湾地区的交通顾问 Martin Tillman 在 BBC 国际台《深度探究》节目中做了进一步解释。","s":165.3,"e":188.48,"wt":[0,15,57,122,142,174,174,222,258,290,372,393,435,463,530,547,688,742,766,820,882,906,928,952,1000,1028,1054,1094,1140,1170,1238,1292,1308,1356,1362,1454,1464,1490,1600,1666,1730,1756,1798,1822,1846,1856,1870,1906,1956,2005,2027,2042,2093,2128,2170,2228,2248]},{"speaker":"Martin Tillman","en":"We also have quality of life and liveability . All the cities in the Gulf are very competitive, and they would love to be ranked very high in those global rankings, and cycling is seen as part of that quality-of-life solution.","zh":"我们还看重生活质量和宜居性。海湾地区的所有城市竞争都非常激烈，它们都希望在全球各类榜单上名列前茅，而骑行被视为提升生活质量方案的一部分。","s":188.64,"e":203.08,"wt":[0,16,48,80,134,153,184,200,280,334,344,370,394,394,402,474,503,532,660,660,664,700,729,743,757,801,830,873,884,925,975,1042,1067,1125,1141,1174,1190,1223,1239,1272,1376]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Gulf cities like Dubai want a reputation as places with a good quality of life . The phrase quality of life refers to someone's level of enjoyment, comfort and health when living in a particular place. It's a measure of a person's overall wellbeing.","zh":"像迪拜这样的海湾城市希望树立起拥有良好 quality of life（生活质量）的声誉。短语 quality of life 指的是人们在某地生活时的愉悦感、舒适度和健康水平，是衡量一个人整体幸福感的指标。","s":203.3,"e":219.74,"wt":[0,42,70,80,152,152,200,266,290,340,340,376,400,422,466,503,552,571,614,670,685,721,764,778,842,878,892,971,1021,1050,1092,1132,1166,1174,1198,1240,1364,1382,1400,1436,1436,1480,1532,1595]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Gulf cities are also interested in liveability – the degree to which somewhere is a pleasant place to live. Cycling is good for mental as well as physical health, and Dubai's town planners are hoping the 600km of cycle tracks already built will make the city more attractive to live in.","zh":"海湾城市同样关注 liveability（宜居性）——即一个地方适合居住、令人愉悦的程度。骑行不仅有益于身体健康，也有益于心理健康；迪拜的城市规划者希望，已经建成的 600 公里自行车道能让这座城市变得更宜居、更具吸引力。","s":220.2,"e":239,"wt":[0,27,58,88,115,176,197,259,285,305,346,359,393,455,468,474,550,592,612,690,760,782,804,834,878,894,910,934,978,1030,1050,1097,1124,1178,1198,1254,1315,1402,1416,1451,1493,1542,1577,1605,1633,1654,1682,1710,1784,1810,1838]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Of course, there are big differences between the Netherlands and UAE – the temperature for one, which in Dubai regularly hits 45ºC. Martin told BBC World Service's The Inquiry that Dubai is studying countries like the Netherlands not to copy them, but to discover what works.","zh":"当然，荷兰和阿联酋之间存在巨大差异——首先就是气温，迪拜的气温经常高达 45 摄氏度。Martin 告诉 BBC 国际台《深度探究》节目，迪拜研究荷兰等国家并不是为了照抄，而是为了发掘行之有效的经验。","s":239.16,"e":259.13,"wt":[0,17,70,92,110,143,212,214,264,296,316,367,420,447,470,490,556,584,600,714,742,849,1028,1092,1126,1160,1196,1260,1281,1331,1359,1395,1478,1520,1530,1558,1588,1714,1730,1744,1773,1802,1824,1838,1897,1926]},{"speaker":"Martin Tillman","en":"It's certainly not a copy and paste of what is good in Holland or Denmark, but the key is that we're looking for the principles.","zh":"这绝不是把荷兰或丹麦好的做法照搬照抄过来，关键在于我们在寻找背后的核心原则。","s":259.13,"e":266.58,"wt":[0,25,93,103,124,147,173,215,230,253,275,303,320,373,388,441,464,487,519,551,573,597,603,611,645]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Cycling in UAE isn't a copy and paste of cycling in Holland. Martin uses the phrase a copy and paste to mean an exact copy of something, which is moved and put somewhere else. Dubai can't simply copy the Netherlands, and a range of measures, including water stations and shaded rest areas, have been created for cycling in the heat.","zh":"阿联酋的骑行模式并不是对荷兰模式的 a copy and paste（照搬照抄）。Martin 用 a copy and paste 这个短语指把某样东西原封不动地复制并搬到别处。迪拜不能简单地照搬荷兰，因此已经推出了一系列应对高温骑行的措施，包括饮水站和遮阳休息区。","s":266.86,"e":288.7,"wt":[0,36,62,142,176,196,224,259,288,322,362,392,507,539,566,586,622,639,666,686,726,739,766,779,868,868,914,914,960,985,1016,1040,1064,1120,1203,1230,1265,1358,1386,1406,1437,1458,1514,1514,1515,1587,1651,1688,1762,1780,1831,1865,1925,1940,1980,1998,2026,2064,2082,2110]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Cycling isn't the perfect solution for everyone, everywhere. But in some places, it can be a popular and green alternative.","zh":"骑行并不是适用于所有人、所有地方的完美方案。但在不少地方，它确实可以成为一种受欢迎的绿色出行替代选择。","s":289.26,"e":297.04,"wt":[0,56,84,116,148,166,234,270,386,400,426,456,501,516,538,552,576,617,638,674]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Yes, just look at the Netherlands. And that reminds me of your question, Becca.","zh":"没错，看看荷兰就知道了。对了，Becca，这让我想起了你的问题。","s":297.09,"e":300.94,"wt":[0,23,46,69,80,97,151,175,201,241,252,263,286,332]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Ah yes. I asked you roughly how many bikes are there in the Netherlands?","zh":"啊对。我问你的是，荷兰大约有多少辆自行车？","s":300.94,"e":305.7,"wt":[0,17,54,118,150,190,238,258,288,320,336,336,339,361]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"I said 34 million. Was I right?","zh":"我猜的是 3400 万辆。我答对了吗？","s":305.73,"e":309.12,"wt":[0,6,119,209,222,256,267]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"There are certainly a lot of bikes in the Netherlands. However, there are only roughly 24 million bikes.","zh":"荷兰的自行车确实非常多。不过，总数大约只有 2400 万辆。","s":309.34,"e":316.64,"wt":[0,18,61,82,122,136,154,158,168,200,286,362,380,422,501,572,621,690]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Only?! That's still quite a lot!","zh":"“只有”？！那也已经相当多了好吗！","s":317,"e":318.7,"wt":[0,44,66,72,88,104]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Right, let's recap the vocabulary we've learned, starting with deeply ingrained – a combination of words meaning well established and unlikely to change.","zh":"好了，我们来回顾一下今天学到的词汇，首先是 deeply ingrained——这是一个常用搭配，意思是根深蒂固、不太可能改变。","s":319.13,"e":328.02,"wt":[0,37,69,102,121,187,219,271,324,350,389,450,480,485,553,566,599,645,671,743,762,814,827]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"If something takes off , it suddenly becomes popular or successful.","zh":"如果某件事 takes off，意思就是它突然变得流行或大获成功。","s":328.09,"e":332.41,"wt":[0,11,75,112,123,134,148,208,259,311,325]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"A milestone is an important event in the development of something or someone.","zh":"milestone（里程碑）是指某人或某事物发展过程中的重要事件。","s":332.41,"e":337.4,"wt":[0,5,70,85,100,168,208,221,243,317,341,407,424]},{"speaker":"Quality of life","en":"describes a person's wellbeing in terms of their level of enjoyment, comfort, and health.","zh":"Quality of life（生活质量）从愉悦感、舒适度和健康状况等方面描述一个人的整体幸福水平。","s":338.39,"e":343.8,"wt":[0,63,70,126,197,208,236,247,275,303,314,381,453,470]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"The noun liveability means the degree to which a place is pleasant to live in.","zh":"名词 liveability（宜居性）指的是一个地方适合并令人愉悦地居住的程度。","s":343.88,"e":348.66,"wt":[0,18,48,130,167,189,234,249,274,293,326,345,384,400,400]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"And finally, a copy and paste is an exact copy of something, which is then moved and put somewhere else. Once again, our six minutes are up, but remember you can find a transcript, and a worksheet and a quiz, all over on our website at bbclearningenglish.com. Goodbye for now.","zh":"最后，a copy and paste 指的是把某样东西一模一样地复制并搬到别处，即照搬照抄。我们的六分钟又到了，别忘了你可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 上找到文本、练习题和小测验。先再见啦。","s":348.84,"e":365.6,"wt":[0,22,64,81,110,132,169,183,197,234,263,277,343,380,394,423,459,492,508,554,652,670,710,734,756,788,802,834,858,904,918,946,968,994,1061,1072,1087,1156,1185,1188,1210,1228,1252,1264,1282,1325,1337,1538,1612,1612]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Bye!","zh":"拜拜！","s":365.74,"e":366.06,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0nxqqhc.mp3"},{"epNum":21,"id":"ep-260702","date":"2026-07-02","title":"Rude emails","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260702","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260702_6_minute_english_rude_emails_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260702_6_minute_english_rude_emails_transcript.pdf","summary":"What is the rudest email you have ever received? We all deal with emails on a daily basis, but sometimes people can write them too quickly, without thinking enough about how it makes the other person feel. Is there anything that people write in emails which you find impolite? And have you ever sent a rude email yourself...? Phil and Becca discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"Who sent an email last year asking thousands of US government workers to justify their job by listing five things they had accomplished that week or resign?","options":["a) Donald Trump","b) JD Vance","c) Elon Musk"],"topic":"职场生活","titleZh":"职场上的“无礼邮件”","summaryZh":"你收到过最无礼的一封邮件是什么样的？我们每天都要处理大量邮件，但有时人们写得太仓促，没有充分顾及对方的感受。邮件里有哪些写法会让你觉得不礼貌？你自己又有没有发过粗鲁的邮件？菲尔和贝卡将一起探讨这个话题，并教你一些实用的新词汇。","questionZh":"去年是谁给数千名美国政府雇员发了一封邮件，要求他们列出当周完成的五项工作来证明自己岗位的合理性，否则就辞职？","optionsZh":["a) 唐纳德·特朗普","b) JD·万斯","c) 埃隆·马斯克"],"answer":{"letter":"c","explanationZh":"贝卡在节目结尾揭晓了答案：菲尔猜的是 JD·万斯，但正确答案是 c) 埃隆·马斯克（Elon Musk）。"},"vocab":[{"word":"put (someone) on the defensive","defEn":"(idiom) make someone feel unsure or threatened by what you do or say","pos":"短语","zh":"使（某人）处于防备/被动状态，让人感到不安或受威胁","exampleEn":"She says the unnecessary formality always has the effect of putting her immediately on the defensive .","ipa":"/pʊt ɒn ðə dɪˈfensɪv/"},{"word":"uncivil","defEn":"not polite; rude","pos":"adj.","zh":"不礼貌的，粗鲁的，失礼的","exampleEn":"These emails are examples of people being uncivil – an adjective meaning rude or impolite.","ipa":"/ʌnˈsɪvəl/"},{"word":"belittle","defEn":"make someone feel inferior or not important","pos":"v.","zh":"贬低，轻视，使人觉得自己低人一等","exampleEn":"So, a fight response is usually when we try to dominate or belittle other people.","ipa":"/bɪˈlɪtəl/"},{"word":"put (someone) in their place","defEn":"(idiom) show or tell someone that they are less important or clever than they think they are","pos":"短语","zh":"杀杀（某人的）威风，让某人认清自己的位置","exampleEn":"The boss wants to put you in your place – an idiom meaning to show someone that they are less important than they think they are.","ipa":"/pʊt ɪn ðeə pleɪs/"},{"word":"get (something) off your plate","defEn":"(idiom) pass a task or problem from your workload to someone else for them to deal with","pos":"短语","zh":"把（某项任务）甩出去/从自己手里脱手","exampleEn":"They're just trying to get it off their plate .","ipa":"/ɡet ɒf jɔː pleɪt/"},{"word":"frazzled","defEn":"tired, nervous or anxious because you have to do too many things at the same time","pos":"adj.","zh":"筋疲力尽的，焦头烂额的","exampleEn":"Because we're operating in these environments where we are all quite frazzled , we are more at risk of engaging in incivility .","ipa":"/ˈfræzəld/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"in response to","zh":"作为对……的回应/答复（职场邮件与雅思写作高频搭配）","contextEn":"We've got an anonymous one here, who recently received a single emoji in response to a carefully considered and worded email."},{"en":"fight-or-flight response","zh":"“战或逃”反应（心理学常用词，指人在压力或威胁下的本能应激反应）","contextEn":"Now, according to some psychologists, rudeness in emails is based on a fight-or-flight response humans feel when stressed."},{"en":"give someone the credit","zh":"把功劳归于某人；认可某人的贡献","contextEn":"For example, a boss sends a group email in which they give someone else the credit for the work you did."},{"en":"work to a deadline","zh":"赶截止日期；在期限内完成工作（地道英式职场表达）","contextEn":"Unfortunately, when stressed and working to a deadline it's hard to make sure all your emails are polite."}],"sentences":[{"en":"Rachel in Manchester says what annoys her is people who start their emails with just your name and without a simple greeting like 'hi'.","zh":"曼彻斯特的瑞秋说，让她恼火的是那些发邮件时一上来只写你名字、连“嗨”这样一句简单问候都没有的人。","coreEn":"Rachel says what annoys her is people.","coreZh":"瑞秋说让她恼火的是某些人。","cars":[{"text":"in Manchester","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"补充说明瑞秋来自曼彻斯特"},{"text":"who start their emails with just your name and without a simple greeting like 'hi'","hook":"关系词 who","noteZh":"详细界定到底是“哪类人”——发邮件只叫名字不打招呼的人"}],"tipZh":"遇到 what 引导的主语从句（what annoys her）时，先把它整体看作一件事“让她烦的事”，再往后找真正被修饰的对象 people。"},{"en":"Last year, thousands of US government workers received an email requiring them to justify their job by listing five things they had accomplished that week... or resign.","zh":"去年，数千名美国政府雇员收到一封邮件，要求他们列出当周完成的五项工作来证明自己岗位的价值……否则就得辞职。","coreEn":"Thousands of US government workers received an email.","coreZh":"数千名美国政府雇员收到了一封邮件。","cars":[{"text":"Last year,","hook":"时间状语","noteZh":"交代事情发生的时间背景"},{"text":"requiring them to justify their job","hook":"-ing 修饰","noteZh":"说明这封邮件的具体内容是“要求他们证明自己岗位的合理性”"},{"text":"by listing five things they had accomplished that week... or resign","hook":"介词短语 + 省略关系词","noteZh":"说明“如何证明”（列出当周完成的五件事）以及不照做的后果（或辞职）"}],"tipZh":"名词后面紧跟动词 -ing（an email requiring...）在雅思阅读中极常见，直接理解成“这封邮件要求……”即可，不用反复回读。"},{"en":"For example, a boss sends a group email in which they give someone else the credit for the work you did.","zh":"举个例子，老板发了一封群发邮件，在邮件里把你做的工作功劳算在了别人头上。","coreEn":"A boss sends a group email.","coreZh":"老板发了一封群发邮件。","cars":[{"text":"For example,","hook":"逗号插入语","noteZh":"引出具体例证"},{"text":"in which they give someone else the credit","hook":"介词 + 关系词 which","noteZh":"把“群发邮件”当作场景，说明“在这封邮件里老板把功劳给了别人”"},{"text":"for the work you did","hook":"介词短语 + 省略关系词","noteZh":"说明这份功劳对应的对象正是“你所做的工作”"}],"tipZh":"看到“介词 + which”（如 in which）别发怵，直接把前面名词代进去读成“在这封邮件里”，语感瞬间顺畅。"},{"en":"Because we're operating in these environments where we are all quite frazzled , we are more at risk of engaging in incivility .","zh":"因为我们都处在这种让人焦头烂额的工作环境中，我们就更有可能做出失礼的举动。","coreEn":"We are more at risk of engaging in incivility.","coreZh":"我们更有可能陷入失礼行为的风险。","cars":[{"text":"Because we're operating in these environments","hook":"状语从句","noteZh":"说明原因：我们身处这样的工作环境之中"},{"text":"where we are all quite frazzled","hook":"关系词 where","noteZh":"具体描绘这些环境的特点：大家都很疲惫焦虑"}],"tipZh":"英语常把“原因长车厢”放句首，真正的主旨往往在逗号后半句（we are more at risk...），听力中要耐心等主句落地。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Hello, this is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Phil.","zh":"大家好，欢迎收听 BBC 英语教学《六分钟英语》。我是菲尔。","s":2.54,"e":12.78,"wt":[0,604,632,674,724,756,788,814,860,898,949,981]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"And I'm Becca.","zh":"我是贝卡。","s":12.78,"e":14.04,"wt":[0,20,20]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Sending emails is a big part of modern work, and most people try to write emails politely. Most, but not all! Recently, BBC Radio 4 programme All in the Mind asked listeners to tell them what they find rude in emails. Here, presenter Claudia Hammond and guest Pete Olusoga discuss what listeners had to say:","zh":"发邮件是现代职场的重要一环，大多数人都会尽量把邮件写得礼貌得体。只是“大多数”，并非所有人！最近，BBC 广播四台的《心理万象》（All in the Mind）节目请听众分享他们在邮件里觉得无礼的行为。下面我们来听主持人克劳迪娅·哈蒙德和嘉宾皮特·奥卢索加讨论听众们的反馈：","s":14.36,"e":39.06,"wt":[0,36,106,106,144,174,203,218,262,305,327,356,400,422,437,474,518,688,750,790,826,922,990,1019,1067,1096,1184,1213,1232,1261,1304,1334,1388,1400,1428,1455,1488,1506,1551,1606,1633,1768,1797,1882,1934,2004,2024,2064,2074,2195,2265,2303,2340,2362,2388]},{"speaker":"Claudia Hammond","en":"Rachel in Manchester says what annoys her is people who start their emails with just your name and without a simple greeting like 'hi'. She says the unnecessary formality always has the effect of putting her immediately on the defensive .","zh":"曼彻斯特的瑞秋说，让她恼火的是那些发邮件时一上来只写你名字、连“嗨”这样一句简单问候都没有的人。她说，这种没必要的严肃感总是会让她立刻进入防备状态。","s":39.23,"e":51.64,"wt":[0,29,41,104,129,145,187,210,234,272,291,321,353,391,416,449,469,491,513,566,573,618,677,697,769,783,799,819,871,945,981,999,1017,1053,1065,1107,1129,1173,1191,1205,1225]},{"speaker":"Dr Peter Olusoga","en":"We've got an anonymous one here, who recently received a single emoji in response to a carefully considered and worded email.","zh":"我们这儿还有一位匿名听众，他最近发了一封深思熟虑、措辞讲究的邮件，结果只收到了一个表情符号作为回复。","s":51.64,"e":58.68,"wt":[0,18,28,52,84,106,203,221,271,306,327,364,395,407,457,480,494,546,596,616,656]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"One listener doesn't like emails which start with just her name because they put her on the defensive . The phrase to put someone on the defensive means to do or say something which makes them feel threatened or unsure.","zh":"有位听众不喜欢一开头只写她名字的邮件，因为这会让她进入防备状态（put her on the defensive）。短语 put someone on the defensive 意思是，你的言行让对方感到受威胁或心里没底。","s":59.06,"e":73.84,"wt":[0,16,76,126,158,203,240,277,307,337,359,389,441,471,498,526,554,572,633,680,701,748,771,794,849,863,887,965,1004,1027,1042,1057,1138,1158,1210,1255,1286,1324,1360,1398]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Another listener found it rude when their email was answered with a single emoji, and I think that would annoy me too. These emails are examples of people being uncivil – an adjective meaning rude or impolite. And just to note, the noun that goes with this is incivility . In this episode, we'll be hearing more about rude emails at work and, as usual, we'll be learning some useful new vocabulary, all of which you can find on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"另一位听众觉得邮件只被回了一个表情符号很没礼貌，老实说换成我也会恼火。这些邮件都是人们表现得“失礼”（uncivil）的例子——这个形容词意思是粗鲁或不礼貌。顺带一提，它对应的名词是 incivility（无礼/失礼行为）。本期节目我们将继续探讨职场中的无礼邮件，并一如既往地学习一些实用的新词汇，所有内容都可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 上找到。","s":74.38,"e":108.02,"wt":[0,52,72,92,130,188,188,222,250,278,312,326,382,424,510,547,552,584,584,602,636,668,818,844,872,946,958,980,1040,1094,1186,1241,1280,1334,1394,1426,1448,1594,1626,1640,1658,1678,1712,1730,1752,1774,1774,1790,1888,1949,1992,2007,2042,2103,2146,2163,2224,2256,2298,2338,2390,2407,2442,2468,2485,2529,2572,2608,2658,2696,2728,2766,2854,2878,2894,2934,2948,2970,2974,3012,3058,3118]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"But first, I have a question for you, Phil. Last year, thousands of US government workers received an email requiring them to justify their job by listing five things they had accomplished that week... or resign. But who sent this email? Was it: a) Donald Trump, b) JD Vance, or c) Elon Musk?","zh":"不过首先，菲尔，我有个问题考考你。去年，数千名美国政府雇员收到一封邮件，要求他们列出当周完成的五项工作来证明自己岗位的价值……否则就得辞职。那么这封邮件是谁发的？是：a) 唐纳德·特朗普，b) JD·万斯，还是 c) 埃隆·马斯克？","s":108.22,"e":131.25,"wt":[0,18,59,67,99,107,162,178,196,268,300,332,405,421,437,518,586,643,653,692,763,794,809,864,903,994,1028,1080,1126,1142,1158,1196,1244,1274,1359,1386,1552,1576,1602,1628,1666,1752,1776,1822,1848,1926,2013,2023,2070,2169,2197,2276,2282]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Ooh, I don't know... Maybe JD Vance?","zh":"噢，我不太确定……也许是 JD·万斯吧？","s":131.25,"e":132.21,"wt":[0,14,20,31,52,66,73]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"We'll find out later in the programme. Now, according to some psychologists, rudeness in emails is based on a fight-or-flight response humans feel when stressed. Here, psychologist Dr Emma Russell explains these responses for BBC Radio 4's All in the Mind.","zh":"答案我们稍后揭晓。一些心理学家认为，邮件中的无礼言辞源于人类在压力下产生的“战或逃”反应。下面，心理学家艾玛·拉塞尔博士将在 BBC 广播四台的《心理万象》中为我们解释这两种反应。","s":132.21,"e":155.94,"wt":[0,453,479,539,557,567,599,705,723,797,813,846,969,1034,1050,1099,1115,1156,1172,1180,1286,1373,1427,1457,1467,1637,1666,1763,1787,1822,1883,1951,1995,2065,2089,2125,2168,2202,2228,2245,2296]},{"speaker":"Dr Emma Russell","en":"So, a fight response is usually when we try to dominate or belittle other people. Emails that are hostile in tone and language, or even cc'ing senior personnel in on the message in order to elevate an issue and put someone in their place . Then the flight response is when people try to protect themselves by withdrawing or avoiding. They won't reply, or if they do reply, maybe they don't answer all of the points in the email. They're just trying to get it off their plate .","zh":"“战”反应通常表现为试图压制或贬低他人，比如语气和措辞充满敌意的邮件，甚至在邮件里抄送高层领导，借此把事情闹大、杀杀对方的威风。而“逃”反应则是人们通过退缩或回避来保护自己。他们要么干脆不回，要么就算回复，也可能避开邮件里的部分要点。他们只是想赶紧把这摊事从自己手里甩出去。","s":156.05,"e":186.28,"wt":[0,13,22,66,137,155,217,252,270,296,363,385,403,473,517,587,660,692,722,793,813,883,941,1007,1035,1102,1179,1239,1295,1321,1337,1363,1396,1403,1434,1446,1489,1501,1532,1550,1568,1599,1611,1647,1665,1685,1697,1728,1765,1814,1843,1864,1908,1930,1945,1997,2053,2087,2121,2183,2242,2267,2307,2351,2367,2379,2391,2411,2434,2465,2490,2521,2607,2607,2607,2657,2697,2709,2741,2781,2781,2790,2835,2851,2872,2883,2908,2947,2983]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"The fight response can make us belittle someone – make them feel inferior or unimportant. For example, a boss sends a group email in which they give someone else the credit for the work you did. The boss wants to put you in your place – an idiom meaning to show someone that they are less important than they think they are.","zh":"“战”反应会让我们去贬低（belittle）别人，也就是让对方觉得自己低人一等或无足轻重。举个例子，老板发了一封群发邮件，在邮件里把你做的工作功劳算在了别人头上。老板就是想杀杀你的威风（put you in your place）——这个习语的意思是让某人明白他们并没有自己想象的那么重要。","s":186.28,"e":209.62,"wt":[0,22,65,130,150,170,192,268,313,340,342,370,460,520,540,674,697,746,767,795,830,837,872,907,921,956,984,1012,1061,1089,1110,1152,1184,1206,1264,1310,1398,1420,1461,1508,1526,1554,1581,1600,1637,1702,1750,1774,1822,1888,1926,1970,2008,2008,2040,2065,2092,2173,2198,2234,2246,2268]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"The flight response, meanwhile, can be seen when co-workers ignore your message or give a quick, unhelpful reply. Such colleagues want to get work off their plate – another idiom, meaning to remove tasks from your own workload and give them to someone else to deal with.","zh":"与此同时，当同事无视你的消息，或者敷衍地回一句帮不上忙的话时，就能看出“逃”反应。这样的同事只是想把工作甩出去（get work off their plate）——这是另一个习语，意思是把任务从自己的工作量里挪出去，推给别人去处理。","s":209.94,"e":228.72,"wt":[0,17,64,141,224,242,264,295,347,402,449,479,533,550,597,608,670,746,886,932,960,984,1044,1084,1110,1130,1136,1205,1256,1264,1328,1346,1388,1406,1483,1504,1528,1557,1611,1631,1658,1685,1698,1759,1792,1813,1838]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Unfortunately, when stressed and working to a deadline it's hard to make sure all your emails are polite. That's what Emma Russell said when she spoke to BBC Radio 4's All in the Mind.","zh":"遗憾的是，当我们压力山大、忙着赶截止日期的时候，很难保证每一封邮件都写得客客气气。艾玛·拉塞尔在 BBC 广播四台《心理万象》节目中也是这么说的。","s":229.4,"e":242.14,"wt":[0,90,136,202,232,272,284,314,383,415,448,464,492,529,564,602,640,671,752,752,766,794,846,872,888,912,934,968,1044,1120,1160,1176,1176,1230]},{"speaker":"Dr Emma Russell","en":"Because we're operating in these environments where we are all quite frazzled , we are more at risk of engaging in incivility .","zh":"因为我们都处在这种让人焦头烂额的工作环境中，我们就更有可能做出失礼的举动。","s":242.5,"e":250.01,"wt":[0,2,66,126,144,178,208,226,244,270,312,340,383,406,435,453,495,506,539,555,621,637,704]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"We're more likely to be uncivil when we're frazzled – an informal adjective describing feeling tired or anxious because you're doing too many things at the same time.","zh":"当我们焦头烂额（frazzled）的时候，就更容易失礼——这是一个非正式形容词，形容因为同时处理太多事情而感到疲惫或焦虑。","s":250.01,"e":261.44,"wt":[0,35,67,115,131,147,203,235,275,327,355,371,435,507,605,650,689,705,760,815,862,901,925,957,1004,1020,1043,1076]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Luckily, there are ways to help. If you feel angry when writing an email, pause before you hit send. One day it might save your friendship or even your job! OK. Becca, I'm going to politely ask you to please reveal the answer to your question.","zh":"好在还是有办法应对的。如果你写邮件时正冒火，按下发送键前先停一停。说不定哪天这就能保住你的友谊，甚至保住你的饭碗！好了，贝卡，我要非常有礼貌地请你揭晓刚才那道题的答案。","s":261.5,"e":282.2,"wt":[0,68,106,132,164,190,267,278,302,345,391,427,472,498,570,621,656,656,688,796,826,866,886,937,964,1014,1112,1138,1178,1214,1295,1347,1419,1440,1446,1518,1605,1636,1667,1750,1864,1892,1924,1924,1926,2020]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Of course, Phil. I asked you who sent the email requiring workers to justify their employment or resign.","zh":"没问题，菲尔。我刚才问你，是谁发邮件要求雇员证明自己岗位的合理性，否则就辞职。","s":282.44,"e":291.72,"wt":[0,20,88,162,172,223,253,330,374,414,476,570,570,650,659,740,830,845]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"I said that I thought it might have been JD Vance.","zh":"我说我觉得可能是 JD·万斯。","s":291.89,"e":295.96,"wt":[0,5,45,85,95,145,177,191,223,299,335]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Well, we're not going to fire you, Phil, but you are wrong. The correct answer was Elon Musk. OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we've learnt, starting with the idiom put someone on the defensive , meaning to make someone feel threatened or unsure.","zh":"好吧，我们不会炒你鱿鱼的，菲尔，但你答错了。正确答案是埃隆·马斯克。好了，我们来复习一下今天学的词汇，首先是习语 put someone on the defensive，意思是让某人感到受威胁或心里没底。","s":296.16,"e":313.44,"wt":[0,40,80,104,138,160,192,216,264,288,312,336,404,428,446,512,540,570,701,743,785,884,884,937,970,1012,1056,1070,1090,1190,1225,1256,1270,1296,1350,1377,1423,1436,1462,1508,1534,1616,1634]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"The adjective uncivil means impolite, and we have a similar noun that's incivility .","zh":"形容词 uncivil 意思是不礼貌的，还有一个对应的名词 incivility（失礼/无礼行为）。","s":313.7,"e":320.77,"wt":[0,27,114,182,230,366,366,366,394,403,471,510,554,643]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"To belittle someone means to make them feel inferior or unimportant.","zh":"动词 belittle 意思是贬低某人，让他们觉得自己低人一等或不受重视。","s":320.77,"e":325.52,"wt":[0,11,75,125,159,177,197,209,285,349,366]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"If you put someone in their place , you show them that they're not as important as they think they are.","zh":"如果你 put someone in their place，意思就是杀杀对方的威风，让他们明白自己没那么重要。","s":325.64,"e":331.76,"wt":[0,13,33,53,100,113,147,174,194,214,241,268,295,342,362,375,435,449,494,530,567]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"To get something off your plate means to give someone else a task or problem instead of dealing with it yourself.","zh":"短语 get something off your plate 意思是把任务或难题推给别人，而不是自己处理。","s":331.88,"e":339.46,"wt":[0,15,38,88,106,114,201,232,246,282,316,328,376,400,425,480,535,560,606,626,646]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"And finally, if you're frazzled , you feel tired or anxious because you're doing too much. Once again, our six minutes are up.","zh":"最后，如果你感到 frazzled，就是因为要做的事太多而疲惫不堪、焦头烂额。我们的六分钟时间又到了。","s":339.76,"e":349.32,"wt":[0,30,96,111,155,193,213,235,264,301,378,378,420,464,501,532,692,734,794,825,851,902,922]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"If you want to know more about communication at work, check out our series Office English.","zh":"如果你想了解更多职场沟通技巧，欢迎收看我们的《办公室英语》（Office English）系列。","s":349.6,"e":355.28,"wt":[0,12,37,60,72,96,126,174,212,240,316,320,348,420,464,504]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"It's on our website, along with a quiz and a worksheet for this episode, and that's bbclearningenglish.com. But now it's goodbye.","zh":"它和本期节目的小测验及学习单都在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 上。现在先说再见啦。","s":355.48,"e":366.14,"wt":[0,24,40,74,153,196,216,243,279,294,315,352,406,459,552,566,592,912,938,972,1010]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Bye!","zh":"拜拜！","s":366.38,"e":366.63,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0nwb722.mp3"},{"epNum":22,"id":"ep-260625","date":"2026-06-25","title":"Stress-free family meals","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260625","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260625_6_minute_english_stress_free_family_meals_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260625_6_minute_english_stress_free_family_meals_transcript.pdf","summary":"Are you a fussy eater? Many of us remember foods that we used to hate as children – some of us still do hate those foods! But what can parents do to help encourage their children to eat a healthy diet, without turning every dinner into disaster? Neil and Georgie discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"Which of the following does the child nutritionist Charlotte Stirling-Reed recommend for stress-free family meals?","options":["a) make sure your child eats five pieces of fruit and veg a day","b) don't pressure your child to eat everything on the plate","c) introduce your child to lots of new flavours quickly"],"topic":"健康医学","titleZh":"轻松无压力的家庭聚餐","summaryZh":"你挑食吗？很多人还记得小时候讨厌吃的食物——有些人到现在依然讨厌！但父母怎样才能鼓励孩子健康饮食，又不至于把每顿晚饭都变成一场“灾难”呢？尼尔和乔治将探讨这个话题，并教你一些实用的新词汇。","questionZh":"为了实现轻松无压力的家庭用餐，儿童营养师夏洛特·斯特林-里德推荐了以下哪项做法？","optionsZh":["a) 确保孩子每天吃五份水果和蔬菜","b) 不要强迫孩子把盘子里的食物全部吃完","c) 快速让孩子尝试大量新口味"],"answer":{"letter":"b","explanationZh":"尼尔在节目后半段公布答案：专家们一致认为，最重要的是营造轻松氛围，不要给孩子施压、强迫他们把盘子里的食物都吃光，因此正确答案是 b)。"},"vocab":[{"word":"ups and downs","defEn":"a mixture of good and bad things happening to someone","pos":"短语","zh":"起起伏伏，时好时坏的波折","exampleEn":"We go through multiple periods of ups and downs where their appetite is much lower, and then all of a sudden it comes right back.","ipa":"/ʌps ənd daʊnz/"},{"word":"mood","defEn":"how a person is feeling at a particular time","pos":"n.","zh":"情绪，心情","exampleEn":"It depends on the weather, what they've been doing that day. It can depend on their mood .","ipa":"/muːd/"},{"word":"battleground","defEn":"place where argument or conflict happens","pos":"n.","zh":"战场，争执/冲突的场所","exampleEn":"Many parents say dinnertime can become a battleground , so why is it that what you're eating can trigger so much tension?","ipa":"/ˈbætəlɡraʊnd/"},{"word":"a big deal","defEn":"something of special importance","pos":"短语","zh":"一件大事，非常重要的事","exampleEn":"And for young kids, that's a big deal . They have some control.","ipa":"/ə bɪɡ diːl/"},{"word":"food allergy","defEn":"medical condition where certain foods make your body sick","pos":"n.","zh":"食物过敏","exampleEn":"If experts like Charlotte and Anne struggle at mealtimes, imagine how hard it could be if your child has a food allergy – a condition where the body makes you sick after eating certain foods.","ipa":"/fuːd ˈælədʒi/"},{"word":"winning formula","defEn":"(idiom) a method or strategy that produces successful results","pos":"短语","zh":"制胜法宝，行之有效的秘诀/方案","exampleEn":"And to be honest with you, we found the winning formula , which was rice and beans, and she grew up eating rice and beans probably for the first five years of her life.","ipa":"/ˈwɪnɪŋ ˈfɔːmjələ/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"all of a sudden","zh":"突然间，猛然（比 suddenly 更有口语画面感）","contextEn":"We go through multiple periods of ups and downs where their appetite is much lower, and then all of a sudden it comes right back."},{"en":"be up to someone","zh":"由某人决定；取决于某人（日常和雅思口语极高频）","contextEn":"I mean, parents can cook what they want and serve it when they want, but it's up to kids to decide whether they're going to eat it."},{"en":"to be honest with you","zh":"老实跟你说，说实话（口语中引出真实感受的缓冲短语）","contextEn":"And to be honest with you, we found the winning formula , which was rice and beans, and she grew up eating rice and beans probably for the first five years of her life."},{"en":"agree upon","zh":"就……达成一致意见（比单纯的 agree 更正式，适合雅思写作）","contextEn":"Well, there are some things that our experts all agree upon."}],"sentences":[{"en":"BBC Radio 4 programme The Food Chain asked child nutritionist and mother of two Charlotte Stirling-Reed whether her kids eat everything she gives them.","zh":"BBC 广播四台的《食物链》节目采访了儿童营养师、同时也是两个孩子母亲的夏洛特·斯特林-里德，问她的孩子是否会把自己给的所有食物都吃光。","coreEn":"The Food Chain asked Charlotte Stirling-Reed whether her kids eat everything.","coreZh":"《食物链》节目问夏洛特·斯特林-里德她的孩子是否什么都吃。","cars":[{"text":"BBC Radio 4 programme","hook":"同位语/身份头衔","noteZh":"说明《食物链》的出处是 BBC 广播四台"},{"text":"child nutritionist and mother of two","hook":"同位语/身份头衔","noteZh":"在人名前一次性交代她的专业身份和育儿背景"},{"text":"she gives them","hook":"省略关系词","noteZh":"限定 everything 的范围：特指“她给孩子们的”食物"}],"tipZh":"英语新闻喜欢在人名前堆砌一长串头衔（child nutritionist and mother of two），阅读时直接扫过头衔抓人名和动词 asked 就能秒懂句意。"},{"en":"I mean, parents can cook what they want and serve it when they want, but it's up to kids to decide whether they're going to eat it.","zh":"我的意思是，父母想做什么菜、什么时候端上桌都可以自己定，但吃不吃最终还是孩子说了算。","coreEn":"Parents can cook and serve it, but it's up to kids to decide.","coreZh":"父母可以做饭并端上桌，但决定权在孩子手里。","cars":[{"text":"I mean,","hook":"逗号插入语","noteZh":"口语缓冲词，引出进一步解释"},{"text":"what they want","hook":"宾语从句","noteZh":"说明父母做的是“他们想做的东西”"},{"text":"when they want","hook":"状语从句","noteZh":"说明上菜的时间是“在他们想上的时候”"},{"text":"whether they're going to eat it","hook":"宾语从句","noteZh":"具体说明孩子要决定的事项是“自己到底吃不吃”"}],"tipZh":"这个句子的对称节奏非常漂亮（cook what they want / serve it when they want / up to kids to decide whether...），可以直接借鉴到雅思口语和写作中讨论亲子关系。"},{"en":"If experts like Charlotte and Anne struggle at mealtimes, imagine how hard it could be if your child has a food allergy – a condition where the body makes you sick after eating certain foods.","zh":"连夏洛特和安妮这样的专家在吃饭问题上都这么头疼，试想一下如果你的孩子还有食物过敏——也就是吃了特定食物后身体会不适的病症——那该有多难啊。","coreEn":"Imagine how hard it could be.","coreZh":"试想一下这会有多难。","cars":[{"text":"If experts like Charlotte and Anne struggle at mealtimes,","hook":"状语从句","noteZh":"先用对比做铺垫：连两位专家都在餐桌上犯难"},{"text":"if your child has a food allergy","hook":"状语从句","noteZh":"给出更严峻的假设条件：要是孩子对食物过敏"},{"text":"– a condition where the body makes you sick after eating certain foods","hook":"破折号解释 + 关系词 where","noteZh":"用通俗语言给 food allergy 下定义"}],"tipZh":"雅思听力里经常用破折号或停顿来解释专业名词（如 food allergy），听到后半句的解释往往能帮你反推出前面的生词意思。"},{"en":"And to be honest with you, we found the winning formula , which was rice and beans, and she grew up eating rice and beans probably for the first five years of her life.","zh":"老实跟你说，我们找到了一个行之有效的秘诀，就是米饭配豆子，她人生头五年大概基本都是吃米饭配豆子长大的。","coreEn":"We found the winning formula, and she grew up eating rice and beans.","coreZh":"我们找到了制胜法宝，她就靠吃米饭配豆子长大了。","cars":[{"text":"to be honest with you,","hook":"逗号插入语","noteZh":"拉近听众距离，表达坦诚态度"},{"text":", which was rice and beans,","hook":"关系词 which","noteZh":"补充说明这个“制胜法宝”具体是什么"},{"text":"probably for the first five years of her life","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"说明这种饮食习惯持续的时间长度"}],"tipZh":"两个逗号夹着的 which 从句属于补充说明，听力和阅读中如果赶时间，先抓住前后主句，再把逗号里的细节贴上去。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Hello, this is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Neil.","zh":"大家好，欢迎收听 BBC 英语教学《六分钟英语》。我是尼尔。","s":2.56,"e":11.9,"wt":[0,554,566,588,618,646,692,724,750,800,874,887]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"And I'm Georgie.","zh":"我是乔治。","s":12,"e":13.4,"wt":[0,24,48]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Some kids hate eating broccoli. For others, it's mushrooms. And worst of all, of course, are Brussels sprouts! Every parent of young children knows what food they will and won't eat. What food did you dislike as a child, Georgie?","zh":"有些孩子讨厌吃西兰花，有些讨厌吃蘑菇，而最招人嫌的当然是抱子甘蓝啦！家里有小孩的父母都知道孩子爱吃什么、不爱吃什么。乔治，你小时候不喜欢吃什么食物？","s":13.64,"e":29.98,"wt":[0,36,84,122,179,286,310,338,374,490,516,544,568,606,622,674,688,706,854,894,931,943,973,1022,1052,1080,1130,1135,1170,1196,1248,1291,1325,1359,1385,1411,1471,1488,1496,1556]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Ooh, when I was little, I used to absolutely hate nuts and avocados. But now, as an adult, I absolutely love both of those things.","zh":"噢，我小时候超级讨厌坚果和牛油果。不过现在长大成人了，我反而特别爱吃这两样。","s":30.1,"e":40.3,"wt":[0,48,48,54,80,102,128,154,246,306,400,442,472,586,610,666,666,706,791,796,862,889,916,929,963]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"I'm not surprised – they're delicious!","zh":"我一点也不意外——它们确实很好吃！","s":40.44,"e":42.08,"wt":[0,12,28,64,82,92]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"What about you, Neil? Did you dislike any foods?","zh":"那你呢，尼尔？你小时候有讨厌吃的食物吗？","s":42.5,"e":45,"wt":[0,16,38,66,94,114,142,182,208]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Do you know what? I think I liked most things.","zh":"你知道吗？我觉得我好像大多数东西都爱吃。","s":45.12,"e":47.98,"wt":[0,4,16,36,76,98,150,165,204,238]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Family dinner time can be fun – a chance to reconnect, hear about everyone's day and share a meal. But when children refuse to eat the food that's been cooked, the result is often conflict rather than family fun. BBC Radio 4 programme The Food Chain asked child nutritionist and mother of two Charlotte Stirling-Reed whether her kids eat everything she gives them.","zh":"一家人吃晚饭本该是很愉快的时光——大家借此增进感情、聊聊各自一天的见闻，共享美食。但当孩子拒绝吃做好的饭菜时，结果往往不是天伦之乐，而是一场冲突。BBC 广播四台的《食物链》（The Food Chain）节目采访了儿童营养师、同时也是两个孩子母亲的夏洛特·斯特林-里德，问她的孩子是否会把自己给的所有食物都吃光。","s":48.46,"e":69.08,"wt":[0,20,52,54,72,112,134,156,170,200,218,286,304,327,374,406,438,471,474,517,536,562,614,653,666,685,704,730,768,794,845,864,903,916,964,1026,1080,1116,1124,1226,1247,1281,1302,1364,1385,1413,1448,1482,1514,1599,1611,1644,1655,1672,1730,1800,1839,1856,1878,1895,1951,1968,2012]},{"speaker":"Charlotte Stirling-Reed","en":"They definitely don't. We go through multiple periods of ups and downs where their appetite is much lower, and then all of a sudden it comes right back. It depends on the weather, what they've been doing that day. It can depend on their mood .","zh":"绝对不会。我们会经历好几次起起伏伏的阶段：有时他们的胃口明显变小，然后突然间胃口又全回来了。这取决于天气、他们那天的活动，也可能取决于他们的心情。","s":69.08,"e":82.4,"wt":[0,52,62,112,152,176,208,258,296,305,316,340,392,406,434,473,482,510,545,566,594,614,620,636,660,680,706,738,812,826,875,889,910,978,994,1014,1036,1060,1084,1136,1151,1170,1202,1222,1262,1292]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"At dinner time, Charlotte's children go through ups and downs , a phrase meaning that a mixture of good and bad things happen to them. Sometimes they eat everything happily. Other times they don't. It depends on many things, including their day, the weather and their mood . A person's mood is the way they are feeling at a particular time.","zh":"到了晚饭时间，夏洛特的孩子们也会经历“起起伏伏”（ups and downs），这个短语表示好事坏事交替发生。有时他们开开心心全吃光，有时却不肯吃。这受很多因素影响，包括当天的经历、天气和他们的心情（mood）。一个人的 mood 就是他们在特定时刻的情绪感受。","s":82.4,"e":102.54,"wt":[0,11,46,80,132,174,196,228,252,277,300,317,323,358,399,441,446,498,520,544,572,603,634,638,654,740,778,798,854,892,977,1010,1040,1042,1152,1167,1208,1220,1242,1288,1341,1376,1464,1488,1543,1573,1602,1629,1664,1670,1721,1747,1760,1779,1798,1823,1843,1892,1906,1913,1984]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"So, how can parents make dinnertime something everyone enjoys? In this episode, we'll be hearing from three experts on young children's eating habits and learning some useful new words and phrases too. And remember, you'll find all the vocabulary from this episode, along with a quiz and worksheet, on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"那么，父母怎样才能让晚餐时光成为每个人都享受的时刻呢？本期节目我们将听取三位专家对幼儿饮食习惯的见解，并学习一些实用的新词和短语。别忘了，本期节目的全部词汇，连同小测验和学习单，都可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 上找到。","s":102.81,"e":122.02,"wt":[0,9,30,51,100,128,198,262,299,383,395,411,445,467,485,521,547,580,626,639,672,738,778,818,838,891,917,957,992,1023,1047,1095,1153,1168,1208,1236,1255,1269,1283,1347,1369,1401,1455,1494,1511,1532,1563,1586,1656,1671,1694,1743]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"But first of course, Georgie, I have a question for you. Charlotte Stirling-Reed is a child nutritionist and she has some tips for stress-free family meals. Which of these do you think she recommends? Is it: a) make sure your child eats five pieces of fruit and veg a day, b) don't pressure your child to eat everything on the plate, or c) introduce your child to lots of new flavours quickly?","zh":"不过按照惯例，乔治，我先来考你一个问题。夏洛特·斯特林-里德是一位儿童营养师，她给出了几条实现无压力家庭聚餐的建议。你觉得下面哪一条是她推荐的？是：a) 确保孩子每天吃五份水果和蔬菜，b) 不要强迫孩子把盘子里的食物全部吃完，还是 c) 快速让孩子尝试大量新口味？","s":122.22,"e":149.42,"wt":[0,24,44,64,108,124,136,144,184,224,250,362,422,494,508,532,581,666,694,726,770,798,851,880,985,1043,1118,1140,1160,1190,1214,1247,1294,1313,1420,1432,1466,1532,1532,1556,1592,1637,1673,1714,1763,1771,1805,1828,1852,1865,1898,1968,2002,2002,2072,2072,2072,2091,2175,2187,2213,2273,2290,2378,2383,2417,2460,2477,2511,2534,2569,2646]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"That's quite hard. They all seem like quite good tips. I'm going to go with c) introduce your child to lots of new flavours quickly.","zh":"这题有点难啊，听起来全都是不错的建议。那我选 c) 快速让孩子尝试大量新口味吧。","s":149.8,"e":157.82,"wt":[0,33,61,98,121,137,164,188,233,266,340,361,397,411,425,454,468,533,562,604,620,648,664,674,728]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK. We'll find out the answer later in the programme. Dr Anne Fishel is another expert in child nutrition. She co-founded the Family Dinner Project, an organisation promoting children's healthy eating in the US. Here, Anne explains one reason why mealtimes can become stressful, to Ruth Alexander for BBC World Service programme The Food Chain.","zh":"好的，我们稍后揭晓答案。安妮·菲舍尔博士是另一位儿童营养专家，她联合创办了“家庭晚餐计划”（Family Dinner Project），这是美国一个倡导儿童健康饮食的组织。下面，安妮在 BBC 国际台《食物链》节目中向露丝·亚历山大解释了用餐时间变得紧张的一个原因。","s":157.96,"e":180.06,"wt":[0,32,65,80,94,126,173,207,220,240,333,345,379,429,446,505,556,573,615,718,760,796,813,849,887,976,992,1061,1115,1175,1217,1256,1274,1304,1376,1411,1435,1484,1514,1551,1574,1644,1667,1714,1799,1814,1845,1932,1952,1972,2003,2047,2112,2131,2164]},{"speaker":"Ruth Alexander","en":"Many parents say dinnertime can become a battleground , so why is it that what you're eating can trigger so much tension?","zh":"很多家长说晚饭时间会变成一个“战场”，为什么吃什么饭能引发这么大的紧张情绪呢？","s":180.5,"e":189.46,"wt":[0,36,56,129,220,247,311,323,398,428,450,474,490,506,590,590,618,670,722,758,782,830]},{"speaker":"Dr Anne Fishel","en":"I think for small children it's one of the few areas that they have control over. I mean, parents can cook what they want and serve it when they want, but it's up to kids to decide whether they're going to eat it. And for young kids, that's a big deal . They have some control.","zh":"我觉得对年幼的孩子来说，这是他们少数能自己掌控的领域之一。我的意思是，父母想做什么菜、什么时候端上桌都可以自己定，但吃不吃最终还是孩子说了算。对小孩子来说，这可是件大事——他们拥有了一点掌控权。","s":189.65,"e":205.96,"wt":[0,4,35,55,89,157,183,199,209,229,256,299,379,395,419,467,499,507,531,595,697,713,713,726,763,867,867,867,894,931,995,1001,1011,1025,1039,1067,1077,1107,1145,1171,1171,1203,1203,1275,1300,1317,1346,1380,1414,1419,1436,1456,1483,1495,1507,1537]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Dinnertime often becomes a battleground – a place where argument or conflict happens. Anne thinks that's because it's one of the few areas where children have control. For kids, controlling what they're willing to eat is a big deal – something of special importance to them.","zh":"晚餐时间常常会变成一个“战场”（battleground）——也就是发生争吵或冲突的地方。安妮认为这是因为吃饭是孩子少数能自主掌控的事情之一。对孩子来说，能自己决定愿意吃什么是“一件大事”（a big deal）——对他们有特别重要的意义。","s":206.14,"e":221.54,"wt":[0,58,95,126,146,206,230,243,272,307,340,372,424,488,522,540,576,588,610,620,634,658,716,760,786,826,858,936,946,977,1050,1072,1098,1100,1130,1174,1174,1210,1237,1261,1282,1341,1354,1410,1468,1484]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"If experts like Charlotte and Anne struggle at mealtimes, imagine how hard it could be if your child has a food allergy – a condition where the body makes you sick after eating certain foods. That was the experience of Sharon Wee, a food writer from Singapore, who speaks here with BBC World Service programme The Food Chain.","zh":"连夏洛特和安妮这样的专家在吃饭问题上都这么头疼，试想一下如果你的孩子还有食物过敏（food allergy）——也就是吃了特定食物后身体会不适的病症——那该有多难啊。这正是新加坡美食作家黄淑仪（Sharon Wee）的亲身经历，下面听她在 BBC 国际台《食物链》节目中的讲述。","s":221.76,"e":241.14,"wt":[0,12,56,81,137,155,180,230,243,310,354,372,397,409,448,465,474,503,540,562,569,598,639,663,718,736,773,795,824,861,883,912,948,1012,1073,1154,1170,1188,1223,1240,1280,1331,1377,1383,1416,1448,1480,1568,1582,1618,1648,1678,1701,1739,1792,1852,1876,1894]},{"speaker":"Sharon Wee","en":"My daughter was born with allergies and it was quite a struggle at first. And to be honest with you, we found the winning formula , which was rice and beans, and she grew up eating rice and beans probably for the first five years of her life.","zh":"我女儿天生就有过敏症，一开始真的非常艰难。老实跟你说，我们找到了一个行之有效的秘诀，就是米饭配豆子，她人生头五年大概基本都是吃米饭配豆子长大的。","s":241.53,"e":257.72,"wt":[0,13,87,115,152,189,273,301,319,347,349,402,463,483,563,589,601,615,660,687,715,749,793,879,882,921,944,988,1015,1041,1069,1125,1155,1174,1199,1213,1252,1285,1300,1336,1391,1403,1431,1465,1489,1513,1529,1569]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Sharon's daughter's allergies restricted what she could eat, making cooking for the whole family a struggle. Luckily, Sharon discovered the winning formula – something her daughter could safely eat – rice and beans. A winning formula is a method or strategy that produces successful results.","zh":"女儿的过敏症限制了她的饮食，让给全家人做饭变成了一件头疼事。幸运的是，黄淑仪发现了“制胜法宝”（the winning formula）——女儿可以安全食用的食物，那就是米饭配豆子。A winning formula 指能带来成功结果的方法或策略。","s":257.89,"e":274.54,"wt":[0,35,90,140,195,217,234,260,287,320,359,375,399,433,479,488,591,638,670,723,739,776,806,823,871,887,944,971,1014,1031,1049,1077,1098,1155,1162,1212,1262,1276,1283,1326,1340,1397,1424,1509,1588]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"So, Georgie, how can parents help their children develop good eating habits? Well, there are some things that our experts all agree upon. For one, give children a choice – for example, by offering small, healthy snacks and dips before the meal. And most importantly of all, create a stress-free atmosphere by not pressurising kids to finish everything on their plate.","zh":"那么乔治，父母该怎么帮助孩子养成良好的饮食习惯呢？有几点是我们的专家一致认同的。首先，给孩子选择权——比如在正餐前提供一些健康小零食和蘸酱。而最重要的一点，是营造轻松无压力的氛围，不要强迫孩子把盘子里的东西都吃完。","s":274.77,"e":297.6,"wt":[0,8,67,87,107,153,179,212,265,311,337,377,443,466,485,507,529,575,599,625,673,695,735,799,820,855,884,942,949,984,1006,1027,1088,1099,1164,1220,1275,1315,1339,1381,1385,1403,1509,1529,1548,1600,1611,1662,1717,1724,1806,1882,1897,1919,2010,2040,2055,2155,2176,2191,2229]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Right. Well, that also helps with your quiz question, Neil, so why not go ahead and reveal the correct answer?","zh":"没错。这刚好也帮我们解开了你的问题，尼尔，赶紧公布正确答案吧？","s":297.84,"e":303.78,"wt":[0,36,61,86,111,143,164,190,218,270,308,330,344,358,380,412,431,485,498,540]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"I asked you what child food nutritionist Charlotte Stirling-Reed recommends for stress-free family meals.","zh":"我刚才问你，儿童营养师夏洛特·斯特林-里德为轻松无压力的家庭聚餐推荐了什么建议。","s":303.85,"e":310.68,"wt":[0,4,37,58,86,121,149,234,301,383,454,475,579,641]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yes. I said c) introduce your child to lots of new flavours quickly.","zh":"对，我选的是 c) 快速让孩子尝试大量新口味。","s":311.1,"e":315.24,"wt":[0,39,45,72,91,153,180,214,227,254,267,287,342]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"I'm afraid you were wrong, Georgie. The answer is actually b) don't pressure your child to eat everything on the plate. OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we've learned, starting with ups and downs – a phrase describing the mixture of good and bad things that happen to someone.","zh":"恐怕你答错了，乔治。正确答案其实是 b) 不要强迫孩子把盘子里的食物全部吃完。好了，我们来复习一下今天学的词汇，首先是 ups and downs，形容好事和坏事交替发生的起起伏伏。","s":315.37,"e":332.36,"wt":[0,13,50,68,92,134,183,208,241,255,304,322,352,401,435,485,513,528,598,612,633,831,855,893,925,959,1011,1037,1091,1108,1141,1165,1189,1222,1246,1254,1315,1381,1396,1435,1459,1487,1508,1529,1571,1599,1629,1655]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Your mood is how you feel at a particular time.","zh":"Mood（情绪/心情）指你在特定时刻的感受。","s":332.58,"e":335.36,"wt":[0,28,60,73,93,113,140,153,159,226]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"A battleground is a place or situation where argument or conflict happens.","zh":"Battleground（战场）指发生争执或冲突的场所或局面。","s":335.41,"e":340.14,"wt":[0,11,83,91,113,145,166,233,270,319,345,405]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"If something is a big deal , it's of special importance to you.","zh":"如果某件事是 a big deal，意思就是它对你来说特别重要。","s":340.22,"e":343.68,"wt":[0,18,62,70,86,111,132,150,172,189,234,299,312]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"A food allergy is a medical condition where certain foods make your body sick.","zh":"Food allergy（食物过敏）是一种吃了特定食物后会让身体不适的病症。","s":343.81,"e":348.74,"wt":[0,4,33,87,102,109,163,223,251,305,337,343,399,437]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"And finally, a winning formula is a method or strategy that produces successful results. Once again, our six minutes are up, but if you'd like to learn more vocabulary on the topic of food, you can head to our topic page on our website, bbclearningenglish.com. See you again soon. But for now, it's goodbye.","zh":"最后，a winning formula（制胜法宝）指能够带来理想结果的方法或策略。我们的六分钟时间又到了，如果你想学习更多关于食物的词汇，可以前往我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 查看专题页面。我们很快再见，现在先说再见啦。","s":349.04,"e":366.46,"wt":[0,22,86,94,145,184,194,217,250,274,328,361,410,480,566,593,622,646,662,690,702,735,751,761,786,804,818,840,866,920,930,946,989,1000,1048,1072,1096,1128,1144,1168,1209,1241,1257,1281,1354,1562,1562,1576,1606,1644,1668,1692,1716,1723]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Goodbye!","zh":"再见！","s":366.46,"e":367.7,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0ntzw6q.mp3"},{"epNum":23,"id":"ep-260618","date":"2026-06-18","title":"Limiting screen time for children","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260618","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260618_6_minute_english_limiting_screen_time_for_children_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260618_6_minute_english_limiting_screen_time_for_children_worksheet_.pdf","summary":"Many parents worry that their children spend too much time on screens. Governments around the world, such as in the UK, Australia and China, are introducing online safety measures to limit the amount of screen time for children or prevent them accessing social media. Neil and Becca talk about their own screen time and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"According to The UK media regulator, how many children aged three to four have a smartphone? Is it:","options":["a) one in twenty","b) one in ten","c) one in five"],"topic":"科技未来","titleZh":"限制儿童的屏幕使用时间","summaryZh":"许多家长担心孩子花在电子屏幕上的时间太多。英国、澳大利亚和中国等世界各国政府都在出台网络安全措施，限制儿童的屏幕使用时间或禁止他们使用社交媒体。尼尔和贝卡聊起了自己的看屏习惯，并教你一些实用的新词汇。","questionZh":"根据英国媒体监管机构的数据，三到四岁的儿童中有多少人拥有智能手机？答案是：","optionsZh":["a) 二十分之一","b) 十分之一","c) 五分之一"],"answer":{"letter":"c","explanationZh":"贝卡在节目最后揭晓了答案：尼尔猜的是二十分之一，但实际上三到四岁儿童中拥有智能手机的比例高达五分之一（c) one in five）。"},"vocab":[{"word":"intentional","defEn":"acting with that plan or reason in mind","pos":"adj.","zh":"有意识的，有目的、有计划地行事的","exampleEn":"Yeah, I think it's not as much as getting them away from technology but being intentional about how we use technology with children.","ipa":"/ɪnˈtenʃənəl/"},{"word":"the bar needs to be higher","defEn":"make your expectations higher","pos":"短语","zh":"标准/门槛需要提得更高","exampleEn":"For me, the bar absolutely needs to be higher , and therefore I think about how to design products that think about childhood first...","ipa":"/ðə bɑː niːdz tə bi ˈhaɪə/"},{"word":"enable","defEn":"encourage someone's ability to do something, or to make something possible","pos":"v.","zh":"使能够，赋能，促成","exampleEn":"Emily believes that being more intentional and setting the bar higher will enable children to learn and develop alongside technology.","ipa":"/ɪˈneɪbəl/"},{"word":"eager","defEn":"want to do or have something very much","pos":"adj.","zh":"渴望的，热切的","exampleEn":"I find parents are very, very eager to understand what's really happening around technology in kids...","ipa":"/ˈiːɡə/"},{"word":"shift","defEn":"a small change","pos":"n./v.","zh":"小幅转变，微调；改变","exampleEn":"...have practical, realistic things they can do in their home to make little shifts that feel better.","ipa":"/ʃɪft/"},{"word":"set someone up for something","defEn":"prepare someone for something","pos":"短语","zh":"为某人做好铺垫/创造条件（常用于 set someone up for success）","exampleEn":"Having the device in view doesn't set them up for success .","ipa":"/set ˈsʌmwʌn ʌp fɔː/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"far too much / way too much","zh":"远远太多了（口语里强调程度过头，比 too much 更地道）","contextEn":"You know, Becca, I spend far too much time looking at my phone, and I really don't like it."},{"en":"rather than","zh":"而不是……（雅思阅读与写作中做对比论证的核心连词）","contextEn":"Emily highlights how, rather than stopping children from using technology, parents need to be more intentional about how their children use it."},{"en":"as a result","zh":"因此，结果是（清晰表达因果关系的衔接词）","contextEn":"It may make you act more carefully as a result."},{"en":"in view","zh":"在视线范围内，看得见的地方","contextEn":"Having the device in view doesn't set them up for success ."}],"sentences":[{"en":"Here, she tells Shiona McCallum, presenter of BBC World Service programme, Tech Life, her thoughts on whether we should take away children's screen devices completely.","zh":"在这里，她向 BBC 国际台《科技生活》节目主持人希奥娜·麦卡勒姆谈了自己对“是否应该彻底收走孩子的电子设备”这一问题的看法。","coreEn":"She tells Shiona McCallum her thoughts.","coreZh":"她向希奥娜·麦卡勒姆讲述了自己的想法。","cars":[{"text":", presenter of BBC World Service programme, Tech Life,","hook":"逗号插入语","noteZh":"补充说明听话人希奥娜·麦卡勒姆的节目主持人身份"},{"text":"on whether we should take away children's screen devices completely","hook":"介词短语 + 宾语从句","noteZh":"交代这些想法针对的核心议题：是否该彻底拿走孩子的电子屏幕设备"}],"tipZh":"看到 tell sb sth 被长长的头衔插在中间时，先跳过逗号里的身份介绍，直接把 tells 和后面的 her thoughts 连起来读。"},{"en":"For me, the bar absolutely needs to be higher , and therefore I think about how to design products that think about childhood first and really think about how children at different stages learn and develop and then see if there's a way that technology can enable that.","zh":"对我来说，标准绝对需要提得更高，因此我会思考如何设计把童年放在首位的产品，认真研究不同阶段的孩子如何学习与成长，然后再看看有没有办法让科技为此赋能。","coreEn":"The bar needs to be higher, and I think about how to design products and see if there's a way.","coreZh":"标准需要更高，我会思考如何设计产品，并看看是否有办法实现。","cars":[{"text":"that think about childhood first and really think about how children at different stages learn and develop","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"具体描述理想产品的两大特征：以童年为先，并考虑各年龄段儿童的学习与成长规律"},{"text":"that technology can enable that","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"解释前面说的“办法”是指“让科技能够促成孩子的学习与发展”"}],"tipZh":"口语采访常把三个 and 串成超长句，遇到这种句子按动词并列来切块（think about... and think about... and then see...），逻辑马上清晰。"},{"en":"Emily highlights how, rather than stopping children from using technology, parents need to be more intentional about how their children use it.","zh":"艾米丽强调，与其禁止孩子使用科技产品，家长更需要有意识、有计划地引导孩子如何使用科技。","coreEn":"Emily highlights how parents need to be more intentional.","coreZh":"艾米丽强调家长需要更有意识地行动。","cars":[{"text":", rather than stopping children from using technology,","hook":"逗号插入语","noteZh":"先否掉一种一刀切的做法（禁止孩子用科技），用来衬托后面的正确做法"},{"text":"about how their children use it","hook":"介词短语 + 宾语从句","noteZh":"说明家长具体要在什么事情上更有计划性：孩子使用科技的方式"}],"tipZh":"“highlights how, rather than A, B”是雅思大作文里非常高分的转折强调句式，用来反驳极端观点、提出理性方案特别好用。"},{"en":"I find parents are very, very eager to understand what's really happening around technology in kids and then have practical, realistic things they can do in their home to make little shifts that feel better.","zh":"我发现家长们非常迫切地想弄明白科技在孩子身上究竟产生了什么影响，然后希望能有一些实用、切实可行的办法，在家里做出让人感觉更舒服的小改变。","coreEn":"I find parents are eager to understand and have practical things.","coreZh":"我发现家长渴望理解现状并拥有实用方法。","cars":[{"text":"what's really happening around technology in kids","hook":"宾语从句","noteZh":"说明家长急于搞明白的内容：科技围绕孩子究竟在发生什么"},{"text":"they can do in their home","hook":"省略关系词","noteZh":"限定这些实用方法的应用场景：能在家里实操的"},{"text":"to make little shifts that feel better","hook":"不定式 to do + 关系词 that","noteZh":"说明做这些事的目的：带来让人感觉更好的小改变"}],"tipZh":"名词 things 后面先跟了一个省略 that 的定语从句（they can do...），再跟不定式表目的（to make...），逐层往后顺译就很自然。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Hello, this is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Neil.","zh":"大家好，欢迎收听 BBC 英语教学《六分钟英语》。我是尼尔。","s":8.22,"e":13.04,"wt":[0,104,116,138,166,202,240,266,301,356,420,442]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"And I'm Becca. Neil, I've noticed that you're pretty good at not checking your phone at work. How much time do you spend on your phone outside of work?","zh":"我是贝卡。尼尔，我注意到你上班时挺克制的，不太刷手机。那你下班后花多少时间看手机呢？","s":13.14,"e":20.32,"wt":[0,16,36,106,132,148,191,202,215,230,242,249,258,310,326,340,348,371,381,393,405,413,442,482,504,539,580,631,653]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Yes, I probably was. You know, Becca, I spend far too much time looking at my phone, and I really don't like it. How about you?","zh":"嗯，上班时大概还行吧。不过你知道吗，贝卡，我平时看手机的时间实在太多了，我自己都很讨厌这样。你呢？","s":20.32,"e":29.42,"wt":[0,41,51,130,190,202,204,286,334,392,422,451,492,531,588,604,643,700,720,725,755,783,805,836,853,881]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"I don't think you're alone. I also spend way too much time looking at my phone.","zh":"我想你不是一个人。我刷手机的时间也远远太多了。","s":29.52,"e":34.86,"wt":[0,2,29,58,92,144,153,190,236,263,290,327,412,428,446,468]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, Becca, we are both adults, but today we're talking about screen time and children screen time. Screen time - that's how much time you spend using devices like smart phones, tablets and laptops.","zh":"贝卡，咱俩毕竟都是成年人了，但今天我们要聊的是屏幕使用时间，尤其是儿童的屏幕使用时间。所谓屏幕使用时间（screen time），就是你使用智能手机、平板电脑和笔记本电脑这类设备的时长。","s":35.12,"e":48.64,"wt":[0,16,68,109,133,165,217,239,338,368,378,428,492,535,567,597,621,655,720,736,744,792,812,848,880,904,944,984,1041,1073,1116,1178,1235,1258]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Yes, and it's a particular worry of many parents that their children spend too much time on these devices. At the end of last year, Australia banned access to social media apps for under 16s, and countries like the UK are considering similar measures.","zh":"没错，很多家长尤其担心孩子在这些设备上花太多时间。去年年底，澳大利亚禁止 16 岁以下青少年使用社交媒体应用，英国等国家也正在考虑采取类似措施。","s":48.74,"e":64.98,"wt":[0,14,34,60,66,134,168,181,208,255,282,316,370,404,424,458,514,531,563,645,661,686,711,727,760,850,867,917,967,983,1033,1073,1114,1132,1172,1218,1242,1315,1347,1370,1387,1411,1500,1548]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"So, why is this? In this episode, we'll hear from a clinical psychologist and research associate at the University of Cambridge talking about screentime and children. As usual, we'll be learning some useful new words and phrases. And remember, you'll find all this episode's vocabulary, along with a transcript, on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"这究竟是为什么呢？本期节目我们将听取一位临床心理学家和一位剑桥大学副研究员关于儿童屏幕时间的看法。照例，我们还会学习一些实用的新词和短语。别忘了，本期节目的全部词汇和文稿都可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 上找到。","s":65.32,"e":86.6,"wt":[0,21,64,93,194,207,230,270,298,321,344,349,400,479,504,543,619,629,651,678,718,766,838,884,928,974,1088,1099,1129,1165,1176,1216,1248,1280,1305,1326,1352,1400,1419,1470,1508,1533,1552,1577,1638,1705,1743,1768,1780,1814,1824,1856,1902]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"But first Neil, I have a question for you. According to The UK media regulator, how many children aged three to four have a smartphone? Is it: a) one in twenty b) one in ten, or c) one in five?","zh":"不过首先，尼尔，我有个问题要考你。根据英国媒体监管机构的数据，三到四岁的儿童中有多少人拥有智能手机？是：a) 二十分之一，b) 十分之一，还是 c) 五分之一？","s":86.84,"e":102.42,"wt":[0,26,66,114,122,154,162,227,251,304,364,376,396,427,466,551,580,626,701,743,775,796,829,870,880,1024,1036,1060,1092,1126,1148,1239,1261,1295,1318,1372,1406,1445,1446,1506]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Wow. Well, I'm going to guess 1 in 20, but I think it's probably more than that.","zh":"哇。那我猜二十分之一吧，虽然我觉得实际比例可能比这个还高。","s":102.72,"e":108.3,"wt":[0,34,68,93,132,152,194,218,236,287,312,320,362,396,464,498,528]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Well, we'll find out the answer later. Dr Emily Goodacre is a research associate at the University of Cambridge. Here, she tells Shiona McCallum, presenter of BBC World Service programme, Tech Life, her thoughts on whether we should take away children's screen devices completely.","zh":"我们稍后揭晓答案。艾米丽·古德克博士是剑桥大学的副研究员。在这里，她向 BBC 国际台《科技生活》（Tech Life）节目主持人希奥娜·麦卡勒姆谈了自己对“是否应该彻底收走孩子的电子设备”这一问题的看法。","s":108.52,"e":126.13,"wt":[0,24,62,80,94,124,144,252,268,311,370,386,394,456,531,548,574,652,670,782,810,835,871,906,977,1048,1064,1096,1150,1188,1254,1277,1308,1326,1375,1387,1430,1442,1478,1502,1526,1587,1623,1666]},{"speaker":"Dr Emily Goodacre","en":"Yeah, I think it's not as much as getting them away from technology but being intentional about how we use technology with children. For me, the bar absolutely needs to be higher , and therefore I think about how to design products that think about childhood first and really think about how children at different stages learn and develop and then see if there's a way that technology can enable that.","zh":"是的，我觉得重点与其说是让孩子远离科技，不如说是要有意识、有计划地去思考如何让孩子使用科技。对我来说，标准绝对需要提得更高，因此我会思考如何设计把童年放在首位的产品，认真研究不同阶段的孩子如何学习与成长，然后再看看有没有办法让科技为此赋能。","s":126.13,"e":155.74,"wt":[0,41,49,91,124,149,166,200,217,276,310,344,378,463,488,603,680,771,825,853,883,943,963,997,1022,1054,1079,1163,1188,1245,1259,1279,1315,1339,1369,1459,1469,1519,1569,1663,1703,1749,1813,1871,1899,1899,2000,2081,2081,2110,2153,2196,2221,2289,2306,2383,2434,2477,2502,2561,2595,2617,2634,2649,2693,2710,2733,2764,2843,2866,2913]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Emily highlights how, rather than stopping children from using technology, parents need to be more intentional about how their children use it. An intention is a plan or reason to do something. So, to be intentional is to act with that plan or reason in mind. It may make you act more carefully as a result.","zh":"艾米丽强调，与其禁止孩子使用科技产品，家长更需要有意识地（intentional）引导孩子如何使用科技。Intention 指做某事的计划或目的，所以 be intentional 就是带着明确的计划或目的去行事，这也会让你因此更加谨慎。","s":155.96,"e":175.1,"wt":[0,23,85,103,140,165,215,265,290,321,395,438,473,483,498,529,614,652,675,713,772,792,836,854,908,914,941,970,984,1027,1053,1065,1160,1177,1194,1211,1307,1324,1341,1367,1402,1437,1472,1489,1532,1558,1626,1634,1644,1692,1709,1734,1768,1830,1842,1869]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Emily also says the bar needs to be higher . The idiom the bar is low means that the standards, expectations, or requirements are minimal and very easy to meet. Therefore, she believes that the bar needs to be higher – parents need to make their expectations higher. They need to think or act more carefully when considering how much time children spend with technology. You might also hear people say set the bar higher or lower.","zh":"艾米丽还说“标准需要提得更高”（the bar needs to be higher）。习语 the bar is low 意思是标准、期望或要求很低、非常容易达到。因此她认为标准必须提高——家长要提高自己的要求，在考虑孩子花多少时间接触科技产品时要更审慎地思考和行动。你有时也会听到人们说 set the bar higher（提高标准）或 set the bar lower（降低标准）。","s":175.44,"e":202.94,"wt":[0,28,78,111,135,159,200,217,232,268,305,329,369,393,417,433,457,498,531,555,676,754,774,856,884,930,956,998,1026,1048,1119,1181,1201,1235,1256,1274,1289,1325,1339,1353,1392,1417,1467,1496,1511,1539,1575,1668,1748,1766,1786,1803,1837,1846,1867,1895,1960,1988,2060,2078,2116,2144,2201,2236,2265,2364,2381,2415,2449,2483,2518,2561,2587,2612,2639,2674,2696]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Emily believes that being more intentional and setting the bar higher will enable children to learn and develop alongside technology. If you enable something, you encourage somebody's ability to do something, or to make something possible.","zh":"艾米丽相信，更有计划性并提高标准，将能够赋能（enable）孩子在科技的陪伴下学习与成长。Enable 的意思是激发某人做某事的能力，或使某件事成为可能。","s":203.18,"e":217.62,"wt":[0,37,68,92,130,169,239,258,298,316,340,378,403,441,503,512,548,569,619,683,824,838,862,916,932,1002,1016,1096,1141,1156,1171,1242,1257,1271,1303,1374]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Now let's hear Dr Becky Kennedy, a clinical psychologist and founder of an online parenting platform, talking about how parents feel about tech and children, for BBC World Service programme, Tech Life:","zh":"接下来我们听听临床心理学家兼在线育儿平台创始人贝基·肯尼迪博士在 BBC 国际台《科技生活》节目中谈家长对孩子接触科技的感受：","s":217.88,"e":230.92,"wt":[0,17,50,80,94,130,194,201,314,344,365,415,429,443,485,570,626,684,720,750,796,841,878,912,940,1006,1031,1056,1097,1155,1230,1262]},{"speaker":"Dr Becky Kennedy","en":"I find parents are very, very eager to understand what's really happening around technology in kids and then have practical, realistic things they can do in their home to make little shifts that feel better.","zh":"我发现家长们非常迫切地想弄明白科技在孩子身上究竟产生了什么影响，然后希望能有一些实用、切实可行的办法，在家里做出让人感觉更舒服的小改变。","s":231.08,"e":244.6,"wt":[0,9,36,42,86,92,158,166,231,356,356,440,456,501,582,609,634,659,693,780,898,996,1006,1006,1008,1025,1042,1085,1118,1136,1228,1228,1286,1303,1326]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Becky has found that many parents are eager to understand their children's use of technology. Eager is an adjective that describes wanting to do or have something very much.","zh":"贝基发现很多家长都渴望（eager）了解孩子使用科技产品的情况。Eager 是一个形容词，形容非常想做或想得到某样东西。","s":244.74,"e":255.28,"wt":[0,32,60,80,102,146,178,216,246,262,328,361,378,422,454,538,602,614,642,690,718,758,790,810,866,894,925,976,1010]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Becky also talks about making little shifts . A shift is a small change. It can be used as a verb or a noun. Becky shares an example of a shift that can help parents reduce children's screen time, moving the device somewhere it can't be seen:","zh":"贝基还提到了做出“小转变”（making little shifts）。Shift 指小幅度的改变，既可以作动词也可以作名词。贝基分享了一个能帮家长减少孩子看屏时间的小微调——把设备移到孩子看不见的地方：","s":255.54,"e":271.4,"wt":[0,32,70,110,148,176,198,258,318,326,366,382,390,430,502,518,541,558,598,619,623,652,667,674,728,820,820,900,900,900,954,1000,1000,1000,1086,1086,1182,1238,1270,1309,1349,1369,1409,1469,1482,1515,1528]},{"speaker":"Dr Becky Kennedy","en":"You can even say to a kid, you know why I moved it? It's actually because it's a really hard thing for me to expect from you, to see a screen in a room and not want to use it. I wasn't setting you up for success .","zh":"你甚至可以对孩子说：“你知道我为什么把它挪走吗？其实是因为房间里摆着一块屏幕却要求你不想去碰它，这对你来说太难了。我这么做并没有帮你创造成功的条件。”","s":271.4,"e":284.2,"wt":[0,17,40,71,94,109,116,154,177,206,218,234,258,324,354,410,468,498,508,548,567,594,604,612,638,674,674,735,748,768,774,815,828,834,861,881,901,928,941,961,1017,1021,1070,1129,1150,1171,1196,1240]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Becky explains how it's difficult for children to be expected to reduce their screen time if they can see their device. Having the device in view doesn't set them up for success . If you set someone up for something , you prepare them for it.","zh":"贝基解释说，如果孩子一眼就能看见设备，还指望他们少看屏幕，这其实很难做到。把设备放在视线范围内并不能“为他们的成功铺路”（set them up for success）。短语 set someone up for something 意思是为某人做好准备或创造条件。","s":284.2,"e":298.48,"wt":[0,35,84,102,126,178,200,236,250,268,322,334,371,402,439,477,486,515,537,559,595,660,704,726,770,784,813,863,885,920,944,964,1028,1086,1094,1109,1130,1179,1193,1214,1266,1291,1312,1356,1380,1402]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Yes. For example, earlier, I set Neil up for a shock when I asked him a question about screen time. Neil, I asked you, according to The UK media regulator, how many children ages three to four have a smartphone.","zh":"没错。比如刚才我问尼尔那道关于屏幕时间的问题时，就已经为他大吃一惊做好了铺垫。尼尔，我问你，根据英国媒体监管机构的数据，三到四岁的儿童中有多少人拥有智能手机。","s":298.76,"e":314.1,"wt":[0,48,76,160,220,235,263,300,318,346,355,408,439,447,486,510,517,581,634,675,736,785,793,834,876,948,964,988,1004,1045,1133,1167,1201,1285,1311,1352,1372,1407,1426,1451]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"And I said 1 in 20.","zh":"我猜的是二十分之一。","s":314.28,"e":316.42,"wt":[0,16,48,90,122,157]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Well, it is in fact, see 1 in 5.","zh":"其实正确答案是 c)，五分之一。","s":316.8,"e":320.58,"wt":[0,26,46,62,90,172,220,256,280]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Wow. I thought it might be more than I said. Now, let's recap the vocabulary we've learned, starting with intentional . It's an adjective that describes acting with a plan or reason in mind.","zh":"哇，我猜到可能会比我说的多。现在让我们来复习今天学过的词汇，首先是 intentional，这个形容词形容带着计划或目的去行动。","s":320.62,"e":332.66,"wt":[0,42,50,107,123,148,166,166,174,198,312,334,366,393,409,463,494,552,612,647,721,770,780,806,854,886,940,988,998,1027,1053,1066,1120,1136]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"We had the expression he bar needs to be higher . To set the bar higher is to make your expectations higher.","zh":"我们学了表达 the bar needs to be higher（标准需要更高）。Set the bar higher 意思就是提高你的期望或标准。","s":332.76,"e":340.12,"wt":[0,15,37,59,131,155,177,214,232,254,285,316,332,356,380,404,451,468,484,516,548,652]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"We also had enable . This is to encourage someone's ability to do something, or to make something possible.","zh":"我们还学了 enable，意思是激发某人做某事的能力，或使某事成为可能。","s":340.2,"e":346.26,"wt":[0,12,37,56,84,113,138,150,161,219,275,319,331,343,400,412,424,449,526]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"To be eager is to want to do or have something very much.","zh":"Be eager 意思是非常渴望做某事或得到某物。","s":346.38,"e":350.74,"wt":[0,12,36,83,102,121,159,178,197,216,254,334,377]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"A shift is a small change. You can also use it as verb with to mean changing something slightly.","zh":"Shift 指小幅改变，也可以用作动词，表示稍微改变某件事。","s":350.88,"e":356.92,"wt":[0,6,44,60,68,108,184,198,216,254,275,289,310,324,338,352,380,437,508]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"And if you set someone up for something , you prepare them for it. Once again, our six minutes are up. If you enjoyed this episode, you'll find a quiz and worksheet to practise the vocabulary we've learnt on our website, bbclearningenglish.com. See you again soon. But for now, it's goodbye.","zh":"而如果你 set someone up for something，就是为某人做好迎接某事的准备或铺垫。我们的六分钟时间又到了。如果你喜欢本期节目，欢迎访问我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com，通过小测验和学习单练习今天学到的词汇。我们很快再见，现在先说再见啦。","s":357.16,"e":375.02,"wt":[0,30,45,65,85,126,145,165,210,232,250,288,310,332,382,418,463,482,514,548,562,614,628,646,688,712,766,802,828,837,876,929,992,1014,1054,1081,1151,1188,1224,1236,1268,1330,1600,1618,1636,1671,1706,1718,1742,1762,1768]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Goodbye!","zh":"再见！","s":375.02,"e":376.28,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0nsp04k.mp3"},{"epNum":24,"id":"ep-260611","date":"2026-06-11","title":"How advertisers make us spend money","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260611","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260611_6_minute_english_how_advertisers_make_us_spend_money_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260611_6_minute_english_how_advertisers_make_us_spend_money_transcript.pdf","summary":"What was the last thing you bought? And can you remember why you bought that particular thing, rather than something else? We all like to think we make rational, independent choices when we buy things, but advertisers are constantly trying different strategies to influence us to behave differently. Pippa and Phil discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"What's the name of the theory, from behavioural science, that says people respond to small prompts?","options":["a) push theory","b) pull theory","c) nudge theory"],"topic":"经济消费","titleZh":"广告商是如何让我们掏钱的","summaryZh":"你最近买的一样东西是什么？你还记得自己为什么买了它，而不是别的商品吗？购物时我们都喜欢以为自己做出了理性、独立的决定，但广告商其实一直在尝试各种策略来悄悄影响我们的消费行为。皮帕和菲尔将一起探讨这个话题，并教你一些实用的新词汇。","questionZh":"行为科学中认为人们会对微小提示做出反应的那项理论叫什么名字？","optionsZh":["a) 推力理论（push theory）","b) 拉力理论（pull theory）","c) 助推理论（nudge theory）"],"answer":{"letter":"c","explanationZh":"菲尔在节目结尾揭晓了答案：皮帕猜的拉力理论不对，正确答案是 c) 助推理论（nudge theory），该理论认为微小的提示或轻推就能改变人的行为。"},"vocab":[{"word":"mental shortcut","defEn":"something that helps us make a decision quickly","pos":"n.","zh":"心理捷径，思维捷径","exampleEn":"It's a mental shortcut . It's something that helps us navigate uncertain times or make quick choices.","ipa":"/ˈmentəl ˈʃɔːtkʌt/"},{"word":"judged","defEn":"worried about what other people think about us","pos":"adj.","zh":"感到被评头论足的，担心别人怎么看自己的","exampleEn":"If people feel judged , then it means that they worry what other people will think of them.","ipa":"/dʒʌdʒd/"},{"word":"upsell","defEn":"selling extra products, or more expensive products to customers","pos":"v./n.","zh":"追加销售，向上推销（劝说顾客买更多或更贵的商品）","exampleEn":"If you upsell something, then you get someone to spend more.","ipa":"/ˈʌpsel/"},{"word":"basket spend","defEn":"amount of money spent on a single transaction","pos":"n.","zh":"客单价，单次购物篮消费额","exampleEn":"And that's typically what we're seeing – an increased basket spend of typically 25–30%.","ipa":"/ˈbɑːskɪt spend/"},{"word":"think outside the box","defEn":"think creatively to come up with a solution","pos":"短语","zh":"跳出固有思维，打破常规思考","exampleEn":"Takwa says that they needed to think outside the box . This means to try and think in a different way, to come up with something creative.","ipa":"/θɪŋk ˌaʊtˈsaɪd ðə bɒks/"},{"word":"model","defEn":"show behaviour that you want others to copy","pos":"v.","zh":"示范（某种行为），树立榜样供他人模仿","exampleEn":"If you model behaviour, then you behave in a way that other people can copy.","ipa":"/ˈmɒdəl/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"follow the lead of","zh":"效仿……的做法；跟随……的引领","contextEn":"When we're unsure, we don't know what to do, it's kind of easy to follow the lead of those who we think are credible or knowledgeable."},{"en":"make sense of","zh":"理解，弄明白（常指理清复杂事物或认识世界）","contextEn":"Our parents... for some people, it's about our teachers – you know, these people who help us learn and make sense of the world."},{"en":"draw on","zh":"利用，借助（经验、心理机制、资源等）","contextEn":"And some businesses can draw on this through things like endorsements – you know, dentists recommending toothpaste, athletes endorsing sportswear."},{"en":"be inclined to","zh":"倾向于……（雅思写作替代 tend to 的高分表达）","contextEn":"Because you've got more time and you're not being judged , you're very much more inclined to actually say yes to these things."}],"sentences":[{"en":"When we're unsure, we don't know what to do, it's kind of easy to follow the lead of those who we think are credible or knowledgeable.","zh":"当我们拿不定主意、不知道该怎么办时，就很容易去效仿那些在我们看来可靠或博学的人。","coreEn":"It's easy to follow the lead of those.","coreZh":"很容易去效仿某些人。","cars":[{"text":"When we're unsure, we don't know what to do,","hook":"状语从句","noteZh":"交代触发场景：拿不准主意、不知所措的时候"},{"text":"who we think are credible or knowledgeable","hook":"关系词 who + 插入语 we think","noteZh":"具体说明效仿的对象是“我们认为可信或有学识的人”"}],"tipZh":"关系词后夹着 we think（who we think are...）是英美口语和雅思阅读的高频陷阱结构，读的时候把 we think 当括号里的“我们认为”顺过去即可。"},{"en":"Not all people, but I think there's definitely a large proportion of people who may feel judged in those instances and may say no.","zh":"虽然不是所有人，但我认为肯定有很大一部分人在这种情况下会觉得被人评头论足，因而选择拒绝。","coreEn":"There's definitely a large proportion of people.","coreZh":"肯定有很大一部分人。","cars":[{"text":"Not all people, but I think","hook":"让步与观点引出","noteZh":"先留余地“并非所有人”，让观点显得更客观严谨"},{"text":"who may feel judged in those instances and may say no","hook":"关系词 who","noteZh":"说明这大部分人在点单被推销时的心理反应和拒绝行为"}],"tipZh":"“Not all..., but a large proportion of people who...”在雅思口语 Part 3 和写作中极实用，既能避免表达绝对化，又能自然拉出定语从句。"},{"en":"Because you've got more time and you're not being judged , you're very much more inclined to actually say yes to these things.","zh":"因为你有了更多考虑时间，而且不会觉得有人在评判你，你就更有可能对这些加购推荐点头答应。","coreEn":"You're very much more inclined to say yes to these things.","coreZh":"你明显更倾向于对这些推荐说“好”。","cars":[{"text":"Because you've got more time and you're not being judged ,","hook":"状语从句","noteZh":"交代促成消费的两个心理原因：时间充裕 + 没有被审视的压力"},{"text":"to actually say yes to these things","hook":"不定式 to do","noteZh":"接在 inclined 后面，具体说明倾向于做出的行为"}],"tipZh":"注意被动进行时 you're not being judged（你当下没有被别人评头论足），在听力中听到 being + 过去分词要反应出“正在被……”的含义。"},{"en":"So, we needed something really creative and really different to think outside of the box , or to let people see themselves in those stories, because people are learning and changing by imitating and modelling and, like, observing others' behaviours and others' actions and emotional reactions to things.","zh":"所以，我们需要一个真正有创意、截然不同的点子来打破常规，让人们在故事中看到自己的影子，因为人们正是通过模仿、效法以及观察他人的行为举止和对事物的情绪反应来学习和改变的。","coreEn":"We needed something really creative and really different.","coreZh":"我们需要一个真正有创意且与众不同的东西。","cars":[{"text":"to think outside of the box , or to let people see themselves in those stories,","hook":"不定式 to do","noteZh":"并列说明需要这个创意的两个目的：打破常规、让观众产生代入感"},{"text":"because people are learning and changing","hook":"状语从句","noteZh":"解释背后的行为科学原理：人们一直在学习和改变"},{"text":"by imitating and modelling and, like, observing others' behaviours and others' actions and emotional reactions to things","hook":"介词短语 by -ing","noteZh":"用一串并列的 -ing 动名词具体交代人们学习改变的途径"}],"tipZh":"真实英语访谈常夹杂口语填充词（如 like）和一连串并列名词，听的时候过滤掉 like，紧盯 by 后面的核心动词模仿、观察即可抓住主旨。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Hello, this is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Pippa.","zh":"大家好，欢迎收听 BBC 英语教学《六分钟英语》。我是皮帕。","s":2.54,"e":12.82,"wt":[0,614,632,662,690,726,764,800,850,886,952,976]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"And I'm Phil. What's the last thing you bought and why did you buy it?","zh":"我是菲尔。你最近买的一样东西是什么？为什么买它呢？","s":13.04,"e":18.32,"wt":[0,16,53,108,156,180,212,242,268,324,348,372,400,434,442]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"I bought a new pair of headphones recently because my old ones broke, and I did lots of research to try and find a good pair. How about you?","zh":"我最近新买了一副耳机，因为旧的那副坏了，我还做了很多功课才挑中一副好用的。你呢？","s":19.09,"e":28.18,"wt":[0,5,53,61,135,135,199,219,286,339,360,385,418,460,475,493,518,564,575,631,645,661,683,703,717,741,807,825,825]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"I bought a soft drink on the way to work this morning. It might be because I saw someone I like promoting it in an advert. We call this a celebrity endorsement. But why do they work? Let's start by hearing from Ben Jones, a behavioural research expert, speaking to BBC World Service programme Business Daily.","zh":"我今天早上上班路上买了瓶软饮料。说不定是因为我在广告里看到自己喜欢的人在代言它。我们把这叫做名人代言（celebrity endorsement）。但名人代言为什么管用呢？我们先听听行为研究专家本·琼斯在 BBC 国际台《每日商业》（Business Daily）节目中的分析。","s":28.57,"e":47.76,"wt":[0,5,33,52,81,107,121,137,153,171,197,227,323,339,378,395,435,451,475,511,516,544,605,621,631,657,725,741,770,799,806,871,966,987,1035,1051,1085,1175,1202,1227,1240,1269,1293,1318,1359,1363,1428,1475,1510,1574,1599,1619,1666,1731,1815,1871]},{"speaker":"Ben Jones","en":"When we're unsure, we don't know what to do, it's kind of easy to follow the lead of those who we think are credible or knowledgeable. It's a mental shortcut . It's something that helps us navigate uncertain times or make quick choices. We start doing that from an early age. Our parents... for some people, it's about our teachers – you know, these people who help us learn and make sense of the world. And some businesses can draw on this through things like endorsements – you know, dentists recommending toothpaste, athletes endorsing sportswear.","zh":"当我们拿不定主意、不知道该怎么办时，就很容易去效仿那些在我们看来可靠或博学的人。这是一种“心理捷径”（mental shortcut），能帮我们应对不确定的处境或快速做出选择。我们从小就会这样做，比如听父母的，或者对有些人来说是听老师的——你知道，就是这些帮我们学习和认识世界的人。有些企业就会通过代言来利用这种心理，比如牙医推荐牙膏、运动员代言运动装。","s":48.02,"e":74.79,"wt":[0,16,46,75,84,104,114,130,148,164,182,194,214,237,247,275,289,322,338,372,387,401,436,457,513,527,636,648,664,693,723,748,756,770,792,828,846,920,1003,1038,1064,1092,1134,1233,1259,1278,1292,1306,1321,1338,1359,1404,1421,1473,1490,1513,1558,1574,1574,1612,1642,1658,1674,1700,1718,1743,1758,1774,1793,1816,1828,1828,1850,1860,1886,1992,2020,2042,2080,2093,2109,2117,2133,2171,2202,2229,2267,2283,2298,2323,2377,2458,2538,2592,2652]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Ben says that endorsements work as a mental shortcut . This is something that helps us make a decision quickly. If we trust the endorsement, then we don't need to think about it that much.","zh":"本说，代言之所以有效，是因为它充当了一种“心理捷径”（mental shortcut），能帮我们迅速做决定。只要我们信任这个代言，就不用费神去多想了。","s":75.84,"e":85.84,"wt":[0,27,57,87,170,198,210,228,277,326,366,374,390,424,439,458,468,493,498,542,602,614,626,656,673,753,777,789,824,842,852,876,900,909,942]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"In this episode, we're going to look at the way that people can be convinced to do things.","zh":"本期节目中，我们将探讨人们是如何被说服去做某些事情的。","s":86.06,"e":90.92,"wt":[0,16,40,96,124,152,164,182,192,212,232,255,290,306,318,371,382,406]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"And as usual, we'll learn some useful new words and phrases. And remember, you'll find all the vocabulary and a quiz on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"照例，我们还会学习一些实用的新词和短语。别忘了，全部词汇和一个小测验都可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 上找到。","s":91.14,"e":100.86,"wt":[0,22,37,86,121,146,176,214,244,268,298,378,395,437,466,492,506,526,594,614,620,647,660,680,741]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"OK. But first I have a question for you, Pippa. Today we're talking about behavioural science. What's the name of the theory that says people respond to small prompts? Is it: a) push theory, b) pull theory, or c) nudge theory?","zh":"好了，不过首先我有个问题要考你，皮帕。我们今天聊的是行为科学。认为人们会对微小提示做出反应的那项理论叫什么名字？是：a) 推力理论，b) 拉力理论，还是 c) 助推理论？","s":101.08,"e":119.57,"wt":[0,0,78,78,154,154,206,217,247,282,380,415,450,498,534,622,712,749,768,793,805,825,862,887,912,956,1032,1056,1111,1224,1236,1261,1292,1342,1456,1489,1534,1622,1654,1691,1747]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Oh, I'm not sure. I'm going to say b) pull theory.","zh":"噢，我不太确定。那我选 b) 拉力理论吧。","s":119.57,"e":123.64,"wt":[0,47,71,96,147,165,165,187,249,283,316]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"OK. Well, we'll find out the answer at the end of the programme. In recent years, many fast-food chains have installed touchscreen machines for people to order their food. They've reported taking more money as a result. Could behavioural science tell us why this happens? Here's product designer Dean Ward speaking to BBC World Service's Business Daily.","zh":"好的，我们在节目最后揭晓答案。近年来，许多快餐连锁店都安装了自助触摸屏点餐机，结果营业额都变高了。行为科学能解释背后的原因吗？下面听产品设计师迪恩·沃德在 BBC 国际台《每日商业》中的解读。","s":123.64,"e":147.2,"wt":[0,35,74,108,124,124,166,186,198,220,234,248,300,418,432,470,514,539,589,627,652,709,779,830,849,901,914,960,996,1069,1122,1160,1202,1246,1284,1294,1346,1454,1484,1544,1617,1641,1657,1681,1714,1785,1821,1876,1942,1975,2008,2074,2090,2114,2154,2228,2302]},{"speaker":"Dean Ward","en":"Firstly, the psychology of speaking to someone and feeling judged – we think is a key factor. So, would you like extra fries? Would you like to go large? Not all people, but I think there's definitely a large proportion of people who may feel judged in those instances and may say no. There's the fact that you've got more time to look. You can see the product, you can see what's in it, you can see all the products linked to it as well. And also around upsell as well. Would you like to add this? Would you like to do that? Because you've got more time and you're not being judged , you're very much more inclined to actually say yes to these things. And that's typically what we're seeing – an increased basket spend of typically 25–30%.","zh":"首先，我们认为面对真人点单时觉得“被评头论足”（feeling judged）的心理是一个关键因素。比如店员问你：“要加份薯条吗？要升级成大份吗？”虽然不是所有人，但我认为肯定有很大一部分人在这种情况下会觉得被人评头论足，因而选择拒绝。另外，用机器点餐时你有更多时间慢慢浏览，可以看到商品外观、配料以及所有相关搭配商品。还有就是追加销售（upsell）——屏幕会提示“要加这个吗？要来份那个吗？”因为你有了更多考虑时间，而且不会觉得有人在评判你，你就更有可能对这些加购推荐点头答应。我们看到的典型结果就是，客单价（basket spend）通常会提高 25% 到 30%。","s":147.66,"e":184.32,"wt":[0,51,71,139,152,206,219,267,287,324,350,367,379,402,412,424,450,526,534,554,571,594,621,667,684,702,720,734,757,810,830,850,904,924,931,970,1020,1064,1079,1091,1110,1166,1190,1223,1241,1266,1303,1315,1346,1398,1414,1434,1460,1508,1524,1544,1558,1572,1590,1606,1630,1654,1674,1794,1811,1824,1837,1850,1890,1903,1916,1940,1967,1978,1998,2008,2026,2046,2056,2080,2110,2138,2148,2162,2180,2238,2252,2271,2300,2336,2352,2408,2408,2420,2438,2470,2496,2496,2510,2524,2540,2556,2596,2656,2674,2686,2694,2716,2732,2748,2780,2796,2823,2850,2867,2899,2923,2943,2965,3009,3020,3064,3080,3111,3118,3149,3206,3220,3248,3291,3310,3333,3360,3378,3391,3432,3459,3482,3504,3610]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"If people feel judged , then it means that they worry what other people will think of them. If we have to ask a human for extra fries, we might worry what they think of us, but no one feels judged by a computer screen.","zh":"如果人们感到“被评判”（feel judged），意思就是他们担心别人怎么看自己。如果非要开口向真人店员加份薯条，我们可能会在意对方怎么想，但没人会觉得一块电脑屏幕在评判自己。","s":184.94,"e":196.24,"wt":[0,12,43,64,89,105,126,136,162,183,204,230,251,277,308,329,355,370,452,462,474,492,511,528,536,568,587,619,663,675,707,739,762,784,802,816,874,885,900,916,954,999,1014,1021,1082]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"If you upsell something, then you get someone to spend more. For example, waiters often try to get people to buy larger portions, more drinks, or dessert.","zh":"如果你进行追加销售（upsell），就是促使顾客花更多的钱。比如服务员经常劝顾客点更大份的餐食、多点些饮料或者加份甜点。","s":196.53,"e":206.92,"wt":[0,11,35,84,174,208,231,255,301,329,370,435,451,498,534,571,588,605,630,661,677,701,723,841,871,938,955]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Dean says that upselling can increase people's basket spend . A basket spend is the total amount that someone spends in one transaction, on everything that's in their basket. Now, this is an example of a business using behavioural science to nudge people to do things. But it's not just businesses that use behavioural science in this way. In Tunisia, the United Nations World Food Programme wanted to encourage people to eat food together more often. They made a TV show set in a restaurant. Let's hear from Takwa Khelifi from the World Food Programme, talking to BBC World Service's Business Daily.","zh":"迪恩说，追加销售可以提高顾客的客单价（basket spend）。Basket spend 指某人在单次交易中为购物篮里所有商品花的总金额。这是企业运用行为科学来“轻推”消费者的例子。不过，并不只有商家会这么运用行为科学。在突尼斯，联合国世界粮食计划署希望鼓励人们更经常地一起聚餐，于是制作了一部以餐厅为背景的电视剧。下面我们听听世界粮食计划署的塔克瓦·哈利菲在 BBC 国际台《每日商业》中的分享。","s":207.06,"e":242.16,"wt":[0,21,48,72,130,156,206,262,302,334,370,377,423,461,470,482,521,578,596,634,662,686,716,792,815,866,874,916,954,1034,1098,1103,1111,1124,1172,1185,1191,1245,1279,1354,1392,1408,1440,1470,1490,1515,1584,1591,1610,1628,1653,1715,1740,1758,1826,1854,1868,1894,1948,1964,2025,2045,2100,2139,2174,2202,2265,2306,2321,2364,2374,2394,2440,2482,2520,2558,2633,2643,2668,2676,2693,2727,2753,2770,2778,2894,2925,2950,2975,3006,3050,3075,3093,3126,3168,3225,3268,3290,3314,3351,3411,3452]},{"speaker":"Takwa Khelifi","en":"So, we needed something really creative and really different to think outside of the box , or to let people see themselves in those stories, because people are learning and changing by imitating and modelling and, like, observing others' behaviours and others' actions and emotional reactions to things. So, this is why we tried this project or this... tried this product or this TV series – in order that you impact people.","zh":"所以，我们需要一个真正有创意、截然不同的点子来打破常规，让人们在故事中看到自己的影子，因为人们正是通过模仿、效法以及观察他人的行为举止和对事物的情绪反应来学习和改变的。这就是我们尝试这个项目——或者说这部电视剧——的原因，为的是真正去影响大众。","s":242.44,"e":274.3,"wt":[0,13,30,78,112,200,200,238,280,346,528,556,557,575,608,626,645,658,676,703,746,770,800,820,856,898,1064,1118,1145,1216,1244,1324,1350,1440,1472,1558,1669,1796,1860,1932,1968,2000,2086,2108,2158,2198,2261,2277,2352,2375,2400,2422,2424,2456,2506,2605,2672,2682,2712,2748,2778,2820,2840,2882,2896,2934,2958,2978,3006,3029,3049,3111]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Takwa says that they needed to think outside the box . This means to try and think in a different way, to come up with something creative.","zh":"塔克瓦说他们需要“跳出固有思维”（think outside the box），意思就是尝试换一种思路去想出有创意的办法。","s":274.58,"e":283.16,"wt":[0,32,47,63,94,141,155,195,249,272,317,390,408,408,452,452,452,488,503,510,596,614,630,662,678,710,776]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"The TV programme leads to people modelling behaviour. If you model behaviour, then you behave in a way that other people can copy. Now Phil , I think it's time to hear the answer to your question.","zh":"这部电视剧起到了行为示范（modelling behaviour）的作用。如果你示范某种行为（model behaviour），意思就是你的举动可供他人效仿。好了菲尔，我想该揭晓你那道题的答案了。","s":283.34,"e":294.94,"wt":[0,20,34,100,136,150,194,260,348,362,384,420,500,537,568,605,616,629,653,678,710,712,736,854,876,892,905,909,933,951,970,979,998,1022,1059,1065,1088]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Yes, it is. I asked what theory says that people respond to small prompts to change their behaviour.","zh":"没错。我刚才问的是，哪种理论认为人们会因为微小的提示而改变自己的行为。","s":294.94,"e":301.88,"wt":[0,34,50,90,164,206,246,292,310,310,336,393,409,450,507,523,582,632]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"I thought it was pull theory...","zh":"我以为是拉力理论……","s":302.05,"e":304.66,"wt":[0,11,31,55,103,161]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"...which I'm afraid was the wrong answer. Nudge theory says that people's behaviour can be changed by small prompts or nudges.","zh":"……很遗憾你答错了。助推理论（Nudge theory）认为，微小的提示或轻推可以改变人们的行为。","s":304.92,"e":314.17,"wt":[0,0,64,110,130,158,205,282,378,432,448,448,492,566,590,603,656,688,728,809,828]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we've learned, starting with mental shortcut , which is something that helps us make a decision quickly, usually because we don't need to think about it.","zh":"好的，我们来复习一下今天学过的词汇，首先是 mental shortcut（心理捷径），指能帮我们快速做决定的方式，通常因为我们不需要过多思考。","s":314.17,"e":325.14,"wt":[0,85,127,205,205,205,233,300,360,390,435,484,510,554,567,607,626,649,671,685,708,757,824,872,920,934,957,971,989,993,1019]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"If someone feels judged , then they're worried about what other people think about them.","zh":"如果某人感到 judged，就是他们担心别人对自己的看法。","s":325.38,"e":330.36,"wt":[0,18,62,96,128,149,176,223,270,300,331,365,396,424,426]},{"speaker":"Upsell","en":"refers to persuading someone to buy something extra, like a dessert at a restaurant.","zh":"（Upsell，追加销售）指说服顾客额外购买商品，比如在餐厅点一份甜点。","s":331.1,"e":336.46,"wt":[0,47,62,139,182,202,228,256,334,334,376,388,400,450]},{"speaker":"Basket spend","en":"is what you spend in one transaction on the things in your shopping basket.","zh":"（Basket spend，客单价）指你在单次交易中为购物篮里的商品花费的总金额。","s":337.54,"e":342.28,"wt":[0,16,16,34,58,80,146,238,250,274,282,296,358,368]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"If you think outside the box , then you think about something in a different way to come up with a creative solution.","zh":"如果你 think outside the box，就是换个角度思考问题，从而想出有创意的解决方案。","s":342.58,"e":348.74,"wt":[0,9,25,53,92,108,121,135,157,173,201,229,276,286,295,345,361,372,407,419,436,462,532]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"And finally, modelling is behaving in a way that others can copy. Once again, our six minutes are up, but why not head over to our website, bbclearningenglish.com, to try the quiz and worksheet for this episode. See you again soon!","zh":"最后，modelling 指做出可供他人效仿的示范行为。我们的六分钟时间又到了，不妨去我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 做做本期节目的小测验和学习单。很快再见！","s":348.86,"e":365.04,"wt":[0,26,98,160,187,242,250,274,296,332,358,390,462,490,531,550,570,610,628,656,688,712,740,774,794,810,851,910,1151,1168,1194,1220,1262,1295,1360,1384,1417,1504,1522,1538,1570]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Bye!","zh":"拜拜！","s":365.26,"e":365.82,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0nrcdc1.mp3"},{"epNum":25,"id":"ep-260604","date":"2026-06-04","title":"The power of poetry","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260604","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260604_6_minute_english_the_power_of_poetry_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260604_6_minute_english_the_power_of_poetry__transcript.pdf","summary":"Do you like to read poetry? When was the last time you heard someone recite a poem? Some people may just think of poetry as something they studied at school, but for many people it has a powerful presence in their lives. Poetry can help you connect with others, come to terms with personal emotions, or even spread the word about important global events. If you think poetry is not for you, perhaps you just haven't found the right poem yet! Neil and Pippa discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"The phrase 'the positive power of poetry' repeats the same initial 'p' sound across a string of words. Doing this is a common feature of poetry, but what is it called?","options":["a) assonance","b) alliteration","c) simile"],"topic":"文化教育","titleZh":"诗歌的力量","summaryZh":"你喜欢读诗吗？你上一次听别人朗诵诗歌是什么时候？有些人可能只把诗歌当成上学时背过的课本内容，但对许多人而言，诗歌在生活中有着强大的力量。诗歌能帮你与他人产生共鸣、抚平内心情绪，甚至传播重要的全球议题。如果你觉得诗歌不适合自己，也许只是还没遇到对的那首诗！尼尔和皮帕将一起探讨这个话题，并教你一些实用的新词汇。","questionZh":"短语“the positive power of poetry”在一连串单词的开头重复了相同的“p”音。这种手法在诗歌中很常见，它叫什么？","optionsZh":["a) 半谐音/元音押韵（assonance）","b) 头韵（alliteration）","c) 明喻（simile）"],"answer":{"letter":"b","explanationZh":"尼尔在节目结尾确认皮帕凭借英语专业功底答对了：在一连串单词开头重复相同发音的修辞手法叫做头韵（b) alliteration）。"},"vocab":[{"word":"open mic night","defEn":"live event where anyone is allowed on stage to perform music, tell jokes or recite poetry","pos":"n.","zh":"开放麦之夜（任何人都能上台表演的现场活动）","exampleEn":"A good place to hear poetry is an open mic night , a live event where anyone is allowed to stand up on stage to sing or recite poems.","ipa":"/ˈəʊpən maɪk naɪt/"},{"word":"touch your soul","defEn":"affect you deeply on an emotional or spiritual level","pos":"短语","zh":"触动你的灵魂，深深打动你","exampleEn":"Sometimes if you go to an open mic night , people can really touch your soul with what they're saying.","ipa":"/tʌtʃ jɔː səʊl/"},{"word":"jargon","defEn":"special words and phrases that are used by a particular profession or group","pos":"n.","zh":"行话，专业术语（常含外行听不懂的意味）","exampleEn":"Yes, a lot of this jargon is really hard to understand, or a lot of the science is quite impenetrable to normal people...","ipa":"/ˈdʒɑːɡən/"},{"word":"impenetrable","defEn":"impossible to understand","pos":"adj.","zh":"晦涩难懂的，难以理解的","exampleEn":"Jargon can make scientific papers impenetrable , an adjective meaning impossible to understand.","ipa":"/ɪmˈpenɪtrəbəl/"},{"word":"turn-off","defEn":"something that people dislike or find uninteresting","pos":"n.","zh":"令人倒胃口/提不起兴趣的事物","exampleEn":"...or a lot of the science is quite impenetrable to normal people, so it's instantly a turn-off .","ipa":"/ˈtɜːnɒf/"},{"word":"get goose bumps","defEn":"become so cold, emotionally affected, or afraid that the hairs on your skin stand up to make tiny bumps","pos":"短语","zh":"起鸡皮疙瘩（因感动、寒冷或害怕）","exampleEn":"It still gives me goosebumps when I hear it now.","ipa":"/ɡet ˈɡuːs bʌmps/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"come in handy","zh":"派上用场（日常口语和雅思口语极高频表达）","contextEn":"Finally, my English degree might come in handy!"},{"en":"be based on","zh":"以……为基础；取材于……","contextEn":"Liv's poem is based on a study into biodiversity."},{"en":"put into words","zh":"用语言表达出来（常用于形容难以言表的情感或想法）","contextEn":"I think it's everything that I wish I could say and put into words myself."},{"en":"make an impact","zh":"产生影响（加 for good 表示“带来积极深远的影响”）","contextEn":"Maybe poetry can't fix everything, but for Kat, it's making an impact for good."}],"sentences":[{"en":"For some, poetry is what you study at school, then forget, while for others it becomes an important part of their life.","zh":"对一些人来说，诗歌不过是上学时学过、后来就忘掉的东西；而对另一些人而言，它成了生命中重要的一部分。","coreEn":"Poetry is what you study, while it becomes an important part of their life.","coreZh":"诗歌是学过就忘的东西，同时它成了另一些人生活的重要部分。","cars":[{"text":"For some,","hook":"介词短语","noteZh":"划定第一类人群：对一部分人来说"},{"text":"what you study at school, then forget,","hook":"表语从句","noteZh":"具体描绘第一类人对诗歌的印象：上学背完就忘的内容"},{"text":"while for others it becomes an important part of their life","hook":"状语从句（对比 while）","noteZh":"形成鲜明对比：引出另一类把诗歌当成生活重心的群体"}],"tipZh":"“For some..., while for others...”是雅思大作文开头段引出两派观点的万能结构，简练又地道。"},{"en":"A good place to hear poetry is an open mic night , a live event where anyone is allowed to stand up on stage to sing or recite poems.","zh":"听诗的好去处是开放麦之夜——这是一种现场活动，任何人都可以走上舞台唱歌或朗诵诗歌。","coreEn":"A good place is an open mic night.","coreZh":"一个好去处是开放麦之夜。","cars":[{"text":"to hear poetry","hook":"不定式 to do","noteZh":"限定这个“好去处”的用途：用来听诗的地方"},{"text":", a live event","hook":"同位语解释","noteZh":"给 open mic night 下定义：它是一种现场活动"},{"text":"where anyone is allowed to stand up on stage to sing or recite poems","hook":"关系词 where","noteZh":"具体说明这场活动怎么玩：谁都能上台唱歌或读诗"}],"tipZh":"英语解释陌生文化概念时，最爱用“名词 + 逗号 + 统称名词 + where/which 从句”的套路，阅读遇到生词时往逗号后面找就能秒懂释义。"},{"en":"And it works, says Kat Bruce, the environmental scientist whose study Liv based her poem on, talking here with Myra Anubi for BBC World Service's People Fixing the World.","zh":"而且这真的管用，环境科学家凯特·布鲁斯说道——丽芙正是根据她的研究写出了那首诗——以下是她在 BBC 国际台《修补世界的人》节目中与迈拉·阿努比的对话。","coreEn":"And it works, says Kat Bruce.","coreZh":"凯特·布鲁斯说，这确实管用。","cars":[{"text":", the environmental scientist","hook":"同位语/身份头衔","noteZh":"交代凯特·布鲁斯的职业身份"},{"text":"whose study Liv based her poem on,","hook":"关系词 whose","noteZh":"建立人物关联：丽芙那首诗所依托的研究正是凯特做的"},{"text":"talking here with Myra Anubi for BBC World Service's People Fixing the World","hook":"-ing 修饰","noteZh":"交代采访出处：她正在这个节目中与主持人对话"}],"tipZh":"遇到 whose 引导的从句（whose study Liv based her poem on）时，把 whose 直接读成“她的”，顺着翻译成“丽芙把诗建立在她的研究之上”，就不会绕晕。"},{"en":"Liv's poem gave Kat goosebumps , meaning she was so emotionally affected that the hairs on her skin stood up, making tiny bumps.","zh":"丽芙的诗让凯特起了鸡皮疙瘩，意思是她在情感上深受触动，以至于皮肤上的汗毛都竖了起来，起了一层小疙瘩。","coreEn":"Liv's poem gave Kat goosebumps.","coreZh":"丽芙的诗让凯特起了鸡皮疙瘩。","cars":[{"text":", meaning she was so emotionally affected","hook":"-ing 修饰","noteZh":"承接主句解释 goosebumps 的含义：她在情绪上受到了极大触动"},{"text":"that the hairs on her skin stood up,","hook":"状语从句（so...that...）","noteZh":"描述触动之后的生理反应：皮肤上的汗毛竖起来了"},{"text":"making tiny bumps","hook":"-ing 修饰","noteZh":"补充结果画面：从而在皮肤上形成细小的凸起"}],"tipZh":"一个句子里连续挂了两节 -ing 车厢（meaning... / making...），它们分别充当“补充释义”和“自然结果”，按时间与逻辑顺序一路往下顺读即可。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Hello, this is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Neil.","zh":"大家好，欢迎收听 BBC 英语教学《六分钟英语》。我是尼尔。","s":2.56,"e":11.88,"wt":[0,554,566,588,618,648,686,724,776,798,861,883]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"And I'm Pippa. In this episode we're discussing poetry. For some, poetry is what you study at school, then forget, while for others it becomes an important part of their life. Have you ever read or written any poetry, Neil?","zh":"我是皮帕。本期节目我们要聊聊诗歌。对一些人来说，诗歌不过是上学时学过、后来就忘掉的东西；而对另一些人而言，它成了生命中重要的一部分。尼尔，你读过或者写过诗吗？","s":12,"e":27.2,"wt":[0,24,50,134,150,179,234,272,336,424,432,485,538,558,572,589,624,637,713,751,834,858,884,922,946,988,1000,1056,1096,1114,1152,1228,1238,1238,1264,1318,1355,1408,1429,1486]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, I haven't written any poetry since I was at school. I would love to read more poetry, but I just don't get it, really. I find it difficult to understand.","zh":"嗯，我从学校毕业后就没写过诗了。我倒是挺想多读点诗的，但老实说我不太能看懂，觉得很难理解。","s":27.56,"e":38.62,"wt":[0,37,44,96,148,170,215,252,259,281,296,368,376,419,454,471,506,554,614,714,732,752,788,810,842,876,883,912,926,992,1006]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"A good place to hear poetry is an open mic night , a live event where anyone is allowed to stand up on stage to sing or recite poems. Here, BBC reporter Maria recites her open mic poem about a friend who suffered a stroke, for BBC World Service programme People Fixing the World.","zh":"听诗的好去处是开放麦之夜（open mic night）——这是一种现场活动，任何人都可以走上舞台唱歌或朗诵诗歌。下面，BBC 记者玛丽亚将在 BBC 国际台《修补世界的人》（People Fixing the World）节目中，朗诵她在开放麦上写给一位中风朋友的诗。","s":38.65,"e":56.88,"wt":[0,4,37,80,97,131,182,199,216,250,275,318,349,357,388,427,466,513,533,571,579,625,640,661,705,734,771,793,837,947,993,1016,1078,1118,1171,1195,1226,1263,1281,1314,1320,1360,1380,1433,1439,1479,1499,1519,1552,1599,1659,1699,1755,1774]},{"speaker":"Maria","en":"This first poem, I wrote it about a friend who I love dearly. It's called Stroke. You're awake And trying to grasp who it is. I pull my mask past my lips and you study my face, Eyes wide. I've enough stuff to tell you, I say, Sinking into a chair. I stare. Sometimes if you go to an open mic night , people can really touch your soul with what they're saying. And even if you're having a bad day, life can feel so much better after going to an open mic night .","zh":"这第一首诗，是我写给一位我深爱的朋友的，名字叫《中风》：“你醒了 / 努力辨认来人是谁。 / 我把口罩拉过嘴唇，你端详着我的脸， / 睁大双眼。 / ‘我有好多话要告诉你，’我说， / 整个人陷进椅子里。 / 我凝望着你。”有时候去听一场开放麦之夜，台上人说的话真的能触动你的灵魂。哪怕你那天过得很糟，去完开放麦之夜后，生活也会瞬间美好很多。","s":57.12,"e":88.34,"wt":[0,21,46,70,82,112,130,150,170,192,192,222,250,328,354,370,486,548,661,673,696,716,747,762,775,803,812,831,846,871,894,905,928,945,962,1000,1033,1132,1178,1340,1340,1383,1428,1452,1478,1542,1561,1613,1692,1716,1738,1890,1967,2098,2130,2140,2152,2168,2180,2196,2218,2246,2271,2289,2324,2342,2377,2406,2430,2454,2478,2516,2558,2624,2644,2662,2672,2694,2694,2726,2756,2790,2816,2828,2844,2852,2869,2908,2940,2940,2950,2970,2992,3018,3067]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"For Maria, poetry has the power to heal emotions and touch your soul , meaning to affect you deeply on an emotional or spiritual level.","zh":"对玛丽亚而言，诗歌拥有疗愈情绪、触动灵魂（touch your soul）的力量，意思是在情感或精神层面上深深打动你。","s":88.34,"e":97.34,"wt":[0,28,80,125,144,169,200,221,251,311,333,370,400,424,445,498,522,560,592,634,651,716,746,763,841]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"In this episode, we'll hear more about the positive power of poetry and, as usual, we'll learn some useful new words and phrases. And remember, you'll find all the vocabulary, plus a quiz and worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"本期节目我们将进一步探讨诗歌的积极力量，并照例学习一些实用的新词和短语。别忘了，全部词汇以及小测验和学习单都可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 上找到。","s":97.42,"e":112.96,"wt":[0,22,54,114,148,184,224,246,286,306,330,360,464,486,518,578,606,632,660,698,726,752,778,860,882,936,969,991,1007,1023,1102,1123,1130,1159,1181,1247,1261,1283,1349]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"But first, I have a question for you, Pippa. Just now, you used the phrase the positive power of poetry, repeating the same initial 'p' sound across a string of words. Doing this is a common feature of poetry, but what's it called? Is it: a) assonance, b) alliteration, or c) simile?","zh":"不过首先，皮帕，我有个问题要考你。刚才你用了短语 the positive power of poetry，在一连串单词的开头重复了相同的“p”音。这种手法在诗歌里很常见，它叫什么？是：a) 半谐音（assonance），b) 头韵（alliteration），还是 c) 明喻（simile）？","s":112.96,"e":135.22,"wt":[0,43,86,104,118,142,202,226,242,318,350,399,418,441,462,505,525,587,630,644,725,800,826,874,900,989,1032,1038,1090,1112,1168,1268,1298,1320,1328,1332,1386,1428,1448,1512,1536,1572,1600,1672,1684,1714,1749,1866,1897,2047,2068,2154]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Hmm. Finally, my English degree might come in handy! And I'm going to say b) alliteration.","zh":"嗯，我的英语学位终于能派上用场啦！我选 b) 头韵（alliteration）。","s":135.22,"e":140.82,"wt":[0,90,141,152,192,226,255,278,289,334,354,372,372,396,424,458]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, we'll find out at the end, and I hope you don't get embarrassed, Pippa! Now, poetry helped Maria overcome sadness, but could its power to heal work in other areas too? Hot Poets is a group helping people understand the science behind global issues like climate change. They go to meetings like the UN Climate Conference and listen to scientific papers being presented. Then they write a poem about it all and perform that back to the audience. Here's Hot Poet Liv Torc, reciting her poem to BBC World Service's People Fixing the World.","zh":"我们到节目最后揭晓答案，皮帕，希望你可别翻车啊！诗歌帮玛丽亚走出了悲伤，但它的疗愈力量也能用在其他领域吗？“炽热诗人”（Hot Poets）是一个帮助公众理解气候变化等全球议题背后科学原理的团体。他们会去参加联合国气候大会之类的会议，听科学家宣读论文，然后把这些内容写成诗，再表演给观众听。下面请听“炽热诗人”丽芙·托克在 BBC 国际台《修补世界的人》节目中朗诵她的诗作。","s":140.92,"e":176.08,"wt":[0,34,69,97,118,132,153,188,209,216,240,250,252,304,318,486,519,569,610,660,726,800,825,866,891,932,948,981,1014,1030,1090,1152,1244,1270,1306,1320,1327,1363,1414,1458,1536,1555,1598,1636,1675,1724,1744,1792,1856,1874,1886,1901,1959,1982,1999,2010,2056,2112,2126,2161,2172,2231,2269,2301,2380,2410,2440,2477,2484,2514,2551,2566,2602,2624,2688,2712,2744,2760,2784,2870,2917,2941,2972,2998,3043,3106,3130,3168,3188,3218,3278,3338,3390,3430,3452]},{"speaker":"Liv Torc","en":"It starts with an adventure at sea, A big blue murder mystery, where the killer is not the whale But an epic humanity fail Of overfishing and polluting at unimaginable scale.","zh":"一切始于一场海上历险， / 一桩蔚蓝色的谋杀悬案， / 凶手并不是鲸鱼， / 而是人类史诗般的溃败—— / 规模超乎想象的过度捕捞与污染。","s":176.32,"e":189.74,"wt":[0,20,80,120,140,220,248,312,320,346,380,432,492,534,560,616,628,666,690,756,756,808,864,888,912,934,1000,1016,1074,1144,1204]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Liv's poem is based on a study into biodiversity. It's a complex research project that many find hard to understand. Here, Liv discusses this with Myra Anubi for BBC World Service programme People Fixing the World:","zh":"丽芙的诗取材于一项关于生物多样性的研究。那是一个非常复杂、许多人难以读懂的科研项目。下面听丽芙在 BBC 国际台《修补世界的人》节目中与迈拉·阿努比探讨这个问题：","s":191.78,"e":205.82,"wt":[0,38,76,96,128,128,170,206,248,398,412,427,463,506,543,564,599,624,658,675,786,828,849,912,940,968,996,1062,1078,1095,1135,1188,1252,1294,1302,1322]},{"speaker":"Myra Anubi","en":"And Liv, I mean, so what you've identified is that there is an issue around the communication of science.","zh":"丽芙，也就是说，你发现的问题在于科学传播本身存在障碍。","s":206.18,"e":212.84,"wt":[0,16,40,54,103,116,143,170,206,238,246,298,320,349,382,422,442,543,554]},{"speaker":"Liv Torc","en":"Yes, a lot of this jargon is really hard to understand, or a lot of the science is quite impenetrable to normal people, so it's instantly a turn-off .","zh":"是的，很多专业行话（jargon）实在太难懂了，或者说很多科学内容对普通人而言晦涩难懂（impenetrable），所以一下子就让人提不起兴趣（a turn-off）。","s":213.04,"e":221.68,"wt":[0,0,10,39,58,97,156,226,234,280,287,350,360,365,380,390,407,445,454,484,528,562,582,640,678,718,718,736,803]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Science uses lots of jargon – specialist or technical vocabulary which is quite different from everyday language. For example, a scientist might use the word precipitation, while in everyday English we would say rain.","zh":"科学界使用大量行话（jargon），也就是与日常用语大相径庭的专业或技术词汇。比如科学家可能会用 precipitation（降水），而在日常英语里我们直接说 rain（雨）。","s":221.68,"e":235.42,"wt":[0,34,72,92,118,148,167,230,242,298,373,404,416,447,520,543,605,690,711,752,774,828,858,872,890,926,1040,1073,1086,1159,1214,1226,1226,1268]},{"speaker":"Jargon","en":"can make scientific papers impenetrable , an adjective meaning impossible to understand. And worse, it makes science a turn-off – something that people dislike or find uninteresting.","zh":"（行话）会让科学论文变得晦涩难懂（impenetrable），这个形容词意思是无法理解。更糟的是，它还会让科学变成令人倒胃口的事物（a turn-off）——也就是人们不喜欢或觉得无趣的东西。","s":236.13,"e":247.84,"wt":[0,23,54,132,167,251,285,300,370,424,485,517,623,647,693,706,739,781,792,834,858,915,937,979,1011,1039,1061]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Hot Poets try to change all of that by making complex environmental ideas easy to understand through poetry. And it works, says Kat Bruce, the environmental scientist whose study Liv based her poem on, talking here with Myra Anubi for BBC World Service's People Fixing the World.","zh":"“炽热诗人”正试图改变这一切，通过诗歌让复杂的环保理念变得通俗易懂。而且这真的管用，环境科学家凯特·布鲁斯说道——丽芙正是根据她的研究写出了那首诗——以下是她在 BBC 国际台《修补世界的人》节目中与迈拉·阿努比的对话。","s":248.02,"e":265.76,"wt":[0,24,60,82,96,140,162,176,205,219,263,314,409,445,474,487,580,618,679,699,712,759,786,806,853,873,970,1029,1069,1109,1143,1171,1193,1223,1306,1306,1335,1365,1395,1440,1462,1484,1536,1611,1656,1714,1733]},{"speaker":"Myra Anubi","en":"What did it mean for you, Kat, hearing the poem, having actually experienced everything she's saying?","zh":"凯特，你自己亲身经历了她诗里写的一切，听到这首诗对你来说意味着什么？","s":266.02,"e":271.54,"wt":[0,10,10,48,78,96,118,153,193,210,294,328,376,387,456,485]},{"speaker":"Kat Bruce","en":"It still gives me goosebumps when I hear it now. I think it's everything that I wish I could say and put into words myself. I feel like Liv just, you know, has done that.","zh":"我现在一听还是会起鸡皮疙瘩。我觉得诗里写出了我一直想说、想用语言表达出来的一切。我觉得丽芙真的替我做到了。","s":271.57,"e":284.66,"wt":[0,12,56,79,99,191,191,233,233,275,363,374,430,474,539,617,642,691,695,775,799,831,849,881,939,965,983,1007,1027,1036,1052,1061,1077,1204,1235]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Liv's poem gave Kat goosebumps , meaning she was so emotionally affected that the hairs on her skin stood up, making tiny bumps. Maybe poetry can't fix everything, but for Kat, it's making an impact for good. Now, what was the answer to your question, Neil?","zh":"丽芙的诗让凯特起了鸡皮疙瘩（goosebumps），意思是她在情感上深受触动，以至于皮肤上的汗毛都竖了起来，起了一层小疙瘩。也许诗歌不能解决所有问题，但对凯特来说，它正带来积极而深远的影响。好了尼尔，你刚才那道题的答案是什么？","s":284.9,"e":300.82,"wt":[0,30,59,88,110,170,197,248,270,292,306,387,446,475,497,548,563,594,636,668,709,728,790,892,924,964,996,1016,1078,1094,1120,1146,1167,1201,1215,1258,1280,1339,1364,1388,1406,1424,1461,1473,1497,1546]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, you seemed very clear, Pippa, that when you use a string of words beginning with the same sound, it's called alliteration... and you're absolutely right.","zh":"皮帕，你刚才非常笃定地说，一连串单词以相同发音开头叫做头韵（alliteration）……你完全答对了！","s":301.04,"e":312.11,"wt":[0,38,61,108,139,193,245,276,307,330,353,422,422,472,520,531,572,589,621,673,714,774,928,965,1023,1074]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Phew!","zh":"呼，好险！","s":312.11,"e":312.42,"wt":[0]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we've learned, starting with open mic night , a live event where anyone is allowed to perform poems or music on stage.","zh":"好了，我们来复习今天学过的词汇，首先是 open mic night（开放麦之夜），指任何人都能上台表演诗歌或音乐的现场活动。","s":312.42,"e":323.6,"wt":[0,196,248,274,304,364,364,425,493,527,561,586,620,645,653,687,747,777,816,836,840,894,906,948,1004,1032,1066]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"If something touches your soul , it affects you deeply on an emotional or spiritual level.","zh":"如果某件事 touches your soul（触动你的灵魂），就是它在情感或精神层面上深深打动了你。","s":323.8,"e":329.46,"wt":[0,16,80,120,158,182,202,224,258,284,334,346,373,428,455,522]},{"speaker":"Jargon","en":"means special words and phrases that are used in a particular profession or subject.","zh":"（Jargon，行话）指在特定行业或学科中使用的专业词汇和短语。","s":329.95,"e":334.78,"wt":[0,33,80,113,133,180,199,227,254,261,273,340,391,420]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"The adjective impenetrable means impossible to understand.","zh":"形容词 impenetrable 意思是晦涩难懂、无法理解的。","s":334.8,"e":338.49,"wt":[0,20,80,159,192,259,272]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"A turn-off is something that people dislike or find uninteresting.","zh":"A turn-off 指人们不喜欢或觉得毫无趣味、令人倒胃口的事物。","s":338.49,"e":342.74,"wt":[0,4,61,76,143,173,218,270,284,315]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"And finally, if you get goosebumps , the hairs on your skin stand up because you're cold, emotionally affected, or afraid. Once again, our six minutes are up, but remember, you'll find lots more episodes as well as a quiz and worksheet for this one on our website, bbclearningenglish.com. See you again soon. But for now, it's goodbye.","zh":"最后，如果你 get goosebumps（起鸡皮疙瘩），就是因为寒冷、情绪触动或害怕而皮肤汗毛竖起。我们的六分钟时间又到了，别忘了在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 上还有更多往期节目，以及本期的小测验和学习单。我们很快再见，现在先说再见啦。","s":342.86,"e":363.94,"wt":[0,24,90,102,120,146,202,228,254,282,294,324,362,396,425,464,514,582,652,688,724,848,878,914,938,956,999,1017,1041,1058,1108,1158,1182,1214,1246,1308,1327,1354,1366,1383,1415,1439,1502,1522,1546,1591,1607,1631,1710,1948,1948,1960,1992,2024,2038,2056,2084,2090]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Goodbye!","zh":"再见！","s":363.94,"e":365.44,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0nq04vv.mp3"},{"epNum":26,"id":"ep-260528","date":"2026-05-28","title":"Living with debt","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260528","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260528_6_minute_english_living_with_debt_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260528_6_minute_english_living_with_debt_transcript.pdf","summary":"Have you ever borrowed money to buy something? Many people have to borrow money to make big purchases, like buying a house or a car. When you do this, you have to pay the money back over time – the money you owe is called a debt. With the current cost of living getting increasingly high, some people are having to borrow money even for everyday purchases like bills and food. Sometimes this debt can get out of control, which causes anxiety and stress. Neil and Pippa discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"According to debt support group The Money Charity, roughly how much is the average British adult in debt through credit cards, overdrafts and personal loans?","options":["a) £2,200","b) £4,200","c) £6,200"],"topic":"经济消费","titleZh":"与债务共存","summaryZh":"你曾经借钱买过东西吗？很多人在进行大额消费时都不得不借钱，比如买房或买车。借钱后，你需要分期偿还——欠下的这笔钱就叫“债务”。随着如今生活成本越来越高，有些人甚至连日常账单和食物开销都要借钱。有时债务会逐渐失控，进而引发焦虑和压力。Neil 和 Pippa 探讨了这一话题，并教你一些实用的新词汇。","questionZh":"根据债务援助机构“金钱慈善会”（The Money Charity）的数据，英国成年人通过信用卡、账户透支和个人贷款背负的债务平均大约是多少？","optionsZh":["a) 2,200英镑","b) 4,200英镑","c) 6,200英镑"],"answer":{"letter":"b","explanationZh":"Neil 在节目末尾揭晓答案：英国成年人平均通过信用卡、透支和个人贷款背负的债务实际为 4,232 英镑，因此最接近的选项是 b) 4,200英镑。"},"vocab":[{"word":"debt","defEn":"money a person has borrowed and owes to someone","pos":"n.","zh":"债务，欠款","exampleEn":"Many people avoid talking about debt, but it affects us all.","ipa":"/det/"},{"word":"pay back / pay off / clear (debt)","defEn":"give back money you have borrowed","pos":"短语","zh":"偿还；还清（债务）","exampleEn":"I'd like to think I could clear them all one day, even if it means, like, five years paying them off.","ipa":"/peɪ bæk/ · /peɪ ɒf/"},{"word":"make ends meet","defEn":"(idiom) have just enough money to pay for basic living expenses like food, bills and rent","pos":"短语","zh":"勉强维持生计，使收支相抵","exampleEn":"I was interested in learning about their lives and how they were making ends meet.","ipa":"/meɪk endz miːt/"},{"word":"in arrears","defEn":"(idiom) still owing money that should have been paid already","pos":"短语","zh":"拖欠（款项），逾期未付","exampleEn":"Ryan found that many residents were in arrears.","ipa":"/ɪn əˈrɪəz/"},{"word":"the never-never","defEn":"(informal) buying something with one large payment followed by smaller regular payments which are made until the debt is repaid","pos":"n.","zh":"（英式非正式）分期付款购物方式（仿佛永远还不完）","exampleEn":"Jason was living on the never-never, an informal phrase for buying the things you need by making regular small payments over a long time.","ipa":"/ðə ˈnevəˌnevə/"},{"word":"financial strain","defEn":"emotional stress caused by a lack of money to meet basic needs or repay debt","pos":"短语","zh":"经济压力，财务重负","exampleEn":"Over the months that I knew him, the financial strain on him and his partner increased.","ipa":"/faɪˈnænʃəl streɪn/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"count every penny","zh":"精打细算、计较每一分钱（口语常用，形容花钱非常节省、每笔账都算得很细）","contextEn":"I don't count every penny that I spend and save every single penny that I earn."},{"en":"get out of control","zh":"失控、失去控制（高频日常搭配，常用于形容债务、局势、情绪等）","contextEn":"And when debt gets out of control, it causes stress and worry."},{"en":"take out loans","zh":"申请贷款、借贷（地道的英语金融生活搭配，指从银行或机构办理贷款）","contextEn":"Sometimes you need to take out loans."},{"en":"fluctuate between ... and ...","zh":"在……和……之间摇摆不定/波动（既可形容内心态度反复，也可在雅思图表作文中形容数据波动）","contextEn":"Jason actually fluctuated between wanting to clear all of his debts on the one hand, and on the other what he described as living on the never-never..."}],"sentences":[{"en":"Debt refers to money a person has borrowed to buy something and which they have to pay back , usually to a bank, a credit card company, or another person.","zh":"债务指的是一个人为购买某物而借入、且必须偿还的钱，通常要还给银行、信用卡公司或他人。","coreEn":"Debt refers to money.","coreZh":"债务指的是钱。","cars":[{"text":"a person has borrowed to buy something","hook":"省略关系词 that 的定语从句","noteZh":"修饰前面的 money，说明是“某人为了买东西而借来的”钱"},{"text":"and which they have to pay back","hook":"并列关系词 and which","noteZh":"与前一节并列修饰 money，补充这笔钱“必须偿还”的属性"},{"text":"usually to a bank, a credit card company, or another person","hook":"逗号补充 + 介词短语 to ...","noteZh":"补充说明还钱的常见对象是谁"}],"tipZh":"遇到两个并列修饰同一名词的定语从句时，第一个常省略 that，第二个会显式加上 and which，抓住核心词 money 就不会晕。"},{"en":"My approach was to let people know that I was interested in learning about their lives and how they were making ends meet , so I did some interviews with residents, and I paid attention to where debt came up in everyday conversations.","zh":"我的做法是让人们知道我对了解他们的生活以及他们如何维持生计很感兴趣，所以我对居民做了一些访谈，并留意债务在日常对话中出现的场合。","coreEn":"My approach was to let people know ..., so I did some interviews, and I paid attention to ...","coreZh":"我的做法是让大家知道……，于是我做了一些访谈，并留意……","cars":[{"text":"that I was interested in learning about their lives and how they were making ends meet","hook":"连接词 that 引导宾语从句","noteZh":"紧跟在 know 后面，说明让居民知道的具体内容（了解他们的生活和生计）"},{"text":"with residents","hook":"介词短语 with ...","noteZh":"补充说明访谈的对象是当地居民"},{"text":"where debt came up in everyday conversations","hook":"疑问词 where 引导宾语从句","noteZh":"作 paid attention to 的宾语，点明他重点观察的对象"}],"tipZh":"英语长句常由 so 和 and 串联起三个简单主干，先按连词切成三段，再分别剥离每段动词后面的长宾语，理解速度翻倍。"},{"en":"With a regular income, debt can be managed, but for those who are unemployed or on low incomes, it can cause serious distress.","zh":"如果有稳定收入，债务尚可掌控；但对于失业或低收入人群来说，债务会带来严重的痛苦。","coreEn":"Debt can be managed, but it can cause serious distress.","coreZh":"债务可以被掌控，但它也会造成严重痛苦。","cars":[{"text":"With a regular income","hook":"句首介词短语 With ...","noteZh":"交代前半句的前提条件：“在有稳定收入的情况下”"},{"text":"for those","hook":"介词短语 for ...","noteZh":"引出后半句的适用人群：“对那些人而言”"},{"text":"who are unemployed or on low incomes","hook":"关系词 who","noteZh":"贴在 those 后面，精准界定是“失业或低收入的”人群"}],"tipZh":"雅思写作讨论社会问题时，非常推荐模仿这种 With A, ... but for those who ..., ... 的对比句型，逻辑清晰又地道。"},{"en":"Jason actually fluctuated between wanting to clear all of his debts on the one hand, and on the other what he described as living on the never-never and actually questioning the supposed moral obligation to pay his debts .","zh":"Jason 其实一直在两种心态间摇摆：一方面想还清所有债务，另一方面则过着他所谓“永远还不完的分期”生活，甚至质疑还债这一所谓的道德义务。","coreEn":"Jason fluctuated between A and B.","coreZh":"Jason 在 A 和 B 之间摇摆不定。","cars":[{"text":"wanting to clear all of his debts on the one hand","hook":"-ing 短语作介词宾语（A端）","noteZh":"说明他摇摆的一端：一方面想把债全还清"},{"text":"what he described as living on the never-never","hook":"what 引导宾语从句（B端之一）","noteZh":"说明另一端：过着他口中那种永远在分期还债的日子"},{"text":"and actually questioning the supposed moral obligation to pay his debts","hook":"并列 -ing 短语 + 不定式 to do","noteZh":"补充 B 端的另一层心态：甚至开始质疑还债的所谓道德义务"}],"tipZh":"看到 between ... and ... 结构时，直接找两端的核心动作（wanting vs. living / questioning），中间再长的修饰都不会打乱节奏。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Hello, this is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Neil.","zh":"大家好，欢迎收听 BBC 英语教学的《六分钟英语》。我是 Neil。","s":2.54,"e":12.26,"wt":[0,582,594,618,646,674,714,758,820,857,898,925]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"And I'm Pippa. Are you good at saving money, Neil? Or do you like to spend it?","zh":"我是 Pippa。Neil，你擅长存钱吗？还是更喜欢花钱？","s":12.36,"e":17.76,"wt":[0,29,50,108,132,156,188,204,252,308,362,378,388,415,444,469,498]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, actually, a bit of both. I like to spend money on nice things, but I also try to save, mainly because I've got children. How about you?","zh":"其实两者都有一点吧。我喜欢花钱买好东西，但也会努力存钱，主要是因为我有孩子。你呢？","s":18,"e":28.66,"wt":[0,44,125,133,157,173,232,298,298,302,352,402,474,474,548,574,590,633,673,684,716,818,822,853,878,958,976,986]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Yeah, I'm the same. I like to save money, or I try to, for the future. But I also do spend it. I don't count every penny that I spend and save every single penny that I earn.","zh":"嗯，我也一样。我喜欢为未来存钱，或者说尽量去存。但我确实也会花钱。我不会花每一分钱都精打细算，也不会把赚来的每一分钱都存起来。","s":28.88,"e":41.8,"wt":[0,37,55,76,128,148,186,202,246,302,302,334,368,388,400,442,596,618,636,672,690,734,784,791,828,888,945,974,1004,1011,1049,1072,1101,1140,1188,1224,1252,1261]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Mmm. Well, whether you're a saver or a spender, being in debt is common in the UK. Debt refers to money a person has borrowed to buy something and which they have to pay back , usually to a bank, a credit card company, or another person.","zh":"嗯。不管你是爱存钱还是爱花钱，背负债务在英国都很常见。Debt（债务）指的是一个人为买东西而借入、并且必须偿还的钱，通常是还给银行、信用卡公司或其他人。","s":41.8,"e":58.4,"wt":[0,28,128,134,184,196,269,281,290,368,412,429,464,481,534,551,577,668,728,756,778,778,834,834,874,949,958,980,1035,1061,1098,1126,1142,1162,1188,1213,1235,1284,1294,1308,1351,1358,1401,1430,1518,1536,1586]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Many people avoid talking about debt , but it affects us all. In 2025, over 1000 people contacted the UK Citizens Advice Bureau every single day of the year for help with their debt . And when debt gets out of control, it causes stress and worry. BBC Radio 4 programme Thinking Allowed interviewed one young man, Jason, about his debt .","zh":"很多人避谈债务，但它影响着我们每一个人。2025年，全年每一天都有超过1000人联系英国公民咨询局寻求债务帮助。一旦债务失控，就会带来压力和焦虑。BBC广播四台节目《畅所欲言》（Thinking Allowed）就债务问题采访了一位名叫 Jason 的年轻人。","s":58.7,"e":82.92,"wt":[0,42,78,129,184,224,251,274,294,310,358,386,468,544,602,623,714,762,780,804,872,928,968,1012,1062,1108,1130,1144,1166,1212,1228,1254,1270,1278,1321,1378,1394,1416,1440,1446,1458,1520,1570,1604,1642,1676,1702,1814,1834,1868,1888,1962,2016,2063,2132,2154,2186,2225,2272,2314,2344,2378]},{"speaker":"Jason","en":"Whatever jobs there are aren't enough to provide for a family. Sometimes you need to take out loans. I've done it a few times, but you can't ever pay it back . You can't see a way out, other than winning the lottery or something. I'd like to think I could clear them all one day, even if it means, like, five years paying them off .","zh":"不管有什么工作，赚的钱都不够养家糊口。有时候你不得不去贷款。我借过几次，但根本还不上。除了中彩票之类的奇迹，根本看不到出路。我倒希望有一天能把债全还清，哪怕那意味着要花上五年时间去慢慢还清。","s":82.92,"e":97.68,"wt":[0,28,54,72,82,103,125,136,172,180,185,230,275,290,317,323,345,362,407,424,434,440,452,474,510,529,546,574,596,613,624,641,663,680,716,732,733,745,758,780,798,830,843,875,884,944,960,983,994,1023,1028,1057,1100,1114,1130,1152,1191,1217,1230,1243,1276,1302,1328,1361,1401,1420,1441]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Jason uses three phrases, pay back , pay off , and clear debt , all of which mean the same thing – to give back the money you've borrowed.","zh":"Jason 用了三个短语：pay back、pay off 和 clear debt，它们的意思完全相同——都是把借来的钱还回去。","s":97.68,"e":108.46,"wt":[0,54,97,151,300,317,343,366,391,435,480,526,562,608,648,668,686,722,738,758,788,789,789,805,837,871,917,951,1003]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"In this episode, we'll hear more about living with debt by learning some useful new words and phrases. And remember, you'll find all the vocabulary used, plus a quiz and worksheet, on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"在本期节目中，我们将深入了解与债务共存的现实，同时学习一些实用的新词和短语。别忘了，你可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 上找到本期所有词汇，还有测验和学习单。","s":108.66,"e":122.66,"wt":[0,15,42,103,136,163,190,230,272,295,326,341,403,434,476,504,532,560,646,664,711,739,758,780,797,875,921,950,959,990,1013,1083,1098,1121,1190]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"But first, I have a question for you, Pippa. According to debt support group The Money Charity, roughly how much is the average British adult in debt through credit cards, overdrafts and personal loans? Is it: a) £2,200, b) £4,200, or c) £6,200?","zh":"不过首先，Pippa，我有个问题考考你。根据债务援助机构“金钱慈善会”的数据，英国成年人通过信用卡、账户透支和个人贷款背负的债务平均大约是多少？是 a) 2,200英镑，b) 4,200英镑，还是 c) 6,200英镑？","s":122.92,"e":144.8,"wt":[0,25,72,81,98,116,164,182,182,284,342,361,389,438,473,494,542,616,682,710,747,766,794,860,926,973,991,1029,1094,1151,1232,1326,1357,1394,1476,1490,1540,1580,1812,1832,2064,2088,2109]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Hmm. I'm not sure. I'll say £2,200.","zh":"嗯，我不太确定。我猜 2,200 英镑吧。","s":146.08,"e":149.24,"wt":[0,35,60,86,145,177,205]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK. Well, we will find out the answer later in the programme. Jason's story features in a new book by sociologist Ryan Davey. Ryan spent months living in a low-income housing estate, which he gave the fictional name Woldham. He talked with residents and listened to their money worries. Here, Ryan explains more to BBC Radio 4's Thinking Allowed:","zh":"好的，我们稍后在节目中揭晓答案。Jason 的故事被收录在社会学家 Ryan Davey 的一本新书里。Ryan 曾在一个低收入住宅区住了几个月，他给那里起了个化名叫“沃尔德姆”（Woldham）。他与当地居民交谈，倾听他们对钱的担忧。下面来听听 Ryan 在 BBC 广播四台《畅所欲言》中的进一步介绍：","s":150.24,"e":173.22,"wt":[0,34,76,90,118,146,167,188,231,267,281,302,396,449,489,557,570,579,607,644,662,758,798,880,908,943,986,1029,1043,1060,1124,1174,1229,1270,1286,1318,1342,1394,1444,1540,1551,1578,1596,1637,1649,1693,1701,1733,1765,1832,1888,1926,2002,2040,2059,2087,2134,2184,2231]},{"speaker":"Ryan Davey","en":"My approach was to let people know that I was interested in learning about their lives and how they were making ends meet , so I did some interviews with residents, and I paid attention to where debt came up in everyday conversations. For many people in Woldham, Jason included, debt was an ordinary feature of daily life. And beyond that, being in arrears – so, being behind with one or more monthly payment commitments – was part of daily life.","zh":"我的做法是让居民知道，我想了解他们的生活以及他们如何勉强维持生计，于是我对他们做了一些访谈，并留意债务在日常对话中出现的时刻。对沃尔德姆的许多人来说，包括 Jason 在内，债务是日常生活中再平常不过的一部分。不仅如此，拖欠账单——也就是有一项或多项每月固定还款逾期未付——也成了家常便饭。","s":173.48,"e":198.58,"wt":[0,10,53,69,79,95,127,148,169,174,190,244,254,297,324,351,389,402,420,436,458,486,520,539,556,566,576,592,630,634,664,782,800,805,834,884,897,930,956,982,995,1008,1061,1172,1189,1212,1247,1258,1305,1338,1401,1500,1562,1616,1684,1718,1738,1778,1806,1834,1874,1874,1904,1923,1966,1992,2000,2030,2066,2097,2120,2135,2166,2220,2274,2327,2350,2370,2388,2410,2458]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"The people of Woldham were making ends meet . To make ends meet means having just enough money to pay for basic living expenses like food, bills and rent.","zh":"沃尔德姆的居民都在勉强维持生计（making ends meet）。To make ends meet 指手头的钱刚好只够支付食物、水电账单和房租等基本生活开销。","s":198.68,"e":209.46,"wt":[0,22,62,88,142,176,228,262,290,328,342,371,400,429,465,508,535,594,628,645,671,697,741,786,846,886,932,974,976]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Ryan found that many residents were in arrears , a phrase meaning to still owe money that should have already been repaid. For most residents, debt was a normal part of daily life.","zh":"Ryan 发现许多居民都处于拖欠状态（in arrears），这个短语的意思是仍然欠着本应早已还清的钱。对大多数居民来说，欠债就是日常生活里的常态。","s":209.64,"e":221.66,"wt":[0,20,55,83,111,170,196,212,255,280,287,330,380,394,429,449,485,524,557,586,644,674,780,802,831,910,939,961,968,1011,1044,1056,1126]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"With a regular income, debt can be managed, but for those who are unemployed or on low incomes, it can cause serious distress. Here, Ryan discusses how the residents he met felt about their debt with BBC Radio 4's Thinking Allowed:","zh":"如果有稳定收入，债务尚可掌控，但对失业或低收入人群而言，债务会引发严重的痛苦。接下来，Ryan 在 BBC 广播四台《畅所欲言》节目中谈到了他遇到的居民对自身债务的感受：","s":221.86,"e":237.38,"wt":[0,24,39,92,152,182,204,219,287,309,331,369,391,413,489,504,519,562,620,635,658,696,749,894,942,968,977,1000,1023,1091,1114,1146,1191,1220,1250,1284,1310,1341,1379,1417,1474]},{"speaker":"Ryan Davey","en":"Jason actually fluctuated between wanting to clear all of his debts on the one hand, and on the other what he described as living on the never-never and actually questioning the supposed moral obligation to pay his debts . Over the months that I knew him, the financial strain on him and his partner increased. They missed some of their bills. Their internet was disconnected.","zh":"Jason 其实一直在两种心态间摇摆：一方面想还清所有债务，另一方面则过着他所谓“永远还不完的分期”生活，甚至开始质疑还债这一所谓的道德义务。在我认识他的那几个月里，他和伴侣承受的经济压力越来越大。他们漏交了一些账单，家里的网络也被断掉了。","s":237.53,"e":262.42,"wt":[0,31,95,175,231,287,303,343,367,381,405,447,463,483,509,550,596,605,626,731,731,781,784,798,841,855,919,969,990,1047,1145,1163,1229,1270,1307,1323,1347,1371,1424,1485,1499,1517,1525,1537,1561,1625,1671,1785,1836,1868,1881,1901,1921,1941,1989,2071,2098,2139,2166,2179,2285,2285,2294,2365,2381]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Jason was living on the never-never , an informal phrase for buying the things you need by making regular small payments over a long time. It's called the never-never because it seems the debt will never be repaid.","zh":"Jason 一直靠“分期赊购”（on the never-never）过日子，这是一个非正式表达，指通过长期定期支付小额款项来购买生活所需。之所以叫 the never-never，是因为这笔债看起来永远也还不完。","s":262.53,"e":276.18,"wt":[0,28,50,95,110,195,217,228,243,303,348,370,415,437,482,504,533,559,605,658,703,775,803,819,856,921,943,971,1004,1048,1085,1095,1127,1146,1174,1211,1258,1276]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Jason experienced financial strain – emotional stress caused by a lack of money to meet his basic needs or to repay his debt . Debt is a serious issue. It affects many people and there are support groups who can help if you need it. OK. Neil, what was the answer to your question?","zh":"Jason 承受着经济压力（financial strain）——也就是因缺钱满足基本生活需求或偿还债务而产生的情绪压力。债务是个严肃的问题，它影响着许多人，如果你需要帮助，也有专门的援助机构可以伸出援手。好了 Neil，你刚才那个问题的答案是什么？","s":276.26,"e":294.5,"wt":[0,35,135,217,268,302,363,404,445,458,464,491,504,536,551,586,612,657,712,734,756,796,820,861,916,944,956,968,1016,1074,1086,1128,1154,1194,1222,1246,1265,1308,1334,1346,1368,1390,1402,1420,1420,1503,1542,1581,1607,1626,1645,1694,1718,1740]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"I asked what the average amount of debt a British adult has through credit cards, overdrafts and personal loans.","zh":"我问的是，英国成年人通过信用卡、账户透支和个人贷款背负的债务平均值是多少。","s":294.57,"e":302.68,"wt":[0,5,44,77,102,160,210,226,259,267,325,366,391,449,499,556,657,676,741]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"I said it was around £2,200.","zh":"我猜的是大约 2,200 英镑。","s":302.85,"e":305.7,"wt":[0,5,45,65,93,109]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"I'm afraid that's not the right answer. In fact, it's £4,232. OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we've learned, starting with debt – money a person has borrowed and needs to give back.","zh":"恐怕没答对。实际上是 4,232 英镑。好了，我们来复习一下本期学到的词汇，首先是 debt（债务）——指一个人借来且需要归还的钱。","s":305.97,"e":320.32,"wt":[0,25,65,109,127,143,189,233,255,287,327,691,723,756,790,810,879,912,949,991,1037,1070,1099,1132,1138,1179,1191,1247,1271,1321,1337,1367]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"The phrases to pay back , to pay off , and to clear a debt all mean to give back money you have borrowed.","zh":"短语 to pay back、to pay off 和 to clear a debt 都是指把借来的钱还清。","s":320.62,"e":327.16,"wt":[0,20,54,92,116,138,156,184,212,226,241,262,285,322,329,366,388,430,443,470,503,531,553,582]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"If someone is making ends meet , they have just enough money to pay for basic living expenses. And if they are in arrears , they still owe money that should have been repaid already.","zh":"如果某人正在 making ends meet，意思是他的钱刚好只够应付基本生活开销。如果他们处于 in arrears 的状态，则表示仍然拖欠着本该早已还清的钱。","s":327.28,"e":338.62,"wt":[0,16,52,78,121,149,172,191,219,247,275,318,354,368,389,410,446,500,620,632,648,664,690,706,749,774,802,838,859,895,923,923,932,964,1018]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"If you buy something on the never-never , you buy it by making small regular payments over a long period.","zh":"如果你用 on the never-never 的方式买东西，就是通过很长一段时间内定期支付小额分期款来购买。","s":338.76,"e":345.34,"wt":[0,13,33,53,108,127,185,218,234,254,274,287,300,341,375,423,488,521,527,573]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"And finally, financial strain is emotional stress caused by a lack of money to meet your basic needs.","zh":"最后，financial strain（经济压力）指因为缺钱满足基本需求而带来的情绪压力。","s":345.62,"e":351.88,"wt":[0,14,74,132,164,187,247,287,327,340,346,373,386,419,432,459,504,554]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Once again, our six minutes are up, but if you'd like to know how debt is spelled and all the other words from this episode, visit our website, bbclearningenglish.com, to find a full vocabulary list.","zh":"六分钟又到了，如果你想了解 debt 以及本期其他所有词汇的拼写，请访问我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 查看完整词汇表。","s":352.08,"e":364.5,"wt":[0,31,68,92,118,146,160,192,208,220,222,238,254,282,312,370,402,484,496,514,529,565,600,630,659,724,760,781,832,1003,1017,1054,1064,1108,1190]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"See you again soon. But for now, it's goodbye.","zh":"我们很快再见。今天就到这里，再见。","s":364.7,"e":366.88,"wt":[0,14,32,64,96,112,136,162,182]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Bye!","zh":"拜拜！","s":367.14,"e":367.42,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0nnpnyn.mp3"},{"epNum":27,"id":"ep-260521","date":"2026-05-21","title":"Making cities feel quieter","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260521","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260521_6_minute_english_making_cities_feel_quieter_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260521_6_minute_english_making_cities_feel_quieter_transcript.pdf","summary":"What do you prefer – the sounds of birdsong and wind rustling in the trees, or the hum of traffic and chatter of people passing by? Some people like the quiet of the countryside and others love the noise of the city, but did you know that constant exposure to loud noise has been found to increase anxiety? That's why some people have been trying to find ways to make our cities feel quieter. Neil and Georgie discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"Sound is measured in units called decibels, but how many decibels is the sound of a voice whispering?","options":["a) 3 decibels","b) 13 decibels","c) 30 decibels"],"topic":"职场生活","titleZh":"如何让城市感觉更安静","summaryZh":"你更喜欢哪一种声音——是清脆的鸟鸣和微风拂过树叶的沙沙声，还是车水马龙的轰鸣与路人的嘈杂交谈？有人偏爱乡村的宁静，有人喜欢都市的喧嚣，但你知道吗？长期暴露在高分贝噪音中已被发现会加剧焦虑。正因如此，人们一直在探索让城市感觉更安静的方法。Neil 和 Georgie 围绕这一话题展开讨论，并教你一些实用的新词汇。","questionZh":"声音的计量单位是分贝，那么人低声耳语的声音大约是多少分贝？","optionsZh":["a) 3分贝","b) 13分贝","c) 30分贝"],"answer":{"letter":"c","explanationZh":"节目最后 Neil 公布答案：Georgie 猜的 13 分贝并不正确，低声耳语的实际音量大约是 30 分贝，所以选 c。"},"vocab":[{"word":"decibel","defEn":"unit for measuring sound","pos":"n.","zh":"分贝（声音响度单位）","exampleEn":"Sound is measured in units called decibels.","ipa":"/ˈdesɪbel/"},{"word":"dress loud","defEn":"wear clothes with bright colours, large patterns and bold designs","pos":"短语","zh":"穿着鲜艳张扬、花哨抢眼","exampleEn":"We dress loud, we talk loud, we celebrate loud, we watch movies loud.","ipa":"/dres laʊd/"},{"word":"handle (a problem)","defEn":"deal with something difficult","pos":"v.","zh":"应付，处理，承受（难题或状况）","exampleEn":"When I got back, I realised I couldn't handle it anymore.","ipa":"/ˈhændəl/"},{"word":"art installation","defEn":"artwork designed to create an interactive experience in a specific space","pos":"短语","zh":"艺术装置","exampleEn":"Charles and his team set up an immersive sound art installation in the form of speakers planted in flower beds.","ipa":"/ɑːt ˌɪnstəˈleɪʃən/"},{"word":"squeaky","defEn":"making a high-pitched sound","pos":"adj.","zh":"发出尖细吱吱声的","exampleEn":"When you walk into the sand firmly, the sand makes some squeaky sounds.","ipa":"/ˈskwiːki/"},{"word":"counterintuitive","defEn":"happening differently from what you would expect","pos":"adj.","zh":"反直觉的，与常理相悖的","exampleEn":"So, it's counterintuitive, but you can make a park less noisy by adding some calmer sounds.","ipa":"/ˌkaʊntərɪnˈtjuːɪtɪv/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"stressed out","zh":"压力山大、极度焦躁（高频日常口语，比单纯说 stressed 程度更重）","contextEn":"I felt quite stressed out actually."},{"en":"be linked to","zh":"与……有关联、被证实与……相关（雅思阅读与大作文高频学术搭配）","contextEn":"Exposure to loud noise has been linked to stress and anxiety."},{"en":"get used to","zh":"习惯于、适应（后接名词或动名词 -ing，如 gotten used to lower levels of noise）","contextEn":"I'd gotten used to lower levels of noise."},{"en":"in the form of","zh":"以……的形式（用于具体说明某事物呈现的形态，口语和书面都很实用）","contextEn":"Charles and his team set up an immersive sound art installation in the form of speakers planted in flower beds."}],"sentences":[{"en":"Yes, whether it's the rattle of train tracks, honking car horns, or the roar of aeroplanes overhead, modern cities are full of noise and this can have serious effects.","zh":"是的，无论是铁轨的轰隆声、刺耳的汽车喇叭声，还是头顶飞机的呼啸声，现代城市充满了噪音，而这会带来严重的后果。","coreEn":"Modern cities are full of noise and this can have serious effects.","coreZh":"现代城市充满噪音，这会带来严重影响。","cars":[{"text":"whether it's the rattle of train tracks, honking car horns, or the roar of aeroplanes overhead","hook":"状语从句 whether ... or ...","noteZh":"列举三种典型的城市噪音，给后文的主句做生动铺垫"}],"tipZh":"雅思口语和写作举例子时，用 whether it's A, B, or C 放在句首，能瞬间把普通陈述句升级成画面感极强的地道长句。"},{"en":"The World Health Organisation reports that by 2050, around 2.5 billion people will experience hearing loss, and exposure to loud noise has been linked to stress and anxiety.","zh":"世界卫生组织报告称，到2050年，全球将有约25亿人出现听力受损，且暴露在强噪音环境中已被证实与压力和焦虑有关。","coreEn":"The World Health Organisation reports that ...","coreZh":"世界卫生组织报告称……","cars":[{"text":"by 2050","hook":"介词短语 by + 年份","noteZh":"限定时间节点：“到2050年之前”"},{"text":"around 2.5 billion people will experience hearing loss","hook":"宾语从句第一部分","noteZh":"报告的第一个数据结论：约25亿人将面临听力受损"},{"text":"and exposure to loud noise has been linked to stress and anxiety","hook":"并列分句 + 被动语态 has been linked to","noteZh":"补充噪音带来的心理健康影响：与压力和焦虑相关"}],"tipZh":"雅思听力与阅读极爱考组织机构（WHO）的数据预测句，抓住主语（2.5 billion people）和核心因果词（has been linked to）就能迅速定位考点。"},{"en":"Let's start in one of the noisiest cities in the world – Mumbai in India, where daytime noise levels regularly hit 80 decibels , which is over 20 decibels higher than World Health Organisation recommendations.","zh":"我们先从世界上最喧闹的城市之一——印度孟买说起，那里白天的噪音水平经常达到80分贝，比世界卫生组织的建议标准高出20多分贝。","coreEn":"Let's start in one of the noisiest cities in the world – Mumbai in India.","coreZh":"我们先从世界最吵的城市之一印度孟买讲起。","cars":[{"text":"where daytime noise levels regularly hit 80 decibels","hook":"关系词 where","noteZh":"补充孟买当地的噪音实况：白天经常达到80分贝"},{"text":"which is over 20 decibels higher than World Health Organisation recommendations","hook":"关系词 which（套娃式非限制性定语从句）","noteZh":"对前面的 80 decibels 做进一步对比说明，指出超标幅度"}],"tipZh":"这种“逗号 + where ...，逗号 + which ...”的连续两节挂钩在英媒报道和雅思阅读中非常常见，按顺序顺着往下翻就最自然。"},{"en":"Inside the small park, surrounded by busy roads, Charles and his team set up an immersive sound art installation in the form of speakers planted in flower beds.","zh":"在这座被繁忙马路环绕的小公园里，Charles 和他的团队搭建了一套沉浸式声音艺术装置，具体形式是藏在花坛里的扬声器。","coreEn":"Charles and his team set up an immersive sound art installation.","coreZh":"Charles 和他的团队搭建了一套沉浸式声音艺术装置。","cars":[{"text":"Inside the small park","hook":"介词短语 Inside ...","noteZh":"交代地点：“在这座小公园内部”"},{"text":"surrounded by busy roads","hook":"-ed 修饰（过去分词短语）","noteZh":"补充说明小公园的周边环境：“被繁忙道路包围着”"},{"text":"in the form of speakers","hook":"介词短语 in the form of ...","noteZh":"解释这套艺术装置的具体呈现形式是扬声器"},{"text":"planted in flower beds","hook":"-ed 修饰（过去分词短语）","noteZh":"紧贴 speakers 后面，说明扬声器被“安放在花坛里”"}],"tipZh":"一个句子里出现两个 -ed 短语（surrounded by / planted in）时，它们都表示被动状态，分别就近修饰前面的名词 park 和 speakers。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Hello. This is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Neil.","zh":"大家好，欢迎收听 BBC 英语教学的《六分钟英语》。我是 Neil。","s":8.02,"e":12.06,"wt":[0,29,50,71,92,132,172,211,232,288,350,372]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"And I'm Georgie. Neil, I'm going to play you two sound recordings, and I want you to tell me how you feel when you hear them.","zh":"我是 Georgie。Neil，我马上给你放两段录音，我想让你说说听完后的感受。","s":12.26,"e":19.2,"wt":[0,20,44,126,145,164,196,210,237,257,277,310,382,403,410,438,459,473,501,515,538,558,610,632,647,668]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK.","zh":"好的。","s":19.44,"e":20.34,"wt":[0]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"First this... And now this.","zh":"先听这段……再听这段。","s":21.14,"e":27.6,"wt":[0,134,348,532,566]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Oh, well, that first clip made me feel really relaxed. But the second one – oh, it's horrible! I felt quite stressed out actually.","zh":"哦，第一段听起来让我觉得特别放松。但第二段——天哪，太难受了！我其实觉得挺焦躁的。","s":31.97,"e":40.28,"wt":[0,11,60,93,134,167,185,216,249,299,411,411,455,457,481,507,523,554,624,627,651,684,736,755]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Oh no! Well, this little experiment shows how much we are affected by the noise around us. And that's a problem if you're one of the four billion people around the world who live in cities.","zh":"天呐！这个小实验说明，我们受周围噪音的影响有多大。如果你是全球40亿城市居民中的一员，这就是个大问题了。","s":40.36,"e":50.56,"wt":[0,20,88,129,148,192,238,276,300,322,332,360,402,416,436,474,504,554,564,580,603,630,646,670,678,690,718,734,770,801,832,847,873,900,929,942]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Yes, whether it's the rattle of train tracks, honking car horns, or the roar of aeroplanes overhead, modern cities are full of noise and this can have serious effects. The World Health Organisation reports that by 2050, around 2.5 billion people will experience hearing loss, and exposure to loud noise has been linked to stress and anxiety.","zh":"没错，无论是铁轨的轰隆声、刺耳的汽车喇叭声，还是头顶飞机的呼啸声，现代城市充满了噪音，而这会带来严重的后果。世界卫生组织报告称，到2050年，全球将有约25亿人出现听力受损，且长期暴露在强噪音中已被证实与压力和焦虑有关。","s":50.66,"e":73,"wt":[0,32,78,108,124,160,182,220,283,337,360,398,413,436,467,482,572,640,687,734,757,788,803,842,865,896,919,950,1005,1100,1113,1145,1183,1259,1303,1324,1340,1416,1454,1511,1555,1593,1618,1700,1758,1818,1854,1908,1925,1954,1991,2013,2042,2042,2090,2126,2158]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"In this episode, we'll be hearing about ways to make our cities quieter, more relaxing places, using some useful new words and phrases. And remember, you'll find all the vocabulary along with a quiz and worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"在本期节目中，我们将探讨如何让城市变得更安静、更令人放松，同时学习一些实用的新词和短语。别忘了，你可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 上找到全部词汇以及测验和学习单。","s":73.14,"e":88.48,"wt":[0,11,34,76,96,127,172,204,230,243,266,287,326,383,409,466,583,606,633,672,691,724,743,789,808,860,898,924,943,962,1038,1075,1094,1108,1142,1167,1240,1258,1285,1314]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"But now I have a question for you, Georgie. Sound is measured in units called decibels . But how many decibels would it be if I whispered? A whisper is speaking like this. Is it: a) 3 decibels , b) 13 decibels , or c) 30 decibels ?","zh":"不过现在我有个问题考考你，Georgie。声音是用“分贝”（decibels）这个单位来衡量的。那如果我低声耳语，音量是多少分贝呢？耳语就是像这样小声说话。是 a) 3分贝，b) 13分贝，还是 c) 30分贝？","s":88.8,"e":107.13,"wt":[0,24,54,62,86,103,154,176,196,288,322,349,384,414,444,472,519,558,586,608,640,714,714,728,802,822,837,922,931,997,1015,1088,1114,1204,1220,1248,1282,1321,1406,1452,1526,1535,1589,1618,1639,1718,1722,1783]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Oh, that's really hard. I'll go for b) 13 decibels .","zh":"哦，这好难猜啊。我选 b) 13分贝吧。","s":107.13,"e":111.41,"wt":[0,13,68,103,165,169,187,207,280,336,386]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, we'll find out the answer at the end of the programme. Let's start in one of the noisiest cities in the world – Mumbai in India, where daytime noise levels regularly hit 80 decibels , which is over 20 decibels higher than World Health Organisation recommendations. Listen to Mumbai native Chhavi Sachdev share her experiences of living in this noisy city with BBC World Service programme People Fixing the World.","zh":"好，我们节目最后揭晓答案。先从世界上最喧闹的城市之一——印度孟买说起，那里白天的噪音水平经常达到80分贝，比世界卫生组织的建议标准高出20多分贝。来听听土生土长的孟买人 Chhavi Sachdev 在 BBC 国际台《改变世界的人》（People Fixing the World）节目中分享她在这座喧嚣城市的生活经历。","s":111.41,"e":138.96,"wt":[0,42,84,118,143,168,207,221,239,239,245,279,413,453,488,502,523,537,557,612,645,655,685,705,719,777,793,841,876,940,968,1013,1080,1102,1147,1195,1220,1258,1273,1303,1348,1407,1452,1482,1519,1588,1679,1841,1861,1879,1936,1963,2001,2062,2096,2116,2190,2203,2243,2256,2283,2327,2403,2424,2446,2482,2533,2598,2641,2684,2705]},{"speaker":"Chhavi Sachdev","en":"I mean, India in general is just really loud. We are a loud people. We dress loud , we talk loud, we celebrate loud, we watch movies loud. When people ask me what I miss about living abroad, I tell them it's the quiet, because after 12 years of living in the US and Europe, when I got back, I realised I couldn't handle it anymore. I'd gotten used to lower levels of noise.","zh":"我是说，印度总体上真的非常热闹喧嚣。我们就是一群大嗓门、爱张扬的人。我们穿着鲜艳抢眼，说话大声，庆祝活动很热闹，看电影声音也开得很大。当别人问我怀念国外生活的哪一点时，我告诉他们是“安静”。因为在美国和欧洲生活了12年之后，我一回到印度就发现自己再也受不了这种吵闹了。我已经习惯了更低分贝的生活环境。","s":139.01,"e":160.18,"wt":[0,4,48,86,101,151,170,201,247,309,321,345,359,389,455,468,501,520,537,553,581,603,631,685,719,747,769,813,871,889,911,931,945,961,983,1012,1040,1076,1122,1127,1150,1173,1195,1212,1241,1304,1328,1367,1400,1413,1452,1465,1484,1497,1516,1568,1594,1600,1619,1645,1711,1711,1761,1761,1816,1827,1869,1915,1943,1969,1993,2028,2053,2067]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Chhavi says, \"Indians talk loud, celebrate loud and dress loud .\" To dress loud means to wear clothes with bright colours and large, bold patterns.","zh":"Chhavi 说：“印度人说话大声、庆祝热闹，穿着也张扬（dress loud）。”To dress loud 的意思是穿颜色鲜艳、图案硕大醒目的衣服。","s":160.28,"e":169.14,"wt":[0,37,67,119,149,194,262,292,314,351,370,396,411,448,478,515,529,578,619,648,691,730,754,788,828]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"When Chhavi returned to India after living overseas, she says she couldn't handle the noise. If you can't handle something, you find it difficult to deal or cope with.","zh":"Chhavi 在海外生活多年回到印度后，她说自己受不了（couldn't handle）那种噪音。如果你 can't handle 某件事，就是觉得很难应付或承受它。","s":169.34,"e":179.1,"wt":[0,24,62,110,125,156,187,225,288,307,332,351,401,438,476,560,574,588,632,672,728,746,746,800,814,854,890,906,942]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"So, what can be done to tackle noise in cities like Mumbai? One interesting approach involves using sound itself to make things feel quieter. Soundscaping is the idea of adding certain sounds to busy public spaces so that they sound and feel more relaxing.","zh":"那么，对于像孟买这样城市的噪音问题，能采取什么措施呢？一个有趣的方法是“以声治噪”，利用声音本身让人觉得更安静。“声景设计”（Soundscaping）的理念就是在繁忙的公共空间里加入特定的声音，让那里听起来和感觉起来都更加放松。","s":179.3,"e":195.48,"wt":[0,13,44,60,80,102,128,168,202,229,268,288,378,407,460,486,540,576,604,660,704,730,746,804,884,956,968,996,1030,1045,1086,1133,1168,1187,1214,1255,1296,1309,1336,1370,1414,1440,1477,1524]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Sound artist Charles Montambault lives near the Parc des Madelinots, a busy city park in Montreal, Canada. Charles was inspired to recreate the ocean sounds he heard on holiday in the Magdalen Islands in his city park back home. On BBC World Service programme People Fixing the World, he tells reporter Natasha Fernandes that these sounds included ocean waves and some unusual-sounding sand.","zh":"声音艺术家 Charles Montambault 住在加拿大蒙特利尔一座繁忙的城市公园——马德兰人公园附近。他突发奇想，打算在家门口的这座城市公园里重现自己去马格达伦群岛度假时听到的海洋之声。在 BBC 国际台《改变世界的人》节目中，他向记者 Natasha Fernandes 介绍说，这些声音包括海浪声，还有一种会发出奇特声响的沙子。","s":195.74,"e":220.44,"wt":[0,28,80,126,188,224,224,240,280,313,404,420,448,476,514,530,618,698,698,752,768,826,868,886,924,958,972,994,1014,1056,1068,1086,1152,1215,1235,1266,1306,1336,1366,1462,1478,1497,1529,1573,1630,1664,1692,1710,1760,1780,1812,1858,1908,1987,2006,2044,2094,2116,2162,2236,2252,2306,2410]},{"speaker":"Charles Montambault","en":"The sand on the Magdalen Islands can sing. When you walk into the sand firmly, the sand makes some squeaky sounds, so that's one sound that is kind of funny, kind of special.","zh":"马格达伦群岛的沙子会“唱歌”。当你用力踩进沙子里时，沙子会发出吱吱的尖细声响，这种声音既有点滑稽，又非常特别。","s":220.7,"e":232.14,"wt":[0,21,57,79,104,166,208,245,278,311,336,369,402,432,468,534,569,611,639,669,720,766,790,820,854,880,902,938,956,987,1024,1024,1078]},{"speaker":"Natasha Fernandes","en":"Inside the small park, surrounded by busy roads, Charles and his team set up an immersive sound art installation in the form of speakers planted in flower beds.","zh":"在这座被繁忙马路环绕的小公园里，Charles 和他的团队搭建了一套沉浸式声音艺术装置，具体形式是藏在花坛里的扬声器。","s":232.37,"e":241.56,"wt":[0,32,52,86,113,182,195,222,269,317,337,357,384,404,417,430,507,534,553,630,644,665,693,708,766,817,831,873]},{"speaker":"Charles Montambault","en":"So, the art installation was really to make this park a little quieter by adding some quiet noises. So, it's counterintuitive , but you can make a park less noisy by adding some calmer sounds.","zh":"所以，这个艺术装置的目的其实就是通过加入一些轻柔的声响，让公园显得更安静一点。这听起来虽然反直觉（counterintuitive），但添加一些更平和的声音，确实能让公园没那么吵闹。","s":241.73,"e":258.46,"wt":[0,12,25,37,133,163,191,209,215,235,257,273,313,427,467,497,552,609,721,789,843,957,995,1033,1061,1101,1160,1172,1223,1273,1338,1363,1440,1512,1567]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"The sand on the Magdalen Islands is squeaky – it makes a high-pitched sound called a squeak when stepped on. Charles played recordings of this sand, ocean waves and other relaxing sounds through speakers hidden in the park. He created an art installation – a work of art designed to give people an interactive experience.","zh":"马格达伦群岛的沙子是会发出吱吱声的（squeaky）——踩上去会发出一种叫 squeak 的高音调声响。Charles 通过藏在公园里的扬声器播放这种沙子、海浪和其他令人放松的录音。他打造了一套艺术装置（art installation）——一种旨在为特定空间内的人们带来互动体验的艺术作品。","s":258.66,"e":278.04,"wt":[0,20,40,50,68,98,142,202,250,278,292,318,335,400,434,456,486,530,558,588,658,678,716,746,762,816,867,897,926,943,974,1023,1059,1110,1172,1212,1226,1254,1316,1334,1382,1396,1417,1487,1516,1523,1551,1565,1586,1643,1657,1685,1727,1741,1826]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"By adding more relaxing sounds, Charles' art installation actually made the park feel quieter. It's an example of something that's counterintuitive , meaning it happens differently from how you would expect.","zh":"通过叠加更令人放松的声音，Charles 的艺术装置反而让公园感觉更安静了。这就是“反直觉”（counterintuitive）的一个例子，意思是事情的发展与你的预期相反。","s":278.2,"e":290.22,"wt":[0,14,57,86,144,200,236,256,346,382,382,396,434,506,586,602,628,658,682,726,772,853,880,922,934,976,1048,1076,1098,1129,1158]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"What a great idea! And similar soundscapes are now helping city dwellers relax in other cities too, including Tokyo and Barcelona. Now Neil, you asked me a question about a very quiet sound – a whisper. So, are you going to reveal the correct answer?","zh":"真是个绝妙的主意！如今类似的声景设计也在帮助东京和巴塞罗那等其他城市的居民放松身心。好了 Neil，你之前问了我一个关于极轻声音——耳语——的问题。现在要揭晓正确答案了吗？","s":290.42,"e":306.72,"wt":[0,0,26,74,146,171,204,240,280,314,356,384,432,466,488,520,558,589,664,702,730,854,871,922,947,988,994,1014,1066,1070,1116,1154,1196,1237,1267,1275,1371,1379,1399,1419,1452,1465,1505,1525,1566]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"I asked you how loud a whisper is.","zh":"我问你低声耳语有多大音量。","s":306.81,"e":310.44,"wt":[0,11,65,114,154,208,220,313]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yes, and I said 13 decibels .","zh":"对，我猜的是 13 分贝。","s":310.72,"e":313.3,"wt":[0,21,50,59,97,155,211]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, 13 is an unlucky number and you are wrong.","zh":"嗯，13 是个不吉利的数字，你猜错了。","s":313.3,"e":316.08,"wt":[0,32,68,68,106,130,170,188,204,228]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Ah!","zh":"啊！","s":316.4,"e":317,"wt":[0]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"It was actually 30. OK. Let's recap the vocabulary we've learned, starting with decibel – the unit for measuring sound.","zh":"正确答案其实是 30 分贝。好了，让我们复习一下本期学到的词汇，首先是 decibel（分贝）——衡量声音的单位。","s":317,"e":325.72,"wt":[0,33,65,152,260,293,327,362,383,452,486,534,589,617,655,678,699,727,756,826]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Someone who dresses loud likes to wear clothes with bright colours and bold designs.","zh":"如果说某人 dresses loud（穿着张扬），意思是他喜欢穿颜色鲜艳、设计大胆的衣服。","s":325.78,"e":330.78,"wt":[0,38,56,122,172,196,212,230,268,296,326,340,362,416]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"If you can't handle something, you find it difficult to deal with.","zh":"如果你 can't handle 某件事，说明你觉得它很难应付或承受。","s":330.84,"e":334.36,"wt":[0,16,34,64,100,162,182,202,221,274,288,312]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"An art installation is an artwork designed to create an interactive experience in a certain space.","zh":"art installation（艺术装置）是指设计用来在特定空间内营造互动体验的艺术作品。","s":334.44,"e":340.38,"wt":[0,13,32,111,124,137,183,236,249,286,301,374,440,453,459,522]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"The adjective squeaky describes things which make a high-pitched sound like a mouse.","zh":"形容词 squeaky 用来形容像老鼠一样发出尖细吱吱声的东西。","s":340.4,"e":345.94,"wt":[0,28,86,156,214,252,258,308,340,421,472,500,508]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Squeak, squeak! And finally, if you call something counterintuitive , you mean it happens differently from how you would expect. Once again, our six minutes are up, but if you'd like to hear more about this and many other trending topics, you'll find more episodes and learning activities on our website, bbclearningenglish.com. See you again soon. But for now, it's goodbye.","zh":"吱吱，吱吱！最后，如果你称某件事 counterintuitive（反直觉的），意思是它的运行方式或结果和你预想的不一样。六分钟又到了，如果你想了解更多关于这个话题以及其他热门话题的内容，可以访问我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 获取更多节目和学习活动。我们很快再见，今天就到这里，再见。","s":346.04,"e":366.36,"wt":[0,16,156,175,220,228,246,267,314,384,407,422,443,453,489,556,581,600,628,656,744,766,790,816,839,862,874,910,928,946,964,982,998,1024,1048,1076,1101,1118,1140,1190,1234,1286,1312,1338,1378,1424,1454,1488,1522,1536,1566,1624,1846,1846,1860,1890,1926,1946,1970,2010,2016]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Goodbye...!","zh":"再见……！","s":366.36,"e":367.88,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0nmfyjw.mp3"},{"epNum":28,"id":"ep-260514","date":"2026-05-14","title":"How reading shapes your brain","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260514","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260514_6_minute_english_how_reading_shapes_your_brain_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260514_6_minute_english_how_reading_shapes_your_brain_worksheet_.pdf","summary":"Do you remember what it was like before you learnt to read? It's hard to think back that far! Scientific research has shown that learning to read changes the way our brains work. Not only that, but depending on the language you are reading, your brain changes in different ways. Becca and Georgie discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"The longest novel in the world is widely thought to be by French author Marcel Proust. Its title has been translated into English as Remembrance of Things Past, and more recently as In Search of Lost Time. But how many words does the book contain?","options":["a) 130,000","b) 1.3 million","c) 13 million"],"topic":"文化教育","titleZh":"阅读如何重塑你的大脑","summaryZh":"你还记得自己学会阅读之前是什么感觉吗？要回想那么久远的事可真不容易！科学研究表明，学习阅读会改变我们大脑的运作方式。不仅如此，根据你所阅读的语言不同，大脑发生的变化也各不相同。Becca 和 Georgie 探讨了这一现象，并教你一些实用的新词汇。","questionZh":"公认的世界上最长的小说出自法国作家马塞尔·普鲁斯特之手。书名在英文中曾译作《往事追忆》（Remembrance of Things Past），近来则译为《追忆似水年华》（In Search of Lost Time）。请问这本书一共包含多少字？","optionsZh":["a) 13万字","b) 130万字","c) 1300万字"],"answer":{"letter":"b","explanationZh":"节目中 Georgie 猜测普鲁斯特的这部巨著约有 130 万字（1.3 million），Becca 确认她答对了，还补充说书中甚至有长达 900 多个词的超级长句。"},"vocab":[{"word":"natural","defEn":"coming from nature; developed without special training","pos":"adj.","zh":"天生的，自然形成的","exampleEn":"We think of language as natural, and reading is written language so it must be natural. But it isn't.","ipa":"/ˈnætʃərəl/"},{"word":"dedicated","defEn":"designed and used for one particular purpose","pos":"adj.","zh":"专用的，专门设计的","exampleEn":"From an evolutionary timescale, our brain hasn't had enough time to develop a dedicated reading brain.","ipa":"/ˈdedɪkeɪtɪd/"},{"word":"co-opt","defEn":"involve someone or something, sometimes against their will","pos":"v.","zh":"征用，借用，拉拢（使其参与某项任务）","exampleEn":"To build a reading brain network, we co-opt parts of the brain involved in vision and auditory processing.","ipa":"/kəʊˈɒpt/"},{"word":"circuit","defEn":"a system of connections","pos":"n.","zh":"回路，神经环路；电路","exampleEn":"It's a beautiful example of how the brain's circuit reflects the requirements of Chinese.","ipa":"/ˈsɜːkɪt/"},{"word":"inevitably","defEn":"in a way that cannot be stopped or avoided","pos":"adv.","zh":"不可避免地，必然地","exampleEn":"The visual qualities of Chinese symbols inevitably mean more visual areas of the brain are developed.","ipa":"/ɪnˈevɪtəbli/"},{"word":"intricate","defEn":"having lots of detail","pos":"adj.","zh":"错综复杂的，精致细密的","exampleEn":"Maryanne describes the symbolic Chinese characters as beautifully intricate.","ipa":"/ˈɪntrɪkət/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"at the moment","zh":"目前、眼下（比 right now 更地道自然，雅思口语谈近况和生活习惯时高频使用）","contextEn":"I would love to read more, but I don't read very much at the moment."},{"en":"be born with","zh":"天生具有、与生俱来（讨论天赋、本能或科学话题时的核心表达）","contextEn":"We're born with the potential to do it and then we learn."},{"en":"compared to","zh":"与……相比（用于在句尾或句首引出对比对象，雅思阅读与写作极常见）","contextEn":"This changes our brain structure compared to someone who hasn't learned to read."},{"en":"suffer a stroke","zh":"突发中风（地道的医学疾病搭配，英语中中风、心脏病常用 suffer）","contextEn":"The man suffered a stroke, which affected parts of his brain, including his ability to read Chinese."}],"sentences":[{"en":"The longest novel in the world is widely thought to be by French author Marcel Proust, a book which, when translated into English, means Remembrance of Things Past.","zh":"公认的世界上最长的小说出自法国作家马塞尔·普鲁斯特之手，这本书的书名译成英文就是《往事追忆》（《追忆似水年华》）。","coreEn":"The longest novel in the world is widely thought to be by French author Marcel Proust.","coreZh":"世界上最长的小说普遍被认为是法国作家普鲁斯特所著。","cars":[{"text":"a book which, when translated into English, means Remembrance of Things Past","hook":"逗号同位语 + 关系词 which","noteZh":"重新指代前面那本“最长的小说”，并补充说明它的英文译名含义"},{"text":"when translated into English","hook":"逗号插入语（嵌套的时间状语）","noteZh":"插在 which 和动词 means 之间，限定“当它被译成英文时”"}],"tipZh":"看到关系词 which 后面紧跟两个逗号夹着一个小短语（when translated...），先把插入语暂时捂住，就能一眼看到 which means 的主干连接。"},{"en":"Scientific studies suggest that when we're born, our brains already have the networks that allow our eyes to see and our vocal cords to produce sounds, but not with the pathways we need to read.","zh":"科学研究表明，当我们出生时，大脑里就已经具备了让眼睛视物、让声带发声的神经网络，但并没有阅读所需的神经通路。","coreEn":"Scientific studies suggest that our brains already have the networks, but not the pathways.","coreZh":"研究表明，我们的大脑已有某些网络，但没有另一些通路。","cars":[{"text":"when we're born","hook":"状语从句 when ...","noteZh":"交代时间点：“在我们出生时”"},{"text":"that allow our eyes to see and our vocal cords to produce sounds","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"修饰 networks，说明天生具备的网络负责“看”和“发声”两项功能"},{"text":"we need to read","hook":"省略关系词 that 的定语从句","noteZh":"修饰 pathways，点明缺失的是“我们阅读所需的”通路"}],"tipZh":"科研科普类句子常在 but 前后形成“已有 A，却无 B”的对比，听力和阅读中注意捕捉 but not 后面的转折核心。"},{"en":"And so, to build a reading brain network, we co-opt parts of the brain involved in vision and auditory processing, and language, and attention and affect.","zh":"因此，为了搭建阅读脑网络，我们会“征用”大脑中负责视觉、听觉处理以及语言、注意力和情感的各个区域。","coreEn":"We co-opt parts of the brain.","coreZh":"我们征用大脑的某些部分。","cars":[{"text":"to build a reading brain network","hook":"句首不定式 to do","noteZh":"点明这样做的目的：“为了搭建阅读脑网络”"},{"text":"involved in vision and auditory processing, and language, and attention and affect","hook":"-ed 修饰（过去分词短语）","noteZh":"贴在 parts of the brain 后面，列举被征用的脑区原本负责的五大功能"}],"tipZh":"involved in ... 是雅思阅读中最高频的后置定语之一，直接理解成“参与……的 / 负责……的”即可快速过句。"},{"en":"It's a beautiful example of how the brain's circuit reflects the requirements of Chinese, which inevitably means more visual memory and visual processing of those beautifully intricate symbols or characters.","zh":"这是一个绝佳的例子，展现了大脑神经回路如何反映中文的特点——这不可避免地意味着需要更多的视觉记忆，以及对那些精美繁复的符号或汉字的视觉处理。","coreEn":"It's a beautiful example of how the brain's circuit reflects the requirements of Chinese.","coreZh":"这是大脑回路如何反映中文特点的绝佳例子。","cars":[{"text":"how the brain's circuit reflects the requirements of Chinese","hook":"疑问词 how 引导宾语从句","noteZh":"紧跟介词 of，说明这究竟是什么现象的例子"},{"text":"which inevitably means more visual memory and visual processing","hook":"关系词 which（逗号非限制性定语从句）","noteZh":"顺承前文，解释“适应中文要求”必然带来的结果"},{"text":"of those beautifully intricate symbols or characters","hook":"介词短语 of ...","noteZh":"补充说明视觉处理的对象正是精致复杂的汉字"}],"tipZh":"雅思学术阅读里，逗号 + which inevitably means ... 是典型的“推演结果”句式，可以用来直接定位因果题答案。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Hello. Welcome to 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Becca.","zh":"大家好，欢迎收听 BBC 英语教学的《六分钟英语》。我是 Becca。","s":2.56,"e":13.82,"wt":[0,662,686,704,734,766,818,854,934,977,1045,1068]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"And I'm Georgie. Remember, you can find all this episode's vocabulary along with a transcript and worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"我是 Georgie。别忘了，你可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 上找到本期节目的全部词汇、文稿和学习单。","s":13.96,"e":23.12,"wt":[0,20,40,104,149,162,183,205,222,244,292,374,408,432,441,518,535,604,619,642,696]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Now, Becca, do you read a lot?","zh":"那么 Becca，你平时读书多吗？","s":23.2,"e":25,"wt":[0,23,66,90,116,128,144]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Hmm, I don't read often. I just feel like I don't have the time, Georgie. How about yourself?","zh":"嗯，我不太常看书。我总觉得没时间，Georgie。你自己呢？","s":25.06,"e":31,"wt":[0,31,84,116,156,216,236,252,270,270,300,300,313,334,376,458,479,514]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"I would love to read more, but I don't read very much at the moment. I read mostly before bed because I feel like it helps me relax and go to sleep. And today we're talking all about reading. We'll be hearing from some experts about how reading can change our brains, and as usual we'll be learning some useful new words and phrases.","zh":"我很想多读点书，但眼下读得也不算多。我大多在睡前看书，因为我觉得这能帮我放松下来、更好入睡。今天我们要聊的就是阅读。我们将听听专家怎么说阅读如何改变我们的大脑，当然还会照例学习一些实用的新词和短语。","s":31.05,"e":50.86,"wt":[0,4,37,65,79,107,215,233,247,269,269,269,294,308,329,395,403,437,491,547,567,609,627,629,647,667,697,741,781,793,809,833,909,1013,1037,1060,1094,1115,1136,1194,1222,1233,1274,1297,1320,1361,1390,1407,1448,1465,1500,1527,1623,1639,1655,1689,1689,1729,1737,1791,1809,1859,1889,1913]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Let's start with a quiz question. The longest novel in the world is widely thought to be by French author Marcel Proust, a book which, when translated into English, means Remembrance of Things Past. But how many words does the book contain? Is it: a) 130,000, b) 1.3 million, or c) 13 million?","zh":"先来一道问答考考你。公认的世界上最长的小说出自法国作家马塞尔·普鲁斯特之手，书名译成英文是《往事追忆》（《追忆似水年华》）。请问这本书一共有多少字？是 a) 13万字，b) 130万字，还是 c) 1300万字？","s":50.94,"e":74.42,"wt":[0,29,64,92,99,127,203,225,274,309,323,344,379,393,435,490,506,527,548,592,645,698,822,844,912,958,1000,1050,1102,1108,1200,1267,1275,1314,1362,1385,1408,1439,1478,1504,1518,1556,1646,1658,1694,1776,1966,2009,2118,2160,2173,2244,2298]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"OK. The longest novel in the world. I still think 13 million words sounds too many, so I'm going to go with b) 1.3 million.","zh":"好吧，世界上最长的小说。我还是觉得1300万字听起来太多了，所以我选 b) 130万字。","s":74.53,"e":83.7,"wt":[0,40,65,123,164,180,205,271,279,320,361,411,463,499,549,585,643,652,665,665,671,715,739,783,848]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Alright. We'll find out at the end of the programme. Now, we might think of reading as like speaking. We're born with the potential to do it and then we learn. It's natural . If something is natural , it's something you were born with or that comes from nature.","zh":"好的，我们在节目最后揭晓答案。平时我们可能会觉得阅读和说话差不多：我们天生具备这种潜能，然后自然而然就学会了，这是天生的（natural）。如果说某件事是 natural，意思就是你与生俱来或源自自然的。","s":83.7,"e":99.56,"wt":[0,56,92,121,143,157,179,203,215,237,334,372,387,425,463,478,533,548,568,645,680,708,736,757,818,834,848,876,897,924,939,998,1025,1063,1098,1118,1160,1189,1226,1248,1275,1312,1312,1330,1362,1418,1432,1456,1488,1518]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"But Maryanne Wolf, author of the book Reader Come Home, says that this isn't true.","zh":"但是《读者归来》（Reader Come Home）一书的作者 Maryanne Wolf 却表示，事实并非如此。","s":99.76,"e":105.76,"wt":[0,25,68,124,124,132,148,188,236,268,338,376,394,430,480]},{"speaker":"Maryanne Wolf","en":"We think of language as natural , and reading is written language so it must be natural . But it isn't. It isn't natural at all.","zh":"我们认为语言是天生的，而阅读是书面语言，所以它一定也是天生的。但其实不是，它一点也不天生。","s":105.96,"e":116.62,"wt":[0,18,50,82,102,150,217,257,366,402,428,482,524,542,560,597,615,663,706,720,720,844,868,929,950,984]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Scientific studies suggest that when we're born, our brains already have the networks that allow our eyes to see and our vocal cords to produce sounds, but not with the pathways we need to read.","zh":"科学研究表明，当我们出生时，大脑里就已经具备了让眼睛视物、让声带发声的神经网络，但并没有我们阅读所需的神经通路。","s":117.06,"e":129.16,"wt":[0,57,104,151,178,205,238,278,298,338,385,412,432,499,523,562,588,620,637,661,685,709,750,788,804,856,946,961,976,996,1012,1080,1095,1128,1148]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Let's hear more from psychologist and neuroscientist Rebecca Gotlieb, speaking to the BBC World Service.","zh":"我们来听听心理学家兼神经科学家 Rebecca Gotlieb 在接受 BBC 国际台采访时的进一步解读。","s":129.32,"e":135.3,"wt":[0,24,42,70,100,156,178,256,305,366,410,424,452,484,534]},{"speaker":"Rebecca Gotlieb","en":"From an evolutionary timescale, our brain hasn't had enough time to develop a dedicated reading brain. And so, to build a reading brain network, we co-opt parts of the brain involved in vision and auditory processing, and language, and attention and affect. Reading is really a whole brain process. It involves activation in all four lobes of the cortex. The process of developing a reading brain alters everything, from brain activity to brain structure and brain connectivity. The power of deep reading is really fundamental to our humanity. When we read deeply, we change our brains and we change who we are.","zh":"从进化的时间尺度来看，我们的大脑还没有足够的时间演化出一个专门用来阅读的大脑区域。因此，为了搭建阅读脑网络，我们会征用大脑中负责视觉、听觉处理、语言、注意力以及情感的部分。阅读其实是一个全脑参与的过程，它会激活大脑皮层的全部四个脑叶。培养阅读大脑的过程会改变一切——从脑部活动到大脑结构，再到神经连接。深度阅读的力量对人性而言至关重要。当我们深度阅读时，我们不仅改变了大脑，也改变了自己成为什么样的人。","s":135.64,"e":182.28,"wt":[0,20,47,118,144,166,184,230,248,290,320,408,424,475,558,600,656,682,698,713,752,761,810,852,921,939,973,1003,1019,1042,1091,1146,1162,1229,1262,1353,1450,1496,1566,1610,1694,1740,1848,1878,1887,1911,1917,1971,2025,2126,2155,2242,2338,2373,2404,2442,2501,2523,2555,2641,2686,2706,2723,2770,2786,2822,2918,3032,3062,3140,3182,3274,3383,3438,3557,3593,3625,3750,3772,3812,3828,3860,3908,3932,3980,4069,4085,4109,4202,4220,4244,4271,4325,4338,4392,4411,4466,4493,4511,4566,4592,4611]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Rebecca says that our brains haven't evolved to include a dedicated reading brain. Dedicated here means designed and used for one particular purpose.","zh":"Rebecca 说，我们的大脑还没有进化出一个“专用的”（dedicated）阅读脑区。这里的 dedicated 意思是专门为某一特定用途设计和使用的。","s":182.36,"e":191.84,"wt":[0,45,71,97,116,155,200,248,264,325,330,402,460,528,589,616,650,705,725,752,772,792,859]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"So, because we don't have a part of the brain designed specifically for reading, when we learn to read, we co-opt other parts of the brain. Co-opt here means to include someone or something, often against their will.","zh":"所以，正因为我们没有专门负责阅读的脑区，当我们学阅读时，就会“征用”（co-opt）大脑的其他区域。这里的 co-opt 指把某人或某物拉进来派上用场，有时甚至是不顾对方意愿的。","s":192.05,"e":203.94,"wt":[0,6,46,57,85,105,113,136,147,164,192,238,305,322,373,396,424,448,461,500,518,552,585,618,631,651,709,743,769,802,815,862,924,935,1002,1040,1093,1131]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Right. Learning to read means using lots of different parts of the brain that are designed for other things, and this changes our brain structure compared to someone who hasn't learned to read.","zh":"没错。学习阅读意味着调用大脑中许多原本负责其他功能的区域，与从未学过阅读的人相比，这会改变我们的大脑结构。","s":204.02,"e":214.98,"wt":[0,78,90,134,168,196,224,246,257,308,336,347,364,392,414,431,488,503,538,580,601,629,678,699,734,797,853,867,916,937,984,1012,1028]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"And the language we read also shapes our brain. Chinese characters, for example, use symbols instead of letters of the alphabet to represent words and ideas.","zh":"而且我们所阅读的语言也会塑造大脑。例如，汉字使用的是符号而非拼音字母来表达词语和概念。","s":215.04,"e":224.8,"wt":[0,10,44,50,92,130,162,202,222,280,325,396,420,477,500,574,618,641,652,666,724,746,774,840,862,896]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Research suggests that learning to read these symbols activates different areas of the brain to reading an alphabet-based system.","zh":"研究表明，学习阅读这类符号文字所激活的大脑区域，与阅读字母文字系统所激活的区域是不同的。","s":225.36,"e":232.38,"wt":[0,48,85,104,137,145,171,203,248,306,364,396,409,428,460,473,518,531,620]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Scientists studied a bilingual man who could read and speak Chinese and English. The man suffered a stroke, which affected parts of his brain, including his ability to read Chinese. But amazingly, he was still able to read English.","zh":"科学家研究了一名精通中英双语读写和口语的男子。这名男子突发中风，大脑部分区域受损，其中包括他阅读中文的能力。但令人惊奇的是，他依然能够阅读英文。","s":232.64,"e":248.32,"wt":[0,52,80,118,174,202,218,244,278,310,360,410,472,556,566,586,634,646,693,732,784,790,802,850,911,972,1002,1036,1062,1100,1198,1226,1320,1336,1362,1404,1434,1456,1492]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Maryanne Wolf explains more to the BBC World Service.","zh":"Maryanne Wolf 向 BBC 国际台做了进一步解释。","s":248.58,"e":252.6,"wt":[0,38,96,124,158,170,190,264,272]},{"speaker":"Maryanne Wolf","en":"It's a beautiful example of how the brain's circuit reflects the requirements of Chinese, which inevitably means more visual memory and visual processing of those beautifully intricate symbols or characters.","zh":"这是一个绝佳的例子，展现了大脑的神经回路如何反映中文的特点——这不可避免地意味着需要更多的视觉记忆，以及对那些精美繁复的符号或汉字的视觉处理。","s":252.82,"e":272.74,"wt":[0,0,42,96,132,160,230,262,354,494,584,620,734,794,904,946,1051,1154,1198,1208,1354,1408,1462,1469,1491,1543,1682,1812,1868,1888]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Maryanne says that the brain's circuit is shaped by learning to read Chinese. A circuit is a system of connections.","zh":"Maryanne 说，大脑的神经回路（circuit）会因学习阅读中文而被重塑。circuit 指的是一个相互连接的系统（回路）。","s":273.05,"e":279.78,"wt":[0,43,69,91,109,157,193,213,253,269,301,323,360,431,439,500,509,516,560,574]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"The visual qualities of Chinese symbols inevitably mean more visual areas of the brain are developed. Inevitably means in a way that cannot be stopped or avoided.","zh":"汉字符号的视觉特征不可避免地（inevitably）意味着大脑中更多与视觉相关的区域会得到开发。inevitably 的意思是无法阻止或避免地、必然地。","s":279.82,"e":290.92,"wt":[0,23,66,114,142,196,247,319,348,377,420,456,470,490,526,547,682,762,808,818,858,888,910,940,970,1008,1038]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Maryanne describes the symbolic Chinese characters as beautifully intricate . If something is intricate , it has lots of detail.","zh":"Maryanne 形容表意汉字美得精妙繁复（intricate）。如果说某样东西是 intricate，就是指它包含大量精细的细节。","s":291.05,"e":298.8,"wt":[0,47,93,127,165,221,255,313,389,441,481,489,519,533,591,619,635,660,687,709]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"And something which also has lots of detail, or certainly lots of words – I asked you, Georgie, how many words are in Marcel Proust's Remembrance of Things Past.","zh":"说到同样细节满满、或者说字数极多的作品——Georgie，我之前问过你，马塞尔·普鲁斯特的《追忆似水年华》一共有多少字。","s":298.9,"e":309.94,"wt":[0,25,84,126,162,185,200,226,268,291,354,372,405,440,466,476,526,576,636,658,692,728,748,774,824,889,968,994,1036]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"I said 1.3 million.","zh":"我猜的是 130 万字。","s":310.05,"e":312.3,"wt":[0,5,46,136]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"And you were correct!","zh":"你答对了！","s":312.58,"e":314.2,"wt":[0,28,52,94]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Yay!","zh":"耶！","s":314.2,"e":314.7,"wt":[0]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"The book also contains lots of very long sentences, including one with over 900 words.","zh":"这本书里还有许多极长的句子，其中有一句竟然超过900个词。","s":314.7,"e":321.22,"wt":[0,19,52,85,152,182,201,234,267,358,412,438,446,523,599]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"One sentence with 900 words? That is a lot. OK. It's time to recap the language we learned during this programme, starting with natural , which describes something you were born with or that comes from nature.","zh":"一个句子就有900个词？那可真是太长了。好了，现在来复习一下本期节目学到的词汇，首先是 natural（天生的），形容你与生俱来或源自自然的事物。","s":321.38,"e":333.54,"wt":[0,24,71,95,148,194,212,230,253,307,328,354,381,394,428,448,502,516,564,605,632,706,735,750,799,828,854,916,920,934,954,982,1016,1072,1076,1100,1136]},{"speaker":"Dedicated","en":"can describe something that is designed and used for one particular purpose.","zh":"Dedicated（专用的）可以用来形容专为某一特定目的而设计和使用的事物。","s":334.42,"e":338.84,"wt":[0,19,72,131,157,170,223,242,268,287,306,372]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"If you co-opt someone or something, you involve them, sometimes against their will.","zh":"如果你 co-opt（征用/拉拢）某人或某物，意思就是把他们纳入某件事中，有时甚至违背其原意。","s":338.88,"e":343.62,"wt":[0,12,32,61,106,118,187,206,252,288,345,390,420]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"A circuit is a system of connections – for example, in the brain.","zh":"circuit（回路）是指一个连接系统——比如大脑中的神经回路。","s":343.69,"e":348.1,"wt":[0,5,60,76,84,133,149,219,249,280,337,353,377]},{"speaker":"Inevitably","en":"means in a way that cannot be stopped or avoided.","zh":"Inevitably（不可避免地）指无法阻止或避免地发生。","s":348.92,"e":351.8,"wt":[0,30,40,57,79,104,140,166,194,230]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"And intricate describes something which has lots of detail.","zh":"而 intricate（繁复精细的）形容包含许多细节的事物。","s":351.94,"e":355.56,"wt":[0,29,88,147,206,236,257,272,294]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"That's it for this episode of 6 Minute English. Test what you've learnt with the worksheet on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"本期《六分钟英语》就到这里。快去我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 用学习单测试一下今天学到的内容吧。","s":355.6,"e":362.78,"wt":[0,32,45,65,92,136,152,172,212,276,304,318,324,344,360,380,424,438,474,512]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Thanks for joining us. Goodbye!","zh":"感谢收听，再见！","s":362.93,"e":364.66,"wt":[0,23,40,77,113]},{"speaker":"Georgie","en":"Bye.","zh":"拜拜。","s":364.66,"e":365.28,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0nl4l5w.mp3"},{"epNum":29,"id":"ep-260507","date":"2026-05-07","title":"Searching for life on another planet","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260507","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260507_searching_for_life_on_another_planet_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260507_searching_for_life_on_another_planet_worksheet.pdf","summary":"Does life on another planet exist? Will we ever be able to live on a planet that isn't Earth? We hear from a science and space journalist as she explains the latest research and data collected from planet TRAPPIST-1e. Neil and Becca teach you some new language to understand this potentially habitable planet.","question":"How long would it take to drive to the Sun at 100km an hour? Is it:","options":["a) 70 years","b) 170 years","c) 1,700 years"],"topic":"科技未来","titleZh":"在外星寻找生命迹象","summaryZh":"其他星球上存在生命吗？人类有朝一日能移居地球之外的星球吗？本期节目中，一位航天科学记者将为我们解读从系外行星 TRAPPIST-1e 收集到的最新研究与数据。Neil 和 Becca 也会教你一些新词汇，帮你读懂这颗潜在宜居行星的故事。","questionZh":"如果以每小时100公里的速度开车前往太阳，需要多长时间？","optionsZh":["a) 70年","b) 170年","c) 1,700年"],"answer":{"letter":"b","explanationZh":"节目尾声 Neil 公布正确答案：虽然太阳非常遥远，但以每小时 100 公里的速度开车前往只需约 170 年（Becca 猜的 1,700 年多了一位数），因此选 b。"},"vocab":[{"word":"habitable","defEn":"describes a place or habitat that is suitable for something to live","pos":"adj.","zh":"宜居的，适合居住的","exampleEn":"It exists in the habitable zone around its sun, and its sun is called TRAPPIST-1.","ipa":"/ˈhæbɪtəbəl/"},{"word":"zone","defEn":"an area with a particular feature","pos":"n.","zh":"区域，地带","exampleEn":"People who live in earthquake zones have to be prepared for danger.","ipa":"/zəʊn/"},{"word":"potential","defEn":"describes possibly having qualities or abilities to do something","pos":"adj.","zh":"潜在的，有可能的（也可作名词表示“潜力”）","exampleEn":"Scientists have been looking at the potential atmosphere around TRAPPIST-1e.","ipa":"/pəˈtenʃəl/"},{"word":"rule out","defEn":"dismiss the possibility or idea of something","pos":"短语","zh":"排除（……的可能性）","exampleEn":"They've ruled out that it's really full of hydrogen or carbon dioxide.","ipa":"/ruːl aʊt/"},{"word":"dominated","defEn":"controlled by or is the most significant element of","pos":"adj.","zh":"以……为主导的，受……支配的","exampleEn":"And there are some small clues that it might be a nitrogen-dominated atmosphere.","ipa":"/ˈdɒmɪneɪtɪd/"},{"word":"fingers crossed","defEn":"an expression we use to wish for hope or luck","pos":"短语","zh":"祈祷好运，但愿如此（源自交叉手指祈福的手势）","exampleEn":"Fingers crossed it has nitrogen.","ipa":"/ˈfɪŋɡəz krɒst/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"little green men","zh":"外星小绿人（英语流行文化里的经典戏称，指科幻电影中常见的外星人形象）","contextEn":"Well, I'm not sure about the kind of little green men that you see in science fiction movies, but also, I think we cannot be alone in the universe."},{"en":"go for","zh":"选择、决定选（日常口语中做决定、点餐或猜题时极为地道）","contextEn":"Well, Neil, the Sun seems very far away so I'll go for c) 1,700 years."},{"en":"work out","zh":"计算出、推算出、弄明白（高频动词短语，此处指通过模型推算温度）","contextEn":"Use models to work out how warm the planet could be."},{"en":"one step closer to","zh":"离……又近了一步（注意 to 是介词，后面接名词或动名词 -ing）","contextEn":"And then that's one step closer to finding life."}],"sentences":[{"en":"Well, I'm not sure about the kind of little green men that you see in science fiction movies, but also, I think we cannot be alone in the universe.","zh":"嗯，科幻电影里那种“外星小绿人”我不太敢说，但同时我也觉得，宇宙中不可能只有我们人类。","coreEn":"I'm not sure about little green men, but I think we cannot be alone.","coreZh":"我不确定外星小绿人是否存在，但我觉得宇宙里不可能只有我们。","cars":[{"text":"that you see in science fiction movies","hook":"关系词 that","noteZh":"修饰 little green men，指明是“你在科幻片里看到的那种”外星人"},{"text":"in the universe","hook":"介词短语 in ...","noteZh":"限定范围：“在茫茫宇宙之中”"}],"tipZh":"雅思口语遇到拿不准的开放题，用 I'm not sure about A, but also, I think B... 既自然留有余地，又能顺畅引出核心观点。"},{"en":"Scientists have been looking at the potential atmosphere around TRAPPIST-1e and they've ruled out that it's really full of hydrogen or carbon dioxide, which is a great thing because they're greenhouse gases, which would make the planet really hot.","zh":"科学家一直在研究 TRAPPIST-1e 周围可能存在的大气层，并且排除了它富含氢气或二氧化碳的可能性——这可是个好消息，因为它们都是温室气体，会让星球变得极其炙热。","coreEn":"Scientists have been looking at the potential atmosphere and they've ruled out that it's full of hydrogen or carbon dioxide.","coreZh":"科学家一直在研究其潜在大气层，并排除了它充满氢气或二氧化碳的可能。","cars":[{"text":"around TRAPPIST-1e","hook":"介词短语 around ...","noteZh":"限定位置：环绕 TRAPPIST-1e 行星的"},{"text":"which is a great thing","hook":"关系词 which（逗号非限制性定语从句）","noteZh":"评价前面“排除温室气体”这件事：“这是一件很棒的事”"},{"text":"because they're greenhouse gases","hook":"状语从句 because ...","noteZh":"解释为什么是好消息：因为氢气和二氧化碳属于温室气体"},{"text":"which would make the planet really hot","hook":"关系词 which（第二层套娃）","noteZh":"进一步补充温室气体的后果：会让星球热得惊人"}],"tipZh":"口语访谈里常出现连续两个 which + because 串起来的“滚雪球长句”，千万别回头重读，按说话人的逻辑一节一节顺着听就完全清楚。"},{"en":"And there are some small clues that it might be a nitrogen- dominated atmosphere, which, again, is another sort of clue that there could be life there.","zh":"此外，还有一些微小的线索表明那里可能是以氮气为主导的大气层，而这同样是另一个指向那里可能存在生命的线索。","coreEn":"There are some small clues.","coreZh":"有一些微小的线索。","cars":[{"text":"that it might be a nitrogen- dominated atmosphere","hook":"同位语从句 that ...","noteZh":"直接解释前面的 clues 具体是什么内容（可能是以氮气为主的大气）"},{"text":"which, again, is another sort of clue","hook":"关系词 which + 逗号插入语 again","noteZh":"补充评价：氮气大气这件事本身“又是一种线索”"},{"text":"that there could be life there","hook":"同位语从句 that ...","noteZh":"解释 another sort of clue 指向的内容：那里可能存在生命"}],"tipZh":"在抽象名词（clue, sign, evidence, idea）后面紧跟 that + 完整句子，就是同位语从句，直接把它当成“说明这个词具体是什么”来读。"},{"en":"My guess is what we'll do is try and see what other gases are in the atmosphere and use models to work out how warm the planet could be and whether or not it's a temperature that basically could support life, or maybe it's too hot or too cold, but fingers crossed it has nitrogen.","zh":"我猜我们接下来要做的，是试着探测大气层中还有哪些其他气体，并利用模型推算这颗星球有多温暖、其温度究竟能不能孕育生命，还是太热或太冷——但愿它真的有氮气吧。","coreEn":"My guess is we'll try to see other gases and use models to work out the temperature, but fingers crossed it has nitrogen.","coreZh":"我猜我们会探测其他气体并用模型推算温度，但愿它含有氮气。","cars":[{"text":"what other gases are in the atmosphere","hook":"what 引导宾语从句","noteZh":"紧跟 see 后面，说明科学家想搞清楚的第一个问题：大气中还有什么气体"},{"text":"to work out how warm the planet could be","hook":"不定式 to do + how 宾语从句","noteZh":"说明使用模型的目的之一：推算星球有多暖和"},{"text":"and whether or not it's a temperature that basically could support life","hook":"并列宾语从句 whether or not + 关系词 that","noteZh":"模型的第二个推算目标：判断这个温度是否适合生命存活"},{"text":"or maybe it's too hot or too cold","hook":"选择并列分句 or ...","noteZh":"补充另外两种不乐观的可能：太热或太冷"}],"tipZh":"这个54词的口语超长句看着吓人，其实只靠两个动词骨架撑起来：try and see（看气体）+ use models to work out（算温度），抓动词就能瞬间降维打击。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Hello. This is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Neil.","zh":"大家好，欢迎收听 BBC 英语教学的《六分钟英语》。我是 Neil。","s":2.54,"e":11.94,"wt":[0,574,586,608,634,660,698,740,792,836,873,889]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"And I'm Becca. Neil, do you believe that life exists away from planet Earth?","zh":"我是 Becca。Neil，你相信地球之外存在生命吗？","s":12.06,"e":17.7,"wt":[0,18,40,106,159,177,204,266,302,338,394,418,450,502]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, I'm not sure about the kind of little green men that you see in science fiction movies, but also, I think we cannot be alone in the universe. What about you, Becca? What do you think?","zh":"嗯，科幻电影里那种“外星小绿人”我不太敢说，但同时我也认为，宇宙中不可能只有我们人类。你呢 Becca？你怎么看？","s":17.87,"e":30.52,"wt":[0,40,64,88,121,162,186,219,235,284,327,357,387,409,431,446,498,563,631,660,699,761,805,837,837,855,904,923,952,1069,1089,1114,1137,1177,1186,1196,1219]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"I agree, I think that maybe life does exist beyond our planet, but maybe not as we see in alien movies.","zh":"我同意，我觉得地球之外或许真的存在生命，但可能不是外星人电影里演的那副模样。","s":30.64,"e":39.48,"wt":[0,12,76,84,124,156,196,228,264,344,392,412,504,522,545,616,634,660,699,725,790]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Well, in this episode, we'll be learning from science and space experts about whether the conditions for life exist on a planet 40 light years away.","zh":"那么在本期节目中，我们将听航天科学专家探讨一颗距离我们40光年远的行星上是否具备生命存在的条件。","s":39.66,"e":49.48,"wt":[0,22,36,56,94,114,122,162,182,226,249,286,380,416,428,488,522,552,604,640,648,684,786,845,884,920]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Yes, and as usual, you can find a transcript for this episode, along with all the vocabulary and a worksheet, on our website bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"没错，和往常一样，你可以在我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 上找到本期节目的文稿、全部词汇以及学习单。","s":49.78,"e":59.5,"wt":[0,36,57,71,120,141,162,190,250,267,288,316,365,400,428,449,470,548,559,566,638,654,678,751]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"OK, a quiz question for you, Becca. How long would it take to drive a car to the Sun at 100km an hour? Is it: a) 70 years, b) 170 years, or c) 1,700 years?","zh":"好的，Becca，先来一道问答考考你。以每小时100公里的速度开车去太阳，需要多长时间？是 a) 70年，b) 170年，还是 c) 1,700年？","s":59.57,"e":75.86,"wt":[0,23,30,59,117,138,173,235,267,310,317,363,377,441,481,513,548,562,583,659,706,793,811,885,899,925,967,1039,1107,1147,1256,1287,1315,1388,1585]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Well, Neil, the Sun seems very far away so I'll go for c) 1,700 years.","zh":"嗯 Neil，太阳听起来超级远，所以我选 c) 1,700年。","s":75.97,"e":83.18,"wt":[0,55,75,97,133,183,235,289,309,337,367,403,427,507,662]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"I hope you have enough petrol for that. Well, we'll find out the answer to the question later. Now, though, some science and space experts are excited about a planet in a different solar system from our own. Science journalist, Caroline Steele, talks about this discovery on the BBC World Service programme What in the World.","zh":"那但愿你准备了足够的汽油。好了，我们稍后揭晓答案。不过眼下，一些航天科学专家正对另一个恒星系统中的一颗行星感到兴奋不已。科学记者 Caroline Steele 在 BBC 国际台《世界大不同》（What in the World）节目中谈到了这项发现。","s":83.72,"e":102.46,"wt":[0,4,24,41,56,94,118,134,192,223,249,270,286,302,334,344,360,418,508,538,587,612,655,673,704,752,764,815,851,858,902,916,923,1002,1044,1090,1092,1122,1232,1276,1314,1374,1411,1440,1484,1504,1568,1582,1603,1624,1659,1708,1772,1800,1810,1835]},{"speaker":"Caroline Steele","en":"So, scientists have been looking at an exoplanet. So, that's a planet that exists in a solar system that isn't our own, called TRAPPIST-1e, um, and it's a rocky planet like Earth. It's a similar size to Earth, and it exists in the habitable zone around its sun, and its sun is called TRAPPIST-1.","zh":"科学家们一直在观测一颗系外行星——也就是存在于太阳系之外的行星——它名叫 TRAPPIST-1e。这是一颗和地球一样的岩石行星，大小与地球相近，而且位于它所环绕的恒星周围的宜居带内，那颗恒星名叫 TRAPPIST-1。","s":102.69,"e":122.58,"wt":[0,69,79,107,135,175,189,212,289,303,339,352,394,422,464,478,485,523,564,585,619,657,702,741,840,884,910,944,1001,1047,1049,1084,1157,1171,1182,1217,1251,1273,1350,1383,1405,1469,1479,1515,1553,1623,1687,1767,1839,1847,1869,1887,1898,1944]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Caroline explains that scientists have discovered that the planet, TRAPPIST-1e, exists in the habitable zone around its sun. The adjective habitable describes a place or habitat that is suitable for something to live.","zh":"Caroline 解释说，科学家发现 TRAPPIST-1e 这颗行星位于其恒星周围的宜居带（habitable zone）内。形容词 habitable 用来描述适合生物生存的地方或栖息环境。","s":123.92,"e":138.2,"wt":[0,47,94,118,177,204,285,298,326,378,501,555,573,600,668,702,706,740,856,879,948,1017,1074,1093,1131,1145,1200,1231,1246,1298,1324,1326,1342]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Yes, and a zone is an area with a particular feature. For example, people who live in earthquake zones have to be prepared for danger.","zh":"对，zone（区域/地带）指具有某种特定特征的区域。比如，住在地震带（earthquake zones）的居民必须时刻做好防灾准备。","s":138.42,"e":147.18,"wt":[0,28,28,64,97,110,125,153,181,188,260,334,355,406,426,453,486,510,572,613,654,674,694,732,760]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"So, a habitable zone is an area where something could live. Caroline goes on to explain why they think TRAPPIST-1e may be habitable .","zh":"所以，habitable zone（宜居带）就是适合生命存活的区域。Caroline 接着解释了科学家为什么认为 TRAPPIST-1e 可能宜居。","s":147.44,"e":155.94,"wt":[0,31,39,110,141,157,173,203,248,301,326,373,428,455,468,481,529,549,576,616,716,746,766,814]},{"speaker":"Caroline Steele","en":"Scientists have been looking at the potential atmosphere around TRAPPIST-1e and they've ruled out that it's really full of hydrogen or carbon dioxide, which is a great thing because they're greenhouse gases, which would make the planet really hot. And there are some small clues that it might be a nitrogen- dominated atmosphere, which, again, is another sort of clue that there could be life there.","zh":"科学家一直在研究 TRAPPIST-1e 周围可能存在的大气层，并排除了它富含氢气或二氧化碳的可能性——这可是个大好消息，因为它们都属于温室气体，会让星球变得极其炙热。此外，还有一些微小的线索显示它可能拥有以氮气为主导的大气层，而这同样是那里可能存在生命的又一线索。","s":155.94,"e":178.35,"wt":[0,22,32,58,86,175,190,236,287,317,387,402,454,474,491,513,535,569,591,602,647,659,692,742,770,781,786,814,850,932,968,1029,1071,1088,1088,1098,1122,1150,1190,1230,1298,1311,1319,1340,1374,1490,1500,1524,1524,1582,1628,1693,1814,1886,1958,1986,2010,2010,2059,2066,2091,2116,2148,2168,2192,2216]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Caroline Steele says that scientists are looking at the potential atmosphere around TRAPPIST-1e. That is, that they want to find out if the atmosphere is capable or likely to become able to host life. If something has potential , it already has the qualities or abilities to do something. The adjective potential has a similar meaning but refers to future capability.","zh":"Caroline Steele 说，科学家正在研究 TRAPPIST-1e 周围“潜在的”（potential）大气层。也就是说，他们想查明那层大气是否有能力或有可能孕育生命。如果说某事物 has potential（作名词），是指它已经具备做某事的特质或能力；而形容词 potential 意思相近，但更侧重指未来的可能性或潜在能力。","s":178.5,"e":201.22,"wt":[0,34,73,90,114,174,190,230,256,276,354,441,476,622,642,653,664,680,696,718,746,764,774,792,860,873,940,959,1008,1028,1050,1072,1134,1166,1260,1273,1308,1342,1384,1405,1416,1468,1483,1504,1567,1581,1644,1658,1672,1756,1777,1840,1903,1924,1931,1980,2052,2076,2118,2134,2178]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Exactly. We could say the data has potential in that it shows signs of helping the research. But when we talk about potential data, we're referring to data that could help us with future research.","zh":"没错。我们可以说“这些数据很有潜力（has potential）”，意思是它已显现出有助于研究的迹象；但当我们说“潜在数据（potential data）”时，指的是将来可能有助于研究的数据。","s":201.28,"e":212.9,"wt":[0,83,99,141,166,191,224,249,333,340,365,377,409,441,453,492,517,588,602,616,634,662,690,750,790,830,870,892,928,944,948,972,996,1024,1066]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"She also mentions how scientists have ruled out that TRAPPIST-1e is full of hydrogen or carbon dioxide. The phrasal verb rule out means to dismiss the possibility or idea of something. Scientists have found that TRAPPIST-1e is not full of those greenhouse gases, they've ruled it out .","zh":"她还提到科学家已经排除（ruled out）了 TRAPPIST-1e 富含氢气或二氧化碳的可能。动词短语 rule out 的意思是排除某种可能性或想法。科学家发现 TRAPPIST-1e 并没有充满那些温室气体，也就是把这种可能排除了。","s":213.36,"e":231.6,"wt":[0,16,38,82,97,165,191,235,261,296,399,416,451,468,539,556,602,707,722,762,791,829,863,907,924,976,1011,1048,1100,1112,1164,1266,1300,1323,1342,1360,1448,1462,1483,1511,1525,1560,1630,1679,1716,1738,1760,1785]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Indeed. And Caroline goes on to say how the atmosphere of TRAPPIST-1e is dominated by nitrogen. If something is dominated by something else, it is controlled by it or is the most significant element of it. TRAPPIST-1e is full of nitrogen, which is exciting because nitrogen is a sign of life and habitability.","zh":"确实如此。Caroline 接着谈到 TRAPPIST-1e 的大气层以氮气为主导（dominated by nitrogen）。如果一件事物被另一事物 dominated，就是受其控制或以它为最主要的成分。TRAPPIST-1e 富含氮气，这非常令人振奋，因为氮气是生命和宜居性的重要信号。","s":231.6,"e":252.42,"wt":[0,77,118,160,162,178,216,240,240,296,320,338,442,476,532,560,714,722,757,768,832,846,912,952,962,983,1010,1046,1078,1088,1100,1118,1162,1196,1228,1244,1316,1442,1458,1484,1500,1568,1586,1621,1678,1716,1780,1790,1806,1830,1848,1897,1924]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"So, what if nitrogen is actually found on this planet? Caroline Steele has more.","zh":"那么，如果真的在这颗星球上发现了氮气呢？来听听 Caroline Steele 怎么说。","s":252.68,"e":257.96,"wt":[0,15,45,60,121,136,197,235,252,272,344,401,444,471]},{"speaker":"Caroline Steele","en":"My guess is what we'll do is try and see what other gases are in the atmosphere and use models to work out how warm the planet could be and whether or not it's a temperature that basically could support life, or maybe it's too hot or too cold, but fingers crossed it has nitrogen. There's enough of it to keep the planet a decent temperature. And then that's one step closer to finding life.","zh":"我猜我们接下来要做的，是试着探测大气层中还有哪些其他气体，并利用模型推算这颗星球有多温暖、其温度究竟能不能孕育生命，还是太热或太冷——但愿它真的有氮气吧，而且含量足够让星球保持适宜的温度。那样我们就离发现外星生命又近了一步。","s":258.14,"e":278.16,"wt":[0,9,34,46,56,92,111,122,139,156,173,194,222,250,264,282,299,357,382,402,455,463,490,510,530,604,640,680,712,736,752,752,752,766,792,798,870,900,982,999,1034,1054,1116,1116,1116,1124,1139,1149,1216,1216,1216,1249,1304,1318,1336,1398,1448,1491,1505,1519,1533,1560,1583,1626,1633,1676,1755,1764,1776,1819,1856,1872,1906,1917,1956]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Caroline guesses that scientists will focus on the planet's temperature and whether it could support life. She uses the expression fingers crossed to express hope for a positive outcome.","zh":"Caroline 猜测科学家会把重点放在行星的温度以及它能否支持生命存活上。她用了 fingers crossed 这个表达，来寄托对好结果的期盼。","s":278.22,"e":288.84,"wt":[0,35,75,97,154,175,204,215,233,277,339,356,402,431,457,506,555,576,604,625,696,745,794,808,857,885,906,913,988]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"Yes. We often make the physical gesture of crossing our fingers for good luck.","zh":"是的，我们经常真的会做出把手指交叉的手势来祈求好运。","s":289.06,"e":293.62,"wt":[0,28,42,76,102,125,176,212,242,294,318,366,384,415]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"We do. That reminds me Neil, the answer to your question! Fingers crossed that I answered correctly.","zh":"没错。这倒提醒我了 Neil，你的问题还没揭晓答案呢！祈祷我刚才答对了！","s":293.76,"e":299.28,"wt":[0,14,49,78,129,157,192,208,220,238,296,334,386,432,432,466,476]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"I see what you've done there, Becca. Yes, I asked you how long it would take to drive to the Sun at 100km an hour, and you answered c) 1,700 years. Well, the Sun is very far away, but the answer was b) only 170 years.","zh":"我明白你的小心思啦，Becca。没错，我问你以每小时100公里的速度开车去太阳要多久，你选了 c) 1,700年。虽然太阳确实非常遥远，但正确答案其实是 b) “只要”170年。","s":299.35,"e":316.17,"wt":[0,15,29,41,57,83,138,209,241,247,280,299,318,344,357,390,421,437,494,507,534,561,605,687,711,737,755,793,821,829,1048,1129,1168,1187,1206,1220,1261,1279,1331,1357,1383,1423,1449,1486,1521,1600]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Oh well, that's still longer than my lifetime! Now let's have a recap of the language we've learnt in this episode, starting with habitable , which describes a place or habitat that is suitable for something to live.","zh":"好吧，那也还是比我一辈子都长！现在让我们复习一下本期学到的词汇，首先是 habitable（宜居的），形容适合生物居住的地方或栖息地。","s":316.17,"e":328.58,"wt":[0,12,61,110,152,193,213,245,351,366,389,401,416,441,451,466,507,537,561,579,612,671,724,750,799,823,856,916,922,955,968,1014,1040,1073,1113,1132,1173,1189]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"A zone is an area with a particular feature.","zh":"zone（区域/地带）指具有某种特定特征的区域。","s":328.64,"e":331.36,"wt":[0,9,37,52,67,97,127,134,209]},{"speaker":"Potential","en":"describes possibly having qualities or abilities to do something.","zh":"Potential（潜在的）形容可能具备做某事的特质或能力。","s":332.08,"e":335.9,"wt":[0,29,56,150,186,218,266,280,306]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"The phrasal verb rule out means to dismiss the possibility or idea of something.","zh":"动词短语 rule out 的意思是排除某种可能性或想法。","s":335.98,"e":341,"wt":[0,12,58,92,122,150,180,201,254,276,344,373,402,417]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"We also had dominated . If something is dominated by something else, it is controlled by it or is the most significant element of it.","zh":"我们还学了 dominated（占主导的）。如果某物被另一事物 dominated，就是受其控制或以它为最主要的组成部分。","s":341.12,"e":350.02,"wt":[0,18,66,99,176,234,248,290,315,370,382,434,473,482,497,550,568,588,606,622,640,676,722,750,766]},{"speaker":"Neil","en":"And finally, the expression fingers crossed is used to wish for hope or luck. Once again, our six minutes are up, but head over to our website, bbclearningenglish.com, for a quiz and worksheet for this episode. See you soon. Goodbye for now.","zh":"最后，表达 fingers crossed 用来祈求希望或好运。六分钟又到了，欢迎前往我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 获取本期的测验和学习单。我们很快再见，今天就到这里，再见。","s":350.02,"e":364.46,"wt":[0,32,100,134,194,230,266,296,322,344,374,402,432,478,544,562,584,597,616,661,674,704,723,748,773,785,804,861,1022,1038,1045,1080,1096,1162,1182,1206,1290,1303,1317,1350,1386,1401]},{"speaker":"Becca","en":"Bye!","zh":"拜拜！","s":364.46,"e":365.18,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0njskv5.mp3"},{"epNum":30,"id":"ep-260430","date":"2026-04-30","title":"Should we eat ultra-processed food?","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/english/features/6-minute-english_2026/ep-260430","mp3":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260430_6_minute_english_should_we_eat_ultra_processed_food_download.mp3","pdf":"https://downloads.bbc.co.uk/learningenglish/features/6min/260430_6_minute_english_should_we_eat_ultra_processed_food_transcript.pdf","summary":"What is your ultra-processed guilty pleasure? Many of us love home-cooked food, but when we're in a rush, it's often more convenient to eat snacks, or to buy quick, easy meals from the supermarket which we simply have to heat up. These are often ultra-processed forms of food, which recent studies have linked to poor health. Phil and Pippa discuss this and teach you some new vocabulary.","question":"When were fizzy drinks invented?","options":["a) 1772","b) 1872","c) 1972"],"topic":"健康医学","titleZh":"我们该吃超加工食品吗？","summaryZh":"哪种超加工食品是你明知不健康却戒不掉的“罪恶快乐”？很多人都喜欢家常菜，但赶时间的时候，吃点零食或者从超市买份只需加热的快手即食餐往往更省事。这些食物大多属于超加工食品，近期多项研究已将其与健康问题联系起来。Phil 和 Pippa 聊了聊这个话题，并教你一些实用新词。","questionZh":"碳酸饮料是什么时候发明的？","optionsZh":["a) 1772年","b) 1872年","c) 1972年"],"answer":{"letter":"a","explanationZh":"Phil 在节目末尾公布答案：碳酸饮料早在 1772 年就被发明了，当时人们原本试图用碳酸水来预防航海途中的坏血病，所以选 a。"},"vocab":[{"word":"ready meal","defEn":"meal from a supermarket that has already been prepared and can be heated up and eaten later","pos":"n.","zh":"预制即食餐，微波快餐","exampleEn":"With a ready meal, you put it in the microwave for a couple of minutes. It's done.","ipa":"/ˈredi miːl/"},{"word":"from scratch","defEn":"(idiom) from the very beginning, without using anything that has already been made","pos":"短语","zh":"从零开始，从头做起（不使用半成品）","exampleEn":"Sometimes cooking a fresh meal from scratch – that can take a really long time.","ipa":"/frəm skrætʃ/"},{"word":"guilty pleasure","defEn":"something you enjoy but feel embarrassed about, or think you should not do","pos":"短语","zh":"罪恶的快乐（明知不该做或有点不好意思但很享受的事物）","exampleEn":"I'm a chocolate girl. A packet of biscuits – something like that. A cake. That's my guilty pleasure.","ipa":"/ˈɡɪlti ˈpleʒə/"},{"word":"once in a while","defEn":"occasionally, not very often","pos":"短语","zh":"偶尔，每隔一段时间","exampleEn":"Is it OK for me to have a packet of crisps once in a while?","ipa":"/wʌns ɪn ə waɪl/"},{"word":"from time to time","defEn":"occasionally, not very often","pos":"短语","zh":"时不时地，偶尔","exampleEn":"Is there a way for us to still enjoy them from time to time?","ipa":"/frəm taɪm tə taɪm/"},{"word":"everything in moderation","defEn":"(idiom) saying advising that it's best to avoid too much of anything","pos":"短语","zh":"凡事皆应有度，适可而止","exampleEn":"I think the best thing to do is just not to panic. Everything is fine in moderation.","ipa":"/ˈevriθɪŋ ɪn ˌmɒdəˈreɪʃən/"}],"phrases":[{"en":"cut ... out of","zh":"把……从（饮食/生活中）彻底剔除、戒掉（健康与生活方式话题超高频短语）","contextEn":"Should we be cutting ultra-processed foods out of our diets completely, or is there a way for us to still enjoy them from time to time?"},{"en":"now and then","zh":"时不时地、偶尔（与 once in a while / from time to time 完美互换的口语高频词）","contextEn":"It also means I won't feel bad about eating chocolate now and then."},{"en":"I wouldn't have thought ...","zh":"我真没想到……（口语里表示出乎意料的地道虚拟语气，比 I didn't think 更自然）","contextEn":"Wow, that is amazing. I wouldn't have thought it was that long ago."},{"en":"hungry for more","zh":"意犹未尽、渴望获取更多（既可字面指饿了，也常喻指充满求知欲或意犹未尽）","contextEn":"Once again, our six minutes are up, but if you're hungry for more, head over to our website, bbclearningenglish.com."}],"sentences":[{"en":"If you've eaten anything today, then it's likely that some of your food was ultra-processed – food containing artificial ingredients like additives and sweeteners.","zh":"如果你今天已经吃过东西了，那么其中很可能有一部分属于超加工食品——也就是含有添加剂和甜味剂等人工成分的食物。","coreEn":"It's likely that some of your food was ultra-processed.","coreZh":"你的部分食物很可能是超加工食品。","cars":[{"text":"If you've eaten anything today","hook":"状语从句 If ...","noteZh":"设定日常前提条件：“只要你今天吃过任何东西”"},{"text":"food containing artificial ingredients like additives and sweeteners","hook":"破折号同位语 + -ing 修饰","noteZh":"紧跟破折号给 ultra-processed 下定义，用 containing... 解释具体含哪些人工成分"}],"tipZh":"科普文章和雅思阅读中，破折号后面常常直接给出陌生专业词（如 ultra-processed）的通俗解释，遇到生词别慌，往后看破折号！"},{"en":"Ultra-processed foods are things that contain five or more ingredients, and things that you wouldn't find in your average kitchen.","zh":"超加工食品是指含有五种及以上配料、且在普通家庭厨房里根本找不到的那些食物。","coreEn":"Ultra-processed foods are things.","coreZh":"超加工食品是一些特定的东西。","cars":[{"text":"that contain five or more ingredients","hook":"关系词 that（第一并列修饰）","noteZh":"第一个判定标准：含有五种或以上配料"},{"text":"and things that you wouldn't find in your average kitchen","hook":"并列结构 + 关系词 that","noteZh":"第二个判定标准：普通人家厨房里不会出现的配料"}],"tipZh":"雅思口语定义某个概念时，用 X are things that ... and things that ... 这种并列定语从句，比生背词典释义听起来自然流利得多。"},{"en":"That's a lot quicker and easier than cooking from scratch – an idiom meaning to do something from the very beginning without using anything that's already been made.","zh":"这比从零开始做饭要快捷省事得多——from scratch 是一个习语，指不使用任何现成的半成品、从最开头一步步做起。","coreEn":"That's a lot quicker and easier than cooking from scratch.","coreZh":"那比从头做饭快得多、容易得多。","cars":[{"text":"an idiom","hook":"破折号同位语","noteZh":"点明前面 from scratch 的属性是一个习惯用语"},{"text":"meaning to do something from the very beginning","hook":"-ing 修饰（现在分词短语）","noteZh":"解释这个习语的核心含义：“从最开始做某事”"},{"text":"without using anything that's already been made","hook":"介词短语 without + 关系词 that","noteZh":"补充限定条件：“不用任何已经做好的东西”"}],"tipZh":"without doing sth. 后面套定语从句（anything that's already been made）非常凝练，写作中表示“完全不依赖现成……”时可直接套用。"},{"en":"Should we be cutting ultra-processed foods out of our diets completely, or is there a way for us to still enjoy them from time to time ?","zh":"我们是否应该把超加工食品彻底从日常饮食中剔除，还是有办法能让我们偶尔依然享受一下它们？","coreEn":"Should we be cutting ultra-processed foods out, or is there a way to enjoy them?","coreZh":"我们该彻底戒掉超加工食品，还是有办法继续享用？","cars":[{"text":"out of our diets completely","hook":"介词短语 out of ... + 副词","noteZh":"紧贴 cutting，强调“彻底从饮食中清除出去”的程度"},{"text":"for us to still enjoy them from time to time","hook":"不定式复合结构 for sb. to do","noteZh":"修饰 a way，说明这种办法的目标是“让我们依然能时不时享用它们”"}],"tipZh":"雅思大作文开头段引出争议话题时，可以借鉴这种“彻底禁止 A，还是有办法适度保留 A”的二选一设问结构，直接带出辩证立场。"}],"transcript":[{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Hello. This is 6 Minute English from BBC Learning English. I'm Phil.","zh":"大家好，欢迎收听 BBC 英语教学的《六分钟英语》。我是 Phil。","s":2.56,"e":12.36,"wt":[0,592,604,628,650,682,728,754,781,857,896,926]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"And I'm Pippa.","zh":"我是 Pippa。","s":12.36,"e":14.04,"wt":[0,26,60]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"If you've eaten anything today, then it's likely that some of your food was ultra-processed – food containing artificial ingredients like additives and sweeteners. Ultra-processed foods are everywhere, from sliced bread to chocolate biscuits and crisps.","zh":"如果你今天已经吃过东西了，那么其中很可能有一部分属于超加工食品——也就是含有添加剂和甜味剂等人工成分的食物。超加工食品无处不在，从切片面包到巧克力饼干、薯片都是。","s":14.04,"e":30.44,"wt":[0,16,64,104,152,208,208,268,288,322,338,352,390,416,480,607,654,692,758,816,846,882,942,968,1130,1176,1202,1268,1296,1326,1376,1428,1466,1502,1522,1544]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"They might taste good, but the bad news is that ultra-processed foods have been linked to poor health. They often contain lots of sugar and salt and have been linked to problems like obesity and diabetes. So, how can we tell what food is ultra-processed and what's not? Here's health reporter Annabel Rackham on BBC World Service programme What in the World?","zh":"它们或许很好吃，但坏消息是超加工食品已被证实与健康状况不佳有关。这类食品通常含糖、含盐量很高，还与肥胖、糖尿病等问题存在关联。那么，我们怎么分辨哪些食物是超加工的、哪些不是呢？来听听健康记者 Annabel Rackham 在 BBC 国际台《世界大不同》节目中的介绍。","s":30.72,"e":53.37,"wt":[0,18,50,88,127,146,165,184,210,223,249,347,380,406,431,486,501,544,646,664,705,748,766,793,827,847,874,894,921,948,989,1002,1056,1084,1149,1177,1321,1331,1354,1377,1392,1422,1452,1481,1497,1610,1633,1676,1740,1769,1807,1858,1912,1954,1970,1989,2021,2066,2124,2149,2161,2191]},{"speaker":"Annabel Rackham","en":"Ultra-processed foods are things that contain five or more ingredients, and things that you wouldn't find in your average kitchen. It's going to have things on there, like emulsifiers, preservatives, additives, dyes and sweeteners.","zh":"超加工食品是指含有五种及以上配料，且在普通家庭厨房里根本找不到的那些东西。配料表上会出现乳化剂、防腐剂、添加剂、色素和甜味剂之类的成分。","s":53.37,"e":67.04,"wt":[0,85,157,188,219,245,301,327,351,392,479,494,525,547,565,616,647,662,693,747,825,845,845,865,865,883,895,911,933,1061,1160,1221,1249,1273]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Annabel describes ultra-processed foods as things containing ingredients you wouldn't find in your kitchen. Do you eat much ultra-processed food, Pippa, or do you try to avoid it?","zh":"Annabel 把超加工食品形容为含有自家厨房里找不到的配料的食物。Pippa，你平时吃超加工食品多吗，还是会尽量避开？","s":67.2,"e":78.46,"wt":[0,52,109,202,233,245,336,346,415,434,502,536,554,586,718,729,740,760,793,872,902,936,948,960,1004,1030,1057,1089]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"I used to eat a lot of ultra-processed foods, and now I try to cook everything myself and not eat things like chocolate and snacks all day. How about you?","zh":"我以前吃很多超加工食品，但现在我会尽量自己做饭，不再整天吃巧克力、零食之类的东西。你呢？","s":78.53,"e":91.3,"wt":[0,67,67,67,97,107,138,158,313,403,427,451,467,497,579,627,715,723,753,783,813,873,913,1002,1033,1099,1139,1189,1203,1223]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"I think the same. I try to cook things using just normal ingredients, just so you know what's gone into it.","zh":"我也差不多。我尽量只用普通食材做饭，这样至少心里清楚放进去了些什么。","s":91.3,"e":98.02,"wt":[0,52,70,94,106,118,140,150,174,228,278,324,358,420,442,453,470,492,539,580,624]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"In this episode, we'll be discussing ultra-processed food as well as learning some useful new vocabulary. And remember, there's also a quiz and worksheet available on our website, bbclearningenglish.com.","zh":"在本期节目中，我们将探讨超加工食品，同时学习一些实用的新词汇。别忘了，我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 上还提供测验和学习单。","s":98.2,"e":112.1,"wt":[0,17,43,102,134,147,213,311,337,350,376,389,442,476,532,558,672,696,730,776,792,814,854,884,940,1006,1030,1068,1134]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"But now I have a question for you, Pippa. Fizzy drinks, like cola and lemonade, are another example of popular ultra-processed foods, but when were fizzy drinks invented? Was it: a) 1772, b) 1872, or c) 1972?","zh":"不过现在我有个问题考考你，Pippa。像可乐和柠檬汽水这样的碳酸饮料，是另一类广受欢迎的超加工食品，那碳酸饮料是什么时候发明的呢？是 a) 1772年，b) 1872年，还是 c) 1972年？","s":112.32,"e":134.35,"wt":[0,26,52,60,95,103,172,198,238,306,346,384,414,462,484,540,564,615,666,680,731,848,980,1012,1050,1088,1135,1191,1298,1300,1382,1447,1700,1796,1978,2005,2178]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Well, I think it was before 1972, but 1772 sounds like too early, so I'm going to say b) 1872.","zh":"嗯，我觉得肯定在1972年之前，但1772年听起来又太早了，所以我猜 b) 1872年。","s":135.22,"e":146.54,"wt":[0,54,84,146,172,230,304,422,468,618,660,726,758,786,808,808,840,876,907,990]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"We'll find out the answer at the end of the programme. One reason for the popularity of ultra-processed food is convenience. Let's hear more from health reporter Annabel, who talks here with Hannah Gelbart, presenter of BBC World Service's What in the World.","zh":"我们将在节目最后揭晓答案。超加工食品如此受欢迎的原因之一就是方便。下面来听听健康记者 Annabel 与 BBC 国际台《世界大不同》主持人 Hannah Gelbart 的进一步对话。","s":146.74,"e":163.72,"wt":[0,28,48,48,100,138,154,192,214,230,258,344,374,415,434,455,523,536,637,664,677,850,874,902,930,930,947,1006,1085,1107,1143,1172,1201,1244,1315,1395,1407,1433,1477,1565,1600,1617,1643]},{"speaker":"Annabel Rackham","en":"So, I do think convenience – it is the main issue there. And again, with a ready meal , you put it in the microwave for a couple of minutes. It's done. It's hot. It serves you. Whereas, you know, sometimes cooking a fresh meal from scratch – that can take a really long time.","zh":"所以我确实认为“方便”是这里最核心的原因。再想想预制即食餐（ready meal），放进微波炉热上几分钟，就好了，热乎乎的，能直接填饱肚子。相反，你知道，有时候从零开始做一顿新鲜饭菜（from scratch）真的要花很长时间。","s":163.96,"e":177.38,"wt":[0,14,21,35,72,118,138,153,167,189,218,255,312,332,362,372,384,410,427,442,456,470,479,488,502,545,559,563,591,600,652,680,709,733,772,786,820,878,899,908,924,980,1024,1084,1108,1108,1111,1146,1167,1192,1210,1235,1241,1278,1303]},{"speaker":"Hannah Gelbart","en":"What's your ultra-processed guilty food?","zh":"你明知不健康却忍不住想吃的超加工食品是什么？","s":177.38,"e":179.37,"wt":[0,18,36,127,156]},{"speaker":"Annabel Rackham","en":"I'm a chocolate girl. A packet of biscuits – something like that. A cake. That is my... that's my guilty pleasure .","zh":"我是个巧克力控。一包饼干——之类的，还有蛋糕。那就是我的……我的“罪恶快乐”（guilty pleasure）。","s":179.37,"e":186.66,"wt":[0,19,27,102,141,151,170,179,238,269,281,299,357,377,426,453,470,509,545,587,641,689]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Ultra-processed foods like ready meals are convenient. A ready meal is a meal from a supermarket that has already been prepared and can be heated up quickly in a microwave. That's a lot quicker and easier than cooking from scratch – an idiom meaning to do something from the very beginning without using anything that's already been made.","zh":"像预制即食餐（ready meals）这样的超加工食品非常省事。ready meal 指超市里买到的已经制作好、放微波炉里快速加热就能吃的饭菜。这比从头做饭（from scratch）要快捷省事得多——from scratch 是一个习语，指不使用任何现成的半成品、从最开头一步步做起。","s":186.66,"e":207.14,"wt":[0,68,142,164,198,242,281,387,391,425,453,467,474,502,530,537,616,644,665,715,743,800,821,842,856,915,926,970,983,989,1082,1122,1130,1154,1211,1235,1283,1317,1368,1405,1460,1492,1505,1533,1573,1584,1595,1647,1670,1687,1710,1762,1802,1831,1877,1924,1966,1976]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Even though ultra-processed foods are often unhealthy, they taste good. That's why Annabel calls chocolate her guilty pleasure . A guilty pleasure is something you enjoy but think you shouldn't and feel a little embarrassed about.","zh":"尽管超加工食品往往不太健康，但它们味道很好。正因如此，Annabel 把巧克力称作自己的“罪恶快乐”（guilty pleasure）。guilty pleasure 指你很享受、但心里觉得不该碰且略感不好意思的事物。","s":207.52,"e":223.14,"wt":[0,26,36,144,188,206,258,386,392,462,532,562,592,652,680,700,750,810,855,893,898,942,1004,1019,1088,1112,1226,1246,1264,1286,1306,1329,1360,1367,1424,1494]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Ultra-processed food is a tricky topic. We know these foods have been linked to poor health, but at the same time they're cheap, convenient and taste good. So, what should we do? Here's Hannah and Annabel discussing this for BBC programme What in the World:","zh":"超加工食品是个让人纠结的话题。我们知道这类食物与健康问题相关，但与此同时它们又便宜、方便、好吃。那我们该怎么办呢？来听听 Hannah 和 Annabel 在 BBC《世界大不同》节目中的讨论：","s":223.52,"e":240.16,"wt":[0,58,92,104,146,204,276,290,318,353,388,406,430,438,480,514,584,596,610,632,668,696,738,810,882,918,964,1038,1054,1072,1072,1098,1216,1248,1278,1278,1356,1394,1414,1432,1510,1558,1558,1566,1610]},{"speaker":"Hannah Gelbart","en":"Is it OK for me to have a packet of crisps once in a while ? Should we be cutting ultra-processed foods out of our diets completely, or is there a way for us to still enjoy them from time to time ?","zh":"我偶尔（once in a while）吃一包薯片可以吗？我们是该把超加工食品彻底从饮食中剔除，还是有办法能让我们时不时地（from time to time）继续享受它们？","s":240.3,"e":250.77,"wt":[0,8,26,51,66,80,92,102,125,140,150,204,228,234,255,287,324,324,324,364,378,466,492,512,522,558,644,710,723,736,769,788,801,820,832,844,875,906,931,956,981,995,1017]},{"speaker":"Annabel Rackham","en":"I think the best thing to do is just not to panic. Everything is fine in moderation .","zh":"我觉得最好的做法就是别恐慌。只要适度（in moderation），什么都可以吃一点。","s":250.77,"e":256.32,"wt":[0,5,25,39,39,53,71,97,131,157,173,193,302,361,391,421,441,508]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Hannah asks if it's OK to eat ultra-processed foods once in a while , or from time to time . The phrases once in a while and from time to time mean occasionally – sometimes, but not very often.","zh":"Hannah 问能不能偶尔（once in a while / from time to time）吃一点超加工食品。短语 once in a while 和 from time to time 都是“偶尔”的意思——也就是有时会做，但不常做。","s":256.32,"e":271.02,"wt":[0,52,86,100,138,172,190,216,330,376,400,400,432,466,491,508,536,556,588,622,666,686,766,792,792,832,886,920,959,996,1017,1056,1156,1210,1232,1320,1360,1389,1413]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Annabel replies using the phrase everything in moderation , which advises us that it's best to avoid too much of anything.","zh":"Annabel 回应时用了短语 everything in moderation（凡事有度），意思是提醒我们最好不要对任何事物过度放纵。","s":271.26,"e":279.2,"wt":[0,58,108,140,164,208,287,301,355,380,416,466,480,509,546,587,599,641,666,700,717]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"That sounds sensible to me, and it also means I won't feel bad about eating chocolate now and then. OK. Pippa, it's time to reveal the answer to my question. Now, I asked you when fizzy drinks were invented. You said 1872. I'm afraid the correct answer was 1772. Apparently carbonated water was used to try to prevent scurvy on sea voyages.","zh":"听起来很有道理，这也意味着我偶尔吃点巧克力就不用内疚了。好了 Pippa，该揭晓刚才那道题的答案了。我问你碳酸饮料是什么时候发明的，你猜的是 1872 年。恐怕正确答案是 1772 年。据说当时人们曾试图用碳酸水来预防航海途中的坏血病。","s":279.36,"e":307.4,"wt":[0,20,58,74,112,148,168,181,207,240,247,279,305,325,358,397,464,484,506,585,631,694,730,767,785,840,867,922,940,958,1072,1108,1172,1188,1296,1340,1347,1427,1480,1636,1660,1698,1831,1859,1894,1924,1934,1978,2044,2254,2329,2406,2446,2472,2494,2522,2538,2566,2608,2666,2701,2726]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Wow, that is amazing. I wouldn't have thought it was that long ago.","zh":"哇，太神奇了，我真没想到早在那么久以前就发明了。","s":307.58,"e":312.06,"wt":[0,31,61,124,144,151,211,241,270,288,326,361,391]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"Right. Let's recap the vocabulary we've learned, starting with ready meal – a meal from a supermarket that has already been prepared so you can heat it up quickly.","zh":"好了，让我们复习一下本期学到的词汇，首先是 ready meal（预制即食餐）——超市里已经做好、拿回家快速加热即可食用的饭菜。","s":312.09,"e":321.54,"wt":[0,38,71,173,173,193,226,286,335,367,399,422,441,447,483,511,519,617,627,643,653,687,785,801,815,833,833,847,895]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"If you do something from scratch , you do it from the very beginning, without using anything that's already been made.","zh":"如果你 from scratch（从零开始）做某事，意思是不借助任何现成半成品，从最开头一步步完成。","s":321.97,"e":328.88,"wt":[0,7,23,41,97,130,160,178,198,211,224,251,271,298,360,408,442,519,558,593,623]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"A guilty pleasure is something you enjoy but feel guilty or embarrassed about because you think you shouldn't do it.","zh":"guilty pleasure（罪恶的快乐）指你很喜欢、但觉得自己不该做而感到一丝内疚或不好意思的事物。","s":329.05,"e":336.44,"wt":[0,5,48,109,124,169,201,254,277,307,353,425,451,490,550,572,603,625,641,655]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"The phrases once in a while and from time to time mean occasionally, not very often.","zh":"短语 once in a while 和 from time to time 都是指“偶尔、不常”。","s":336.62,"e":342.88,"wt":[0,24,80,110,110,146,192,216,244,270,310,344,378,499,525,558]},{"speaker":"Phil","en":"And finally, the phrase everything in moderation is used to advise someone that it's best to avoid too much of anything. Once again, our six minutes are up, but if you're hungry for more, head over to our website, bbclearningenglish.com, for more tasty topics and useful vocabulary. See you again soon. But for now, it's goodbye.","zh":"最后，短语 everything in moderation（凡事皆应有度）用来建议别人不要对任何事物过度。六分钟又到了，如果你还意犹未尽，欢迎前往我们的网站 bbclearningenglish.com 探索更多精彩话题和实用词汇。我们很快再见，今天就到这里，再见。","s":342.98,"e":366.42,"wt":[0,23,64,94,137,214,229,307,321,349,363,406,456,490,523,557,576,626,662,696,734,878,896,931,949,970,1016,1038,1055,1076,1090,1132,1175,1196,1225,1254,1283,1297,1318,1378,1614,1634,1674,1722,1774,1806,1862,1982,1982,1990,2014,2060,2072,2092,2156,2178]},{"speaker":"Pippa","en":"Bye!","zh":"拜拜！","s":366.42,"e":366.85,"wt":[0]}],"mp3Fallback":"https://open.live.bbc.co.uk/mediaselector/6/redir/version/2.0/mediaset/audio-nondrm-download-rss-low/proto/https/vpid/p0nhfvh9.mp3"}];
